vity to pain, and food intolerances.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.6010488784458656, "g_worst_claim": 0.3234225908915202, "g_content": 2.2249345779418945, "g_traj_mean": 1.4389492273330688}}], "selected_rollout": 1, "random_sel_rollout": 1, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 70.81, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "82", "question": "symptoms and causes of ibs", "gt": "Symptoms of irritable bowel syndrome include diarrhea (often described as violent episodes of diarrhea), constipation, constipation alternating with diarrhea, belly pains or cramps, usually in the lower half of the belly, that get worse after meals and feel better after a bowel movement, and a lot of gas or bloating. Causes of irritable bowel syndrome are unknown but it is believed to be due to a number of factors including alteration in gastrointestinal (GI) tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances.", "pred": "The causes of IBS are thought to be a combination of abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, and a disruption in the communication between the brain and the GI tract. It is also believed to be due to a number of factors, including alteration in the gastrointestinal (GI) tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances. Risk factors thought to cause IBS include abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (too fast or slow, or too strong), hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities, possibly caused by poor absorption of sugars or acids in food.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "The causes of IBS are thought to be a combination of abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, and a disruption in the communication between the brain and the GI tract. It is also believed to be due to a number of factors, including alteration in the gastrointestinal (GI) tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances. Risk factors thought to cause IBS include abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (too fast or slow, or too strong), hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities, possibly caused by poor absorption of sugars or acids in food.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, the symptoms of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) include diarrhea, constipation, and alternating between the two. Belly pains or cramps, usually in the lower half of the belly, that get worse after meals and feel better after a bowel movement. A lot of gas or bloating.\\n\\nThe causes of IBS are thought to be a combination of abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, and a disruption in the communication between the brain and the GI tract. It is also believed to be due to a number of factors, including alteration in the gastrointestinal (GI) tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances. Risk factors thought to cause IBS include abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (too fast or slow, or too strong), hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities, possibly caused by poor absorption of sugars or acids in food.\", \"Based on the given passages, the symptoms of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) include diarrhea (often described as violent episodes of diarrhea), constipation, and alternating between constipation and diarrhea. People with IBS also experience belly pains or cramps, usually in the lower half of the belly, that get worse after meals and feel better after a bowel movement. Additionally, there is a lot of gas or bloating.\\n\\nThe causes of IBS are not fully understood, but it is believed to be due to a combination of factors. These factors include altered gastrointestinal (GI) tract motility, abnormal signals from the nervous system, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances. Specifically, the passages mention that abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (too fast or slow, or too strong), hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities, possibly caused by poor absorption of sugars or acids in food, are risk factors thought to cause IBS.\", \"Based on the given passages, the symptoms of IBS include diarrhea, constipation, and alternating between constipation and diarrhea. Belly pains or cramps, usually in the lower half of the belly, that get worse after meals and feel better after a bowel movement. A lot of gas or bloating.\\n\\nThe causes of IBS are believed to be due to a number of factors, including alteration in the gastrointestinal (GI) tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances. Risk factors thought to cause IBS include abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (too fast or slow, or too strong), hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities, possibly caused by poor absorption of sugars or acids in food.\", \"Based on the given passages, the symptoms of IBS include diarrhea (often described as violent episodes of diarrhea), constipation, and alternating constipation with diarrhea. Belly pains or cramps, usually in the lower half of the belly, that get worse after meals and feel better after a bowel movement. A lot of gas or bloating.\\n\\nThe causes of IBS are not fully understood but are believed to be due to a number of factors, including alteration in the gastrointestinal (GI) tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances. Risk factors thought to cause IBS include 1) Abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (too fast or slow, or too strong), 2) Hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and 3) Food sensitivities, possibly caused by poor absorption of sugars or acids in food.\"]", "time_sec": 35.711, "generation_time_seconds": 34.826, "selection_time_seconds": 0.876, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389