{"id": "84", "question": "symptoms and causes of ibs", "gt": "Sure, I can help you with that! Here are the symptoms and causes of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the given passages:\n\nSymptoms:\n\n* Diarrhea (often described as violent episodes of diarrhea)\n* Constipation\n* Constipation alternating with diarrhea\n* Belly pains or cramps, usually in the lower half of the belly, that get worse after meals and feel better after a bowel movement\n* A lot of gas or bloating\n\nCauses:\n\n* Abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements\n* Increased awareness of bodily functions\n* Disruption in the communication between the brain and the GI tract\n* Alterations in the GI tract motility\n* Abnormal nervous system signals\n* Increased sensitivity to pain\n* Food intolerances\n* Poor absorption of sugars or acids in food\n\nIt's worth noting that the exact cause of IBS is still unknown, and it's believed to be a complex interaction of various factors.", "pred": "The causes of IBS are thought to be a combination of abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, and a disruption in the communication between the brain and the GI tract. It is also believed to be due to a number of factors, including alteration in the gastrointestinal (GI) tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances. Risk factors thought to cause IBS include abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (too fast or slow, or too strong), hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities, possibly caused by poor absorption of sugars or acids in food.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "The causes of IBS are thought to be a combination of abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, and a disruption in the communication between the brain and the GI tract. It is also believed to be due to a number of factors, including alteration in the gastrointestinal (GI) tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances. Risk factors thought to cause IBS include abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (too fast or slow, or too strong), hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities, possibly caused by poor absorption of sugars or acids in food.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, the symptoms of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) include diarrhea, constipation, and alternating between the two. Belly pains or cramps, usually in the lower half of the belly, that get worse after meals and feel better after a bowel movement. A lot of gas or bloating.\\n\\nThe causes of IBS are thought to be a combination of abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, and a disruption in the communication between the brain and the GI tract. It is also believed to be due to a number of factors, including alteration in the gastrointestinal (GI) tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances. Risk factors thought to cause IBS include abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (too fast or slow, or too strong), hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities, possibly caused by poor absorption of sugars or acids in food.\", \"Based on the given passages, the symptoms of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) include diarrhea, constipation, and alternating diarrhea and constipation. There are also symptoms of belly pains or cramps, especially in the lower half of the belly, which worsen after meals and improve after bowel movements. Additionally, people with IBS often experience a lot of gas or bloating.\\n\\nThe causes of irritable bowel syndrome are not fully understood, but it is believed to be due to a combination of factors. These include alterations in the gastrointestinal (GI) tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances. Risk factors for IBS include abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (too fast, too slow, or too strong), hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities, possibly caused by poor absorption of sugars or acids in food.\", \"Based on the given passages, irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) is characterized by a variety of symptoms including:\\n\\n1. Diarrhea (often described as violent episodes of diarrhea)\\n2. Constipation\\n3. Constipation alternating with diarrhea\\n4. Belly pains or cramps, usually in the lower half of the belly, that get worse after meals and feel better after a bowel movement\\n5. A lot of gas or bloating\\n\\nThe causes of IBS are currently unknown but are thought to result from a combination of abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, and a disruption in the communication between the brain and the GI tract. Additionally, it is believed to be due to a number of factors including alteration in GI tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances.\", \"Based on the given passages, the symptoms of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) include diarrhea, constipation, alternating constipation and diarrhea, belly pains or cramps, and a lot of gas or bloating. The passage mentions that the symptoms often get worse after meals and feel better after a bowel movement.\\n\\nThe causes of IBS are also described as being unknown, but thought to be the result of a combination of factors. These include abnormal gastrointestinal tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, and a disruption in the communication between the brain and the GI tract. The passages do not provide specific examples or numbers related to these causes.\"]", "time_sec": 30.848, "generat