35, 315, 279, 2086, 14983, 773, 279, 6543, 8473, 3156, 279, 6821, 315, 279, 14983, 553, 2062, 34505, 1917, 624, 20, 13, 22950, 279, 2086, 14983, 15516, 3156, 279, 3084, 315, 279, 1156, 14983, 311, 8193, 264, 15430, 80503, 315, 6543, 624, 21, 13, 7405, 220, 17, 1525, 7444, 11, 2138, 1105, 311, 3720, 9058, 11, 323, 2383, 1105, 9355, 624, 22, 13, 9646, 31620, 11, 1992, 279, 21521, 304, 279, 3897, 14983, 7557, 23853, 13, 151645], "pred": "1. Place a clean glass slide on a flat surface.\n2. Add one small drop of blood to one end of the slide.\n3. Take another clean slide and hold it at an angle of about 45 degrees.\n4. Touch the blood with one end of the second slide so the blood runs along the edge of the slide by capillary action.\n5. Push the second slide carefully along the length of the first slide to produce a thin smear of blood.\n6. Make 2 smears, allow them to air dry, and label them clearly.\n7. Once dried, place the slides in the provided slide transport containers.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.83259216816675, "g_worst_claim": 0.1961742639541626, "g_content": 2.1249369303385417, "g_traj_mean": 1.449413537979126}}], "selected_rollout": 2, "random_sel_rollout": 1, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 77.53, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "91", "question": "what is the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids", "gt": "Essential amino acids are ones that the body cannot produce on its own, thus they must be obtained through diet. In adults, there are eight essential amino acids: valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan. For infants, histidine is also considered essential. On the other hand, nonessential amino acids are those that the body can produce, even if they are not present in the diet. These include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine. While not essential to obtain from diet, these amino acids are still necessary as they are used by the body to build proteins needed for cell repair, growth, and maintenance.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids is that essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be made by the body.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids is that essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be made by the body.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, essential amino acids are those that cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. Essential amino acids include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan, while nonessential amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.\", \"Based on the given passages, the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids is that essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be made by the body.\", \"Based on the given passages, the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids is that essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be made by the body or obtained from the diet by breaking down protein products. Essential amino acids are crucial for the body to build proteins necessary for cell repair, growth, and maintenance. Nonessential amino acids are not considered unnecessary; instead, they are created by the body using essential amino acids or from protein breakdown.\", \"Based on the given passages, essential amino acids are those that cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet. The adult body only needs to obtain eight of the nine essential amino acids: valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan. The ninth essential amino acid, histidine, is only necessary for infants.\\n\\nNonessential amino acids, on the other hand, can be made by the body using essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. These acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.\"]", "time_sec": 25.284, "generation_time_seconds": 24.3017, "selection_time_seconds": 0.9307, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 7565, 41400, 32869, 525, 1846, 429, 4157, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 323, 1969, 387, 12180, 504, 279, 9968, 11, 1393, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 646, 387, 8947, 553, 279, 2487, 504, 7565, 41400, 32869, 476, 553, 14719, 1495, 12833, 3871, 13, 47733, 41400, 32869, 2924, 1044, 482, 11, 374, 1263, 1754, 482, 11, 512, 1754, 482, 11, 83395, 482, 11, 21068, 290, 482, 11, 14018, 3923, 32283, 482, 11, 270, 49790, 482, 11, 323, 1430, 417, 5127, 276, 11, 1393, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 2924, 72562, 482, 11, 438, 1732, 23392, 11, 438, 4480, 292, 13621, 11, 34069, 3991, 13621, 11, 1392, 82234, 11, 62481, 68, 482, 11, 34069, 19991, 11, 71057, 482, 11, 39445, 410, 482, 11, 462, 1056, 11, 1420, 482, 11, 323, 13580, 3630, 482, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, essential amino acids are those that cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. Essential amino acids include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan, while nonessential amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.550052501468374, "g_worst_claim": -0.06802832418017918, "g_content": 1.099471971100452, "g_traj_mean": 0.5158284902572632}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 6672, 1948, 7565, 323, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 374, 429, 7565, 41400, 32869, 4157, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 323, 1969, 387, 12180, 504, 279, 9968, 11, 1393, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 646, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids is that essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be made by the body.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.2708603276146783, "g_worst_claim": 0.046616196632385254, "g_content": 0.4036792257557745, "g_traj_mean": 0.28194448351860046}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 6672, 1948, 7565, 323, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 374, 429, 7565, 41400, 32869, 4157, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 323, 1969, 387, 12180, 504, 279, 9968, 11, 1393, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 646, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 476, 12180, 504, 279, 9968, 553, 14719, 1495, 12833, 3871, 13, 47733, 41400, 32869, 525, 16587, 369, 279, 2487, 311, 1936, 27796, 5871, 369, 2779, 12733, 11, 6513, 11, 323, 13404, 13, 11581, 48482, 41400, 32869, 525, 537, 6509, 25165, 26, 4518, 11, 807, 525, 3465, 553, 279, 2487, 1667, 7565, 41400, 32869, 476, 504, 12833, 29985, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids is that essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be made by the body or obtained from the diet by breaking down protein products. Essential amino acids are crucial for the body to build proteins necessary for cell repair, growth, and maintenance. Nonessential amino acids are not considered unnecessary; instead, they are created by the body using essential amino acids or from protein breakdown.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.8001907908096516, "g_worst_claim": 0.3333299557367961, "g_content": 1.0154404816804108, "g_traj_mean": 0.7148635387420654}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 7565, 41400, 32869, 525, 1846, 429, 4157, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 323, 1969, 387, 12180, 504, 279, 9968, 13, 576, 6683, 2487, 1172, 3880, 311, 6851, 8063, 315, 279, 11627, 7565, 41400, 32869, 25, 1044, 482, 11, 374, 1263, 1754, 482, 11, 512, 1754, 482, 11, 83395, 482, 11, 21068, 290, 482, 11, 14018, 3923, 32283, 482, 11, 270, 49790, 482, 11, 323, 1430, 417, 5127, 276, 13, 576, 42541, 7565, 41400, 13621, 11, 12744, 89973, 11, 374, 1172, 5871, 369, 41434, 382, 8121, 48482, 41400, 32869, 11, 389, 279, 1008, 1424, 11, 646, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 1667, 7565, 41400, 32869, 476, 553, 14719, 1495, 12833, 3871, 13, 4220, 32869, 2924, 72562, 482, 11, 438, 1732, 23392, 11, 438, 4480, 292, 13621, 11, 34069, 3991, 13621, 11, 1392, 82234, 11, 62481, 68, 482, 11, 34069, 19991, 11, 71057, 482, 11, 39445, 410, 482, 11, 462, 1056, 11, 1420, 482, 11, 323, 13580, 3630, 482, 13, 151645], "pred": "Nonessential amino acids, on the other hand, can be made by the body using essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. These acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.7310450305086855, "g_worst_claim": 0.21319923400878907, "g_content": 1.1223877195328, "g_traj_mean": 0.6274074912071228}}], "selected_rollout": 3, "random_sel_rollout": 1, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 33.33, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "92", "question": "what is the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids", "gt": "Essential amino acids are those that cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet. Adults need to obtain eight essential amino acids: valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan. The ninth essential amino acid, histidine, is only essential for infants. Nonessential amino acids, on the other hand, can be made by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. Some examples of nonessential amino acids are alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids is fundamental in terms of human health and nutrition. Essential amino acids, such as valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan, are crucial for adults since they cannot be synthesized by the body and must be obtained through diet. In contrast, nonessential amino acids like alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine can be produced by the body itself, either from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. This distinction highlights the importance of a balanced diet in providing the necessary building blocks for various bodily functions and structures.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids is fundamental in terms of human health and nutrition. Essential amino acids, such as valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan, are crucial for adults since they cannot be synthesized by the body and must be obtained through diet. In contrast, nonessential amino acids like alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine can be produced by the body itself, either from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. This distinction highlights the importance of a balanced diet in providing the necessary building blocks for various bodily functions and structures.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, essential amino acids are those that cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. Essential amino acids include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan, while nonessential amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.\", \"Based on the given passages, the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids is fundamental in terms of human health and nutrition. Essential amino acids, such as valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan, are crucial for adults since they cannot be synthesized by the body and must be obtained through diet. In contrast, nonessential amino acids like alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine can be produced by the body itself, either from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. This distinction highlights the importance of a balanced diet in providing the necessary building blocks for various bodily functions and structures.\", \"Based on the given passages, essential amino acids are necessary for the body's function and cannot be produced by the body, so they must be obtained through diet. Examples of essential amino acids include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan. Nonessential amino acids, on the other hand, can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, so they are not essential to obtain from the diet. Examples of nonessential amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.\", \"Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be made by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products.\"]", "time_sec": 27.0, "generation_time_seconds": 25.8499, "selection_time_seconds": 1.1397, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 7565, 41400, 32869, 525, 1846, 429, 4157, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 323, 1969, 387, 12180, 504, 279, 9968, 11, 1393, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 646, 387, 8947, 553, 279, 2487, 504, 7565, 41400, 32869, 476, 553, 14719, 1495, 12833, 3871, 13, 47733, 41400, 32869, 2924, 1044, 482, 11, 374, 1263, 1754, 482, 11, 512, 1754, 482, 11, 83395, 482, 11, 21068, 290, 482, 11, 14018, 3923, 32283, 482, 11, 270, 49790, 482, 11, 323, 1430, 417, 5127, 276, 11, 1393, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 2924, 72562, 482, 11, 438, 1732, 23392, 11, 438, 4480, 292, 13621, 11, 34069, 3991, 13621, 11, 1392, 82234, 11, 62481, 68, 482, 11, 34069, 19991, 11, 71057, 482, 11, 39445, 410, 482, 11, 462, 1056, 11, 1420, 482, 11, 323, 13580, 3630, 482, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, essential amino acids are those that cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. Essential amino acids include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan, while nonessential amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.5362732680070968, "g_worst_claim": -0.06627140442530315, "g_content": 1.0934147554285385, "g_traj_mean": 0.5101417303085327}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 6672, 1948, 7565, 323, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 374, 15811, 304, 3793, 315, 3738, 2820, 323, 25595, 13, 47733, 41400, 32869, 11, 1741, 438, 1044, 482, 11, 374, 1263, 1754, 482, 11, 512, 1754, 482, 11, 83395, 482, 11, 21068, 290, 482, 11, 14018, 3923, 32283, 482, 11, 270, 49790, 482, 11, 323, 1430, 417, 5127, 276, 11, 525, 16587, 369, 12598, 2474, 807, 4157, 387, 91006, 553, 279, 2487, 323, 1969, 387, 12180, 1526, 9968, 13, 758, 12872, 11, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 1075, 72562, 482, 11, 438, 1732, 23392, 11, 438, 4480, 292, 13621, 11, 34069, 3991, 13621, 11, 1392, 82234, 11, 62481, 68, 482, 11, 34069, 19991, 11, 71057, 482, 11, 39445, 410, 482, 11, 462, 1056, 11, 1420, 482, 11, 323, 13580, 3630, 482, 646, 387, 8947, 553, 279, 2487, 5086, 11, 2987, 504, 7565, 41400, 32869, 476, 553, 14719, 1495, 12833, 3871, 13, 1096, 29196, 21314, 279, 12650, 315, 264, 23831, 9968, 304, 8241, 279, 5871, 4752, 10010, 369, 5257, 47031, 5746, 323, 14389, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids is fundamental in terms of human health and nutrition. Essential amino acids, such as valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan, are crucial for adults since they cannot be synthesized by the body and must be obtained through diet. In contrast, nonessential amino acids like alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine can be produced by the body itself, either from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. This distinction highlights the importance of a balanced diet in providing the necessary building blocks for various bodily functions and structures.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.5068372094433674, "g_worst_claim": 0.07385702431201935, "g_content": 0.9031853146023221, "g_traj_mean": 0.47030553221702576}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 7565, 41400, 32869, 525, 5871, 369, 279, 2487, 594, 729, 323, 4157, 387, 8947, 553, 279, 2487, 11, 773, 807, 1969, 387, 12180, 1526, 9968, 13, 25311, 315, 7565, 41400, 32869, 2924, 1044, 482, 11, 374, 1263, 1754, 482, 11, 512, 1754, 482, 11, 83395, 482, 11, 21068, 290, 482, 11, 14018, 3923, 32283, 482, 11, 270, 49790, 482, 11, 323, 1430, 417, 5127, 276, 13, 11581, 48482, 41400, 32869, 11, 389, 279, 1008, 1424, 11, 646, 387, 8947, 553, 279, 2487, 504, 7565, 41400, 32869, 476, 553, 14719, 1495, 12833, 3871, 11, 773, 807, 525, 537, 7565, 311, 6851, 504, 279, 9968, 13, 25311, 315, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 2924, 72562, 482, 11, 438, 1732, 23392, 11, 438, 4480, 292, 13621, 11, 34069, 3991, 13621, 11, 1392, 82234, 11, 62481, 68, 482, 11, 34069, 19991, 11, 71057, 482, 11, 39445, 410, 482, 11, 462, 1056, 11, 1420, 482, 11, 323, 13580, 3630, 482, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, essential amino acids are necessary for the body's function and cannot be produced by the body, so they must be obtained through diet. Examples of essential amino acids include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan. Nonessential amino acids, on the other hand, can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, so they are not essential to obtain from the diet. Examples of nonessential amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.4726845927374657, "g_worst_claim": 0.01597011387348175, "g_content": 0.898974609375, "g_traj_mean": 0.4571887254714966}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [37438, 2283, 41400, 32869, 4157, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 323, 1969, 387, 12180, 504, 279, 9968, 11, 1393, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 646, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 504, 7565, 41400, 32869, 476, 553, 14719, 1495, 12833, 3871, 13, 151645], "pred": "Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be made by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.0775344010555383, "g_worst_claim": 0.9161017735799154, "g_content": 1.6941441127232142, "g_traj_mean": 1.0479815006256104}}], "selected_rollout": 3, "random_sel_rollout": 1, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 66.23, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "94", "question": "what is the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, the main differences between essential and non-essential amino acids are:\n\nEssential Amino Acids:\n\n* Must be obtained from the diet\n* Cannot be produced by the body\n* Nine out of 20 amino acids are considered essential (valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, tryptophan, and histidine for infants)\n\nNon-Essential Amino Acids:\n\n* Can be produced by the body\n* Not necessarily required to obtain from the diet\n* Include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine\n\nIt is important to note that even though non-essential amino acids can be produced by the body, it is still important to consume a balanced diet that includes all of the essential amino acids to ensure proper bodily functions.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids is that essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be made by the body and do not need to be obtained from the diet. Essential amino acids include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan, while nonessential amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids is that essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be made by the body and do not need to be obtained from the diet. Essential amino acids include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan, while nonessential amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, essential amino acids are those that cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. Essential amino acids include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan, while nonessential amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.\", \"Based on the given passages, the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids is that essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be made by the body and do not need to be obtained from the diet. Essential amino acids include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan, while nonessential amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.\", \"Based on the given passages, essential amino acids cannot be made by the body, so you must get them from your diet, while nonessential amino acids can be made by the body. For example, valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine and tryptophan are essential amino acids that adults need to obtain from their diet, while alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine and tyrosine are nonessential amino acids that can be made by the body.\", \"Based on the given passages, essential amino acids are those that cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet. Examples of essential amino acids include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan. Nonessential amino acids, on the other hand, can be produced by the body either from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. Examples of nonessential amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine. The main difference between the two types of amino acids is that essential amino acids must be obtained from food, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body.\"]", "time_sec": 29.035, "generation_time_seconds": 28.3399, "selection_time_seconds": 0.6828, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 7565, 41400, 32869, 525, 1846, 429, 4157, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 323, 1969, 387, 12180, 504, 279, 9968, 11, 1393, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 646, 387, 8947, 553, 279, 2487, 504, 7565, 41400, 32869, 476, 553, 14719, 1495, 12833, 3871, 13, 47733, 41400, 32869, 2924, 1044, 482, 11, 374, 1263, 1754, 482, 11, 512, 1754, 482, 11, 83395, 482, 11, 21068, 290, 482, 11, 14018, 3923, 32283, 482, 11, 270, 49790, 482, 11, 323, 1430, 417, 5127, 276, 11, 1393, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 2924, 72562, 482, 11, 438, 1732, 23392, 11, 438, 4480, 292, 13621, 11, 34069, 3991, 13621, 11, 1392, 82234, 11, 62481, 68, 482, 11, 34069, 19991, 11, 71057, 482, 11, 39445, 410, 482, 11, 462, 1056, 11, 1420, 482, 11, 323, 13580, 3630, 482, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, essential amino acids are those that cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. Essential amino acids include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan, while nonessential amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.5362732680070968, "g_worst_claim": -0.06627140442530315, "g_content": 1.0934147554285385, "g_traj_mean": 0.5101417303085327}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 6672, 1948, 7565, 323, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 374, 429, 7565, 41400, 32869, 4157, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 323, 1969, 387, 12180, 504, 279, 9968, 11, 1393, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 646, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 323, 653, 537, 1184, 311, 387, 12180, 504, 279, 9968, 13, 47733, 41400, 32869, 2924, 1044, 482, 11, 374, 1263, 1754, 482, 11, 512, 1754, 482, 11, 83395, 482, 11, 21068, 290, 482, 11, 14018, 3923, 32283, 482, 11, 270, 49790, 482, 11, 323, 1430, 417, 5127, 276, 11, 1393, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 2924, 72562, 482, 11, 438, 1732, 23392, 11, 438, 4480, 292, 13621, 11, 34069, 3991, 13621, 11, 1392, 82234, 11, 62481, 68, 482, 11, 34069, 19991, 11, 71057, 482, 11, 39445, 410, 482, 11, 462, 1056, 11, 1420, 482, 11, 323, 13580, 3630, 482, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids is that essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be made by the body and do not need to be obtained from the diet. Essential amino acids include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan, while nonessential amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.32543913999634216, "g_worst_claim": -0.05179702242215475, "g_content": 0.5896195658931026, "g_traj_mean": 0.3016093969345093}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 7565, 41400, 32869, 4157, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 11, 773, 498, 1969, 633, 1105, 504, 697, 9968, 11, 1393, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 646, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 13, 1752, 3110, 11, 1044, 482, 11, 374, 1263, 1754, 482, 11, 512, 1754, 482, 11, 83395, 482, 11, 21068, 290, 482, 11, 14018, 3923, 32283, 482, 11, 270, 49790, 482, 323, 1430, 417, 5127, 276, 525, 7565, 41400, 32869, 429, 12598, 1184, 311, 6851, 504, 862, 9968, 11, 1393, 72562, 482, 11, 438, 1732, 23392, 11, 438, 4480, 292, 13621, 11, 34069, 3991, 13621, 11, 1392, 82234, 11, 62481, 68, 482, 11, 34069, 19991, 11, 71057, 482, 11, 39445, 410, 482, 11, 462, 1056, 11, 1420, 482, 323, 13580, 3630, 482, 525, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 429, 646, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, essential amino acids cannot be made by the body, so you must get them from your diet, while nonessential amino acids can be made by the body. For example, valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine and tryptophan are essential amino acids that adults need to obtain from their diet, while alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine and tyrosine are nonessential amino acids that can be made by the body.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.5491673996268117, "g_worst_claim": 0.09980627110129908, "g_content": 0.8666981506347656, "g_traj_mean": 0.48734089732170105}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 7565, 41400, 32869, 525, 1846, 429, 4157, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 323, 1969, 387, 12180, 504, 279, 9968, 13, 25311, 315, 7565, 41400, 32869, 2924, 1044, 482, 11, 374, 1263, 1754, 482, 11, 512, 1754, 482, 11, 83395, 482, 11, 21068, 290, 482, 11, 14018, 3923, 32283, 482, 11, 270, 49790, 482, 11, 323, 1430, 417, 5127, 276, 13, 11581, 48482, 41400, 32869, 11, 389, 279, 1008, 1424, 11, 646, 387, 8947, 553, 279, 2487, 2987, 504, 7565, 41400, 32869, 476, 553, 14719, 1495, 12833, 3871, 13, 25311, 315, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 2924, 72562, 482, 11, 438, 1732, 23392, 11, 438, 4480, 292, 13621, 11, 34069, 3991, 13621, 11, 1392, 82234, 11, 62481, 68, 482, 11, 34069, 19991, 11, 71057, 482, 11, 39445, 410, 482, 11, 462, 1056, 11, 1420, 482, 11, 323, 13580, 3630, 482, 13, 576, 1887, 6672, 1948, 279, 1378, 4494, 315, 41400, 32869, 374, 429, 7565, 41400, 32869, 1969, 387, 12180, 504, 3607, 11, 1393, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 646, 387, 8947, 553, 279, 2487, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, essential amino acids are those that cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet. Examples of essential amino acids include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan. Nonessential amino acids, on the other hand, can be produced by the body either from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. Examples of nonessential amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine. The main difference between the two types of amino acids is that essential amino acids must be obtained from food, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.38882524703072485, "g_worst_claim": -0.04287730654080709, "g_content": 0.7607960113107342, "g_traj_mean": 0.3966633975505829}}], "selected_rollout": 0, "random_sel_rollout": 1, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 72.61, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.8, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "95", "question": "what is the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is the answer to the question:\n\nEssential amino acids are those that cannot be produced by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids are those that can be produced by the body from other amino acids or by breaking down protein products.\n\nPassage 1 states that nine amino acids are essential for humans, but only eight of them are essential for adults (histidine is only essential for infants). Passage 2 explains that nonessential amino acids are not unnecessary, as the body can produce them from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. Passage 3 reiterates that nonessential amino acids can be made by the body, while essential amino acids cannot be produced and must be obtained from the diet.\n\nTherefore, the main difference between essential and nonessential amino acids is that essential amino acids cannot be produced by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from other amino acids or by breaking down protein products.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids is that nonessential amino acids are those that your body can make from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, whereas essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from your diet. Essential amino acids include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan, while nonessential amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids is that nonessential amino acids are those that your body can make from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, whereas essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from your diet. Essential amino acids include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan, while nonessential amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, essential amino acids are those that cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. Essential amino acids include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan, while nonessential amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.\", \"Based on the given passages:\\nThe difference between essential and nonessential amino acids is that essential amino acids are necessary for the body to function properly and can only be obtained from the diet. Nonessential amino acids, on the other hand, can be made by the body and do not need to be obtained from the diet. Essential amino acids include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan. Nonessential amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.\", \"Based on the given passages, essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, whereas nonessential amino acids can be synthesized by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. Essential amino acids are crucial for the body's ability to build proteins necessary for cell repair, growth, and maintenance, whereas nonessential amino acids are not necessary for the body's immediate needs but can still play important roles in various biochemical processes.\", \"Based on the given passages, the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids is that nonessential amino acids are those that your body can make from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, whereas essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from your diet. Essential amino acids include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan, while nonessential amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.\"]", "time_sec": 27.058, "generation_time_seconds": 26.3826, "selection_time_seconds": 0.6481, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 7565, 41400, 32869, 525, 1846, 429, 4157, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 323, 1969, 387, 12180, 504, 279, 9968, 11, 1393, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 646, 387, 8947, 553, 279, 2487, 504, 7565, 41400, 32869, 476, 553, 14719, 1495, 12833, 3871, 13, 47733, 41400, 32869, 2924, 1044, 482, 11, 374, 1263, 1754, 482, 11, 512, 1754, 482, 11, 83395, 482, 11, 21068, 290, 482, 11, 14018, 3923, 32283, 482, 11, 270, 49790, 482, 11, 323, 1430, 417, 5127, 276, 11, 1393, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 2924, 72562, 482, 11, 438, 1732, 23392, 11, 438, 4480, 292, 13621, 11, 34069, 3991, 13621, 11, 1392, 82234, 11, 62481, 68, 482, 11, 34069, 19991, 11, 71057, 482, 11, 39445, 410, 482, 11, 462, 1056, 11, 1420, 482, 11, 323, 13580, 3630, 482, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, essential amino acids are those that cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. Essential amino acids include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan, while nonessential amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.549867732376362, "g_worst_claim": -0.06362048122617933, "g_content": 1.0996757208132277, "g_traj_mean": 0.5166028738021851}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 510, 785, 6672, 1948, 7565, 323, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 374, 429, 7565, 41400, 32869, 525, 5871, 369, 279, 2487, 311, 729, 10277, 323, 646, 1172, 387, 12180, 504, 279, 9968, 13, 11581, 48482, 41400, 32869, 11, 389, 279, 1008, 1424, 11, 646, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 323, 653, 537, 1184, 311, 387, 12180, 504, 279, 9968, 13, 47733, 41400, 32869, 2924, 1044, 482, 11, 374, 1263, 1754, 482, 11, 512, 1754, 482, 11, 83395, 482, 11, 21068, 290, 482, 11, 14018, 3923, 32283, 482, 11, 270, 49790, 482, 11, 323, 1430, 417, 5127, 276, 13, 11581, 48482, 41400, 32869, 2924, 72562, 482, 11, 438, 1732, 23392, 11, 438, 4480, 292, 13621, 11, 34069, 3991, 13621, 11, 1392, 82234, 11, 62481, 68, 482, 11, 34069, 19991, 11, 71057, 482, 11, 39445, 410, 482, 11, 462, 1056, 11, 1420, 482, 11, 323, 13580, 3630, 482, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages:\nThe difference between essential and nonessential amino acids is that essential amino acids are necessary for the body to function properly and can only be obtained from the diet. Nonessential amino acids, on the other hand, can be made by the body and do not need to be obtained from the diet. Essential amino acids include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan. Nonessential amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.26263401858447705, "g_worst_claim": -0.038042080402374265, "g_content": 0.5246005058288574, "g_traj_mean": 0.2559795379638672}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 7565, 41400, 32869, 4157, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 323, 1969, 387, 12180, 504, 279, 9968, 11, 19853, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 646, 387, 91006, 553, 279, 2487, 504, 7565, 41400, 32869, 476, 553, 14719, 1495, 12833, 3871, 13, 47733, 41400, 32869, 525, 16587, 369, 279, 2487, 594, 5726, 311, 1936, 27796, 5871, 369, 2779, 12733, 11, 6513, 11, 323, 13404, 11, 19853, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 525, 537, 5871, 369, 279, 2487, 594, 13922, 3880, 714, 646, 2058, 1486, 2989, 12783, 304, 5257, 92432, 11364, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, whereas nonessential amino acids can be synthesized by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. Essential amino acids are crucial for the body's ability to build proteins necessary for cell repair, growth, and maintenance, whereas nonessential amino acids are not necessary for the body's immediate needs but can still play important roles in various biochemical processes.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.6488475116258282, "g_worst_claim": 0.08005861282348632, "g_content": 0.9303118147940006, "g_traj_mean": 0.6530617475509644}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 6672, 1948, 7565, 323, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 374, 429, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 525, 1846, 429, 697, 2487, 646, 1281, 504, 7565, 41400, 32869, 476, 553, 14719, 1495, 12833, 3871, 11, 19853, 7565, 41400, 32869, 4157, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 323, 1969, 387, 12180, 504, 697, 9968, 13, 47733, 41400, 32869, 2924, 1044, 482, 11, 374, 1263, 1754, 482, 11, 512, 1754, 482, 11, 83395, 482, 11, 21068, 290, 482, 11, 14018, 3923, 32283, 482, 11, 270, 49790, 482, 11, 323, 1430, 417, 5127, 276, 11, 1393, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 2924, 72562, 482, 11, 438, 1732, 23392, 11, 438, 4480, 292, 13621, 11, 34069, 3991, 13621, 11, 1392, 82234, 11, 62481, 68, 482, 11, 34069, 19991, 11, 71057, 482, 11, 39445, 410, 482, 11, 462, 1056, 11, 1420, 482, 11, 323, 13580, 3630, 482, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids is that nonessential amino acids are those that your body can make from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, whereas essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from your diet. Essential amino acids include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan, while nonessential amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.5117083199504994, "g_worst_claim": 0.0214840786324607, "g_content": 0.8596854891095843, "g_traj_mean": 0.5066511034965515}}], "selected_rollout": 0, "random_sel_rollout": 3, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 40.21, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "96", "question": "typical weather in indiana", "gt": "The typical weather in Indiana is characterized by long, warm, humid, and wet summers. The winters are chilly and windy with some snowfall. The temperature typically varies from 22°F to 85°F and is rarely below 3°F or above 92°F. The state also experiences some rainfall, with the wettest year recording 57.65 inches of rain and the driest 24.97 inches. The most snowfall recorded in a season was 74.5 inches.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, the typical weather in Indiana includes:\n\n- Summers that are long, warm, humid, and wet.\n- Winters that are chilly and windy.\n- It is partly cloudy year-round.\n\nThe temperature in Indianapolis typically varies from 22°F to 85°F and is rarely below 3°F or above 92°F.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the typical weather in Indiana includes:\n\n- Summers that are long, warm, humid, and wet.\n- Winters that are chilly and windy.\n- It is partly cloudy year-round.\n\nThe temperature in Indianapolis typically varies from 22°F to 85°F and is rarely below 3°F or above 92°F.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, the typical weather in Indiana includes:\\n\\n1. Summers are long, warm, humid, and wet.\\n2. Winters are chilly and windy.\\n3. It is partly cloudy year-round.\\n4. The temperature typically varies from 22°F to 85°F.\\n5. The temperature is rarely below 3°F or above 92°F.\\n6. The wettest year recorded in Indianapolis was 1876 with 57.65 inches of measured rain.\\n7. The driest year recorded in Indianapolis was 1934 with 24.97 inches of measured rain.\\n8. The most snowfall recorded in Rockford was 74.5 inches in one season.\\n9. The maximum monthly precipitation was over 16 inches in July 2003 and 2004 at Kokomo.\", \"Based on the given passages, the typical weather in Indiana includes:\\n\\n1. Summers: Long, warm, humid, and wet.\\n2. Winters: Chilly and windy.\\n3. Year-round: Partly cloudy conditions.\\n4. Temperature variation: Ranging from 22°F to 85°F.\\n5. Rarely below 3°F or above 92°F.\\n6. Snowfall and accumulation: Monthly and yearly averages provided for Indianapolis, Fort Wayne, Gary & Michigan City, South Bend.\\n7. Extreme precipitation: Wettest year in 1876 at 57.65 inches of rain, driest year in 1934 at 24.97 inches of rain, and most snowfall in 1979 in Rockford at 74.5 inches in one season.\", \"Based on the given passages, the typical weather in Indiana is characterized by hot, humid summers and chilly, windy winters. The temperature in Indianapolis typically varies from 22°F to 85°F, and it is rarely below 3°F or above 92°F throughout the year.\", \"Based on the given passages, the typical weather in Indiana includes:\\n\\n- Summers that are long, warm, humid, and wet.\\n- Winters that are chilly and windy.\\n- It is partly cloudy year-round.\\n\\nThe temperature in Indianapolis typically varies from 22°F to 85°F and is rarely below 3°F or above 92°F.\"]", "time_sec": 25.086, "generation_time_seconds": 24.136, "selection_time_seconds": 0.9404, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 14260, 9104, 304, 21591, 5646, 1447, 16, 13, 79648, 525, 1293, 11, 8205, 11, 65938, 11, 323, 14401, 624, 17, 13, 467, 49940, 525, 80875, 323, 92921, 624, 18, 13, 1084, 374, 27037, 73549, 1042, 21015, 624, 19, 13, 576, 9315, 11136, 34227, 504, 220, 17, 17, 58472, 311, 220, 23, 20, 58472, 624, 20, 13, 576, 9315, 374, 18521, 3685, 220, 18, 58472, 476, 3403, 220, 24, 17, 58472, 624, 21, 13, 576, 14401, 1944, 1042, 12433, 304, 41351, 572, 220, 16, 23, 22, 21, 448, 220, 20, 22, 13, 21, 20, 14924, 315, 16878, 11174, 624, 22, 13, 576, 49411, 477, 1042, 12433, 304, 41351, 572, 220, 16, 24, 18, 19, 448, 220, 17, 19, 13, 24, 22, 14924, 315, 16878, 11174, 624, 23, 13, 576, 1429, 11794, 13464, 12433, 304, 9127, 8187, 572, 220, 22, 19, 13, 20, 14924, 304, 825, 3200, 624, 24, 13, 576, 7192, 15088, 59950, 572, 916, 220, 16, 21, 14924, 304, 5768, 220, 17, 15, 15, 18, 323, 220, 17, 15, 15, 19, 518, 64715, 15900, 13, 151645], "pred": "1. Summers are long, warm, humid, and wet.\n2. Winters are chilly and windy.\n3. It is partly cloudy year-round.\n4. The temperature typically varies from 22°F to 85°F.\n5. The temperature is rarely below 3°F or above 92°F.\n6. The wettest year recorded in Indianapolis was 1876 with 57.65 inches of measured rain.\n7. The driest year recorded in Indianapolis was 1934 with 24.97 inches of measured rain.\n8. The most snowfall recorded in Rockford was 74.5 inches in one season.\n9. The maximum monthly precipitation was over 16 inches in July 2003 and 2004 at Kokomo.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.7894778328750347, "g_worst_claim": 0.5024096965789795, "g_content": 2.3179601381902826, "g_traj_mean": 1.4703481197357178}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 14260, 9104, 304, 21591, 5646, 1447, 16, 13, 79648, 25, 5724, 11, 8205, 11, 65938, 11, 323, 14401, 624, 17, 13, 467, 49940, 25, 910, 14385, 323, 92921, 624, 18, 13, 9742, 21015, 25, 3660, 398, 73549, 4682, 624, 19, 13, 37022, 22990, 25, 431, 8595, 504, 220, 17, 17, 58472, 311, 220, 23, 20, 58472, 624, 20, 13, 34959, 398, 3685, 220, 18, 58472, 476, 3403, 220, 24, 17, 58472, 624, 21, 13, 18901, 13464, 323, 45735, 25, 44334, 323, 44270, 48820, 3897, 369, 41351, 11, 11002, 27540, 11, 23826, 609, 14630, 4311, 11, 4882, 47738, 624, 22, 13, 49395, 59950, 25, 44856, 1944, 1042, 304, 220, 16, 23, 22, 21, 518, 220, 20, 22, 13, 21, 20, 14924, 315, 11174, 11, 49411, 477, 1042, 304, 220, 16, 24, 18, 19, 518, 220, 17, 19, 13, 24, 22, 14924, 315, 11174, 11, 323, 1429, 11794, 13464, 304, 220, 16, 24, 22, 24, 304, 9127, 8187, 518, 220, 22, 19, 13, 20, 14924, 304, 825, 3200, 13, 151645], "pred": "1. Summers: Long, warm, humid, and wet.\n2. Winters: Chilly and windy.\n3. Year-round: Partly cloudy conditions.\n4. Temperature variation: Ranging from 22°F to 85°F.\n5. Rarely below 3°F or above 92°F.\n6. Snowfall and accumulation: Monthly and yearly averages provided for Indianapolis, Fort Wayne, Gary & Michigan City, South Bend.\n7. Extreme precipitation: Wettest year in 1876 at 57.65 inches of rain, driest year in 1934 at 24.97 inches of rain, and most snowfall in 1979 in Rockford at 74.5 inches in one season.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.7590641745907916, "g_worst_claim": 0.5024096965789795, "g_content": 2.3121700286865234, "g_traj_mean": 1.5981640815734863}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 14260, 9104, 304, 21591, 374, 31871, 553, 4017, 11, 65938, 79669, 323, 80875, 11, 92921, 84982, 13, 576, 9315, 304, 41351, 11136, 34227, 504, 220, 17, 17, 58472, 311, 220, 23, 20, 58472, 11, 323, 432, 374, 18521, 3685, 220, 18, 58472, 476, 3403, 220, 24, 17, 58472, 6814, 279, 1042, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, the typical weather in Indiana is characterized by hot, humid summers and chilly, windy winters. The temperature in Indianapolis typically varies from 22°F to 85°F, and it is rarely below 3°F or above 92°F throughout the year.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.1668364294733617, "g_worst_claim": 0.5028968652089437, "g_content": 1.9437863032023113, "g_traj_mean": 1.2998558282852173}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 14260, 9104, 304, 21591, 5646, 1447, 12, 79648, 429, 525, 1293, 11, 8205, 11, 65938, 11, 323, 14401, 624, 12, 467, 49940, 429, 525, 80875, 323, 92921, 624, 12, 1084, 374, 27037, 73549, 1042, 21015, 382, 785, 9315, 304, 41351, 11136, 34227, 504, 220, 17, 17, 58472, 311, 220, 23, 20, 58472, 323, 374, 18521, 3685, 220, 18, 58472, 476, 3403, 220, 24, 17, 58472, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, the typical weather in Indiana includes:\n\n- Summers that are long, warm, humid, and wet.\n- Winters that are chilly and windy.\n- It is partly cloudy year-round.\n\nThe temperature in Indianapolis typically varies from 22°F to 85°F and is rarely below 3°F or above 92°F.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.6382675925890606, "g_worst_claim": 0.5028968652089437, "g_content": 2.8609017266167536, "g_traj_mean": 1.5755550861358643}}], "selected_rollout": 3, "random_sel_rollout": 3, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 58.67, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "97", "question": "typical weather in indiana", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the typical weather in Indiana includes long, warm, humid, and wet summers, and chilly and windy winters. The temperature ranges from 22°F to 85°F throughout the year. The passages also mention snowfall and snow accumulation in specific cities, but do not provide a comprehensive overview of the typical weather in Indiana. Therefore, it is not possible to provide a complete answer based solely on the given passages.", "pred": "1. Warm, humid summers with temperatures typically ranging from 22°F to 85°F.\n2. Chilly and windy winters.\n3. Partly cloudy conditions throughout the year.\n4. Annual averages for snowfall and snow accumulation, including first snowfall and heavy snowstorms.\n5. Historical records of the wettest and driest years in Indianapolis, with the wettest year recorded as 57.65 inches of rain in 1876 and the driest year as 24.97 inches of rain in 1934.\n6. Extreme snowfall recorded in 1979 in Rockford, where 74.5 inches fell in one season.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "1. Warm, humid summers with temperatures typically ranging from 22°F to 85°F.\n2. Chilly and windy winters.\n3. Partly cloudy conditions throughout the year.\n4. Annual averages for snowfall and snow accumulation, including first snowfall and heavy snowstorms.\n5. Historical records of the wettest and driest years in Indianapolis, with the wettest year recorded as 57.65 inches of rain in 1876 and the driest year as 24.97 inches of rain in 1934.\n6. Extreme snowfall recorded in 1979 in Rockford, where 74.5 inches fell in one season.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, the typical weather in Indiana includes:\\n\\n1. Summers are long, warm, humid, and wet.\\n2. Winters are chilly and windy.\\n3. It is partly cloudy year-round.\\n4. The temperature typically varies from 22°F to 85°F.\\n5. The temperature is rarely below 3°F or above 92°F.\\n6. The wettest year recorded in Indianapolis was 1876 with 57.65 inches of measured rain.\\n7. The driest year recorded in Indianapolis was 1934 with 24.97 inches of measured rain.\\n8. The most snowfall recorded in Rockford was 74.5 inches in one season.\\n9. The maximum monthly precipitation was over 16 inches in July 2003 and 2004 at Kokomo.\", \"Based on the given passages, typical weather in Indiana includes:\\n\\n1. Warm, humid summers with temperatures typically ranging from 22°F to 85°F.\\n2. Chilly and windy winters.\\n3. Partly cloudy conditions throughout the year.\\n4. Annual averages for snowfall and snow accumulation, including first snowfall and heavy snowstorms.\\n5. Historical records of the wettest and driest years in Indianapolis, with the wettest year recorded as 57.65 inches of rain in 1876 and the driest year as 24.97 inches of rain in 1934.\\n6. Extreme snowfall recorded in 1979 in Rockford, where 74.5 inches fell in one season.\", \"Based on the given passages, in Indiana, the summers are long, warm, humid, and wet; the winters are chilly and windy; and it is partly cloudy year round. Over the course of the year, the temperature typically varies from 22°F to 85°F and is rarely below 3°F or above 92°F.\", \"Based on the given passages, the typical weather in Indiana includes:\\n1. Summers are long, warm, humid, and wet.\\n2. Winters are chilly and windy.\\n3. It is partly cloudy year-round.\\n4. Temperatures typically vary from 22°F to 85°F.\\n5. The driest year recorded at Indianapolis was 1934 with 24.97 inches of measured rain.\\n6. The wettest year recorded at Indianapolis was 1876 with 57.65 inches of measured rain.\\n7. The most snowfall recorded in one season was 74.5 inches in Rockford.\\n8. The maximum monthly precipitation was over 16 inches in July 2003 and 2004 at Kokomo.\"]", "time_sec": 28.079, "generation_time_seconds": 27.2171, "selection_time_seconds": 0.8521, "record_process": tru