{"id": "124", "question": "building a lean-to shed", "gt": "To build a lean-to shed, start by preparing the site with a 4\" layer of compacted gravel. Cut two 4 x 4 skids to a length of 70 2/4\" and set and level them according to the shed building plans. Cut two 2 x 6 rim joists at 7 3/4\" and six joists at 44 3/4\". Assemble the floor and place it on the skids as indicated in the floor framing section of the plans. Check for squareness and anchor the frame to the skids with four joist clip angles. Cut two more 4 x 4s to a length of 6 feet for the skids and, when the frame is square, toenail the floor joists to the skids. Attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring onto the frame. The walls for the shed are constructed separately and then raised onto the frame. The size of the shed can be adjusted as needed.", "pred": "These steps outline the process of building a lean-to shed, focusing on the site preparation, skids, floor construction, and additional considerations for expanding the shed's dimensions.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "These steps outline the process of building a lean-to shed, focusing on the site preparation, skids, floor construction, and additional considerations for expanding the shed's dimensions.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, building a lean-to shed involves several steps:\\n\\n1. Prepare the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel.\\n2. Cut two 4 x 4 skids at 70 2/4 inches.\\n3. Set and level the skids following the shed building plans.\\n4. Cut two 2 x 6 rim joists at 7 3/4 inches and six joists at 44 3/4 inches.\\n5. Assemble the floor and set it on the skids as shown in the floor framing section of the lean-to shed plans.\\n6. Check for square, and then anchor the frame to the skids with four joist clip angles.\\n7. Cut two pressure-treated 4 by 4s to a length of 6 feet for the skids.\\n8. Position the frame on the skids, and, when square, toenail the floor joists to the skids.\\n9. Attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring onto the frame.\\n10. The walls for the shed are constructed separately and then raised onto the frame.\\n11. The shed dimensions are 6 feet long by 4 feet wide, with the option to expand in increments of 2 or 4 feet.\", \"Based on the given passages, the steps for building a lean-to shed include:\\n\\n1. Prepare the site by compacting gravel to a depth of 4 inches.\\n2. Cut two 4 x 4 skids to the required length.\\n3. Set and level the skids according to the shed building plans.\\n4. Cut two 2 x 6 rim joists and six joists to the appropriate lengths.\\n5. Assemble the floor and set it on the skids as described in the lean-to shed plans.\\n6. Check for square and anchor the frame to the skids with four joist clip angles.\\n7. Position the frame on the skids and toenail the floor joists to the skids.\\n8. Attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring onto the frame.\\n9. Construct the walls for the shed separately and then raise them onto the frame.\\n10. The shed can be easily expanded in increments of 2 or 4 feet if desired.\\n11. The framing for the shed’s floor should be constructed using two pressure-treated 2 by 4s cut to a length of 6 feet for the rim joists.\", \"Based on the given passages, building a lean-to shed involves the following steps:\\n1. Prepare the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel.\\n2. Cut two 4 x 4 skids at 70 2/4 inches, set and level them according to the shed building plans.\\n3. Cut two 2 x 6 rim joists at 7 3/4 inches and six joists at 44 3/4 inches.\\n4. Assemble the floor and set it on the skids as shown in the floor framing section of the lean-to shed plans.\\n5. Check for square and anchor the frame to the skids with four joist clip angles.\\n6. Cut two pressure-treated 4 by 4s to a length of 6 feet for the skids.\\n7. Position the frame on the skids and toenail the floor joists to the skids when square.\\n8. Attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring onto the frame.\\n9. Construct the walls for the shed separately and then raise them onto the frame.\\n10. The shed dimensions are 6 feet long by 4 feet wide, with the option to expand in increments of 2 or 4 feet.\", \"Based on the given passages, building a lean-to shed involves several steps as described:\\n\\n1. **Prepare the Site**: Start by preparing the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel. This ensures a stable foundation for the shed.\\n\\n2. **Cut and Set the Skids**: Cut the two 4 x 4 skids to a length of 70 2/4 inches. Set these skids on the prepared site, following the shed building plans. Ensure they are level and secure.\\n\\n3. **Construct the Floor**: Cut two 2 x 6 rim joists to a length of 7 3/4 inches and six joists to a length of 44 3/4 inches. Assemble the floor and set it on the skids. Check for square and anchor the frame to the skids with four joist clip angles.\\n\\n4. **Attach Flooring**: Attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring onto the frame. The walls are constructed separately and then raised onto the frame.\\n\\n5. **Expand Dimensions**: The shed dimensions provided are 6 feet long by 4 feet wide, but you can easily expand these dimensions in increments of 2 or 4 feet.\\n\\nThese steps outline the process of building a lean-to shed, focusing on the site preparation, skids, floor construction, and additional considerations for expanding the shed's dimensions.\"]", "time_sec": 45.164, "generation_time_seconds": 44.3499, "selection_time_seconds": 0.8033, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 4752, 264, 15651, 4686, 24354, 17601, 3807, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 31166, 279, 2747, 448, 264, 220, 19, 24241, 6193, 315, 16830, 291, 41523, 624, 17, 13, 19649, 1378, 220, 19, 856, 220, 19, 1901, 3365, 518, 220, 22, 15, 220, 17, 14, 19, 14924, 624, 18, 13, 2573, 323, 2188, 279, 1901, 3365, 2701, 2