{"id": "135", "question": "how to roast individual garlic cloves", "gt": "Here are brief answers to the question on how to roast individual garlic cloves based on the provided passages:\n\nPassage 1: The passage does not provide information on how to roast individual garlic cloves specifically. However, it does mention that larger garlic cloves may take up to an hour to soften when roasted, so it is possible to assume that smaller cloves will take less time.\n\nPassage 2: This passage provides some options for seasoning the garlic before roasting, but it does not provide instructions on how to roast individual garlic cloves.\n\nPassage 3: This passage provides a method for roasting entire garlic heads, but it does not address how to roast individual garlic cloves. The passage does mention that cutting the top of the garlic bulb and exposing the individual cloves can help them roast more evenly, but it does not provide specific instructions on how to do this.\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages.", "pred": "1. Preheat your oven to 400°F (205°C).\n2. Peel and discard the papery outer layers of the whole garlic bulb, leaving intact the skins of the individual cloves.\n3. Using a sharp knife, cut 1/4 to 1/2 inch from the top of the cloves, exposing the individual cloves.\n4. Place the garlic heads in a baking pan, cut side up. (A muffin pan works great for this, as it keeps the garlic bulbs from rolling around.)\n5. Drizzle a couple teaspoons of olive oil over each exposed garlic head and rub the oil over all the cut, exposed garlic cloves.\n6. Roast the garlic in the preheated oven for the appropriate time, depending on the size of the cloves, which could take up to 45 minutes or even an hour.\n7. Store the roasted garlic cloves in an airtight container in the refrigerator for up to 10 days.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "1. Preheat your oven to 400°F (205°C).\n2. Peel and discard the papery outer layers of the whole garlic bulb, leaving intact the skins of the individual cloves.\n3. Using a sharp knife, cut 1/4 to 1/2 inch from the top of the cloves, exposing the individual cloves.\n4. Place the garlic heads in a baking pan, cut side up. (A muffin pan works great for this, as it keeps the garlic bulbs from rolling around.)\n5. Drizzle a couple teaspoons of olive oil over each exposed garlic head and rub the oil over all the cut, exposed garlic cloves.\n6. Roast the garlic in the preheated oven for the appropriate time, depending on the size of the cloves, which could take up to 45 minutes or even an hour.\n7. Store the roasted garlic cloves in an airtight container in the refrigerator for up to 10 days.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, to roast individual garlic cloves, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. Preheat your oven to 400°F (205°C).\\n2. Peel and discard the papery outer layers of the whole garlic bulb, leaving intact the skins of the individual cloves of garlic.\\n3. Using a sharp knife, cut 1/4 to 1/2 inch from the top of cloves, exposing the individual cloves of garlic.\\n4. Place the garlic heads in a baking pan, cut side up. (A muffin pan works great for this, as it keeps the garlic bulbs from rolling around.)\\n5. Drizzle a couple teaspoons of olive oil over each exposed head, using your fingers to rub the olive oil over all the cut, exposed garlic cloves.\", \"Based on the given passages, here is how to roast individual garlic cloves:\\n\\n1. Preheat your oven to 400°F (205°C).\\n2. Peel and discard the papery outer layers of the whole garlic bulb, leaving intact the skins of the individual cloves of garlic.\\n3. Using a sharp knife, cut 1/4 to 1/2 inch from the top of cloves, exposing the individual cloves of garlic.\\n4. Place the garlic heads in a baking pan, cut side up.\\n5. Drizzle a couple teaspoons of olive oil over each exposed head, using your fingers to rub the olive oil over all the cut, exposed garlic cloves.\", \"Based on the given passages, here's how to roast individual garlic cloves:\\n\\n1. Preheat your oven to 400°F (205°C).\\n2. Peel and discard the papery outer layers of the whole garlic bulb, leaving intact the skins of the individual cloves.\\n3. Using a sharp knife, cut 1/4 to 1/2 inch from the top of the cloves, exposing the individual cloves.\\n4. Place the garlic heads in a baking pan, cut side up. (A muffin pan works great for this, as it keeps the garlic bulbs from rolling around.)\\n5. Drizzle a couple teaspoons of olive oil over each exposed garlic head and rub the oil over all the cut, exposed garlic cloves.\\n6. Roast the garlic in the preheated oven for the appropriate time, depending on the size of the cloves, which could take up to 45 minutes or even an hour.\\n7. Store the roasted garlic cloves in an airtight container in the refrigerator for up to 10 days.\", \"Based on the given passages:\\n\\n1. Preheat the oven to 400°F (205°C).\\n2. Peel and discard the papery outer layers of the whole garlic bulb while leaving the skins of the individual cloves intact.\\n3. Using a sharp knife, cut 1/4 to 1/2 inch from the top of the cloves to expose them.\\n4. Place the garlic heads in a baking pan with the cut side up.\\n5. Drizzle a couple of teaspoons of olive oil over each exposed head.\\n6. Rub the olive oil over the exposed garlic cloves using your fingers.\\n7. Roast the garlic in the oven for a time dependent on the size of the cloves, which may take up to 45 minutes or even an hour for larger cloves.\\n8. Store the roasted garlic cloves in an airtight container in the refrigerator for up to 10 days.\"]", "time_sec": 33.269, "generation_time_seconds": 32.4961, "selection_time_seconds": 0.7637, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 311, 57685, 3842, 30635, 84288, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 4968, 19963, 697, 23387, 311, 220, 19, 15, 15, 58472, 3