mount of prostate-specific antigen in a man's blood. The PSA test is used to detect prostate cancer, as well as noncancerous conditions such as prostatitis or an enlarged prostate. Elevated PSA levels may indicate these conditions, while moderately elevated levels could suggest prostate cancer.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.45224311042715004, "g_worst_claim": 0.05180437564849853, "g_content": 0.6042147804709042, "g_traj_mean": 0.4634033739566803}}], "selected_rollout": 1, "random_sel_rollout": 3, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 47.54, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "167", "question": "show me how can i make gragh for my research", "gt": "To create a graph for your research, you first need to collect all of your data, remembering that the graph should show a comparison among categories. If you are creating a bar graph, you will use rectangular bars to represent different values. You need to title your graph and determine how high your graph needs to go. If your data points are substantially different in scale, you can use a pie chart. The y-axis of your graph will have a scale, and each bar’s height should be proportionate to the size of the category it represents. Make sure to consider any disproportionate responses that might impact the scale of your graph. Regardless of the type of graph you choose, it should provide a clear visual representation of your information.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, to make a graph for your research, follow these steps:\n1. Decide on the type of graph you want to use. For a comparison among categories, a bar graph would be suitable.\n2. Collect your data. For instance, if you're comparing rainfall between February 2005 and February 2006, gather data on the rainfall for those months.\n3. Choose to use graph paper to ensure your graph is neat and professional.\n4. Title your graph and determine the scale for the y-axis.\n5. Ensure that each bar's height is proportionate to the data it represents. If you have disproportionate responses, consider using a pie chart as a backup.\n6. Visualize the data in a way that effectively shows the comparisons among categories. For example, if you're showing rainfall, ensure that the bars visually represent the different levels of rainfall in a clear and effective manner.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, to make a graph for your research, follow these steps:\n1. Decide on the type of graph you want to use. For a comparison among categories, a bar graph would be suitable.\n2. Collect your data. For instance, if you're comparing rainfall between February 2005 and February 2006, gather data on the rainfall for those months.\n3. Choose to use graph paper to ensure your graph is neat and professional.\n4. Title your graph and determine the scale for the y-axis.\n5. Ensure that each bar's height is proportionate to the data it represents. If you have disproportionate responses, consider using a pie chart as a backup.\n6. Visualize the data in a way that effectively shows the comparisons among categories. For example, if you're showing rainfall, ensure that the bars visually represent the different levels of rainfall in a clear and effective manner.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, here is how you can make a graph for your research:\\n\\n1. Determine the type of graph you need. For comparing groups or types, a bar graph is suitable.\\n2. Collect your data. For example, if you want to compare the amount of rainfall in a city between February 2005 and February 2006, gather the rainfall data for those months.\\n3. Title your graph. Clearly label what the graph represents.\\n4. Decide on the scale for the y-axis. This will depend on the range of your data.\\n5. Create the bars. Each bar should represent a category and its height should be proportionate to the value it represents.\\n6. Add color to your graph to make it visually appealing.\\n7. Use graph paper if necessary to help you create a neat and precise graph.\", \"Based on the given passages, to make a graph for your research, you should follow these steps:\\n\\n1. Decide on the type of graph you want to use. For comparing groups or types, a bar graph is recommended.\\n\\n2. Collect your data. Make sure the data is organized to compare different categories effectively. For example, if you want to compare rainfall in a city over two years, you need to gather rainfall data for each month.\\n\\n3. Choose a suitable scale for the y-axis. The scale should be appropriate for the data you have. If the data scales are very different, it might be necessary to adjust the scale to make the graph more interpretable.\\n\\n4. Create the graph. If using graph paper, it can help in making a neat and accurate graph. The graph should be visually appealing and add color to your research presentation.\", \"Based on the given passages, here are the steps to create a bar graph for your research:\\n\\n1. First, you'll need to title your graph and find out how high your graph needs to go.\\n2. Decide what categories you want to compare. For example, you might want to compare the amount of rainfall a city received between February 2005 and February 2006.\\n3. Collect your data for each category. For instance, you could record the rainfall in inches for each month.\\n4. Create a bar for each category in your graph, with the height of the bar representing the value of the category.\\n5. Label each bar with the category name and its corresponding value.\\n6. Make sure the bars are evenly spaced along the x-axis.\\n7. Add a legend to your graph if necessary, to explain what each bar represents.\\n8. Finally, check your graph for accuracy and clarity, and make any necessary adjustments.\", \"Based on the given passages, to make a graph for your research, follow these steps:\\n1. Decide on the type of graph you want to use. For a comparison among categories, a bar graph would be suitable.\\n2. Collect your data. For instance, if you're comparing rainfall between February 2005 and February 2006, gather data on the rainfall for those months.\\n3. Choose to use graph paper to ensure your graph is neat and professional.\\n4. Title your graph and determine the scale for the y-axis.\\n5. Ensure that each bar's height is proportionate to the data it represents. If you have disproportionate responses, consider using a pie chart as a backup.\\n6. Visualize the data in a way that effectively shows the comparisons among categories. For example, if you're showing rainfall, ensure that the bars visually represent the different levels of rainfall in a clear and effective manner.\"]", "time_sec": 34.699, "generation_time_seconds": 33.3662, "selection_time_seconds": 1.3222, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 1588, 374, 1246, 498, 646, 1281, 264, 4771, 369, 697, 3412, 1447, 16, 13, 29901, 279, 943, 315, 4771, 498, 1184, 13, 1752, 26297, 5203, 476, 4494, 11, 264, 3619, 4771, 374, 14452, 624, 17, 13, 20513, 697, 821, 13, 1752, 3110, 11, 421, 498, 1366, 311, 9429, 279, 3311, 315, 52858, 304, 264, 3283, 1948, 7400, 220, 17, 15, 15, 20, 323, 7400, 220, 17, 15, 15, 21, 11, 9567, 279, 52858, 821, 369, 1846, 3951, 624, 18, 13, 10869, 697, 4771, 13, 53404, 2383, 1128, 279, 4771, 10868, 624, 19, 13, 98881, 389, 279, 5452, 369, 279, 379, 35321, 13, 1096, 686, 6761, 389, 279, 2088, 315, 697, 821, 624, 20, 13, 4230, 279, 15904, 13, 8886, 3619, 1265, 4009, 264, 5582, 323, 1181, 2608, 1265, 387, 21117, 349, 311, 279, 897, 432, 10868, 624, 21, 13, 2691, 1894, 311, 697, 4771, 311, 1281, 432, 42295, 32252, 624, 22, 13, 5443, 4771, 5567, 421, 5871, 311, 1492, 498, 1855, 264, 28485, 323, 23560, 4771, 13, 151645], "pred": "1. Determine the type of graph you need. For comparing groups or types, a bar graph is suitable.\n2. Collect your data. For example, if you want to compare the amount of rainfall in a city between February 2005 and February 2006, gather the rainfall data for those months.\n3. Title your graph. Clearly label what the graph represents.\n4. Decide on the scale for the y-axis. This will depend on the range of your data.\n5. Create the bars. Each bar should represent a category and its height should be proportionate to the value it represents.\n6. Add color to your graph to make it visually appealing.\n7. Use graph paper if necessary to help you create a neat and precise graph.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 