 18, 13, 22201, 311, 990, 4771, 5567, 311, 5978, 697, 4771, 374, 28485, 323, 6584, 624, 19, 13, 10869, 697, 4771, 323, 8253, 279, 5452, 369, 279, 379, 35321, 624, 20, 13, 29279, 429, 1817, 3619, 594, 2608, 374, 21117, 349, 311, 279, 821, 432, 10868, 13, 1416, 498, 614, 79053, 14507, 11, 2908, 1667, 264, 4349, 9487, 438, 264, 15725, 624, 21, 13, 20185, 551, 279, 821, 304, 264, 1616, 429, 13444, 4933, 279, 35495, 4221, 11059, 13, 1752, 3110, 11, 421, 498, 2299, 9027, 52858, 11, 5978, 429, 279, 15904, 42295, 4009, 279, 2155, 5866, 315, 52858, 304, 264, 2797, 323, 7373, 11566, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, to make a graph for your research, follow these steps:\n1. Decide on the type of graph you want to use. For a comparison among categories, a bar graph would be suitable.\n2. Collect your data. For instance, if you're comparing rainfall between February 2005 and February 2006, gather data on the rainfall for those months.\n3. Choose to use graph paper to ensure your graph is neat and professional.\n4. Title your graph and determine the scale for the y-axis.\n5. Ensure that each bar's height is proportionate to the data it represents. If you have disproportionate responses, consider using a pie chart as a backup.\n6. Visualize the data in a way that effectively shows the comparisons among categories. For example, if you're showing rainfall, ensure that the bars visually represent the different levels of rainfall in a clear and effective manner.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.1285457513715291, "g_worst_claim": 0.13917667667071024, "g_content": 1.4540064194623161, "g_traj_mean": 0.9706157445907593}}], "selected_rollout": 3, "random_sel_rollout": 3, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 46.56, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "169", "question": "show me how can i make gragh for my research", "gt": "Sure, I can help you with that! Based on the given passages, here's a step-by-step guide on how to create a bar graph for your research:\n\n1. Collect your data: The first step is to gather all the data you want to represent in your graph. Make sure the data is organized and easy to compare.\n2. Decide on the x-axis and y-axis: The x-axis typically represents the category or group being compared, while the y-axis represents the value or amount of each category.\n3. Determine the height of the graph: The height of the graph will depend on the highest value in your data set. Make sure to choose a height that allows for clear representation of all the data points.\n4. Create the bars: Use rectangular bars to represent each category, making sure the height of each bar is proportional to the value it represents.\n5. Add labels and titles: Label each bar with its corresponding category and add a title to the graph that describes the data being represented.\n6. Consider using a pie chart: If you have only a few data points to display and each one is substantially different in scale, a pie chart might be a better option.\n7. Format and refine: Make sure the graph is visually appealing and easy to read. Adjust the scaling of the y-axis if needed, and consider adding colors or shading to make the graph more engaging.\n\nBy following these steps, you should be able to create a clear and effective bar graph for your research. Good luck!", "pred": "1. Determine the type of graph you need. For comparing groups or types, a bar graph is suitable.\n2. Collect your data. For example, if you want to compare the amount of rainfall in a city between February 2005 and February 2006, gather the rainfall data for those months.\n3. Title your graph. Clearly label what the graph represents.\n4. Decide on the scale for the y-axis. This will depend on the range of your data.\n5. Create the bars. Each bar should represent a category and its height should be proportionate to the value it represents.\n6. Add color to your graph to make it visually appealing.\n7. Use graph paper if necessary to help you create a neat and precise graph.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "1. Determine the type of graph you need. For comparing groups or types, a bar graph is suitable.\n2. Collect your data. For example, if you want to compare the amount of rainfall in a city between February 2005 and February 2006, gather the rainfall data for those months.\n3. Title your graph. Clearly label what the graph represents.\n4. Decide on the scale for the y-axis. This will depend on the range of your data.\n5. Create the bars. Each bar should represent a category and its height should be proportionate to the value it represents.\n6. Add color to your graph to make it visually appealing.\n7. Use graph paper if necessary to help you create a neat and precise graph.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, here is how you can make a graph for your research:\\n\\n1. Determine the type of graph you need. For comparing groups or types, a bar graph is suitable.\\n2. Collect your data. For example, if you want to compare the amount of rainfall in a city between February 2005 and February 2006, gather the rainfall data for those months.\\n3. Title your graph. Clearly label what the graph represents.\\n4. Decide on the scale for the y-axis. This will depend on the range of your data.\\n5. Create the bars. Each bar should represent a category and its height should be proportionate to the value it represents.\\n6. Add color to your graph to make it visually appealing.\\n7. Use graph paper if necessary to help you create a neat and precise graph.\", \"Based on the given passages, to make a graph for your research, you can follow these steps:\\n1. Decide on the type of graph you need. For comparing categories, a bar graph is suitable.\\n2. Collect all your data. For example, if your goal is to organize data on how many inches of rainfall a city received between the months of February 2005 and February 2006, you need to gather the rainfall data for those months.\\n3. Title your graph. Make sure to accurately represent your data.\\n4. Determine how high your graph needs to go. This depends on the range of your data, in this case, the range of inches of rainfall.\\n5. Use graph paper if available to create a clear and visually appealing graph.\\n6. Color your graph to enhance its presentation.\\n7. If you still think the story of the data is unclear, consider using a pie chart as a backup, especially if you have only a few data points to display and they are substantially different in scale.\", \"Based on the given passages, to make a graph for your research, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. Determine the type of graph you need based on your data. For example, if you want to compare groups or types, a bar graph would be appropriate.\\n2. Collect your data. Make sure it is suitable for the type of graph you are using. For instance, if you are using a bar graph, you need data on categories that can be compared, such as rainfall amounts.\\n3. Choose a method for creating the graph. You can use graph paper or another tool to help you make a visually appealing and informative graph.\\n4. Title the graph and determine the scale for the y-axis. This will help you accurately represent the data.\\n5. Create the bars for the graph, ensuring they are proportionate to the data they represent. This will make the graph easier to understand and visually appealing.\\n6. Use colors to enhance the visual representation and make the graph more engaging.\\n7. Review the graph to ensure the story of your data is clear and well represented. If necessary, consider using a pie chart as a backup option when dealing with a small number of data points or disproportionate responses.\", \"Based on the given passages, here are the steps to make a bar graph:\\n\\n1. Collect your data: Gather all the information you need to compare among categories. For example, if you want to organize data on how many inches of rainfall a city received between the months of February 2005 and February 2006, you need to collect rainfall data for those two months.\\n\\n2. Determine the height of the graph: Decide how high your graph needs to go. This will depend on the range of the data you're comparing. For instance, if you're comparing rainfall, the height of the bars will represent the amount of rainfall in inches.\\n\\n3. Title the graph: Give your graph a descriptive title so it's clear what the data represents. For example, you could title it \\\"Rainfall in [City Name] from February 2005 to February 2006.\\\"\\n\\n4. Create the bars: Draw bars for each category. The height of each bar should be proportionate to the value it represents. For example, if one bar represents 5 inches of rainfall and another represents 10 inches of rainfall, the second bar should be twice as tall as the first.\\n\\n5. Use graph paper: If you want, you can use graph paper to help you make a neat and organized graph. This will make your display look professional and be easier to read.\\n\\n6. Add color: Adding color to your graph can help make it more visually appealing and easier to understand. Use different colors for different categories to make it clear which bars belong to which category.\\n\\n7. Check the scale: Ensure that the y-axis has a scale that accurately represents the values of the data. This will prevent the graph from looking distorted due to disproportionate responses.\\n\\nBy following these steps, you can create a bar graph that effectively compares the rainfall data for the two months you've collected.\"]", "time_sec": 45.908, "generation_time_seconds": 44.7343, "selection_time_seconds": 1.1612, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 1588, 374, 1246, 498, 646, 1281, 264, 4771, 369, 697, 3412, 1447, 16, 13, 29901, 279, 943, 315, 4771, 498, 1184, 13, 1752, 26297, 5203, 476, 4494, 11, 264, 3619, 4771, 374, 14452, 624, 17, 13, 20513, 697, 821, 13, 1752, 3110, 11, 421, 498, 1366, 311, 9429, 279, 3311, 315, 52858, 304, 264, 3283, 1948, 7400, 220, 17, 15, 15, 20, 323, 7400, 220, 17, 15, 15, 21, 11, 9567, 279, 52858, 821, 369, 1846, 3951, 624, 18, 13, 10869, 697, 4771, 13, 53404, 2383, 1128, 279, 4771, 10868, 624, 19, 13, 98881, 389, 279, 5452, 369, 279, 379, 35321, 13, 1096, 686, 6761, 389, 279, 2088, 315, 697, 821, 624, 20, 13, 4230, 279, 15904, 13, 8886, 3619, 1265, 4009, 264, 5582, 323, 1181, 2608, 1265, 387, 21117, 349, 311, 279, 897, 432, 10868, 624, 21, 13, 2691, 1894, 311, 697, 4771, 311, 1281, 432, 42295, 32252, 624, 22, 13, 5443, 4771, 5567, 421, 5871, 311, 1492, 498, 1855, 264, 28485, 323, 23560, 4771, 13, 151645], "pred": "1. Determine the type of graph you need. For comparing groups or types, a bar graph is suitable.\n2. Collect your data. For example, if you want to compare the amount of rainfall in a city between February 2005 and February 2006, gather the rainfall data for those months.\n3. Title your graph. Clearly label what the graph represents.\n4. Decide on the scale for the y-axis. This will depend on the range of your data.\n5. Create the bars. Each bar should represent a category and its height should be proportionate to the value it represents.\n6. Add color to your graph to make it visually appealing.\n7. Use graph paper if necessary to help you create a neat and precise graph.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.9865200353023909, "g_worst_claim": 0.08697724342346191, "g_content": 1.3591316731770833, "g_traj_mean": 0.9054723978042603}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 311, 1281, 264, 4771, 369, 697, 3412, 11, 498, 646, 1795, 1493, 7354, 510, 16, 13, 98881, 389, 279, 943, 315, 4771, 498, 1184, 13, 1752, 26297