{"id": "199", "question": "what causes necrotizing fasciitis", "gt": "Necrotizing fasciitis is caused primarily by bacteria, specifically group A beta-hemolytic streptococci, also known as Streptococcus pyogenes. This is the same bacteria that causes strep throat, impetigo, and rheumatic fever. Other bacteria types, such as staphylococcus, are also associated with the disease. The bacteria infect the superficial fascia, a layer of connective tissue below the skin. Prior trauma, such as a cut or scrape, insect bite, burn, or needle puncture wound can serve as a source of infection as well as surgical incision sites and various surgical procedures. In rare cases, fungi can lead to this condition.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, necrotizing fasciitis is caused by several kinds of bacteria, including group A Streptococcus (GAS) bacteria, staphylococcus, and others. These bacteria can infect the superficial fascia, a layer of connective tissue below the skin. Examples of these bacteria include Streptococcus pyogenes, which can also cause strep throat and impetigo. Necrotizing fasciitis can be caused by these bacteria in rare cases and can be a more dangerous infection compared to the infections they usually cause, which are often mild.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, necrotizing fasciitis is caused by several kinds of bacteria, including group A Streptococcus (GAS) bacteria, staphylococcus, and others. These bacteria can infect the superficial fascia, a layer of connective tissue below the skin. Examples of these bacteria include Streptococcus pyogenes, which can also cause strep throat and impetigo. Necrotizing fasciitis can be caused by these bacteria in rare cases and can be a more dangerous infection compared to the infections they usually cause, which are often mild.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, necrotizing fasciitis is caused by several kinds of bacteria. These bacteria can include group A Streptococcus (GAS), staphylococcus, and others. The infections caused by these bacteria are usually mild, but in rare cases, they can lead to a more dangerous infection that can destroy skin, fat, and the tissue covering the muscles within a very short time. This condition is sometimes referred to as flesh-eating bacteria.\", \"Based on the given passages, necrotizing fasciitis is caused by several types of bacteria, including group A Streptococcus (GAS), which is also responsible for strep throat and impetigo. Other types of bacteria, such as staphylococcus, can also cause the disease. The bacteria infect the superficial fascia, a layer of connective tissue below the skin.\", \"Based on the given passages, necrotizing fasciitis is caused by several kinds of bacteria, including group A Streptococcus (GAS) bacteria, staphylococcus, and others. These bacteria can infect the superficial fascia, a layer of connective tissue below the skin. Examples of these bacteria include Streptococcus pyogenes, which can also cause strep throat and impetigo. Necrotizing fasciitis can be caused by these bacteria in rare cases and can be a more dangerous infection compared to the infections they usually cause, which are often mild.\", \"Based on the given passages, necrotizing fasciitis is caused by several kinds of bacteria. These bacteria include group A beta-hemolytic streptococci (Streptococcus pyogenes), staphylococcus, and others. The infections caused by these bacteria are usually mild, but in rare cases, they can lead to a more dangerous infection that can destroy skin, fat, and the tissue covering the muscles. The disease is sometimes referred to as flesh-eating bacteria and can be very rare but serious.\"]", "time_sec": 21.831, "generation_time_seconds": 21.0355, "selection_time_seconds": 0.7838, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 266