{"id": "206", "question": "what is chard vegetable", "gt": "Based on the given passages, Swiss chard (also known as just plain chard) is a type of beet plant that has been selected for its leaf production rather than storage root formation. It produces large, tender deep green leaves with thick, crisp stalks. The leaves have a more assertive and sometimes bitter taste compared to spinach. Swiss chard is a perishable vegetable and should be stored in the refrigerator. It can be used raw in salads or cooked like in pizzoccheri.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, chard is a leafy green vegetable that is a member of the beet family. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor. Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked. Chard is extremely perishable, so it should be stored in the refrigerator for 2 to 3 days.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, chard is a leafy green vegetable that is a member of the beet family. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor. Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked. Chard is extremely perishable, so it should be stored in the refrigerator for 2 to 3 days.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, chard is a leafy green vegetable that is a member of the beet family. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor. Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked. Chard is extremely perishable, so it should be stored in the refrigerator for 2 to 3 days.\", \"Based on the given passages, chard is a beet that has been cultivated for leaf production, often used in salads or cooked dishes like pizzoccheri. It has large, deep green leaves, thick stalks, and a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor compared to spinach. Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked to reduce their bitterness. The leaves are an attractive ornamental addition to the vegetable garden.\", \"Based on the given passages, Swiss chard, also known as plain chard, is a type of leafy green vegetable that belongs to the same subspecies as beetroot and other beet cultivars. Chard has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. It is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor. Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked, like in pizzoccheri or sauteed. Chard is an attractive ornamental vegetable that adds color to the vegetable garden. It can be harvested while the leaves are young and tender or after maturity when they have slightly tougher stems. Chard is extremely perishable, so it should be kept in refrigerator storage for 2 to 3 days. The vegetable is also known for its ornamental value, with varieties producing fresh white, yellow, or red leaf stalks.\", \"Based on the given passages, chard is a beet vegetable that has been cultivated for its large, deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Unlike spinach, chard has a stronger, more assertive flavor that can be perceived as bitter by some. Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature leaves and stalks are typically cooked, such as in pizzoccheri or sauteed dishes. The bitterness of chard is known to fade with cooking, leaving a refined flavor that is more delicate than cooked spinach.\"]", "time_sec": 26.595, "generation_time_seconds": 25.5514, "selection_time_seconds": 1.0327, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 521, 567, 374, 264, 15933, 88, 6176, 35481, 429, 374, 264, 4462, 315, 279, 48012, 2997, 13, 1084, 702, 3460, 11, 282, 642, 8503, 714, 27582, 5538, 6176, 10901, 323, 12045, 11, 41854, 54872, 82, 13, 910, 567, 374, 4428, 311, 67698, 714, 702, 264, 16245, 11, 803, 2060, 533, 320, 14689, 1744, 11, 25180, 8, 17172, 13, 29160, 3908, 521, 567, 646, 387, 1483, 7112, 304, 76752, 11, 1393, 14851, 521, 567, 10901, 323, 54872, 82, 525, 11136, 29105, 13, 910, 567, 374, 9016, 82117, 480, 11, 773, 432, 1265, 387, 9768, 304, 279, 44944, 369, 220, 17, 311, 220, 18, 2849, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, chard is a leafy green vegetable that is a member of the beet family. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor. Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked. Chard is extremely perishable, so it should be stored in the refrigerator for 2 to 3 days.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.543254347341597, "g_worst_claim": 0.28993502785177794, "g_content": 1.9474619651327327, "g_traj_mean": 1.4091274738311768}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 521, 567, 374, 264, 48012, 429, 702, 1012, 66066, 369, 15933, 5670, 11, 3545, 1483, 304, 76752, 476, 29105, 25779, 1075, 281, 8759, 509, 9034, 72, 13, 1084, 702, 3460, 11, 5538, 6176, 10901, 11, 12045, 54872, 82, 11, 323, 264, 16245, 11, 803, 2060, 533, 320, 14689, 1744, 11, 25180, 8, 17172, 7707, 311, 67698, 13, 29160, 3908, 521, 567, 646, 387, 1483, 7112, 304, 76752, 11, 1393, 14851, 521, 567, 10901, 323, 54872, 82, 525, 11136, 29105, 311, 7949, 862, 78996, 13, 576, 10901, 525, 458, 18879, 39445, 43086, 5256, 311, 279, 35481, 13551, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, chard is a beet that has been cultivated for leaf production, often used in salads or cooked dishes like pizzoccheri. It has large, deep green leaves, thick stalks, and a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor compared to spinach. Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked to reduce their bitterness. The leaves are an attractive ornamental addition to the vegetable garden.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.4909241059324245, "g_worst_claim": 0.3195227384567261, "g_content": 2.2027019578583387, "g_traj_mean": 1.5643469095230103}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 29691, 521, 567, 11, 1083, 3881, 438, 14396, 521, 567, 11, 374, 264, 943, 315, 15933, 88, 6176, 35481, 429, 17180, 311, 279, 1852, 5147, 88621, 438, 48012, 2888, 323, 1008, 48012, 26883, 1561, 13, 910, 567, 702, 3460, 11, 282, 642, 8503, 714, 27582, 5538, 6176, 10901, 323, 12045, 11, 41854, 54872, 82, 13, 1084, 374, 4428, 311, 67698, 714, 702, 264, 16245, 11, 803, 2060, 533, 320, 14689, 1744, 11, 25180, 8, 17172, 13, 29160, 3908, 521, 567, 646, 387, 1483, 7112, 304, 76752, 11, 1393, 14851, 521, 567, 10901, 323, 54872, 82, 525, 11136, 29105, 11, 1075, 304, 281, 8759, 509, 9034, 72, 476, 822, 1070, 291, 13, 910, 567, 374, 458, 18879, 39445, 43086, 35481, 429, 11367, 1894, 311, 279, 35481, 13551, 13, 1084, 646, 387, 66472, 1393, 279, 10901, 525, 3908, 323, 27582, 476, 1283, 47161, 979, 807, 614, 10078, 48231, 43714, 13, 910, 567, 374, 9016, 82117, 480, 11, 773, 432, 1265, 387, 8604, 304, 44944, 5819, 369, 220, 17, 311, 220, 18, 2849, 13, 576, 35481, 374, 1083, 3881, 369, 1181, 39445, 43086, 897, 11, 448, 35580, 17387, 7722, 4158, 11, 13753, 11, 476, 2518, 15933, 54872, 82, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, Swiss chard, also known as plain chard, is a type of leafy green vegetable that belongs to the same subspecies as beetroot and other beet cultivars. Chard has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. It is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor. Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked, like in pizzoccheri or sauteed. Chard is an attractive ornamental vegetable that adds color to the vegetable garden. It can be harvested while the leaves are young and tender or after maturity when they have slightly tougher stems. Chard is extremely perishable, so it should be kept in refrigerator storage for 2 to 3 days. The vegetable is also known for its ornamental value, with varieties producing fresh white, yellow, or red leaf stalks.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.8234643460248972, "g_worst_claim": 0.3216976324717204, "g_content": 2.7252233269509305, "g_traj_mean": 1.7221484184265137}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 521, 567, 374, 264, 48012, 35481, 429, 702, 1012, 66066, 369, 1181, 3460, 11, 5538, 6176, 10901, 323, 12045, 11, 41854, 54872, 82, 13, 26048, 67698, 11, 521, 567, 702, 264, 16245, 11, 803, 2060, 533, 17172, 429, 646, 387, 25536, 438, 25180, 553, 1045, 13, 29160, 3908, 521, 567, 646, 387, 1483, 7112, 304, 76752, 11, 1393, 14851, 10901, 323, 54872, 82, 525, 11136, 29105, 11, 1741, 438, 304, 281, 8759, 509, 9034, 72, 476, 822, 1070, 291, 25779, 13, 576, 78996, 315, 521, 567, 374, 3881, 311, 15016, 448, 17233, 11, 9380, 264, 37191, 17172, 429, 374, 803, 35201, 1091, 29105, 67698, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, chard is a beet vegetable that has been cultivated for its large, deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Unlike spinach, chard has a stronger, more assertive flavor that can be perceived as bitter by some. Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature leaves and stalks are typically cooked, such as in pizzoccheri or sauteed dishes. The bitterness of chard is known to fade with cooking, leaving a refined flavor that is more delicate than cooked spinach.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.413871316474317, "g_worst_claim": 0.3195227384567261, "g_content": 2.4485039224430007, "g_traj_mean": 1.4717023372650146}}], "selected_rollout": 2, "random_sel_rollout": 0, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 59.46, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "207", "question": "what is chard vegetable", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is what can be inferred about the vegetable \"chard\":\n\n* Chard is a type of beet (Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima) that has been chosen for leaf production over storage root formation.\n* Chard has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks.\n* The flavor of chard is similar to spinach but with a stronger, more assertive taste (some consider it bitter).\n* Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked to reduce bitterness and bring out a refined flavor.\n* Chard is an attractive ornamental plant that adds color to the vegetable garden.\n* Chard is perishable and should be stored in the refrigerator to minimize spoilage.\n\nTherefore, the answer to the question \"What is chard vegetable?\" is: A beet variety (Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima) grown for leaf production, characterized by large, fleshy leaves and stalks with a strong, bitter flavor that becomes more refined with cooking.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, chard is a leafy green vegetable that is closely related to spinach in terms of plant family but differs in its flavor, which is described as stronger and more assertive, sometimes perceived as bitter. It is known for its large, fleshy, but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked or sauteed. Chard is a cultivated descendant of the sea beet (Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima) and is sometimes grown for ornamental purposes due to its attractive color.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, chard is a leafy green vegetable that is closely related to spinach in terms of plant family but differs in its flavor, which is described as stronger and more assertive, sometimes perceived as bitter. It is known for its large, fleshy, but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked or sauteed. Chard is a cultivated descendant of the sea beet (Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima) and is sometimes grown for ornamental purposes due to its attractive color.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, chard is a leafy green vegetable that is a member of the beet family. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor. Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked. Chard is extremely perishable, so it should be stored in the refrigerator for 2 to 3 days.\", \"Based on the given passages, chard is a beet that has been cultivated for leaf production. It is characterized by large, fleshy, and tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor. It can be used raw in salads when young and tender, or cooked when mature, which reduces its bitterness and enhances its delicate flavor. Chard is extremely perishable and should be stored in the refrigerator for a maximum of 2 to 3 days.\", \"Based on the given passages, chard is a type of vegetable that is similar to spinach but with a stronger, more assertive flavor. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard can be used fresh in salads or cooked, and its bitterness fades with cooking. It is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation, and it comes in white, yellow, or red varieties. Chard is extremely perishable and should be stored unwashed in plastic bags in the crisper for 2 to 3 days.\", \"Based on the given passages, chard is a leafy green vegetable that is closely related to spinach in terms of plant family but differs in its flavor, which is described as stronger and more assertive, sometimes perceived as bitter. It is known for its large, fleshy, but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked or sauteed. Chard is a cultivated descendant of the sea beet (Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima) and is sometimes grown for ornamental purposes due to its attractive color.\"]", "time_sec": 26.173, "generation_time_seconds": 25.2431, "selection_time_seconds": 0.9146, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 521, 567, 374, 264, 15933, 88, 6176, 35481, 429, 374, 264, 4462, 315, 279, 48012, 2997, 13, 1084, 702, 3460, 11, 282, 642, 8503, 714, 27582, 5538, 6176, 10901, 323, 12045, 11, 41854, 54872, 82, 13, 910, 567, 374, 4428, 311, 67698, 714, 702, 264, 16245, 11, 803, 2060, 533, 320, 14689, 1744, 11, 25180, 8, 17172, 13, 29160, 3908, 521, 567, 646, 387, 1483, 7112, 304, 76752, 11, 1393, 14851, 521, 567, 10901, 323, 54872, 82, 525, 11136, 29105, 13, 910, 567, 374, 9016, 82117, 480, 11, 773, 432, 1265, 387, 9768, 304, 279, 44944, 369, 220, 17, 311, 220, 18, 2849, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, chard is a leafy green vegetable that is a member of the beet family. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor. Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked. Chard is extremely perishable, so it should be stored in the refrigerator for 2 to 3 days.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.506619073498687, "g_worst_claim": 0.23247963190078735, "g_content": 1.933681565888074, "g_traj_mean": 1.3771862983703613}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_