304, 279, 1560, 27370, 369, 220, 17, 311, 220, 18, 2849, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, chard is a type of vegetable that is similar to spinach but with a stronger, more assertive flavor. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard can be used fresh in salads or cooked, and its bitterness fades with cooking. It is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation, and it comes in white, yellow, or red varieties. Chard is extremely perishable and should be stored unwashed in plastic bags in the crisper for 2 to 3 days.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.485151180024282, "g_worst_claim": 0.23247963190078735, "g_content": 2.344185723198785, "g_traj_mean": 1.588573932647705}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 521, 567, 374, 264, 15933, 88, 6176, 35481, 429, 374, 15148, 5435, 311, 67698, 304, 3793, 315, 6008, 2997, 714, 43542, 304, 1181, 17172, 11, 892, 374, 7481, 438, 16245, 323, 803, 2060, 533, 11, 7025, 25536, 438, 25180, 13, 1084, 374, 3881, 369, 1181, 3460, 11, 282, 642, 8503, 11, 714, 27582, 5538, 6176, 10901, 323, 12045, 11, 41854, 54872, 82, 13, 29160, 3908, 521, 567, 646, 387, 1483, 7112, 304, 76752, 11, 1393, 14851, 521, 567, 10901, 323, 54872, 82, 525, 11136, 29105, 476, 822, 1070, 291, 13, 910, 567, 374, 264, 66066, 75851, 315, 279, 9396, 48012, 320, 64811, 73160, 285, 1186, 2154, 13, 3594, 275, 7523, 8, 323, 374, 7025, 14700, 369, 39445, 43086, 9895, 4152, 311, 1181, 18879, 1894, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, chard is a leafy green vegetable that is closely related to spinach in terms of plant family but differs in its flavor, which is described as stronger and more assertive, sometimes perceived as bitter. It is known for its large, fleshy, but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked or sauteed. Chard is a cultivated descendant of the sea beet (Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima) and is sometimes grown for ornamental purposes due to its attractive color.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.1899864013548955, "g_worst_claim": 0.19716388838631765, "g_content": 1.8507051311555456, "g_traj_mean": 1.1301569938659668}}], "selected_rollout": 2, "random_sel_rollout": 3, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 50.29, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "209", "question": "what is chard vegetable", "gt": "Based on the given passages, chard is a type of vegetable that belongs to the same subspecies as beetroot and is characterized by its large, fleshy leaves and thick, crisp stalks. It has a stronger, more assertive flavor than spinach and can be used raw in salads or cooked like in pizzoccheri. Chard is a beet that has been selected for leaf production rather than storage root formation and can be harvested when young and tender or after maturity. It is an attractive ornamental that adds color to the vegetable garden, but it is extremely perishable and should be stored in the refrigerator for a maximum of 2-3 days.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, chard is a type of beet. It has large, fleshy, and tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard can be grown with white, yellow, or red leaf stalks. The vegetable can be harvested either when the leaves are young and tender or after maturity when the leaves are larger with slightly tougher stems. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive (some think bitter) flavor. Young chard leaves and stalks can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked. Chard can be found in the same subspecies as beetroot (garden beet) and all other beet cultivars. It is an attractive ornamental that adds color to the vegetable garden. Chard is extremely perishable, so it should be stored unwashed in plastic bags in the crisper for 2 to 3 days.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, chard is a type of beet. It has large, fleshy, and tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard can be grown with white, yellow, or red leaf stalks. The vegetable can be harvested either when the leaves are young and tender or after maturity when the leaves are larger with slightly tougher stems. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive (some think bitter) flavor. Young chard leaves and stalks can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked. Chard can be found in the same subspecies as beetroot (garden beet) and all other beet cultivars. It is an attractive ornamental that adds color to the vegetable garden. Chard is extremely perishable, so it should be stored unwashed in plastic bags in the crisper for 2 to 3 days.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, chard is a leafy green vegetable that is a member of the beet family. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor. Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked. Chard is extremely perishable, so it should be stored in the refrigerator for 2 to 3 days.\", \"Based on the given passages, chard is a type of leafy green vegetable related to beets. It is characterized by its large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor. Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked. Chard can be harvested while the leaves are young and tender or after maturity when the stalks have slightly tougher stems. It is known for its attractive ornamental value, adding color to the vegetable garden. Chard is extremely perishable and should be stored unwashed in plastic bags in the crisper for 2 to 3 days to maintain freshness.\", \"Based on the given passages, chard is a leafy green vegetable that is similar to spinach but with a stronger, more assertive flavor. It is the cultivated descendant of the sea beet, Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima, and has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard can be used fresh in salads or cooked, and its bitterness fades with cooking, leaving a refined flavor which is more delicate than that of cooked spinach.\", \"Based on the given passages, chard is a type of beet. It has large, fleshy, and tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard can be grown with white, yellow, or red leaf stalks. The vegetable can be harvested either when the leaves are young and tender or after maturity when the leaves are larger with slightly tougher stems. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive (some think bitter) flavor. Young chard leaves and stalks can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked. Chard can be found in the same subspecies as beetroot (garden beet) and all other beet cultivars. It is an attractive ornamental that adds color to the vegetable garden. Chard is extremely perishable, so it should be stored unwashed in plastic bags in the crisper for 2 to 3 days.\"]", "time_sec": 28.625, "generation_time_seconds": 27.5505, "selection_time_seconds": 1.0569, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 521, 567, 374, 264, 15933, 88, 6176, 35481, 429, 374, 264, 4462, 315, 279, 48012, 2997, 13, 1084, 702, 3460, 11, 282, 642, 8503, 714, 27582, 5538, 6176, 10901, 323, 12045, 11, 41854, 54872, 82, 13, 910, 567, 374, 4428, 311, 67698, 714, 702, 264, 16245, 11, 803, 2060, 533, 320, 14689, 1744, 11, 25180, 8, 17172, 13, 29160, 3908, 521, 567, 646, 387, 1483, 7112, 304, 76752, 11, 1393, 14851, 521, 567, 10901, 323, 54872, 82, 525, 11136, 29105, 13, 910, 567, 374, 9016, 82117, 480, 11, 773, 432, 1265, 387, 9768, 304, 279, 44944, 369, 220, 17, 311, 220, 18, 2849, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, chard is a leafy green vegetable that is a member of the beet family. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor. Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked. Chard is extremely perishable, so it should be stored in the refrigerator for 2 to 3 days.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.5594073906221781, "g_worst_claim": 0.3723152279853821, "g_content": 1.9790309983856824, "g_traj_mean": 1.4226560592651367}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 521, 567, 374, 264, 943, 315, 15933, 88, 6176, 35481, 5435, 311, 387, 1415, 13, 1084, 374, 31871, 553, 1181, 3460, 11, 282, 642, 8503, 714, 27582, 5538, 6176, 10901, 323, 12045, 11, 41854, 54872, 82, 13, 910, 567, 374, 4428, 311, 67698, 714, 702, 264, 16245, 11, 803, 2060, 533, 320, 14689, 1744, 11, 25180, 8, 17172, 13, 29160, 3908, 521, 567, 646, 387, 1483, 7112, 304, 76752, 11, 1393, 14851, 521, 567, 10901, 323, 54872, 82, 525, 11136, 29105, 13, 910, 567, 646, 387, 66472, 1393, 279, 10901, 525, 3908, 323, 27582, 476, 1283, 47161, 979, 279, 54872, 82, 614, 10078, 48231, 43714, 13, 1084, 374, 3881, 369, 1181, 18879, 39445, 43086, 897, 11, 7842, 1894, 311, 279, 35481, 13551, 13, 910, 567, 374, 9016, 82117, 480, 323, 1265, 387, 9768, 15025, 13571, 304, 12188, 17899, 304, 279, 1560, 27370, 369, 220, 17, 311, 220, 18, 2849, 311, 10306, 98216, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, chard is a type of leafy green vegetable related to beets. It is characterized by its large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor. Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked. Chard can be harvested while the leaves are young and tender or after maturity when the stalks have slightly tougher stems. It is known for its attractive ornamental value, adding color to the vegetable garden. Chard is extremely perishable and should be stored unwashed in plastic bags in the crisper for 2 to 3 days to maintain freshness.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.535135977964528, "g_worst_claim": 0.29198544366019114, "g_content": 2.3283215084591427, "g_traj_mean": 1.5478336811065674}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 521, 567, 374, 264, 15933, 88, 6176, 35481, 429, 374, 4428, 311, 67698, 714, 448, 264, 16245, 11, 803, 2060, 533, 17172, 13, 1084, 374, 279, 66066, 75851, 315, 279, 9396, 48012, 11, 34243, 73160, 285, 1186, 2154, 13, 3594, 275, 7523, 11, 323, 702, 3460, 11, 282, 642, 8503, 714, 27582, 5538, 6176, 10901, 323, 12045, 11, 41854, 54872, 82, 13, 910, 567, 646, 387, 1483, 7722, 304, 76752, 476, 29105, 11, 323, 1181, 78996, 86409, 448, 17233, 11, 9380, 264, 37191, 17172, 892, 374, 803, 35201, 1091, 429, 315, 29105, 67698, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, chard is a leafy green vegetable that is similar to spinach but with a stronger, more assertive flavor. It is the cultivated descendant of the sea beet, Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima, and has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard can be used fresh in salads or cooked, and its bitterness fades with cooking, leaving a refined flavor which is more delicate than that of cooked spinach.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.6304221860808554, "g_worst_claim": 0.385466734568278, "g_content": 2.605234839699485, "g_traj_mean": 1.637321949005127}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 521, 567, 374, 264, 943, 315, 48012, 13, 1084, 702, 3460, 11, 282, 642, 8503, 11, 323, 27582, 5538, 6176, 10901, 323, 12045, 11, 41854, 54872, 82, 13, 910, 567, 646, 387, 14700, 448, 4158, 11, 13753, 11, 476, 2518, 15933, 54872, 82, 13, 576, 35481, 646, 387, 66472, 2987, 979, 279, 10901, 525, 3908, 323, 27582, 476, 1283, 47161, 979, 279, 10901, 525, 8131, 448, 10078, 48231, 43714, 13, 910, 567, 374, 4428, 311, 67698, 714, 702, 264, 16245, 11, 803, 2060, 533, 320, 14689, 1744, 25180, 8, 17172, 13, 13263, 521, 567, 10901, 323, 54872, 82, 646, 387, 1483, 7112, 304, 76752, 11, 1393, 14851, 521, 567, 10901, 323, 54872, 82, 525, 11136, 29105, 13, 910, 567, 646, 387, 1730, 304, 279, 1852, 5147, 88621, 438, 48012, 2888, 320, 70, 8341, 48012, 8, 323, 678, 1008, 48012, 26883, 1561, 13, 1084, 374, 458, 18879, 39445, 43086, 429, 11367, 1894, 311, 279, 35481, 13551, 13, 910, 567, 374, 9016, 82117, 480, 11, 773, 432, 1265, 387, 9768, 15025, 13571, 304, 12188, 17899, 304, 279, 1560, 27370, 369, 220, 17, 311, 220, 18, 2849, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, chard is a type of beet. It has large, fleshy, and tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard can be grown with white, yellow, or red leaf stalks. The vegetable can be harvested either when the leaves are young and tender or after maturity when the leaves are larger with slightly tougher stems. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive (some think bitter) flavor. Young chard leaves and stalks can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked. Chard can be found in the same subspecies as beetroot (garden beet) and all other beet cultivars. It is an attractive ornamental that adds color to the vegetable garden. Chard is extremely perishable, so it should be stored unwashed in plastic bags in the crisper for 2 to 3 days.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.414400703297324, "g_worst_claim": 0.3865390618642171, "g_content": 2.1833751005284925, "g_traj_mean": 1.4680862426757812}}], "selected_rollout": 2, "random_sel_rollout": 3, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 61.45, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.2, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "210", "question": "differences between elements compounds and mixtures", "gt": "Elements are the purest form of a chemical substance and are composed of only one type of atom, meaning all the atoms in an element have the same number of protons. Compounds, on the other hand, are substances composed of two or more different elements and are also pure substances. Mixtures are physical combinations of elements or compounds where the parts of the mixture are not chemically bonded and so retain their own identity. The individual components of a mixture can be physically separated from each other. Mixtures have variable compositions and show the properties of their constituent substances. Elements and compounds just mix together to form a mixture and no new compound is formed.", "pred": "1. Elements: Pure substances composed of only one type of atom, with all atoms having the same number of protons.\n2. Compounds: Substances composed of two or more different elements, where the elements are chemically bonded and retain their own identity.\n3. Mixtures: Physical combinations of elements or compounds where the parts are not chemically bonded and can be physically separated.\n4. Separation: Elements and compounds can be separated by physical means, whereas mixtures have a variable composition and show the properties of constituent substances.\n5. Atmosphere: An example of a mixture is the atmosphere, which contains various gases that can be physically separated.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "1. Elements: Pure substances composed of only one type of atom, with all atoms having the same number of protons.\n2. Compounds: Substances composed of two or more different elements, where the elements are chemically bonded and retain their own identity.\n3. Mixtures: Physical combinations of elements or compounds where the parts are not chemically bonded and can be physically separated.\n4. Separation: Elements and compounds can be separated by physical means, whereas mixtures have a variable composition and show the properties of constituent substances.\n5. Atmosphere: An example of a mixture is the atmosphere, which contains various gases that can be physically separated.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, the differences between elements, compounds, and mixtures are as follows:\\n\\n1. Elements are composed of only one type of atom and are the purest form of a chemical substance.\\n2. Compounds are substances composed of two or more different elements and are also pure substances. The elements in a compound are chemically bonded and cannot be separated by physical means.\\n3. Mixtures are a physical combination of elements or compounds where the parts of the mixture are not chemically bonded and can be separated by physical means. Mixtures can have a variable composition and show the properties of their constituent substances.\", \"Based on the given passages, there are several differences between elements, compounds, and mixtures:\\n\\n1. Elements are the purest form of a chemical substance and are composed of only one type of atom, meaning all the atoms in an element have the same number of protons.\\n\\n2. Compounds are substances made up of two or more different elements chemically bonded together. They are also pure substances, meaning they cannot be separated into different substances by physical means.\\n\\n3. Mixtures are a combination of elements or compounds where the parts retain their own identities and are not chemically bonded. Mixtures can be separated by physical means, such as filtering or distillation.\\n\\n4. Elements, compounds, and mixtures differ in how easily their components can be separated. Mixtures can be easily separated by physical methods, while compounds require chemical means to separate their constituent elements. Elements are the most difficult to separate as they are the purest form of substances.\", \"Based on the given passages, the differences between elements, compounds, and mixtures are as follows:\\n\\n1. Elements are composed of only one type of atom and are the purest form of a chemical substance.\\n2. Compounds are substances composed of two or more different elements and are pure substances. They can be chemically bonded and their properties are distinct from their constituent elements.\\n3. Mixtures are physical combinations of elements or compounds where the parts are not chemically bonded and retain their own identity. Mixtures can have variable compositions and show the properties of their constituent substances. They can be separated easily by physical means.\", \"Based on the given passages, here are the differences between elements, compounds, and mixtures:\\n\\n1. Elements: Pure substances composed of only one type of atom, with all atoms having the same number of protons.\\n2. Compounds: Substances composed of two or more different elements, where the elements are chemically bonded and retain their own identity.\\n3. Mixtures: Physical combinations of elements or compounds where the parts are not chemically bonded and can be physically separated.\\n4. Separation: Elements and compounds can be separated by physical means, whereas mixtures have a variable composition and show the properties of constituent substances.\\n5. Atmosphere: An example of a mixture is the atmosphere, which contains various gases that can be physically separated.\"]", "time_sec": 28.588, "generation_time_seconds": 27.6031, "selection_time_seconds": 0.9731, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 11799, 1948, 5424, 11, 31146, 11, 323, 6514, 18513, 525, 438, 11017, 1447, 16, 13, 34157, 525, 23415, 315, 1172, 825, 943, 315, 19124, 323, 525, 279, 10526, 267, 1352, 315, 264, 11483, 19699, 624, 17, 13, 13317, 3095, 525, 32055, 23415, 315, 1378, 476, 803, 2155, 5424, 323, 525, 1083, 1052