{"id": "213", "question": "differences between elements compounds and mixtures", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the main differences between elements, compounds, and mixtures are:\n\n* Elements are the purest form of a chemical substance and are composed of only one type of atom. They have a fixed composition and cannot be broken down into simpler substances by physical means.\n* Compounds, on the other hand, are substances composed of two or more different elements that are chemically bonded together. They have a fixed composition and are pure substances.\n* Mixtures are physical combinations of elements or compounds where the parts of the mixture are not chemically bonded and retain their own identity. They have a variable composition and can be separated by physical means.\n\nTherefore, the key differences between elements, compounds, and mixtures are their composition, bonding, and separability. Elements are homogeneous and have a fixed composition, compounds are heterogeneous and have a fixed composition, and mixtures are heterogeneous and have a variable composition. Additionally, elements and compounds are chemically bonded, while mixtures are not.", "pred": "3. Mixtures are a physical combination of elements or compounds where the parts of the mixture are not chemically bonded and retain their own identity. Mixtures can be separated by physical means, such as filtration or distillation.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "3. Mixtures are a physical combination of elements or compounds where the parts of the mixture are not chemically bonded and retain their own identity. Mixtures can be separated by physical means, such as filtration or distillation.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, the differences between elements, compounds, and mixtures are as follows:\\n\\n1. Elements are composed of only one type of atom and are the purest form of a chemical substance.\\n2. Compounds are substances composed of two or more different elements and are also pure substances. The elements in a compound are chemically bonded and cannot be separated by physical means.\\n3. Mixtures are a physical combination of elements or compounds where the parts of the mixture are not chemically bonded and can be separated by physical means. Mixtures can have a variable composition and show the properties of their constituent substances.\", \"Based on the given passages, the differences between elements, compounds, and mixtures are as follows:\\n\\n1. Elements:\\n   - Composed of only one type of atom\\n   - Have a fixed composition\\n   - Cannot be separated by chemical means\\n   - Are the simplest form of matter\\n\\n2. Compounds:\\n   - Composed of two or more different elements\\n   - A mixture of elements or compounds\\n   - The elements in a compound are chemically bonded and cannot be separated by physical means\\n   - Show the properties of constituent substances\\n   - Can be separated by physical means\\n\\n3. Mixtures:\\n   - Physical combination of elements or compounds\\n   - Components are not chemically bonded\\n   - Retain their own identity\\n   - Can be separated by physical methods\\n   - Have a variable composition\", \"Based on the given passages, the differences between elements, compounds, and mixtures are as follows:\\n\\n1. **Elements**:\\n   - Elements are the purest form of a chemical substance.\\n   - They are composed of only one type of atom.\\n   - All of the atoms in an element will have the same number of protons.\\n\\n2. **Compounds**:\\n   - Compounds are substances composed of two or more different elements.\\n   - Compounds are pure substances.\\n   - The elements in a compound are chemically bonded together.\\n   - These elements and compounds can be separated by physical means.\\n\\n3. **Mixtures**:\\n   - Mixtures contain two or more substances (elements or compounds) that are relatively easy to separate.\\n   - Mixtures have a variable composition.\\n   - The constituents of a mixture retain their own identity and can still show the properties of the constituent substances.\\n   - The constituents can be separated easily by physical methods.\\n\\nThese distinctions highlight the fundamental differences in how elements, compounds, and mixtures are defined and how they can be separated.\", \"Based on the given passages, the differences between elements, compounds, and mixtures are as follows:\\n\\n1. Elements are the purest form of a chemical substance and are composed of only one type of atom. All of the atoms in an element will have the same number of protons.\\n\\n2. Compounds are substances composed of two or more different elements. They are also pure substances. The elements in a compound are chemically bonded and thus cannot be easily separated by physical means.\\n\\n3. Mixtures are a physical combination of elements or compounds where the parts of the mixture are not chemically bonded and retain their own identity. Mixtures can be separated by physical means, such as filtration or distillation.\"]", "time_sec": 30.543, "generation_time_seconds": 29.8862, "selection_time_seconds": 0.6478, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 11799, 1948, 5424, 11, 31146, 11, 323, 6514, 18513, 525, 438, 11017, 1447, 16, 13, 34157, 525, 23415, 315, 1172, 825, 943, 315, 19124, 323, 525, 279, 10526, 267, 1352, 315, 264, 11483, 19699, 624, 17, 13, 13317, 3095, 525, 32055, 23415, 315, 1378, 476, 803, 2155, 5424, 323, 525, 1083, 10526, 32055, 13, 576, 5424, 304, 264, 23628, 525, 8424, 2673, 69141, 323, 4157, 387, 18663, 553, 6961, 3363, 624, 18, 13, 19219, 18513, 525, 264, 6961, 10601, 315, 5424, 476, 31146, 1380, 279, 5479, 315, 279, 20980, 525, 537, 8424, 2673, 69141, 323, 646, 387, 18663, 553, 6961, 3363, 13, 19219, 18513, 646, 614, 264, 3890, 18037, 323, 1473, 279, 5888, 315, 862, 74064, 32055, 13, 151645], "pred": "1. Elements are composed of only one type of atom and are the purest form of a chemical substance.\n2. Compounds are substances composed of two or more different elements and are also pure substances. The elements in a compound are chemically bonded and cannot be separated by physical means.\n3. Mixtures are a physical combination of elements or compounds where the parts of the mixture are not chemically bonded and can be separated by physical means. Mixtures can have a variable composition and show the properties of their constituent substances.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.86550107846037, "g_worst_claim": 0.14933252334594727, "g_content": 1.1652708053588867, "g_traj_mean": 0.8894843459129333}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 11799, 1948, 5424, 11, 31146, 11, 323, 6514, 18513, 525, 438, 11017, 1447, 16, 13, 34157, 510, 256, 481, 1198, 3865, 315, 1172, 825, 943, 315, 19124, 198, 256, 481, 12243, 264, 8356, 18037, 198, 256, 481, 34655, 387, 18663, 553, 11483, 3363, 198, 256, 481, 8713, 279, 44548, 1352, 315, 4925, 271, 17, 13, 13317, 3095, 510, 256, 481, 1198, 3865, 315, 1378, 476, 803, 2155, 5424, 198, 256, 481, 362, 20980, 315, 5424, 476, 31146, 198, 256, 481, 576, 5424, 304, 264, 23628, 525, 8424, 2673, 69141, 323, 4157, 387, 18663, 553, 6961, 3363, 198, 256, 481, 6928, 279, 5888, 315, 74064, 32055, 198, 256, 481, 2980, 387, 18663, 553, 6961, 3363, 271, 18, 13, 19219, 18513, 510, 256, 481, 27379, 10601, 315, 5424, 476, 31146, 198, 256, 481, 34085, 525, 537, 8424, 2673, 69141, 198, 256, 481, 10392, 466, 862, 1828, 9569, 198, 256, 481, 2980, 387, 18663, 553, 6961, 5413, 198, 256, 481, 12243, 264, 3890, 18037, 151645], "pred": "3. Mixtures:\n   - Physical combination of elements or compounds\n   - Components are not chemically bonded\n   - Retain their own identity\n   - Can be separated by physical methods\n   - Have a variable composition", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.754510748700824, "g_worst_claim": 0.10793028771877289, "g_content": 1.0017100407527044, "g_traj_mean": 0.5996181964874268}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 11799, 1948, 5424, 11, 31146, 11, 323, 6514, 18513, 525, 438, 11017, 1447, 16, 13, 3070, 11868, 334, 510, 256, 481, 34157, 525, 279, 10526, 267, 1352, 315, 264, 11483, 19699, 624, 256, 481, 2379, 525, 23415, 315, 1172, 825, 943, 315, 19124, 624, 256, 481, 2009, 315, 279, 32199, 304, 458, 2392, 686, 614, 279, 1852, 1372, 315, 462, 34411, 382, 17, 13, 3070, 13552, 3095, 334, 510, 256, 481, 13317, 3095, 525, 32055, 23415, 315, 1378, 476, 803, 2155, 5424, 624, 256, 481, 13317, 3095, 525, 10526, 32055, 624, 256, 481, 576, 5424, 304, 264, 23628, 525, 8424, 2673, 69141, 3786, 624, 256, 481, 4220, 5424, 323, 31146, 646, 387, 18663, 553, 6961, 3363, 382, 18, 13, 3070, 58083, 18513, 334, 510, 256, 481, 19219, 18513, 6644, 1378, 476, 803, 32055, 320, 21423, 476, 31146, 8, 429, 525, 12040, 4135, 311, 8651, 624, 256, 481, 19219, 18513, 614, 264, 3890, 18037, 624, 256, 481, 576, 50620, 315, 264, 20980, 14060, 862, 1828, 9569, 323, 646, 2058, 1473, 279, 5888, 315, 279, 74064, 32055, 624, 256, 481, 576, 50620, 646, 387, 18663, 6707, 553, 6961, 5413, 382, 9485, 86159, 11167, 279, 15811, 11799, 304, 1246, 5424, 11, 31146, 11, 323, 6514, 18513, 525, 4512, 323, 1246, 807, 646, 387, 18663, 13, 151645], "pred": "These distinctions highlight the fundamental differences in how elements, compounds, and mixtures are defined and how they can be separated.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.7309748961858968, "g_worst_claim": 0.16897475719451904, "g_content": 0.9979384286063058, "g_traj_mean": 0.6970234513282776}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 11799, 1948, 5424, 11, 31146, 11, 323, 6514, 18513, 525, 438, 11017, 1447, 16, 13, 34157, 525, 279, 10526, 267, 1352, 315, 264, 11483, 19699, 323, 525, 23415, 315, 1172, 825, 943, 315, 19124, 13, 2009, 315, 279, 32199, 304, 458, 2392, 686, 614, 279, 1852, 1372, 315, 462, 34411, 382, 17, 13, 13317, 3095, 525, 32055, 23415, 315, 1378, 476, 803, 2155, 5424, 13, 2379, 525, 1083, 10526, 32055, 13, 576, 5424, 304, 264, 23628, 525, 8424, 2673, 69141, 323, 8450, 4157, 387, 6707, 18663, 553, 6961, 3363, 382, 18, 13, 19219, 18513, 525, 264, 6961, 10601, 315, 5424, 476, 31146, 1380, 279, 5479, 315, 279, 20980, 525, 537, 8424, 2673, 69141, 323, 14060, 862, 1828, 9569, 13, 19219, 18513, 646, 387, 18663, 553, 6961, 3363, 11, 1741, 438, 74938, 476, 1582, 66084, 13, 151645], "pred": "3. Mixtures are a physical combination of elements or compounds where the parts of the mixture are not chemically bonded and retain their own identity. Mixtures can be separated by physical means, such as filtration or distillation.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.738770054486464, "g_worst_claim": -0.14480042457580566, "g_content": 0.8977776866848186, "g_traj_mean": 0.8054302930831909}}], "selected_rollout": 0, "random_sel_rollout": 3, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 30.53, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "214", "question": "how to wash wine glasses properly", "gt": "To properly wash wine glasses, you should scrub them by hand as it's the safest cleaning method. First, lay a towel in the sink before filling it with hot water. Wash one wine glass at a time to avoid damage. Use a small amount of detergent only where it's needed -- in the bottom of the glass and around the rim. To clean the inside of a glass, use a stemware brush with soft-foam bristles. If hot water and mild detergent can’t get your glasses clean, use baking soda to remove any stubborn wine residue. Dissolve a small amount of soda with hot water and let it stand in the glass for a few minutes. The soda will dissolve any residual wine adhering to the glass. Cradle the bowl of the glass in one hand while washing to protect the stem. After rinsing thoroughly with hot water, dry the wine glasses with a smooth lint-free cloth, such as a microfiber or flour sack towel.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, to wash wine glasses properly, follow these steps:\n1. Use a small amount of detergent and wash one wine glass at a time to avoid damage.\n2. Cradle the bowl of the glass in one hand while washing to protect the stem.\n3. Use a stemware brush with soft-foam bristles to clean the inside of the glass.\n4. Rinsing thoroughly with hot water and dry the wine glasses with a smooth lint-free cloth, such as a microfiber or flour sack towel.\n5. For stubborn wine residue, dissolve a small amount of baking soda in hot water and let it stand in the glass for a few minutes to remove any residual wine adhering to the glass.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, to wash wine glasses properly, follow these steps:\n1. Use a small amount of detergent and wash one wine glass at a time to avoid damage.\n2. Cradle the bowl of the glass in one hand while washing to protect the stem.\n3. Use a stemware brush with soft-foam bristles to clean the inside of the glass.\n4. Rinsing thoroughly with hot water and dry the wine glasses with a smooth lint-free cloth, such as a microfiber or flour sack towel.\n5. For stubborn wine residue, dissolve a small amount of baking soda in hot water and let it stand in the glass for a few minutes to remove any residual wine adhering to the glass.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, to wash wine glasses properly, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. Use a stemware brush with soft-foam bristles to clean the inside of the glass.\\n2. After rinsing thoroughly with hot water, dry the wine glasses with a smooth lint-free cloth, such as a microfiber or flour sack towel.\\n3. If hot water and mild detergent can't get your glasses clean, use baking soda to remove any stubborn wine residue. Dissolve a small amount of soda with hot water and let it stand in the glass for a few minutes. The soda will dissolve any residual wine adhering to the glass.\\n4. Clean your wine glasses by hand for the safest method. Lay a towel in the sink before filling with hot water. Wash one wine glass at a time to avoid damage.\\n5. Use a small amount of detergent and use it only where it's needed -- in the bottom of the glass and around the rim. Cradle the bowl of the glass in one hand while washing to protect the stem.\\n6. Avoid chips and spots by following these tips.\", \"Based on the given passages:\\n\\n1. Clean your wine glasses by hand for best results.\\n2. Use hot water and a mild detergent to clean the wine glasses.\\n3. Dissolve baking soda in hot water and let it stand in the glass for a few minutes to remove stubborn wine residue.\\n4. Use a stemware brush with soft-foam bristles to clean the inside of the glass.\\n5. Dry the wine glasses with a smooth lint-free cloth, such as a microfiber or flour sack towel.\\n6. Lay a towel in the sink before filling with hot water and wash one wine glass at a time to avoid damage.\\n7. Use a small amount of detergent only where it's needed, in the bottom of the glass and around the rim.\", \"Based on the given passages, to wash wine glasses properly, you should:\\n\\n1. Lay a towel in the sink before filling with hot water.\\n2. Wash one wine glass at a time to avoid damage.\\n3. Use a small amount of detergent only where it's needed -- in the bottom of the glass and around the rim.\\n4. Cradle the bowl of the glass in one hand while washing to protect the stem.\\n5. Use a stemware brush with soft-foam bristles to clean the inside of the glass.\\n6. Rinse thoroughly with hot water.\\n7. Dry the wine glasses with a smooth lint-free cloth, such as a microfiber or flour sack towel.\\n8. For stubborn wine residue, dissolve baking soda with hot water and let it stand in the glass for a few minutes to remove the residue.\", \"Based on the given passages, to wash wine glasses properly, follow these steps:\\n1. Use a small amount of detergent and wash one wine glass at a time to avoid damage.\\n2. Cradle the bowl of the glass in one hand while washing to protect the stem.\\n3. Use a stemware brush with soft-foam bristles to clean the inside of the glass.\\n4. Rinsing thoroughly with hot water and dry the wine glasses with a smooth lint-free cloth, such as a microfiber or flour sack towel.\\n5. For stubborn wine residue, dissolve a small amount of baking soda in hot water and let it stand in the glass for a few minutes to remove any residual wine adhering to the glass.\"]", "time_sec": 33.781, "generation_time_seconds": 32.6824, "selection_time_seconds": 1.0881, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 311, 11369, 13078, 28147, 10277, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 5443, 264, 19101, 1664, 15625, 448, 8413, 12, 824, 309, 1411, 65943, 311, 4240, 279, 4766, 315, 279, 8991, 624, 17, 13, 4636, 96039, 287, 26364, 448, 4017, 3015, 11, 9058, 279, 13078, 28147, 448, 264, 10876, 57920, 12577, 27292, 11, 1741, 438, 264, 8003, 82945, 476, 19828, 52333, 42613, 624, 18, 13, 1416, 4017, 3015, 323, 23034, 90134, 646, 944, 633, 697, 28147, 4240, 11, 990, 27815, 38862, 311, 4057, 894, 51396, 13078, 48132, 13, 51032, 3948, 264, 2613, 3311, 315, 38862, 448, 4017, 3015, 323, 1077, 432, 2498, 304, 279, 8991, 369, 264, 2421, 4420, 13, 576, 38862, 686, 72639, 894, 32147, 13078, 34951, 287, 311, 279, 8991, 624, 19, 13, 9590, 697, 13078, 28147, 553, 1424, 369, 279, 61744, 1714, 13, 43924, 264, 42613, 304, 279, 19309, 1573, 21274, 448, 4017, 3015, 13, 5985, 825, 13078, 8991, 518, 264, 882, 311, 5648, 5557, 624, 20, 13, 5443, 264, 2613, 3311, 315, 90134, 323, 990, 432, 1172, 1380, 432, 594, 4362, 1177, 304, 279, 5622, 315, 279, 8991, 323, 2163, 279, 30130, 13, 4553, 71355, 279, 19212, 315, 279, 8991, 304, 825, 1424, 1393, 27686, 311, 6016, 279, 19101, 624, 21, 13, 34006, 23595, 323, 18773, 553, 2701, 1493, 10414, 13, 151645], "pred": "1. Use a stemware brush with soft-foam bristles to clean the inside of the glass.\n2. After rinsing thoroughly with hot water, dry the wine glasses with a smooth lint-free cloth, such as a microfiber or flour sack towel.\n3. If hot water and mild detergent can't get your glasses clean, use baking soda to remove any stubborn wine residue. Dissolve a small amount of soda with hot water and let it stand in the glass for a few minutes. The soda will dissolve any residual wine adhering to the glass.\n4. Clean your wine glasses by hand for the safest method. Lay a towel in the sink before filling with hot water. Wash one wine glass at a time to avoid damage.\n5. Use a small amount of detergent and use it only where it's needed -- in the bottom of the glass and around the rim. Cradle the bowl of the glass in one hand while washing to protect the stem.\n6. Avoid chips and spots by following these tips.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.955400226113433, "g_worst_claim": 0.13161726792653403, "g_content": 2.4250707452947444, "g_traj_mean": 1.948915719985962}}