ity loss leads to weakened bones, making them susceptible to compression fractures, even without a fall or injury.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.2449871009834212, "g_worst_claim": 0.3974179540361677, "g_content": 1.702504877458539, "g_traj_mean": 1.152290940284729}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [1514, 19289, 51268, 88666, 10704, 374, 31871, 553, 15386, 17240, 5557, 429, 374, 21214, 2030, 13176, 480, 3080, 13495, 3635, 9981, 13, 576, 13495, 315, 15386, 51268, 88666, 10704, 2924, 1182, 6646, 11, 4709, 315, 2608, 916, 882, 448, 264, 42032, 16367, 47278, 11, 77232, 315, 279, 5198, 50213, 68, 11, 85778, 11, 42423, 11, 476, 1008, 24854, 11, 323, 264, 1550, 28636, 315, 77232, 11, 5310, 304, 279, 34676, 476, 42423, 13, 17147, 6380, 11, 1493, 77232, 646, 12170, 1496, 2041, 264, 4399, 476, 10895, 11, 438, 279, 24854, 3635, 773, 7469, 429, 807, 1191, 311, 24611, 13, 151645], "pred": "Severe osteoporosis is characterized by severe bone damage that is virtually undetectable until symptoms become apparent. The symptoms of severe osteoporosis include back pain, loss of height over time with a stooped posture, fractures of the vertebrae, wrists, hips, or other bones, and a high likelihood of fractures, especially in the spine or hips. Without treatment, these fractures can occur even without a fall or injury, as the bones become so weak that they start to compress.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.2638485107128294, "g_worst_claim": 0.3656576330011541, "g_content": 1.8770304140837297, "g_traj_mean": 1.2727605104446411}}], "selected_rollout": 1, "random_sel_rollout": 0, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 41.07, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "283", "question": "difference between an adverb clause and an adjective clause", "gt": "Based on the given passages, an adverb clause modifies a verb, adjective or another adverb, while an adjective clause modifies a noun or pronoun.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, the difference between an adverb clause and an adjective clause is that an adverb clause modifies a verb, adjective, or another adverb, usually the verb, and is introduced by subordinate conjunctions such as after, although, as, as if, before, because, if, since, so that, than, though, unless, until, when, where, and while. An adjective clause, on the other hand, modifies a noun or a pronoun and begins with a relative pronoun (who, whose, whom, which, and that) or a subordinate conjunction (when and where).", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the difference between an adverb clause and an adjective clause is that an adverb clause modifies a verb, adjective, or another adverb, usually the verb, and is introduced by subordinate conjunctions such as after, although, as, as if, before, because, if, since, so that, than, though, unless, until, when, where, and while. An adjective clause, on the other hand, modifies a noun or a pronoun and begins with a relative pronoun (who, whose, whom, which, and that) or a subordinate conjunction (when and where).", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, the difference between an adverb clause and an adjective clause is that an adverb clause modifies a verb, adjective, or another adverb, usually the verb, and is introduced by subordinate conjunctions such as after, although, as, as if, before, because, if, since, so that, than, though, unless, until, when, where, and while. An adjective clause, on the other hand, modifies a noun or a pronoun and begins with a relative pronoun (who, whose, whom, which, and that) or a subordinate conjunction (when and where).\", \"Based on the given passages, an adverb clause is a dependent clause that modifies a verb, adjective, or adverb, usually the verb, and it is introduced by a subordinate conjunction such as after, although, as, as if, before, because, if, since, so that, than, though, unless, until, when, where, or while. An adjective clause, on the other hand, is a dependent clause that modifies a noun or a pronoun; it begins with a relative pronoun (who, whose, whom, which, that) or a subordinate conjunction (when, where).\", \"Based on the given passages, an adverb clause is a dependent clause that modifies a verb, adjective, or another adverb. Adverb clauses are introduced by subordinate conjunctions such as after, although, as, as if, before, because, if, since, so that, than, though, unless, until, when, where, and while. An adjective clause, on the other hand, is used to modify a noun or a pronoun. It begins with a relative pronoun (who, whose, whom, which, that) or a subordinate conjunction (when and where). The main difference is that adverb clauses modify verbs, adjectives, or other adverbs, while adjective clauses modify nouns or pronouns.\", \"Based on the given passages, the difference between an adverb clause and an adjective clause is as follows:\\n\\n1. Function: An adverb clause modifies a verb, adjective, or another adverb, typically the verb. An adjective clause modifies a noun or a pronoun.\\n\\n2. Subordination: Both adverb clauses and adjective clauses are dependent clauses, meaning they cannot stand alone as sentences but are used to provide additional information.\\n\\n3. Introduction: Adverb clauses are introduced by subordinate conjunctions such as after, although, as, as if, before, because, if, since, so that, than, though, unless, until, when, where, and while. Adjective clauses are introduced by relative pronouns (who, whose, whom, which, that) or certain subordinate conjunctions (when and where).\\n\\n4. Purpose: Adverb clauses serve to modify verbs, adjectives, or other adverbs, providing information about time, place, manner, reason, condition, or comparison. Adjective clauses, on the other hand, provide information about the noun or pronoun they modify, such as describing its qualities, attributes, or relationships.\"]", "time_sec": 28.551, "generation_time_seconds": 27.7893, "selection_time_seconds": 0.7522, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 6672, 1948, 458, 993, 22328, 21647, 323, 458, 84492, 21647, 374, 429, 458, 993, 22328, 21647, 82949, 264, 18607, 11, 84492, 11, 476, 2441, 993, 22328, 11, 5990, 279, 18607, 11, 323, 374, 11523, 553, 78163, 31446, 82, 1741, 438, 1283, 11, 7892, 11, 438, 11, 438, 421, 11, 1573, 11, 1576, 11, 421, 11, 2474, 11, 773, 429, 11, 1091, 11, 3498, 11, 7241, 11, 3080, 11, 979, 11, 1380, 11, 323, 1393, 13, 1527, 84492, 21647, 11, 389, 279, 1008, 1424, 11, 82949, 264, 36921, 476, 264, 18613, 1624, 323, 12033, 448, 264, 8674, 18613, 1624, 320, 14623, 11, 6693, 11, 8711, 11, 892, 11, 323, 429, 8, 476, 264, 78163, 31446, 320, 9309, 323, 1380, 568, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, the difference between an adverb clause and an adjective clause is that an adverb clause modifies a verb, adjective, or another adverb, usually the verb, and is introduced by subordinate conjunctions such as after, although, as, as if, before, because, if, since, so that, than, though, unless, until, when, where, and while. An adjective clause, on the other hand, modifies a noun or a pronoun and begins with a relative pronoun (who, whose, whom, which, and that) or a subordinate conjunction (when and where).", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.345021374651299, "g_worst_claim": 0.27933817818051293, "g_content": 1.3605731419154576, "g_traj_mean": 0.8879000544548035}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 458, 993, 22328, 21647, 374, 264, 17749, 21647, 429, 82949, 264, 18607, 11, 84492, 11, 476, 993, 22328, 11, 5990, 279, 18607, 11, 323, 432, 374, 11523, 553, 264, 78163, 31446, 1741, 438, 1283, 11, 7892, 11, 438, 11, 438, 421, 11, 1573, 11, 1576, 11, 421, 11, 2474, 11, 773, 429, 11, 1091, 11, 3498, 11, 7241, 11, 3080, 11, 979, 11, 1380, 11, 476, 1393, 13, 1527, 84492, 21647, 11, 389, 279, 1008, 1424, 11, 374, 264, 17749, 21647, 429, 82949, 264, 36921, 476, 264, 18613, 1624, 26, 432, 12033, 448, 264, 8674, 18613, 1624, 320, 14623, 11, 6693, 11, 8711, 11, 892, 11, 429, 8, 476, 264, 78163, 31446, 320, 9309, 11, 1380, 568, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, an adverb clause is a dependent clause that modifies a verb, adjective, or adverb, usually the verb, and it is introduced by a subordinate conjunction such as after, although, as, as if, before, because, if, since, so that, than, though, unless, until, when, where, or while. An adjective clause, on the other hand, is a dependent clause that modifies a noun or a pronoun; it begins with a relative pronoun (who, whose, whom, which, that) or a subordinate conjunction (when, where).", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.0414403119339393, "g_worst_claim": 0.20074671965378982, "g_content": 1.1121351578656364, "g_traj_mean": 0.7786881923675537}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 458, 993, 22328, 21647, 374, 264, 17749, 21647, 429, 82949, 264, 18607, 11, 84492, 11, 476, 2441, 993, 22328, 13, 2410, 22328, 49098, 525, 11523, 553, 78163, 31446, 82, 1741, 438, 1283, 11, 7892, 11, 438, 11, 438, 421, 11, 1573, 11, 1576, 11, 421, 11, 2474, 11, 773, 429, 11, 1091, 11, 3498, 11, 7241, 11, 3080, 11, 979, 11, 1380, 11, 323, 1393, 13, 1527, 84492, 21647, 11, 389, 279, 1008, 1424, 11, 374, 1483, 311, 5602, 264, 36921, 476, 264, 18613, 1624, 13, 1084, 12033, 448, 264, 8674, 18613, 1624, 320, 14623, 11, 6693, 11, 8711, 11, 892, 11, 429, 8, 476, 264, 78163, 31446, 320, 9309, 323, 1380, 568, 576, 1887, 6672, 374, 429, 993, 22328, 49098, 5602, 61846, 11, 993, 86578, 11, 476, 1008, 993, 54939, 11, 1393, 84492, 49098, 5602, 89838, 476, 18613, 58986, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, an adverb clause is a dependent clause that modifies a verb, adjective, or another adverb. Adverb clauses are introduced by subordinate conjunctions such as after, although, as, as if, before, because, if, since, so that, than, though, unless, until, when, where, and while. An adjective clause, on the other hand, is used to modify a noun or a pronoun. It begins with a relative pronoun (who, whose, whom, which, that) or a subordinate conjunction (when and where). The main difference is that adverb clauses modify verbs, adjectives, or other adverbs, while adjective clauses modify nouns or pronouns.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.6011858541354104, "g_worst_claim": 0.00027450339661704167, "g_content": 0.8954329283341117, "g_traj_mean": 0.6308003067970276}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 6672, 1948, 458, 993, 22328, 21647, 323, 458, 84492, 21647, 374, 438, 11017, 1447, 16, 13, 5712, 25, 1527, 993, 22328, 21647, 82949, 264, 18607, 11, 84492, 11, 476, 2441, 993, 22328, 11, 11136, 279, 18607, 13, 1527, 84492, 21647, 82949, 264, 36921, 476, 264, 18613, 1624, 382, 17, 13, 3719, 98244, 25, 11733, 993, 22328, 49098, 323, 84492, 49098, 525, 17749, 49098, 11, 7290, 807, 4157, 2498, 7484, 438, 22870, 714, 525, 1483, 311, 3410, 5107, 1995, 382, 18, 13, 28338, 25, 2410, 22328, 49098, 525, 11523, 553, 78163, 31446, 82, 1741, 438, 1283, 11, 7892, 11, 438, 11, 438, 421, 11, 1573, 11, 1576, 11, 421, 11, 2474, 11, 773, 429, 11, 1091, 11, 3498, 11, 7241, 11, 3080, 11, 979, 11, 1380, 11, 323, 1393, 13, 2410, 50491, 49098, 525, 11523, 553, 8674, 18613, 58986, 320, 14623, 11, 6693, 11, 8711, 11, 892, 11, 429, 8, 476, 3654, 78163, 31446, 82, 320, 9309, 323, 1380, 3593, 19, 13, 29045, 25, 2410, 22328, 49098, 8683, 311, 5602, 61846, 11, 993, 86578, 11, 476, 1008, 993, 54939, 11, 8241, 1995, 911, 882, 11, 1992, 11, 11566, 11, 2874, 11, 2971, 11, 476, 12313, 13, 2410, 50491, 49098, 11, 389, 279, 1008, 1424, 11, 3410, 1995, 911, 279, 36921, 476, 18613, 1624, 807, 5602, 11, 1741, 438, 22692, 1181, 28500, 11, 8201, 11, 476, 11871, 13, 151645], "pred": "4. Purpose: Adverb clauses serve to modify verbs, adjectives, or other adverbs, providing information about time, place, manner, reason, condition, or comparison. Adjective clauses, on the other hand, provide information about the noun or pronoun they modify, such as describing its qualities, attributes, or relationships.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.5731416492716745, "g_worst_claim": -0.15727504662105016, "g_content": 0.582672119140625, "g_traj_mean": 0.45703834295272827}}], "selected_rollout": 0, "random_sel_rollout": 0, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 48.39, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "286", "question": "how to cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven", "gt": "To cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven, start by preheating the oven to 325-350 degrees Fahrenheit. If you don't have time to defrost the roast, put it in the oven at 250 F for about an hour. Afterwards, take it out and season it with salt, pepper, and any seasonings you prefer, such as thyme, oregano or garlic. Rub the surface with 2 tsp. of olive oil. Place the roast in a roasting pan with the fat of the pork loin facing upwards, and put it back in the oven for about 1.5 to 3 hours depending on its size, or about 20-30 minutes per pound. Make sure the roast is cooked thoroughly by using a meat thermometer; the internal temperature should reach 150 degrees Fahrenheit. Once it is cooked, cover the roast with aluminum foil and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.", "pred": "1. Preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit.\n2. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack with the fat of the pork loin facing upwards.\n3. Cook the pork loin for about 20-30 minutes per pound.\n4. Insert a meat thermometer and test the meat after cooking.\n5. When the internal temperature of the meat reaches 150 degrees Fahrenheit, the meat is ready to be taken out of the oven.\n6. Cover the roast with aluminum foil and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "1. Preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit.\n2. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack with the fat of the pork loin facing upwards.\n3. Cook the pork loin for about 20-30 minutes per pound.\n4. Insert a meat thermometer and test the meat after cooking.\n5. When the internal temperature of the meat reaches 150 degrees Fahrenheit, the meat is ready to be taken out of the oven.\n6. Cover the roast with aluminum foil and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, to cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. Preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit.\\n2. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack with the fat of the pork loin facing upwards.\\n3. Cook the pork loin for about 20-30 minutes per pound.\\n4. Insert a meat thermometer and test the meat after cooking.\\n5. When the internal temperature of the meat reaches 150 degrees Fahrenheit, the meat is ready to be taken out of the oven.\\n6. Cover the roast with aluminum foil and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.\", \"Based on the given passages, to cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. Preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit.\\n2. Place the frozen pork loin roast in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack with the fat of the pork loin facing upwards.\\n3. Cook the pork loin for about 20-30 minutes per pound. \\n4. Insert a meat thermometer and test the meat after cooking.\\n5. When the internal temperature of the meat reaches 150 degrees Fahrenheit, the meat is ready to be taken out of the oven.\\n6. Cover the roast with aluminum foil and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.\", \"Based on the given passages, to cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven, you should preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack with the fat of the pork loin facing upwards. Cook the pork loin for about 20-30 minutes per pound. Insert a meat thermometer, and test the meat after cooking. When the internal temperature of the meat reaches 150 degrees Fahrenheit, the meat is ready to be taken out of the oven. Cover the roast with aluminum foil, and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.\", \"Based on the given passages, to cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven, follow these steps:\\n1. Preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit.\\n2. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack with the fat of the pork loin facing upwards.\\n3. Cook the pork loin for about 20-30 minutes per pound.\\n4. Insert a meat thermometer and test the meat after cooking.\\n5. When the internal temperature of the meat reaches 150 degrees Fahrenheit, the pork loin is ready to be taken out of the oven.\\n6. Cover the roast with aluminum foil and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.\"]", "time_sec": 29.568, "generation_time_seconds": 28.5412, "selection_time_seconds": 1.0172, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 311, 4296, 264, 19690, 35067, 76960, 57685, 304, 279, 23387, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 4968, 19963, 279, 23387, 311, 220, 18, 20, 15, 12348, 68723, 624, 17, 13, 10771, 279, 19690, 35067, 76960, 304, 264, 25600, 27815, 7215, 389, 1909, 315, 264, 926, 14725, 29659, 448, 279, 8664, 315, 279, 35067, 76960, 12880, 43954, 624, 18, 13, 12514, 279, 35067, 76960, 369, 911, 220, 17, 15, 12, 18, 15, 4420, 817, 30023, 624, 19, 13, 17101, 264, 13041, 92197, 323, 1273, 279, 13041, 1283, 17233, 624, 20, 13, 3197, 279, 5306, 9315, 315, 279, 13041, 24491, 220, 16, 20, 15, 12348, 68723, 11, 279, 13041, 374, 5527, 311, 387, 4429, 700, 315, 279, 23387, 624, 21, 13, 17757, 279, 57685, 448, 24649, 46399, 323, 1077, 432, 2444, 369, 220, 18, 15, 4420, 1573, 13480, 13, 151645], "pred": "1. Preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit.\n2. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack with the fat of the pork loin facing upwards.\n3. Cook the pork loin for about 20-30 minutes per pound.\n4. Insert a meat thermometer and test the meat after cooking.\n5. When the internal temperature of the meat reaches 150 degrees Fahrenheit, the meat is ready to be taken out of the oven.\n6. Cover the roast with aluminum foil and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim"