685, 448, 24649, 46399, 323, 1077, 432, 2444, 369, 220, 18, 15, 4420, 1573, 13480, 13, 151645], "pred": "1. Preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit.\n2. Place the frozen pork loin roast in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack, with the fat of the pork loin facing upwards.\n3. Cook the pork loin for about 20-30 minutes per pound.\n4. Insert a meat thermometer and test the meat after cooking.\n5. When the internal temperature of the meat reaches 150 degrees Fahrenheit, the meat is ready to be taken out of the oven.\n6. Cover the roast with aluminum foil and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.0247322607501894, "g_worst_claim": 0.10665797193845113, "g_content": 1.0386740279524294, "g_traj_mean": 1.0144742727279663}}], "selected_rollout": 1, "random_sel_rollout": 1, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 72.5, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "294", "question": "what is the proper way to fertilize your grass", "gt": "To properly fertilize your grass, you should fertilize at the beginning of the growing season for your specific grass type. For warm season grasses, this would be when the grass turns green in the early spring. You should also know the type of fertilizer to use based on the type of grass you have and do a pH soil test to ensure the fertilizer has the appropriate pH for your soil. Once you've determined the correct fertilizer and timing, apply it in a perpendicular pattern and check the lawn after application by stepping on the turf. If the leaf blade bounces back up, the lawn is in good shape, but if it lays flat, you've over-fertilized.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, the proper way to fertilize your grass involves the following steps:\n1. Timing: Fertilize at the beginning of the growing season, which is different for different types of grasses. For warm season grasses, fertilize when the grass begins to turn green at the beginning of spring.\n2. Type of grass: Choose a fertilizer appropriate for the type of grass you have.\n3. Soil pH: Perform a pH soil test to determine the alkalinity or acidity of the soil and adjust the fertilizer accordingly.\n4. Lawn size: Measure your lawn to determine the amount of fertilizer needed.\n5. Application: Apply the fertilizer in a perpendicular pattern and check if the leaf blade bounces back up or lays flat. If the leaf blade lays flat, you have over-fertilized.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the proper way to fertilize your grass involves the following steps:\n1. Timing: Fertilize at the beginning of the growing season, which is different for different types of grasses. For warm season grasses, fertilize when the grass begins to turn green at the beginning of spring.\n2. Type of grass: Choose a fertilizer appropriate for the type of grass you have.\n3. Soil pH: Perform a pH soil test to determine the alkalinity or acidity of the soil and adjust the fertilizer accordingly.\n4. Lawn size: Measure your lawn to determine the amount of fertilizer needed.\n5. Application: Apply the fertilizer in a perpendicular pattern and check if the leaf blade bounces back up or lays flat. If the leaf blade lays flat, you have over-fertilized.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, the proper way to fertilize your grass involves several steps:\\n\\n1. Timing: Fertilize at the right time of year, specifically at the beginning of the growing season for warm season grasses when the grass begins to turn green at the beginning of spring.\\n2. Type of grass: Know your grass type to determine the appropriate fertilizer and frequency of application.\\n3. Soil pH: Conduct a pH soil test to measure the alkalinity or acidity of the soil, which helps in determining the appropriate pH for your fertilizer.\\n4. Lawn size: Measure your lawn to ensure you have the right amount of fertilizer.\\n5. Application: Pour in the rest of the fertilizer in a perpendicular pattern.\\n6. Check: Step on the turf to check if the leaf blade bounces back up, indicating that the lawn is in good shape. If it lays flat, you have over-fertilized.\", \"Based on the given passages, the proper way to fertilize your grass involves the following steps:\\n1. Timing: Fertilize at the beginning of the growing season, which is different for different types of grasses. For warm season grasses, fertilize when the grass begins to turn green at the beginning of spring.\\n2. Type of grass: Choose a fertilizer appropriate for the type of grass you have.\\n3. Soil pH: Perform a pH soil test to determine the alkalinity or acidity of the soil and adjust the fertilizer accordingly.\\n4. Lawn size: Measure your lawn to determine the amount of fertilizer needed.\\n5. Application: Apply the fertilizer in a perpendicular pattern and check if the leaf blade bounces back up or lays flat. If the leaf blade lays flat, you have over-fertilized.\", \"Based on the given passages, the proper way to fertilize your grass involves several steps: first, fertilize at the right time of year, such as at the beginning of the growing season for warm season grasses when they turn green at the beginning of spring. Second, know the type of grass you have, as it will determine the type and frequency of fertilizer needed. Third, perform a pH soil test to measure the alkalinity or acidity of the soil, and use a fertilizer with the appropriate pH. Finally, apply the rest of the fertilizer in a perpendicular pattern and check that the leaf blades bounce back up when stepped on; if they lay flat, you have over-fertilized.\", \"Based on the given passages, the proper way to fertilize your grass involves several steps:\\n\\n1. Choose the right time of year: Fertilize at the beginning of the growing season for warm season grasses when the grass begins to turn green at the beginning of spring.\\n2. Know your grass type: The type of grass you have will determine the type of fertilizer needed and how often it should be applied.\\n3. Do a pH soil test: This measures the alkalinity or acidity of the soil and helps to determine the pH level your fertilizer should have.\\n4. Measure your lawn: This ensures you have the correct amount of fertilizer for your lawn size.\\n5. Apply the fertilizer: Pour the rest of the fertilizer in a perpendicular pattern.\\n6. Check the soil response: Step on the turf. If the leaf blade bounces back up, your lawn is in good shape. If it lays flat, you've over-fertilized.\"]", "time_sec": 31.69, "generation_time_seconds": 30.6628, "selection_time_seconds": 1.0152, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 6169, 1616, 311, 35114, 551, 697, 16359, 17601, 3807, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 70813, 25, 434, 85695, 551, 518, 279, 1290, 882, 31