{"id": "359", "question": "What’s the difference between ad hominem fallacy and the poisoning the well fallacy?", "gt": "Based on the given passages, an ad hominem fallacy is an attack on an argument made by attacking the character, motive, or other attribute of the person making the argument, rather than attacking the argument directly. On the other hand, poisoning the well fallacy is an ad hominem attack that takes place before the merits of a case can be considered and redirects a discussion to the faults of one of the parties. In summary, both types of attacks involve attacking the person making the argument, but poisoning the well fallacy specifically takes place before the merits of a case are considered.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, the difference between ad hominem fallacy and the poisoning the well fallacy is that the ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument directly, whereas the poisoning the well fallacy involves attacking the person's integrity or intelligence before the merits of the argument can be discussed.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the difference between ad hominem fallacy and the poisoning the well fallacy is that the ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument directly, whereas the poisoning the well fallacy involves attacking the person's integrity or intelligence before the merits of the argument can be discussed.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, the ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument rather than attacking the argument itself. The poisoning the well fallacy is an ad hominem attack on a person's integrity or intelligence that takes place before the merits of a case can be considered, redirecting the discussion to the faults of one of the parties. The key difference is that the ad hominem fallacy occurs during the argument, while the poisoning the well fallacy happens before the argument begins.\", \"Based on the given passages, the difference between the ad hominem fallacy and the poisoning the well fallacy is that the ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making an argument rather than addressing the argument itself directly, whereas the poisoning the well fallacy involves attacking the integrity or intelligence of a person before the merits of their argument are considered, redirecting the discussion to the faults of one of the parties.\", \"Based on the given passages, the ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making an argument rather than addressing the argument itself. It is a form of attacking the person to invalidate their argument, which is considered a logical fallacy.\\n\\nThe poisoning the well fallacy, on the other hand, is an ad hominem attack that targets a person’s integrity or intelligence before the merits of a case can be considered. This fallacy redirects a discussion to the faults of one of the parties, thereby influencing the audience's perception of the argument without actually addressing its content.\\n\\nIn summary, the ad hominem fallacy is an attack on the person, while the poisoning the well fallacy is an attack on the person's character or intelligence before the argument is even presented, both aiming to undermine the argument without direct engagement with its content.\", \"Based on the given passages, the difference between ad hominem fallacy and the poisoning the well fallacy is that the ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument directly, whereas the poisoning the well fallacy involves attacking the person's integrity or intelligence before the merits of the argument can be discussed.\"]", "time_sec": 20.202, "generation_time_seconds": 19.2534, "selection_time_seconds": 0.9413, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 17601, 22818, 279, 3668, 11, 45994, 11, 476, 1008, 8201, 315, 279, 1697, 3259, 279, 5693, 4751, 1091, 22818, 279, 5693, 5086, 13, 576, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 374, 458, 993, 4998, 68413, 3359, 389, 264, 1697, 594, 16614, 476, 11229, 429, 4990, 1992, 1573, 279, 49832, 315, 264, 1142, 646, 387, 6509, 11, 6423, 287, 279, 10219, 311, 279, 56690, 315, 825, 315, 279, 9677, 13, 576, 1376, 6672, 374, 429, 279, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 13666, 2337, 279, 5693, 11, 1393, 279, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 8573, 1573, 279, 5693, 12033, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, the ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument rather than attacking the argument itself. The poisoning the well fallacy is an ad hominem attack on a person's integrity or intelligence that takes place before the merits of a case can be considered, redirecting the discussion to the faults of one of the parties. The key difference is that the ad hominem fallacy occurs during the argument, while the poisoning the well fallacy happens before the argument begins.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.985796021494197, "g_worst_claim": 0.05251613947061392, "g_content": 1.8240805053710938, "g_traj_mean": 1.1366997957229614}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 6672, 1948, 279, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 323, 279, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 374, 429, 279, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 17601, 22818, 279, 3668, 11, 45994, 11, 476, 1008, 8201, 315, 279, 1697, 3259, 458, 5693, 4751, 1091, 27020, 279, 5693, 5086, 5961, 11, 19853, 279, 51136, 279, 1632, 439