soning the well fallacy is an attack on the person's character or intelligence before the argument is even presented, both aiming to undermine the argument without direct engagement with its content.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.7125924949994366, "g_worst_claim": -0.15118660376622126, "g_content": 1.2658335367838542, "g_traj_mean": 0.7909500002861023}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 6672, 1948, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 323, 279, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 374, 429, 279, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 17601, 22818, 279, 3668, 11, 45994, 11, 476, 1008, 8201, 315, 279, 1697, 3259, 279, 5693, 5961, 11, 19853, 279, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 17601, 22818, 279, 1697, 594, 16614, 476, 11229, 1573, 279, 49832, 315, 279, 5693, 646, 387, 14078, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, the difference between ad hominem fallacy and the poisoning the well fallacy is that the ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument directly, whereas the poisoning the well fallacy involves attacking the person's integrity or intelligence before the merits of the argument can be discussed.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.8022981196306485, "g_worst_claim": 0.26315685113271076, "g_content": 1.3304593767438615, "g_traj_mean": 0.8180719017982483}}], "selected_rollout": 0, "random_sel_rollout": 3, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 56.57, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "360", "question": "What’s the difference between ad hominem fallacy and the poisoning the well fallacy?", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is the difference between ad hominem fallacy and poisoning the well fallacy:\n\nAd hominem fallacy refers to attacking an argument by attacking the character, motive, or other attribute of the person making the argument, rather than attacking the argument directly. Poisoning the well fallacy, on the other hand, is a specific type of ad hominem attack that occurs before the merits of a case can be considered, and it redirects the discussion to the faults of one of the parties.\n\nIn other words, ad hominem fallacy is a broader term that encompasses various types of attacks on the person making the argument, while poisoning the well fallacy is a specific type of ad hominem attack that focuses on the person's integrity or intelligence.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, the ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument rather than attacking the argument itself. The poisoning the well fallacy, on the other hand, is an ad hominem attack on a person's integrity or intelligence that takes place before the merits of a case can be considered, redirecting the discussion to the faults of one of the parties.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument rather than attacking the argument itself. The poisoning the well fallacy, on the other hand, is an ad hominem attack on a person's integrity or intelligence that takes place before the merits of a case can be considered, redirecting the discussion to the faults of one of the parties.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, the ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument rather than attacking the argument itself. The poisoning the well fallacy is an ad hominem attack on a person's integrity or intelligence that takes place before the merits of a case can be considered, redirecting the discussion to the faults of one of the parties. The key difference is that the ad hominem fallacy occurs during the argument, while the poisoning the well fallacy happens before the argument begins.\", \"Based on the given passages, the difference between ad hominem fallacy and the poisoning the well fallacy lies in the timing and intent of the attack.\\n\\nAn ad hominem fallacy is an attack on the person rather than the argument itself. It can occur at any point during the discussion. For example, if someone argues that climate change is caused by human activity, and another person responds by calling them a liar or a fool, they are committing an ad hominem fallacy because they are attacking the character of the person rather than addressing the argument.\\n\\nThe poisoning the well fallacy is an ad hominem attack that takes place before the merits of a case can be considered. It is intended to redirect attention away from the facts of the case by damaging the reputation of one of the parties. For example, if someone is about to argue for a new policy, and their opponent starts by saying that the person is a bad person for a reason not related to the policy, they are committing a poisoning the well fallacy.\\n\\nIn summary, the ad hominem fallacy is a general attack on a person, while the poisoning the well fallacy is a specific attack on a person that is intended to distract from the actual argument or case at hand.\", \"Based on the given passages, the ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of a person making an argument, while the poisoning the well fallacy is an ad hominem attack on a person's integrity or intelligence that occurs before the merits of a case can be considered, redirecting the discussion to the faults of the parties involved. The main difference lies in the timing of the attack: ad hominem is a direct attack on the person making the argument, while poisoning the well involves attacking the person's credibility before the argument can be evaluated.\", \"Based on the given passages, the ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument rather than attacking the argument itself. The poisoning the well fallacy, on the other hand, is an ad hominem attack on a person's integrity or intelligence that takes place before the merits of a case can be considered, redirecting the discussion to the faults of one of the parties.\"]", "time_sec": 22.64, "generation_time_seconds": 21.4578, "selection_time_seconds": 1.1764, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 17601, 22818, 279, 3668, 11, 45994, 11, 476, 1008, 8201, 315, 279, 1697, 3259, 279, 5693, 4751, 1091, 22818, 279, 5693, 5086, 13, 576, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 374, 458, 993, 4998, 68413, 3359, 389, 264, 1697, 594, 16614, 476, 11229, 429, 4990, 1992, 1573, 279, 49832, 315, 264, 1142, 646, 387, 6509, 11, 6423, 287, 279, 10219, 311, 279, 56690, 315, 825, 315, 279, 9677, 13, 576, 1376, 6672, 374, 429, 279, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 13666, 2337, 279, 5693, 11, 1393, 279, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 8573, 1573, 279, 5693, 12033, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, the ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument rather than attacking the argument itself. The poisoning the well fallacy is an ad hominem attack on a person's integrity or intelligence that takes place before the merits of a case can be considered, redirecting the discussion to the faults of one of the parties. The key difference is that the ad hominem fallacy occurs during the argument, while the poisoning the well fallacy happens before the argument begins.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.9672219671192385, "g_worst_claim": 0.0511213678580064, "g_content": 1.7884007263183594, "g_traj_mean": 1.1272406578063965}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 6672, 1948, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 323, 279, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 15448, 304, 279, 18405, 323, 7385, 315, 279, 3359, 382, 2082, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 374, 458, 3359, 389, 279, 1697, 4751, 1091, 279, 5693, 5086, 13, 1084, 646, 12170, 518, 894, 1459, 2337, 279, 10219, 13, 1752, 3110, 11, 421, 4325, 28533, 429, 9977, 2297, 374, 8881, 553, 3738, 5702, 11, 323, 2441, 1697, 30580, 553, 8098, 1105, 264, 72624, 476, 264, 23977, 11, 807, 525, 39483, 458, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 1576, 807, 525, 22818, 279, 3668, 315, 279, 1697, 4751, 1091, 27020, 279, 5693, 382, 785, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 374, 458, 993, 4998, 68413, 3359, 429, 4990, 1992, 1573, 279, 49832, 315, 264, 1142, 646, 387, 6509, 13, 1084, 374, 10602, 311, 6423, 6529, 3123, 504, 279, 13064, 315, 279, 1142, 553, 33346, 279, 17011, 315, 825, 315, 279, 9677, 13, 1752, 3110, 11, 421, 4325, 374, 911, 311, 17585, 369, 264, 501, 4842, 11, 323, 862, 14704, 8471, 553, 5488, 429, 279, 1697, 374, 264, 3873, 1697, 369, 264, 2874, 537, 5435, 311, 279, 4842, 11, 807, 525, 39483, 264, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 382, 641, 12126, 11, 279, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 374, 264, 4586, 3359, 389, 264, 1697, 11, 1393, 279, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 374, 264, 3151, 3359, 389, 264, 1697, 429, 374, 10602, 311, 63817, 504, 279, 5042, 5693, 476, 1142, 518, 1424, 13, 151645], "pred": "In summary, the ad hominem fallacy is a general attack on a person, while the poisoning the well fallacy is a specific attack on a person that is intended to distract from the actual argument or case at hand.", "grounding_sco