4, 476, 11229, 429, 4990, 1992, 1573, 279, 49832, 315, 264, 1142, 646, 387, 6509, 11, 6423, 287, 279, 10219, 311, 279, 56690, 315, 825, 315, 279, 9677, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, the ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument rather than attacking the argument itself. The poisoning the well fallacy, on the other hand, is an ad hominem attack on a person's integrity or intelligence that takes place before the merits of a case can be considered, redirecting the discussion to the faults of one of the parties.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.1757027318368067, "g_worst_claim": 0.20159593650272914, "g_content": 2.259872637296978, "g_traj_mean": 1.378051996231079}}], "selected_rollout": 3, "random_sel_rollout": 3, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 61.16, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "361", "question": "What’s the difference between ad hominem fallacy and the poisoning the well fallacy?", "gt": "The main difference between ad hominem fallacy and poisoning the well fallacy is the timing of the attack. Ad hominem fallacy attacks the person making the argument instead of attacking the argument directly, but poisoning the well fallacy is a preemptive attack on a person's character or integrity before they have even made their argument. In other words, ad hominem fallacy is a reaction to an argument, while poisoning the well fallacy is a preemptive strike to discredit an opponent before they have a chance to present their argument.", "pred": "In essence, while both fallacies involve attacking the person rather than the argument, the ad hominem fallacy does so directly during the argument, whereas the poisoning the well fallacy does so preemptively.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "In essence, while both fallacies involve attacking the person rather than the argument, the ad hominem fallacy does so directly during the argument, whereas the poisoning the well fallacy does so preemptively.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, the ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument rather than attacking the argument itself. The poisoning the well fallacy is an ad hominem attack on a person's integrity or intelligence that takes place before the merits of a case can be considered, redirecting the discussion to the faults of one of the parties. The key difference is that the ad hominem fallacy occurs during the argument, while the poisoning the well fallacy happens before the argument begins.\", \"Based on the given passages, the primary difference between an ad hominem fallacy and a poisoning the well fallacy is in their timing and form:\\n\\n1. Ad Hominem Fallacy: This involves an attack on the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument, rather than attacking the argument itself. It is an immediate and direct attack on the person.\\n\\n2. Poisoning the Well Fallacy: This is an ad hominem attack that occurs before the merits of a case can be considered. Its goal is to redirect attention to the faults of one party, thereby invalidating any subsequent argument from that person.\\n\\nIn essence, while both fallacies involve attacking the person rather than the argument, the ad hominem fallacy does so directly during the argument, whereas the poisoning the well fallacy does so preemptively.\", \"The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attribute of the person making the argument, rather than addressing the argument itself. The poisoning the well fallacy involves attacking a person's integrity or intelligence before the merits of a case can be considered, redirecting the discussion to the faults of one of the parties.\", \"Based on the given passages, the ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument rather than directly addressing the argument itself. An example of ad hominem fallacy is equating someone's character with the soundness of their argument.\\n\\nThe poisoning the well fallacy is an ad hominem attack that takes place before the merits of a case can be considered. It redirects a discussion to the faults of one of the parties involved. An example of poisoning the well fallacy is attacking a person's integrity or intelligence before the discussion of the actual issue at hand.\"]", "time_sec": 20.981, "generation_time_seconds": 20.1276, "selection_time_seconds": 0.8446, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 17601, 22818, 279, 3668, 11, 45994, 11, 476, 1008, 8201, 315, 279, 1697, 3259, 279, 5693, 4