0, 21058, 22818, 279, 1697, 4751, 1091, 279, 5693, 11, 279, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 1558, 773, 5961, 2337, 279, 5693, 11, 19853, 279, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 1558, 773, 81747, 3132, 13, 151645], "pred": "In essence, while both fallacies involve attacking the person rather than the argument, the ad hominem fallacy does so directly during the argument, whereas the poisoning the well fallacy does so preemptively.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.5815777632614755, "g_worst_claim": 0.074415718515714, "g_content": 1.1797258589002821, "g_traj_mean": 0.6557942032814026}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [785, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 17601, 22818, 279, 3668, 11, 45994, 11, 476, 1008, 7035, 315, 279, 1697, 3259, 279, 5693, 11, 4751, 1091, 27020, 279, 5693, 5086, 13, 576, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 17601, 22818, 264, 1697, 594, 16614, 476, 11229, 1573, 279, 49832, 315, 264, 1142, 646, 387, 6509, 11, 6423, 287, 279, 10219, 311, 279, 56690, 315, 825, 315, 279, 9677, 13, 151645], "pred": "The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attribute of the person making the argument, rather than addressing the argument itself. The poisoning the well fallacy involves attacking a person's integrity or intelligence before the merits of a case can be considered, redirecting the discussion to the faults of one of the parties.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.1556892885591759, "g_worst_claim": -0.30782379422869, "g_content": 2.1698665003622732, "g_traj_mean": 1.3657011985778809}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 17601, 22818, 279, 3668, 11, 45994, 11, 476, 1008, 8201, 315, 279, 1697, 3259, 279, 5693, 4751, 1091, 5961, 27020, 279, 5693, 5086, 13, 1527, 3110, 315, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 374, 3232, 1095, 4325, 594, 3668, 448, 279, 5112, 2090, 315, 862, 5693, 382, 785, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 374, 458, 993, 4998, 68413, 3359, 429, 4990, 1992, 1573, 279, 49832, 315, 264, 1142, 646, 387, 6509, 13, 1084, 71171, 264, 10219, 311, 279, 56690, 315, 825, 315, 279, 9677, 6398, 13, 1527, 3110, 315, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 374, 22818, 264, 1697, 594, 16614, 476, 11229, 1573, 279, 10219, 315, 279, 5042, 4265, 518, 1424, 13, 151645], "pred": "The poisoning the well fallacy is an ad hominem attack that takes place before the merits of a case can be considered. It redirects a discussion to the faults of one of the parties involved. An example of poisoning the well fallacy is attacking a person's integrity or intelligence before the discussion of the actual issue at hand.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.1003975630546814, "g_worst_claim": 0.2846124569574992, "g_content": 1.7592116238778097, "g_traj_mean": 1.1156643629074097}}], "selected_rollout": 2, "random_sel_rollout": 1, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 28.57, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "364", "question": "how to get antique effect on wood using normal paint", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here are some steps to achieve an antique effect on wood using normal paint:\n\n1. Apply a basecoat of flat or satin paint to the wood surface ( passage 3). Satin paint is easier to work with, but flat paint will give a more aged look.\n2. Mix paint that is two shades darker than the basecoat with an equal amount of glaze ( passage 3). Once the basecoat is dry, drizzle a crumpled rag or plastic bag in the glaze mixture to achieve an aged appearance.\n3. Layer stain on top of the paint to create a cool effect ( passage 1). This technique involves applying multiple layers of stain and allowing them to dry between coats.\n4. Use a palette knife to spread a mixture of molding paste and a rust or dull color on a canvas, letting it dry before adding additional layers ( passage 2). This can help create an embossed design with an antique finish.\n5. Apply a thin layer of burnt sienna or a similar color to give the wood a vintage look ( passage 3). Dabbing the paint with a paper towel or cloth can help remove excess paint and achieve a more subtle, aged appearance.\n\nOverall, the key to achieving an antique effect on wood using normal paint is to layer different materials and colors to create a weathered and worn appearance.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, to get an antique effect on wood using normal paint, follow this step-by-step guide:\n\n1. Paint your wood piece with a basecoat of flat or satin paint. Satin paint is easier to work with, but flat paint results in an older look.\n\n2. Mix paint that is two shades darker than your basecoat with an equal amount of glaze.\n\n3. Once your basecoat dries, drip a crumpled rag or plastic bag into the glaze mixture.\n\nThis technique mimics the aged finish of antique wooden items.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, to get an antique effect on wood using normal paint, follow this step-by-step guide:\n\n1. Paint your wood piece with a basecoat of flat or satin paint. Satin paint is easier to work with, but flat paint results in an older look.\n\n2. Mix paint that is two shades darker than your basecoat with an equal amount of glaze.\n\n3. Once your basecoat dries, drip a crumpled rag or plastic bag into the glaze mixture.\n\nThis technique mimics the aged finish of antique wooden items.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, to get an antique effect on wood using normal paint, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. Paint your wood piece with a basecoat of flat or satin paint. Satin paint is easier to work with, but flat paint results in an older look.\\n2. Mix paint that’s two shades darker than your basecoat with an equal amount of glaze.\\n3. Once your basecoat dries, drip a crumpled rag or plastic bag in the glaze mixture. This technique helps create a distressed, aged look on the wood.\", \"Based on the given passages, to get an antique effect on wood using normal paint, follow this step-by-step guide:\\n\\n1. Paint your wood piece with a basecoat of flat or satin paint. Satin paint is easier to work with, but flat paint results in an older look.\\n\\n2. Mix paint that is two shades darker than your basecoat with an equal amount of glaze.\\n\\n3. Once your basecoat dries, drip a crumpled rag or plastic bag into the glaze mixture.\\n\\nThis technique mimics the aged finish of antique wooden items.\", \"Based on the given passages, to achieve an antique effect on wood using normal paint, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. Paint your wood piece with a basecoat of flat or satin paint. Satin paint is easier to work with, but flat paint results in an older look.\\n2. Mix paint that's two shades darker than your basecoat with an equal amount of glaze.\\n3. Once your basecoat dries, drip a crumpled rag or plastic bag in the glaze mixture.\\n4. Let the mixture drip down the piece, creating an aged and worn effect.\\n\\nThis process mimics the natural aging of wood over time and can give your projects an antique appearance quickly.\", \"Based on the given passages, to get an antique effect on wood using normal paint, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. Paint your wood piece with a basecoat of flat or satin paint. Satin paint is easier to work with, but flat paint results in an older look.\\n2. Mix paint that's two shades darker than your basecoat with an equal amount of glaze.\\n3. Once your basecoat dries, drip a crumpled rag or plastic bag in the glaze mixture. This will create a distressed look, mimicking the effect of years of use.\\n4. Allow the glaze to dry completely. The combination of the darker paint and the glaze will give your wood a vintage patina, similar to that found in antique furniture.\"]", "time_sec": 23.481, "generation_time_seconds": 22.6193, "selection_time_seconds": 0.8545, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 311, 633, 458, 46591, 2456, 389, 7579, 1667, 4622, 6177, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 17203, 697, 7579, 6573, 448, 264, 2331, 67412, 315, 10063, 476, 77084, 6177, 13, 13177, 258, 6177, 374, 8661, 311, 975, 448, 11, 714, 10063, 6177, 3059, 304, 458, 9014, 1401, 624, 17, 13, 19219, 6177, 429, 748, 1378, 36099, 39030, 1091, 697, 2331, 67412, 448, 458, 6144, 3311, 315, 2770, 9832, 624, 18, 13, 9646, 697, 2331, 67412, 294, 4019, 11, 72499, 264, 1560, 372, 49085, 20475, 476, 12188, 8968, 304, 279, 2770, 9832, 20980, 13, 1096, 14762, 8609, 1855, 264, 70390, 11, 19749, 1401, 389, 279, 7579, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, to get an antique effect on wood using normal paint, follow these steps:\n\n1. Paint your wood piece with a basecoat of flat or satin paint. Satin paint is easier to work with, but flat paint results in an older look.\n2. Mix paint that’s two shades darker than your basecoat with an equal amount of glaze.\n3. Once your basecoat dries, drip a crumpled rag or plastic bag in the glaze mixture. This technique helps create a distressed, aged look on the wood.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.5735964291475038, "g_worst_claim": 0.061084662874539696, "g_content": 2.0659748424183237, "g_traj_mean": 1.7592291831970215}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 311, 633, 458, 46591, 2456, 389, 7579, 1667, 4622, 6177, 11, 1795, 419, 3019, 14319, 29208, 8474, 1447, 16, 13, 17203, 697, 7579, 6573, 448, 264, 2331, 67412, 315, 10063, 476, 77084, 6177, 13, 13177, 258, 6177, 374, 8661, 311, 975, 448, 11, 714, 10063, 6177, 3059, 304, 458, 9014, 1401, 382, 17, 13, 19219, 6177, 429, 374, 1378, 36099, 39030, 1091, 697, 2331, 67412, 448, 458, 6144, 3311, 315, 2770, 9832, 382, 18, 13, 9646, 697, 2331, 67412, 294, 4019, 11, 72499, 264, 1560, 372, 49085, 20475, 476, 12188, 8968, 1119, 279, 2770, 9832, 20980, 382, 1986, 14762, 26905, 1211, 279, 19749, 6248, 315, 46591, 22360, 3589, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, to get an antique effect on wood using normal paint, follow this step-by-step guide:\n\n1. Paint your wood piece with a basecoat of flat or satin paint. Satin paint is easier to work with, but flat paint results in an older look.\n\n2. Mix paint that is two shades darker than your basecoat with an equal amount of glaze.\n\n3. Once your basecoat dries, drip a crumpled rag or plastic bag into the glaze mixture.\n\nThis technique mimics the aged finish of antique wooden items.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.5669950243752795, "g_worst_claim": -0.18189372618993124, "g_content": 2.027952194213867, "g_traj_mean": 1.6164867877960205}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 311, 11075, 458, 46591, 2456, 389, 7579, 1667, 4622, 6177, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 17203, 697, 7579, 6573, 448, 264, 2331, 67412, 315, 10063, 476, 77084, 6177, 13, 13177, 258, 6177, 374, 8661, 311, 975, 448, 11, 714, 10063, 6177, 3059, 304, 458, 9014, 1401, 624, 17, 13, 19219, 6177, 429, 594, 1378, 36099, 39030, 1091, 697, 2331, 67412, 448, 458, 6144, 3311, 315, 2770, 9832, 624, 18, 13, 9646, 697, 2331, 67412, 294, 4019, 11, 72499, 264, 1560, 372, 49085, 20475, 476, 12188, 8968, 304, 279, 2770, 9832, 20980, 624, 19, 13, 6771, 279, 20980, 72499, 1495, 279, 6573, 11, 6825, 458, 19749, 323, 23704, 2456, 382, 1986, 1882, 26905, 1211, 279, 5810, 28984, 315, 7579, 916, 882, 323, 646, 2968, 697, 7079, 458, 46591, 11094, 6157, 13, 151645], "pred": "This process mimics the natural aging of wood over time and can give your projects an antique appearance quickly.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.4195213074894004, "g_worst_claim": 0.10296604037284851, "g_content": 1.7414935616885914, "g_traj_mean": 1.4829922914505005}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 311, 633, 458, 46591, 2456, 389, 7579, 1667, 4622, 6177, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 17203, 697, 7579, 6573, 448, 264, 2331, 67412, 315, 10063, 476, 77084, 6177, 13, 13177, 258, 6177, 374, 8661, 311, 975, 448, 11, 714, 10063, 6177, 3059, 304, 458, 9014, 1401, 624, 17, 13, 19219, 6177, 429, 594, 1378, 36099, 39030, 1091, 697, 2331, 67412, 448, 458, 6144, 3311, 315, 2770, 9832, 624, 18, 13, 9646, 697, 2331, 67412, 294, 4019, 11, 72499, 264, 1560, 372, 49085, 20475, 476, 12188, 8968, 304, 279, 2770, 9832, 20980, 13, 1096, 686, 1855, 264, 70390, 1401, 11, 26905, 16272, 279, 2456, 315, 1635, 315, 990, 624, 19, 13, 26530, 279, 2770, 9832, 311, 9058, 6587, 13, 576, 10601, 315, 279, 39030, 6177, 323, 279, 2770, 9832, 686, 2968, 697, 7579, 264, 23790, 3272, 2210, 11, 4428, 311, 429, 1730, 304, 46591, 14549, 13, 151645], "pred": "1. Paint your wood piece with a basecoat of flat or satin paint. Satin paint is easier to work with, but flat paint results in an older look.\n2. Mix paint that's two shades darker than your basecoat with an equal amount of glaze.\n3. Once your basecoat dries, drip a crumpled rag or plastic bag in the glaze mixture. This will create a distressed look, mimicking the effect of years of use.\n4. Allow the glaze to dry completely. The combination of the darker paint and the glaze will give your wood a vintage patina, similar to that found in antique furniture.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.2683138302956516, "g_worst_claim": 0.061084662874539696, "g_content": 1.8127206166585286, "g_traj_mean": 1.4043662548065186}}], "selected_rollout": 0, "random_sel_rollout": 1, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 41.86, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "367", "question": "what is the difference between a rock and a stone", "gt": "Based on the given passages, a rock is defined as the hard material of the earth's crust that is normally exposed on the surface or underlying the soil. On the other hand, a stone is described as a smaller piece or pebble that can be carried in the hand. The passages also mention that rocks are made of smaller stones and stones are made from rocks. Additionally, stones can be pelted easily, while rocks cannot. Therefore, the main difference between a rock and a stone is their size, with rocks being larger and stones being smaller and easier to handle.", "pred": "3. Use: Stones can easily be pelted or used as projectiles, as evidenced by their use in the examples of \"The Rock and Stone Cold Steve Austin.\" In contrast, rocks are less likely to be used in such a manner due to their size and weight.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "3. Use: Stones can easily be pelted or used as projectiles, as evidenced by their use in the examples of \"The Rock and Stone Cold Steve Austin.\" In contrast, rocks are less likely to be used in such a manner due to their size and weight.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, the difference between a rock and a stone is that a rock is the hard material of the earth's crust, normally exposed on the surface of the earth or sometimes underlying the soil, while a stone is a small piece or pebble that can be carried in the hand. Rocks are made of smaller stones, and stones are made from rocks. Additionally, stones can be pelted easily, but one cannot do so with rocks.\", \"The difference between a rock and a stone lies in their size and the context in which they are used. A rock is defined as the hard material of the earth's crust, which is usually exposed on the surface or sometimes underlying the soil. Rocks are typically large pieces of stone that are difficult to carry in the hand. In contrast, stones are smaller pieces of rock, often referred to as pebbles, which are smaller and can be easily carried in the hand. Additionally, stones can be pelted more easily than rocks, which are generally more substantial and cannot be pelted easily. The passage also mentions that rocks are made of smaller stones and stones are made from rocks, further emphasizing the relationship between the two terms.\", \"Based on the given passages, the difference between a rock and a stone can be described as follows:\\n\\n1. Rocks are made of smaller stones and stones are made from rocks, indicating a hierarchical relationship in their formation.\\n2. Rocks can be described as a large piece of stone that is difficult to be carried in the hand, while stones are just small pieces or pebbles that can be carried in the hand.\\n3. Stones can be pelted easily, whereas one cannot pelt rocks effectively due to their size and hardness.\", \"Based on the given passages, there are several key differences between a rock and a stone:\\n\\n1. Definition: A rock is the hard material of the earth's crust, typically exposed on the surface or underlying the soil, whereas a stone is a smaller piece, such as a pebble, that can be carried in the hand.\\n\\n2. Size: Rocks are generally larger and more substantial, while stones are smaller and more manageable.\\n\\n3. Use: Stones can easily be pelted or used as projectiles, as evidenced by their use in the examples of \\\"The Rock and Stone Cold Steve Austin.\\\" In contrast, rocks are less likely to be used in such a manner due to their size and weight.\"]", "time_sec": 21.908, "generation_time_seconds": 20.7919, "selection_time_seconds": 1.1071, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 6672, 1948, 264, 6946, 323, 264, 9798, 374, 429, 264, 6946, 374, 279, 2588, 3684, 315, 279, 9393, 594, 39612, 11, 14279, 14900, 389, 279, 7329, 315, 279, 9393, 476, 7025, 16533, 279, 17171, 11, 1393, 264, 9798, 374, 264, 2613, 6573, 476, 1051, 59880, 429, 646, 387, 11691, 304, 279, 1424, 13, 68031, 525, 1865, 315, 9155, 26210, 11, 323, 26210, 525, 1865, 504, 23035, 13, 22406, 11, 26210, 646, 387, 281, 3818, 291, 6707, 11, 714, 825, 4157, 653, 773, 448, 23035, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, the difference between a rock and a stone is that a rock is the hard material of the earth's crust, normally exposed on the surface of the earth or sometimes underlying the soil, while a stone is a small piece or pebble that can be carried in the hand. Rocks are made of smaller stones, and stones are made from rocks. Additionally, stones can be pelted easily, but one cannot do so with rocks.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.8670146804050278, "g_worst_claim": 0.38862089316050213, "g_content": 2.971120934737356, "g_traj_mean": 1.8375879526138306}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [785, 6672, 1948, 264, 6946, 323, 264, 9798, 15448, 304, 862, 1379, 323, 279, 2266, 304, 892, 807, 525, 1483, 13, 362, 6946, 374, 4512, 438, 279, 2588, 3684, 315, 279, 9393, 594, 39612, 11, 892, 374, 5990, 14900, 389, 279, 7329, 476, 7025, 16533, 279, 17171, 13, 68031, 525, 11136, 3460, 9666, 315, 9798, 429, 525, 5000, 311, 6777, 304, 279, 1424, 13, 758, 12872, 11, 26210, 525, 9155, 9666, 315, 6946, 11, 3545, 13862, 311, 438, 1051, 6066, 642, 11, 892, 525, 9155, 323, 646, 387, 6707, 11691, 304, 279, 1424, 13, 22406, 11, 26210, 646, 387, 281, 3818, 291, 803, 6707, 1091, 23035, 11, 892, 525, 8789, 803, 11924, 323, 4157, 387, 281, 3818, 291, 6707, 13, 576, 21085, 1083, 33845, 429, 23035, 525, 1865, 315, 9155, 26210, 323, 26210, 525, 1865, 504, 23035, 11, 4623, 80903, 279, 5025, 1948, 279, 1378, 3793, 13, 151645], "pred": "The difference between a rock and a stone lies in their size and the context in which they are used. A rock is defined as the hard material of the earth's crust, which is usually exposed on the surface or sometimes underlying the soil. Rocks are typically large pieces of stone that are difficult to carry in the hand. In contrast, stones are smaller pieces of rock, often referred to as pebbles, which are smaller and can be easily carried in the hand. Additionally, stones can be pelted more easily than rocks, which are generally more substantial and cannot be pelted easily. The passage also mentions that rocks are made of smaller stones and stones are made from rocks, further emphasizing the relationship between the two terms.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.0656628471022112, "g_worst_claim": 0.06736956323896136, "g_content": 1.7524628412155878, "g_traj_mean": 1.160157322883606}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 6672, 1948, 264, 6946, 323, 264, 9798, 646, 387, 7481, 438, 11017, 1447, 16, 13, 68031, 525, 1865, 315, 9155, 26210, 323, 26210, 525, 1865, 504, 23035, 11, 18860, 264, 69894, 5025, 304, 862, 18003, 624, 17, 13, 68031, 646, 387, 7481, 438, 264, 3460, 6573, 315, 9798, 429, 374, 5000, 311, 387, 11691, 304, 279, 1424, 11, 1393, 26210, 525, 1101, 2613, 9666, 476, 1051, 6066, 642, 429, 646, 387, 11691, 304, 279, 1424, 624, 18, 13, 56100, 646, 387, 281, 3818, 291, 6707, 11, 19853, 825, 4157, 281, 3818, 23035, 13444, 4152, 311, 862, 1379, 323, 70903, 13, 151645], "pred": "1. Rocks are made of smaller stones and stones are made from rocks, indicating a hierarchical relationship in their formation.\n2. Rocks can be described as a large piece of stone that is difficult to be carried in the hand, while stones are just small pieces or pebbles that can be carried in the hand.\n3. Stones can be pelted easily, whereas one cannot pelt rocks effectively due to their size and hardness.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.4537008867203944, "g_worst_claim": 0.38862089316050213, "g_content": 1.9330287346473107, "g_traj_mean": 1.3705313205718994}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 1052, 525, 3807, 1376, 11799, 1948, 264, 6946, 323, 264, 9798, 1447, 16, 13, 19709, 25, 362, 6946, 374, 279, 2588, 3684, 315, 279, 9393, 594, 39612, 11, 11136, 14900, 389, 279, 7329, 476, 16533, 279, 17171, 11, 19853, 264, 9798, 374, 264, 9155, 6573, 11, 1741, 438, 264, 1051, 59880, 11, 429, 646, 387, 11691, 304, 279, 1424, 382, 17, 13, 8478, 25, 68031, 525, 8789, 8131, 323, 803, 11924, 11, 1393, 26210, 525, 9155, 323, 803, 70028, 382, 18, 13, 5443, 25, 56100, 646, 6707, 387, 281, 3818, 291, 476, 1483, 438, 79037, 11, 438, 68357, 553, 862, 990, 304, 279, 10295, 315, 330, 785, 9127, 323, 14302, 23185, 13809, 19260, 1189, 758, 12872, 11, 23035, 525, 2686, 4363, 311, 387, 1483, 304, 1741, 264, 11566, 4152, 311, 862, 1379, 323, 4680, 13, 151645], "pred": "3. Use: Stones can easily be pelted or used as projectiles, as evidenced by their use in the examples of \"The Rock and Stone Cold Steve Austin.\" In contrast, rocks are less likely to be used in such a manner due to their size and weight.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.059712988764505, "g_worst_claim": -0.04769936629704067, "g_content": 1.4439916459340898, "g_traj_mean": 1.0173449516296387}}], "selected_rollout": 0, "random_sel_rollout": 3, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 24.72, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 5.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "368", "question": "what is the difference between a rock and a stone", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, the main difference between a rock and a stone is their size. Rocks are typically larger and more massive than stones, which are smaller and more fragmented.\n\nPassage 1 states that rocks are often used to refer to something solid, large, and grounded, such as a mountain or palisade, while stones are often used to refer to small pieces of rock or pebbles. Passage 2 defines rocks as the hard material of the earth's crust that is exposed on the surface of the earth or underlying the soil, while stones are smaller fragments of rock that can be carried in the hand.\n\nPassage 3 further clarifies the distinction by stating that rocks are made up of smaller stones, while stones are made from rocks. Additionally, rocks are described as being difficult to carry in the hand, while stones are small enough to be carried easily.\n\nIn summary, while both rocks and stones are made of the same material, they differ in size and usage. Rocks are generally larger and more massive, while stones are smaller and more fragmented.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, the difference between a rock and a stone is that a rock is the hard material of the earth's crust, typically exposed on the surface of the earth or sometimes underlying the soil, while a stone can be described as a small piece or pebble that can be carried in the hand. Rocks are made of smaller stones and stones are made from rocks. Additionally, stones can be pelted easily, but rocks cannot.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the difference between a rock and a stone is that a rock is the hard material of the earth's crust, typically exposed on the surface of the earth or sometimes underlying the soil, while a stone can be described as a small piece or pebble that can be carried in the hand. Rocks are made of smaller stones and stones are made from rocks. Additionally, stones can be pelted easily, but rocks cannot.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, the difference between a rock and a stone is that a rock is the hard material of the earth's crust, normally exposed on the surface of the earth or sometimes underlying the soil, while a stone is a small piece or pebble that can be carried in the hand. Rocks are made of smaller stones, and stones are made from rocks. Additionally, stones can be pelted easily, but one cannot do so with rocks.\", \"Based on the given passages, the difference between a rock and a stone is as follows:\\n\\n1. Rocks are made of smaller stones, and stones are made from rocks.\\n2. Rocks can be described as a large piece of stone that is difficult to be carried in the hand, while stones are small pieces or pebbles that can be easily carried in the hand.\\n3. Stones can be pelted easily, but one cannot do so with rocks.\", \"Based on the given passages, the difference between a rock and a stone involves their physical attributes and context of use. A rock is defined as the hard material of the earth's crust, typically exposed on the surface of the earth or sometimes underlying the soil. Rocks are generally larger and harder than stones. Stones, on the other hand, are smaller pieces of rock that can be carried in the hand, and they are not as hard or heavy as rocks. Additionally, rocks are often used metaphorically to represent something solid, large, and grounded, whereas stones are more commonly associated with smaller, handheld objects and less often used for metaphoric contexts.\", \"Based on the given passages, the difference between a rock and a stone is that a rock is the hard material of the earth's crust, typically exposed on the surface of the earth or sometimes underlying the soil, while a stone can be described as a small piece or pebble that can be carried in the hand. Rocks are made of smaller stones and stones are made from rocks. Additionally, stones can be pelted easily, but rocks cannot.\"]", "time_sec": 19.497, "generation_time_seconds": 18.5303, "selection_time_seconds": 0.9593, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 6672, 1948, 264, 6946, 323, 264, 9798, 374, 429, 264, 6946, 374, 279, 2588, 3684, 315, 279, 9393, 594, 39612, 11, 14279, 14900, 389, 279, 7329, 315, 279, 9393, 476, 7025, 16533, 279, 17171, 11, 1393, 264, 9798, 374, 264, 2613, 6573, 476, 1051, 59880, 429, 646, 387, 11691, 304, 279, 1424, 13, 68031, 525, 1865, 315, 9155, 26210, 11, 323, 26210, 525, 1865, 504, 23035, 13, 22406, 11, 26210, 646, 387, 281, 3818, 291, 6707, 11, 714, 825, 4157, 653, 773, 448, 23035, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, the difference between a rock and a stone is that a rock is the hard material of the earth's crust, normally exposed on the surface of the earth or sometimes underlying the soil, while a stone is a small piece or pebble that can be carried in the hand. Rocks are made of smaller stones, and stones are made from rocks. Additionally, stones can be pelted easily, but one cannot do so with rocks.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.867236225451054, "g_worst_claim": 0.43244389692942303, "g_content": 2.915800596538343, "g_traj_mean": 1.8327332735061646}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 6672, 1948, 264, 6946, 323, 264, 9798, 374, 438, 11017, 1447, 16, 13, 68031, 525, 1865, 315, 9155, 26210, 11, 323, 26210, 525, 1865, 504, 23035, 624, 17, 13, 68031, 646, 387, 7481, 438, 264, 3460, 6573, 315, 9798, 429, 374, 5000, 311, 387, 11691, 304, 279, 1424, 11, 1393, 26210, 525, 2613, 9666, 476, 1051, 6066, 642, 429, 646, 387, 6707, 11691, 304, 279, 1424, 624, 18, 13, 56100, 646, 387, 281, 3818, 291, 6707, 11, 714, 825, 4157, 653, 773, 448, 23035, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, the difference be