{"id": "432", "question": "how to clean tarnished jewelry at home", "gt": "To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a few different methods based on the passages provided. For silver jewelry, you can mix mild dish soap with warm water, dip a soft cloth into the mixture, and rub the jewelry. Then rinse it in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. If the tarnish is heavier, you can make a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, apply it to the silver with a soft cloth, and rinse it off. For diamonds, you can clean them with warm soapy water and a soft-bristle brush or old toothbrush, then pat them dry with a soft cloth. If you have silver jewelry with gemstones, make sure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.", "pred": "5. Pat the jewelry dry with a soft cloth, as suggested by Loretta Castoro, vice president and international jewelry buyer for Jewelry Television.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "5. Pat the jewelry dry with a soft cloth, as suggested by Loretta Castoro, vice president and international jewelry buyer for Jewelry Television.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, to clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a few different methods:\\n\\n1. For items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip the jewelry in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry.\\n\\n2. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water. Wet the silver and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth (not paper towels, which can scratch).\\n\\n3. For diamonds, the best way to clean them is with warm soapy water and a soft-bristle brush or old toothbrush. Pat it dry with a soft cloth.\\n\\n4. For silver jewelry, you can pour two cups of hot water into a bowl. This method works as a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your silver is lightly tarnished, the salt bath should take the tarnish right off. If you're cleaning a lot of jewelry at once, you can use more water. For just one piece of jewelry, use less water. If your jewelry has gemstones, make sure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.\", \"Based on the given passages, to clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a mild dish soap solution with warm water for items that are merely dull or starting to discolor. Mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip the jewelry in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse it in cool water and buff it with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, you can create a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth. This method is gentle and effective for removing tarnish without damaging the silver.\", \"Based on the given passages, to clean tarnished jewelry at home, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. For items that are merely dull or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip the jewelry in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, rinse in cool water, and buff with a soft cloth until dry.\\n\\n2. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water. Wet the silver and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth.\\n\\n3. To clean silver with less tarnish, pour two cups of hot water into a bowl and submerge the jewelry in the solution. This method works as a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver.\\n\\n4. If the jewelry has gemstones, ensure they won't be affected by being submerged in the saltwater solution.\\n\\n5. Pat the jewelry dry with a soft cloth, as suggested by Loretta Castoro, vice president and international jewelry buyer for Jewelry Television.\", \"Based on the given passages, to clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can follow these steps:\\n\\n1. For items that are merely dull or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry.\\n\\n2. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water. Wet the silver and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth. Avoid using paper towels as they can scratch the jewelry.\\n\\n3. If the jewelry has gemstones, ensure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.\\n\\n4. For diamonds, use warm soapy water and a soft-bristle brush or old toothbrush to clean. Pat it dry with a soft cloth after cleaning.\\n\\n5. Alternatively, pour two cups of hot water into a bowl to create a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver. This method works well for lightly tarnished jewelry. If cleaning a lot of jewelry, use more water; for just one piece, use less water.\"]", "time_sec": 27.281, "generation_time_seconds": 26.4691, "selection_time_seconds": 0.8029, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 311, 4240, 86480, 3304, 30717, 518, 2114, 11, 498, 646, 990, 264, 2421, 2155, 5413, 1447, 16, 13, 1752, 3589, 429, 525, 16234, 40530, 11, 89394, 11, 476, 5916, 311, 2560, 789, 11, 6514, 264, 2421, 21025, 315, 23034, 12000, 26785, 448, 8205, 3015, 323, 23604, 279, 30717, 304, 264, 8413, 27292, 13, 12839, 279, 30717, 11, 1221, 73241, 304, 7010, 3015, 323, 11522, 448, 264, 27292, 3080, 9058, 382, 17, 13, 1752, 43822, 86480, 812, 11, 6514, 264, 24937, 315, 2326, 5479, 27815, 38862, 311, 825, 949, 3015, 13, 44856, 279, 14961, 323, 3796, 279, 31881, 448, 264, 8413, 11, 57920, 12577, 27292, 320, 1921, 5567, 52884, 11, 892, 646, 18778, 3593, 18, 13, 1752, 48051, 11, 279, 1850, 1616, 311, 4240, 1105, 374, 448, 8205, 773, 9667, 3015, 323, 264, 8413, 30117, 28300, 15625, 476, 2310, 25507, 36061, 13, 7135, 432, 9058, 448,