369, 17592, 86480, 812, 2041, 33346, 279, 14961, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, to clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a mild dish soap solution with warm water for items that are merely dull or starting to discolor. Mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip the jewelry in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse it in cool water and buff it with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, you can create a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth. This method is gentle and effective for removing tarnish without damaging the silver.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.5970847699665216, "g_worst_claim": 0.35993674596150715, "g_content": 2.0664548873901367, "g_traj_mean": 1.4728106260299683}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 311, 4240, 86480, 3304, 30717, 518, 2114, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 1752, 3589, 429, 525, 16234, 40530, 476, 5916, 311, 2560, 789, 11, 6514, 264, 2421, 21025, 315, 23034, 12000, 26785, 448, 8205, 3015, 323, 23604, 279, 30717, 304, 264, 8413, 27292, 13, 12839, 279, 30717, 11, 73241, 304, 7010, 3015, 11, 323, 11522, 448, 264, 8413, 27292, 3080, 9058, 382, 17, 13, 1752, 43822, 86480, 812, 11, 6514, 264, 24937, 315, 2326, 5479, 27815, 38862, 311, 825, 949, 3015, 13, 44856, 279, 14961, 323, 3796, 279, 31881, 448, 264, 8413, 11, 57920, 12577, 27292, 382, 18, 13, 2014, 4240, 14961, 448, 2686, 86480, 812, 11, 4914, 1378, 25374, 315, 4017, 3015, 1119, 264, 19212, 323, 1186, 19052, 279, 30717, 304, 279, 6291, 13, 1096, 1714, 4278, 438, 264, 21700, 34194, 261, 429, 28160, 86480, 812, 2041, 668, 81, 2228, 279, 14961, 382, 19, 13, 1416, 279, 30717, 702, 18747, 32510, 11, 5978, 807, 2765, 944, 387, 11495, 553, 1660, 84077, 304, 279, 12021, 12987, 6291, 382, 20, 13, 7135, 279, 30717, 9058, 448, 264, 8413, 27292, 11, 438, 11827, 553, 444, 18174, 2565, 11264, 18307, 11, 16774, 4767, 323, 6489, 30717, 25114, 369, 61868, 40345, 13, 151645], "pred": "5. Pat the jewelry dry with a soft cloth, as suggested by Loretta Castoro, vice president and international jewelry buyer for Jewelry Television.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.7699608929091226, "g_worst_claim": 0.24947146574656168, "g_content": 2.451303818646599, "g_traj_mean": 1.7701029777526855}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 311, 4240, 86480, 3304, 30717, 518, 2114, 11, 498, 646, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 1752, 3589, 429, 525, 16234, 40530, 476, 5916, 311, 2560, 789, 11, 6514, 264, 2421, 21025, 315, 23034, 12000, 26785, 448, 8205, 3015, 323, 23604, 304, 264, 8413, 27292, 13, 12839, 279, 30717, 11, 1221, 73241, 304, 7010, 3015, 323, 11522, 448, 264, 27292, 3080, 9058, 382, 17, 13, 1752, 43822, 86480, 812, 11, 6514, 264, 24937, 315, 2326, 5479, 27815, 38862, 311, 825, 949, 3015, 13, 44856, 279, 14961, 323, 3796, 279, 31881, 448, 264, 8413, 11, 57920, 12577, 27292, 13, 34006, 1667, 5567, 52884, 438, 807, 646, 18778, 279, 30717, 382, 18, 13, 1416, 279, 30717, 702, 18747, 32510, 11, 5978, 807, 2765, 944, 387, 11495, 553, 1660, 84077, 304, 264, 12021, 12987, 6291, 382, 19, 13, 1752, 48051, 11, 990, 8205, 773, 9667, 3015, 323, 264, 8413, 30117, 28300, 15625, 476, 2310, 25507, 36061, 311, 4240, 13, 7135, 432, 9058, 448, 264, 8413, 27292, 1283, 15826, 382, 20, 13, 38478, 11, 4914, 1378, 25374, 315, 4017, 3015, 1119, 264, 19212, 311, 1855, 264, 21700, 34194, 261, 429, 28160, 86480, 812, 2041, 668, 81, 2228, 279, 14961, 13, 1096, 1714, 4278, 1632, 369, 33404, 86480, 3304, 30717, 13, 1416, 15826, 264, 2696, 315, 30717, 11, 990, 803, 3015, 26, 369, 1101, 825, 6573, 11, 990, 2686, 3015, 13, 151645], "pred": "5. Alternatively, pour two cups of hot water into a bowl to create a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver. This method works well for lightly tarnished jewelry. If cleaning a lot of jewelry, use more water; for just one piece, use less water.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.651862949997406, "g_worst_claim": 0.3236509323120117, "g_content": 2.200264913993969, "g_traj_mean": 1.6780869960784912}}], "selected_rollout": 2, "random_sel_rollout": 2, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 13.46, "GAVIE-Rel": 0.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 0.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "433", "question": "how to clean tarnished jewelry at home", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here are some ways to clean tarnished jewelry at home:\n\n1. Mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip a soft cloth to shine silver jewelry that is merely dull or starting to discolor. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry.\n2. Use warm soapy water and a soft-bristle brush or old toothbrush to clean diamonds. Pat the jewelry dry with a soft cloth.\n3. Soak tarnished silver jewelry in a saltwater solution of two cups of hot water. This method is gentle and can remove tarnish without abrading the silver. Make sure the gemstones will not be affected by the solution before submerging them.\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages. The passages do not provide information on how to clean other types of jewelry, such as gold or platinum, or what to do if the tarnish is heavy or if the jewelry has intricate designs that may be damaged during cleaning.", "pred": "4. Pour two cups of hot water into a bowl, enough to cover the jewelry. This method works as a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your jewelry has gemstones, make sure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "4. Pour two cups of hot water into a bowl, enough to cover the jewelry. This method works as a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your jewelry has gemstones, make sure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, to clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a few different methods:\\n\\n1. For items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip the jewelry in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry.\\n\\n2. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water. Wet the silver and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth (not paper towels, which can scratch).\\n\\n3. For diamonds, the best way to clean them is with warm soapy water and a soft-bristle brush or old toothbrush. Pat it dry with a soft cloth.\\n\\n4. For silver jewelry, you can pour two cups of hot water into a bowl. This method works as a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your silver is lightly tarnished, the salt bath should take the tarnish right off. If you're cleaning a lot of jewelry at once, you can use more water. For just one piece of jewelry, use less water. If your jewelry has gemstones, make sure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.\", \"Based on the given passages, to clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use the following methods:\\n\\n1. For items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. Use a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water for heavier tarnish. Wet the silver and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth (not paper towels, which can scratch).\\n\\n2. Use warm soapy water and a soft-bristle brush or old toothbrush to clean a diamond. Pat it dry with a soft cloth.\\n\\n3. Pour two cups of hot water into a bowl. You just need enough to cover the jewelry you're cleaning. This method works as a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your silver is lightly tarnished, the salt bath should take the tarnish right off. If you're cleaning a lot of jewelry at once, you can use more water. For just one piece of jewelry, use less water. If your jewelry has gemstones, make sure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.\", \"Based on the given passages:\\n\\n1. For minor tarnishing, use a mixture of mild dish soap and warm water to dip the jewelry in. Buff the jewelry dry with a cloth after rinsing in cool water.\\n2. For more severe tarnish, create a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water and apply it with a soft, lint-free cloth.\\n3. A salt bath can be used for silver jewelry. Pour two cups of hot water into a bowl and submerge the jewelry. This method is gentle and effective for removing tarnish without causing damage to the silver. Adjust the amount of water based on the quantity of jewelry being cleaned. Ensure that any gemstones on the jewelry are not affected by the saltwater solution.\", \"Based on the given passages, here's how to clean tarnished jewelry at home:\\n\\n1. Mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip the jewelry in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, rinse in cool water, and buff dry with a cloth.\\n\\n2. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth.\\n\\n3. For diamond jewelry, use warm soapy water and a soft-bristle brush or old toothbrush. Pat it dry with a soft cloth.\\n\\n4. Pour two cups of hot water into a bowl, enough to cover the jewelry. This method works as a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your jewelry has gemstones, make sure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.\"]", "time_sec": 28.241, "generation_time_seconds": 27.4776, "selection_time_seconds": 0.7574, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 311, 4240, 86480, 3304, 30717, 518, 2114, 11, 498, 646, 990, 264, 2421, 2155, 5413, 1447, 16, 13, 1752, 3589, 429, 525, 16234, 40530, 11, 89394, 11, 476, 5916, 311, 2560, 789, 11, 6514, 264, 2421, 21025, 315, 23034, 12000, 26785, 448, 8205, 3015, 323, 23604, 279, 30717, 304, 264, 8413, 27292, 13, 12839, 279, 30717, 11, 1221, 73241, 304, 7010, 3015, 323, 11522, 448, 264, 27292, 3080, 9058, 382, 17, 13, 1752, 43822, 86480, 812, 11, 6514, 264, 24937, 315, 2326, 5479, 27815, 38862, 311, 825, 949, 3015, 13, 44856, 279, 14961, 323, 3796, 279, 31881, 448, 264, 8413, 11, 57920, 12577, 27292, 320, 1921, 5567, 52884, 11, 892, 646, 18778, 3593, 18, 13, 1752, 48051, 11, 279, 1850, 1616, 311, 4240, 1105, 374, 448, 8205, 773, 9667, 3015, 323, 264, 8413, 30117, 28300, 15625, 476, 2310, 25507, 36061, 13, 7135, 432, 9058, 448, 264, 8413, 27292, 382, 19, 13, 1752, 14961, 30717, 11, 498, 646, 4914, 1378, 25374, 315, 4017, 3015, 1119, 264, 19212, 13, 1096, 1714, 4278, 438, 264, 21700, 34194, 261, 429, 28160, 86480, 812, 2041, 668, 81, 2228, 279, 14961, 13, 1416, 697, 14961, 374, 33404, 86480, 3304, 11, 279, 12021, 8885, 1265, 1896, 279, 86480, 812, 1290, 1007, 13, 1416, 498, 2299, 15826, 264, 2696, 315, 30717, 518, 3055, 11, 498, 646, 990, 803, 3015, 13, 1752, 1101, 825, 6573, 315, 30717, 11, 990, 2686, 3015, 13, 1416, 697, 30717, 702, 18747, 32510, 11, 1281, 2704, 807, 2765, 944, 387, 11495, 553, 1660, 84077, 304, 264, 12021, 12987, 6291, 13, 151645], "pred": "4. For silver jewelry, you can pour two cups of hot water into a bowl. This method works as a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your silver is lightly tarnished, the salt bath should take the tarnish right off. If you're cleaning a lot of jewelry at once, you can use more water. For just one piece of jewelry, use less water. If your jewelry has gemstones, make sure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.78391631785438, "g_worst_claim": 0.4331065813700358, "g_content": 2.327735195640756, "g_traj_mean": 1.7975720167160034}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 311, 4240, 86480, 3304, 30717, 518, 2114, 11, 498, 646, 990, 279, 2701, 5413, 1447, 16, 13, 1752, 3589, 429, 52