{"id": "451", "question": "how is a caldera different from a crater", "gt": "A caldera is a large depression created by volcanic activity when a chamber of magma and lava empties, causing the overlying rocks to collapse. It is formed by the collapsing of the ground. On the other hand, a crater is a bowl-shaped structure at the top of a volcano around the opening used for the eruption of magma and lava. It is formed by the sinking in of rocks due to high pressure.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, a caldera is different from a crater in how they are formed. Craters are caused by impacts from meteorites and can range in size from very small to several hundred km across. In contrast, a caldera is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, a caldera is different from a crater in how they are formed. Craters are caused by impacts from meteorites and can range in size from very small to several hundred km across. In contrast, a caldera is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, a caldera is different from a crater in how they are formed. Craters are caused by impacts from meteorites and can range in size from very small to several hundred kilometers across. In contrast, calderas are formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, resulting in a large, circular depression.\", \"Based on the given passages, a caldera is different from a crater in how they are formed. Craters are caused by impacts from meteorites and can range in size from very small to several hundred km across. In contrast, a caldera is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses.\", \"Based on the given passages:\\n\\nA caldera and a crater differ in their formation processes. A caldera is a large, circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, resulting from volcanic activity. The size of a caldera is typically at least one kilometer across.\\n\\nA crater, on the other hand, is formed by the impact of meteorites. Craters can vary in size, ranging from very small to several hundred kilometers across.\\n\\nThe main difference between a caldera and a crater is their origin. Calderas are created by volcanic processes, whereas craters are formed by meteorite impacts.\", \"Based on the given passages, a caldera is different from a crater in how they are formed. Craters are caused by impacts from meteorites and can range in size from very small to several hundred kilometers across. In contrast, calderas are formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses. This creates a large, circular depression that can be at least one kilometer across.\"]", "time_sec": 15.31, "generation_time_seconds": 14.5171, "selection_time_seconds": 0.7848, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 264, 1620, 97903, 374, 2155, 504, 264, 74707, 304, 1246, 807, 525, 14122, 13, 4553, 11535, 525, 8881, 553, 24905, 504, 41042, 3611, 323, 646, 2088, 304, 1379, 504, 1602, 2613, 311, 3807, 7739, 40568, 3941, 13, 758, 12872, 11, 1620, 1107, 300, 525, 14122, 518, 279, 1909, 315, 264, 65206, 979, 279, 63505, 24220, 86358, 11, 12942, 304, 264, 3460, 11, 26931, 18210, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, a caldera is different from a crater in how they are formed. Craters are caused by impacts from meteorites and can range in size from very small to several hundred kilometers across. In contrast, calderas are formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, resulting in a large, circular depression.", "gr