ater is their origin. Calderas are created by volcanic processes, whereas craters are formed by meteorite impacts.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.5986496900282209, "g_worst_claim": -0.01614025660923549, "g_content": 0.7923128897683662, "g_traj_mean": 0.5512197017669678}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 264, 1620, 97903, 374, 2155, 504, 264, 74707, 304, 1246, 807, 525, 14122, 13, 4553, 11535, 525, 8881, 553, 24905, 504, 41042, 3611, 323, 646, 2088, 304, 1379, 504, 1602, 2613, 311, 3807, 7739, 40568, 3941, 13, 758, 12872, 11, 1620, 1107, 300, 525, 14122, 518, 279, 1909, 315, 264, 65206, 979, 279, 63505, 24220, 86358, 13, 1096, 11450, 264, 3460, 11, 26931, 18210, 429, 646, 387, 518, 3245, 825, 15045, 20408, 3941, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, a caldera is different from a crater in how they are formed. Craters are caused by impacts from meteorites and can range in size from very small to several hundred kilometers across. In contrast, calderas are formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses. This creates a large, circular depression that can be at least one kilometer across.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.8607983654976298, "g_worst_claim": 0.18702727556228638, "g_content": 1.2102102703518338, "g_traj_mean": 1.0381155014038086}}], "selected_rollout": 1, "random_sel_rollout": 1, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 28.21, "GAVIE-Rel": 5.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 3.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "452", "question": "how is a caldera different from a crater", "gt": "Based on the given passages, a caldera is a large depression made due to volcanic activity, while a volcanic crater is a bowl-like structure at the top of a volcano that allows for the eruption of magma and lava. The primary difference between the two is the method of formation. Calderas are formed when a magma chamber collapses, resulting in a large depression, while craters are formed by impacts from meteorites.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, a caldera is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses. A crater, on the other hand, is caused by impacts from meteorites. Craters can range in size from very small to several hundred km across. The main difference between craters and calderas is how they are formed.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, a caldera is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses. A crater, on the other hand, is caused by impacts from meteorites. Craters can range in size from very small to several hundred km across. The main difference between craters and calderas is how they are formed.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, a caldera is different from a crater in how they are formed. Craters are caused by impacts from meteorites and can range in size from very small to several hundred kilometers across. In contrast, calderas are formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, resulting in a large, circular depression.\", \"Based on the given passages, a caldera is different from a crater in how they are formed. Calders are formed by the collapse of a magma chamber underground, while craters are caused by impacts from meteorites. The main difference is that calders are typically formed by volcanic activity, whereas craters are formed by external impacts.\", \"Based on the given passages, a caldera is different from a crater in terms of their formation processes. A caldera is formed by the collapse of a magma chamber, resulting in a large, circular depression at the top of a volcano, typically at least one kilometer across. This process is due to the emptying of a large cavity underground that creates pressure, causing the overlying rocks to collapse.\\n\\nIn contrast, a crater is formed by impacts from meteorites, ranging in size from very small to several hundred kilometers across. It is a depression at the top of a volcano, used for the eruption of magma and lava, and is a result of the sinking in of rocks due to high pressure. The main difference between the two is their origin: calderas are formed by volcanic activity, while craters are caused by meteorite impacts.\", \"Based on the given passages, a caldera is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses. A crater, on the other hand, is caused by impacts from meteorites. Craters can range in size from very small to several hundred km across. The main difference between craters and calderas is how they are formed.\"]", "time_sec": 16.771, "generation_time_seconds": 15.8834, "selection_time_seconds": 0.8806, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 264, 1620, 97903, 374, 2155, 504, 264, 74707, 304, 1246, 807, 525, 14122, 13, 4553, 11535, 525, 8881, 553, 24905, 504, 41042, 3611, 323, 646, 2088, 304, 1379, 504, 1602, 2613, 311, 3807, 7739, 40568, 3941, 13, 758, 12872, 11, 1620, 1107, 300, 525, 14122, 518, 279, 1909, 315, 264, 65206, 979, 279, 63505, 24220, 86358, 11, 12942, 304, 264, 3460, 11, 26931, 18210, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, a caldera is different from a crater in how they are formed. Craters are caused by impacts from meteorites and can range in size from very small to several hundred kilometers across. In contrast, calderas are formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, resulting in a large, circular depression.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.8472780794448482, "g_worst_claim": 0.3735511302947998, "g_content": 1.3278112103862147, "g_traj_mean": 0.993188202381134}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 264, 1620, 97903, 374, 2155, 504, 264, 74707, 304, 1246, 807, 525, 14122, 13, 3319, 12321, 525, 14122, 553, 279, 18179, 315, 264, 63505, 24220, 25260, 11, 1393, 1560, 11535, 525, 8881, 553, 24905, 504, 41042, 3611, 13, 576, 1887, 6672, 374, 429, 1620, 12321, 525, 11136, 14122, 553, 66064, 5702, 11, 19853, 1560, 11535, 525, 14122, 553, 9250, 24905, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, a caldera is different from a crater in how they are formed. Calders are formed by the collapse of a magma chamber underground, while craters are caused by impacts from meteorites. The main difference is that calders are typically formed by volcanic activity, whereas craters are formed by external impacts.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.502195224070534, "g_worst_claim": 0.17727453178829616, "g_content": 0.9855567011339911, "g_traj_mean": 0.5087037682533264}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 264, 1620, 97903, 374, 2155, 504, 264, 74707, 304, 3793, 315, 862, 18003, 11364, 13, 362, 1620, 97903, 374, 14122, 553, 279, 18179, 315, 264, 63505, 24220, 11, 12942, 304, 264, 3460, 11, 26931, 18210, 518, 279, 1909, 315, 264, 65206, 11, 11136, 518, 3245, 825, 15045, 20408, 3941, 13, 1096, 1882, 374, 4152, 311, 279, 4287, 287, 315, 264, 3460, 55329, 25260, 429, 11450, 7262, 11, 14381, 279, 916, 6711, 23035, 311, 18179, 382, 641, 12872, 11, 264, 74707, 374, 14122, 553, 24905, 504, 41042, 3611, 11, 23994, 304, 1379, 504, 1602, 2613, 311, 3807, 7739, 40568, 3941, 13, 1084, 374, 264, 18210, 518, 279, 1909, 315, 264, 65206, 11, 1483, 369, 279, 79402, 315, 63505, 323, 57568, 11, 323, 374, 264, 1102, 315, 279, 61093, 304, 315, 23035, 4152, 311, 1550, 7262, 13, 576, 1887, 6672, 1948, 279, 1378, 374, 862, 6238, 25, 1620, 1107, 300, 525, 14122, 553, 66064, 5702, 11, 1393, 1560, 11535, 525, 8881, 553, 41042, 632, 24905, 13, 151645], "pred": "In contrast, a crater is formed by impacts from meteorites, ranging in size from very small to several hundred kilometers across. It is a depression at the top of a volcano, used for the eruption of magma and lava, and is a result of the sinking in of rocks due to high pressure. The main difference between the two is their origin: calderas are formed by volcanic activity, while craters are caused by meteorite impacts.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.9703206286746849, "g_worst_claim": 0.259370231628418, "g_content": 1.672787331246041, "g_traj_mean": 0.9662106037139893}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 264, 1620, 97903, 374, 264, 3460, 320, 266, 3245, 825, 13136, 3941, 701, 26931, 18210, 14122, 518, 279, 1909, 315, 264, 65206, 979, 279, 63505, 24220, 86358, 13, 362, 74707, 11, 389, 279, 1008, 1424, 11, 374, 8881, 553, 24905, 504, 41042, 3611, 13, 4553, 11535, 646, 2088, 304, 1379, 504, 1602, 2613, 311, 3807, 7739, 13136, 3941, 13, 576, 1887, 6672, 1948, 1560, 11535, 323, 1620, 1107, 300, 374, 1246, 807, 525, 14122, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, a caldera is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses. A crater, on the other hand, is caused by impacts from meteorites. Craters can range in size from very small to several hundred km across. The main difference between craters and calderas is how they are formed.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.3844608057258476, "g_worst_claim": 0.3988282283147176, "g_content": 1.6902811262342665, "g_traj_mean": 1.2632977962493896}}], "selected_rollout": 3, "random_sel_rollout": 3, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 52.38, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "460", "question": "how the colonists respond to the stamp act. why was it so upsetting?", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the colonists responded to the Stamp Act of 1765 with a mix of violent and non-violent protests. They held peaceful protests, chanting slogans such as \"No Taxation without Representation,\" but these were often met with violence from British soldiers, known as Red Coats, who would come to cause fights and make arrests. The colonists also organized public protests, legislative resolutions, town meetings, and boycotts of British goods throughout the colonies. The Stamp Act was seen as particularly egregious because it was the first direct tax imposed by Parliament on the colonies, and it required the use of tax-stamped paper for all newspapers, legal documents, and other printed materials. The colonists felt that this was an overreach of British authority and a violation of their rights as British subjects. The Act was eventually repealed in 1766, but it had already sparked widespread outrage and resistance among the colonists, contributing to the growing tensions that would eventually lead to the American Revolution.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, the colonists responded to the Stamp Act of 1765 in various ways, both violent and non-violent. The initial reaction started with peaceful protests such as holding signs and chanting slogans like \"No Taxation without Representation.\" However, these peaceful protests often escalated into confrontations with British soldiers, leading to arrests. The Stamp Act was seen as a significant infringement on the colonists' rights by imposing direct taxation without their consent, which was a major point of contention.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the colonists responded to the Stamp Act of 1765 in various ways, both violent and non-violent. The initial reaction started with peaceful protests such as holding signs and chanting slogans like \"No Taxation without Representation.\" However, these peaceful protests often escalated into confrontations with British soldiers, leading to arrests. The Stamp Act was seen as a significant infringement on the colonists' rights by imposing direct taxation without their consent, which was a major point of contention.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, the colonists responded to the Stamp Act in various ways, both violent and non-violent. They initially protested peacefully by holding signs and chanting slogans like \\\"No Taxation without Representation.\\\" However, some of the Red Coats would come along to cause a fight and get people arrested. The Stamp Act was so upsetting because it was the first direct tax imposed on the colonists, requiring the use of tax-stamped paper for all newspapers, legal documents, etc. This was seen as a violation of their rights and a way for the British government to exert control over the colonies.\", \"Based on the given passages, the colonists responded to the Stamp Act of 1765 in various ways, both violent and non-violent. Peaceful protests were common, such as holding signs and chanting \\\"No Taxation without Representation.\\\" However, some colonists escalated the situation by violently confronting the British troops, resulting in arrests. The Stamp Act was particularly upsetting because it was the first direct tax imposed on the colonists, requiring them to use tax-stamped paper for newspapers, legal documents, and other items. This was seen as a direct infringement on their rights and freedoms.\", \"Based on the given passages, the colonists responded to the Stamp Act in various ways, both violent and non-violent. They often began with peaceful protests, such as holding signs and chanting \\\"No Taxation without Representation.\\\" However, disputes would escalate, with some Red Coats arriving to cause fights and arrests. This act was particularly upsetting because it was the first direct tax imposed on the colonists, requiring them to use tax-stamped paper for all newspapers and legal documents. The requirement for tax-stamped paper represented a significant intrusion into their privacy and autonomy. Additionally, the Stamp Act was passed by Parliament to fund the maintenance of British troops in the colonies after the French and Indian War, which further fueled the colonists' resentment towards British control and taxation policies.\", \"Based on the given passages, the colonists responded to the Stamp Act of 1765 in various ways, both violent and non-violent. The initial reaction started with peaceful protests such as holding signs and chanting slogans like \\\"No Taxation without Representation.\\\" However, these peaceful protests often escalated into confrontations with British soldiers, leading to arrests. The Stamp Act was seen as a significant infringement on the colonists' rights by imposing direct taxation without their consent, which was a major point of contention.\"]", "time_sec": 18.741, "generation_time_seconds": 17.9791, "selection_time_seconds": 0.756, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 14889, 1671, 16441, 311, 279, 54787, 3218, 304, 5257, 5510, 11, 2176, 16401, 323, 2477, 75727, 306, 13, 2379, 15102, 69405, 66779, 553, 9963, 11929, 323, 76574, 77001, 1075, 330, 2753, 15190, 367, 2041, 78046, 1189, 4354, 11, 1045, 315, 279, 3731, 3539, 1862, 1035, 2525, 3156, 311, 5240, 264, 4367, 323, 633, 1251, 12517, 13, 576, 54787, 3218, 572, 773, 89969, 1576, 432, 572, 279, 1156, 2118, 3742, 25980, 389, 279, 14889, 1671, 11, 22703, 279, 990, 315, 3742, 5477, 32198, 5567, 369, 678, 31494, 11, 5777, 9293, 11, 4992, 13, 1096, 572, 3884, 438, 264, 19940, 315, 862, 3188, 323, 264, 1616, 369, 279, 7855, 3033, 311, 42744, 2524, 916, 279, 47928, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, the colonists responded to the Stamp Act in various ways, both violent and non-violent. They initially protested peacefully by holding signs and chanting slogans like \"No Taxation without Representation.\" However, some of the Red Coats would come along to cause a fight and get people arrested. The Stamp Act was so upsetting because it was the first direct tax imposed on the colonists, requiring the use of tax-stamped paper for all newspapers, legal documents, etc. This was seen as a violation of their rights and a way for the British government to exert control over the colonies.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.1489179445847606, "g_worst_claim": -0.1000475287437439, "g_content": 1.9904674802507674, "g_traj_mean": 1.2749786376953125}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 14889, 1671, 16441, 311, 279, 54787, 3218, 315, 220, 16, 22, 21, 20, 304, 5257, 5510, 11, 2176, 16401, 323, 2477, 75727, 306, 13, 25803, 1262, 21907, 1033, 4185, 11, 1741, 438, 9963, 11929, 323, 76574, 330, 2753, 15190, 367, 2041, 78046, 1189, 4354, 11, 1045, 14889, 1671, 80600, 279, 6534, 553, 64200, 74767, 279, 7855, 16886, 11, 12942, 304, 37711, 13, 576, 54787, 3218, 572, 7945, 89969, 1576, 432, 572, 279, 1156, 2118, 3742, 25980, 389, 279, 14889, 1671, 11, 22703, 1105, 311, 990, 3742, 5477, 32198, 5567, 369, 31494, 11, 5777, 9293, 11, 323, 1008, 3589, 13, 1096, 572, 3884, 438, 264, 2118, 53343, 389, 862, 3188, 323, 59977, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, the colonists responded to the Stamp Act of 1765 in various ways, both violent and non-violent. Peaceful protests were common, such as holding signs and chanting \"No Taxation without Representation.\" However, some colonists escalated the situation by violently confronting the British troops, resulting in arrests. The Stamp Act was particularly upsetting because it was the first direct tax imposed on the colonists, requiring them to use tax-stamped paper for newspapers, legal documen