15423, 825, 315, 697, 23150, 438, 825, 315, 862, 26574, 13, 2014, 653, 419, 11, 728, 311, 6271, 905, 11, 1487, 304, 448, 697, 6271, 5934, 323, 3552, 11, 4205, 389, 697, 6271, 829, 11, 7407, 389, 279, 8416, 1290, 1424, 9131, 315, 697, 2692, 594, 5379, 2150, 11, 4205, 389, 279, 11296, 2999, 429, 7952, 304, 279, 5943, 14875, 5022, 11, 323, 4205, 389, 279, 6303, 52938, 2656, 518, 279, 1909, 315, 279, 4171, 13, 151645], "pred": "1. Use a website like Favotter.net or Favstar.fm to track who is favoriting your tweets.\n2. These two sites allow you to see who is favoriting your tweets and which tweets that you and others are also favoriting.\n3. You can also set up your Twitter account so that you're notified via email whenever someone marks one of your tweets as one of their favorites. To do this, go to Twitter.com, log in with your Twitter username and password, click on your Twitter name, located on the upper right hand corner of your account's Home page, click on the Settings option that appears in the drop-down menu, and click on the blue Notifications link at the top of the screen.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.41954270310091, "g_worst_claim": 0.28290163386951794, "g_content": 1.91497562953404, "g_traj_mean": 1.50203275680542}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 498, 646, 3291, 879, 14915, 697, 11809, 553, 1667, 13037, 1075, 80941, 354, 465, 5071, 323, 80941, 11870, 70485, 13, 4220, 6594, 2138, 498, 311, 3754, 879, 374, 4694, 5853, 697, 23150, 476, 14890, 279, 1852, 23150, 438, 498, 13, 22406, 11, 498, 646, 738, 705, 2551, 21969, 389, 6271, 773, 429, 498, 525, 29216, 4566, 2551, 15356, 4325, 15423, 825, 315, 697, 23150, 438, 264, 6930, 13, 13655, 304, 3971, 429, 421, 498, 2299, 1602, 5411, 389, 6271, 11, 498, 2578, 5258, 264, 2696, 315, 2551, 21969, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, you can tell who liked your tweet by using websites like Favotter.net and Favstar.fm. These sites allow you to track who is favoriting your tweets or preferred the same tweets as you. Additionally, you can set up email notifications on Twitter so that you are notified via email whenever someone marks one of your tweets as a favorite. Keep in mind that if you're very popular on Twitter, you might receive a lot of email notifications.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.443137329059934, "g_worst_claim": 0.30309834082921344, "g_content": 2.0698406219482424, "g_traj_mean": 1.5698366165161133}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 498, 646, 3291, 879, 14915, 697, 11809, 553, 1667, 13037, 1075, 80941, 354, 465, 5071, 476, 80941, 11870, 70485, 13, 4220, 13037, 2138, 498, 311, 3754, 892, 23150, 498, 323, 3800, 614, 4694, 1608, 11, 7086, 498, 20017, 1119, 879, 374, 8014, 304, 697, 2213, 323, 10476, 498, 1477, 1008, 3847, 311, 1795, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, you can tell who liked your tweet by using websites like Favotter.net or Favstar.fm. These websites allow you to track which tweets you and others have favorited, giving you insight into who is interested in your content and helping you find other users to follow.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.2725448895964706, "g_worst_claim": 0.3050640821456909, "g_content": 1.820279541015625, "g_traj_mean": 1.471313238143921}}], "selected_rollout": 0, "random_sel_rollout": 3, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 35.11, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "493", "question": "how can you tell who liked your tweet", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is how you can tell who liked your tweet:\n\nPassage 1 suggests using websites such as Favotter.net and Favstar.fm to track who has favorited your tweets. These websites show you a list of users who have favorited your tweets, allowing you to see who has shown interest in your content.\n\nPassage 2 provides a step-by-step guide on how to track who favorited your tweets. According to the passage, you can use a simple web-based website to check who favorited your tweets. Once you have access to this website, you can enter your tweet's URL to view a list of users who have favorited it.\n\nPassage 3 does not provide any information on tracking who favorited your tweets. However, it does mention that you can set up your Twitter account to notify you via email whenever someone marks one of your tweets as one of their favorites.\n\nIn summary, to tell who liked your tweet, you can use websites like Favotter.net and Favstar.fm, or set up your Twitter account to notify you via email whenever someone marks one of your tweets as one of their favorites.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, you can tell who liked your tweet by using a website that can check Twitterers who favorite tweets. Additionally, you can set up your account on Twitter to receive email notifications whenever someone marks one of your tweets as a favorite.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, you can tell who liked your tweet by using a website that can check Twitterers who favorite tweets. Additionally, you can set up your account on Twitter to receive email notifications whenever someone marks one of your tweets as a favorite.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, you can tell who liked your tweet by using a website that tracks favorited tweets. One such website mentioned is Favotter.net. To use this website, you need to go to Twitter.com, log in with your Twitter username and password, click on your Twitter name located on the upper right hand corner of your account's Home page, click on the Settings option that appears in the drop-down menu, and then click on the blue Notifications link at the top of the screen. This will allow you to set up your account so that you're notified via email whenever someone marks one of your tweets as one of their favorites.\", \"Based on the given passages, you can tell who liked your tweet by visiting a website that tracks favorite tweets, such as Favotter.net or Favstar.fm. These websites allow you to see who has marked your tweets as favorites. Additionally, you can set up notifications on your Twitter account so that you're notified via email whenever someone marks one of your tweets as a favorite.\", \"Based on the given passages, you can tell who liked your tweet by using websites such as Favotter.net and Favstar.fm. These sites allow you to track who has favorited your tweets. Additionally, you can set up your Twitter account to receive email notifications whenever someone marks one of your tweets as a favorite. If you're very popular on Twitter, you may receive a lot of email notifications.\", \"Based on the given passages, you can tell who liked your tweet by using a website that can check Twitterers who favorite tweets. Additionally, you can set up your account on Twitter to receive email notifications whenever someone marks one of your tweets as a favorite.\"]", "time_sec": 14.631, "generation_time_seconds": 13.9109, "selection_time_seconds": 0.7153, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 498, 646, 3291, 879, 14915, 697, 11809, 553, 1667, 264, 3910, 429, 13918, 4694, 1608, 23150, 13, 3776, 1741, 3910, 9733, 374, 80941, 354, 465, 5071, 13, 2014, 990, 419, 3910, 11, 498, 1184, 311, 728, 311, 6271, 905, 11, 1487, 304, 448, 697, 6271, 5934, 323, 3552, 11, 4205, 389, 697, 6271, 829, 7407, 389, 279, 8416, 1290, 1424, 9131, 315, 697, 2692, 594, 5379, 2150, 11, 4205, 389, 279, 11296, 2999, 429, 7952, 304, 279, 5943, 14875, 5022, 11, 323, 1221, 4205, 389, 279, 6303, 52938, 2656, 518, 279, 1909, 315, 279, 4171, 13, 1096, 686, 2138, 498, 311, 738, 705, 697, 2692, 773, 429, 498, 2299, 29216, 4566, 2551, 15356, 4325, 15423, 825, 315, 697, 23150, 438, 825, 315, 862, 26574, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, you can tell who liked your tweet by using a website that tracks favorited tweets. One such website mentioned is Favotter.net. To use this website, you need to go to Twitter.com, log in with your Twitter username and password, click on your Twitter name located on the upper right hand corner of your account's Home page, click on the Settings option that appears in the drop-down menu, and then click on the blue Notifications link at the top of the screen. This will allow you to set up your account so that you're notified via email whenever someone marks one of your tweets as one of their favorites.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.4976762435948647, "g_worst_claim": 0.2851632634798686, "g_content": 2.5180285135904947, "g_traj_mean": 1.6307648420333862}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 498, 646, 3291, 879, 14915, 697, 11809, 553, 16721, 264, 3910, 429, 13918, 6930, 23150, 11, 1741, 438, 80941, 354, 465, 5071, 476, 80941, 11870, 70485, 13, 4220, 13037, 2138, 498, 311, 1490, 879, 702, 12864, 697, 23150, 438, 26574, 13, 22406, 11, 498, 646, 738, 705, 21969, 389, 697, 6271, 2692, 773, 429, 498, 2299, 29216, 4566, 2551, 15356, 4325, 15423, 825, 315, 697, 23150, 438, 264, 6930, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, you can tell who liked your tweet by visiting a website that tracks favorite tweets, such as Favotter.net or Favstar.fm. These websites allow you to see who has marked your tweets as favorites. Additionally, you can set up notifications on your Twitter account so that you're notified via email whenever someone marks one of your tweets as a favorite.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.2650353392961755, "g_worst_claim": 0.28460445006688434, "g_content": 1.543291449546814, "g_traj_mean": 1.2300711870193481}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 498, 646, 3291, 879, 14915, 697, 11809, 553, 1667, 13037, 1741, 438, 80941, 354, 465, 5071, 323, 80941, 11870, 70485, 13, 4220, 6594, 2138, 498, 311, 3754, 879, 702, 4694, 1608, 697, 23150, 13, 22406, 11, 498, 646, 738, 705, 697, 6271, 2692, 311, 5258, 2551, 21969, 15356, 4325, 15423, 825, 315, 697, 23150, 438, 264, 6930, 13, 1416, 498, 2299, 1602, 5411, 389, 6271, 11, 498, 1231, 5258, 264, 2696, 315, 2551, 21969, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, you can tell who liked your tweet by using websites such as Favotter.net and Favstar.fm. These sites allow you to track who has favorited your tweets. Additionally, you can set up your Twitter account to receive email notifications whenever someone marks one of your tweets as a favorite. If you're very popular on Twitter, you may receive a lot of email notifications.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.4446393822842516, "g_worst_claim": 0.28460445006688434, "g_content": 2.32408613317153, "g_traj_mean": 1.5723956823349}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 498, 646, 3291, 879, 14915, 697, 11809, 553, 1667, 264, 3910, 429, 646, 1779, 6271, 388, 879, 6930, 23150, 13, 22406, 11, 498, 646, 738, 705, 697, 2692, 389, 6271, 311, 5258, 2551, 21969, 15356, 4325, 15423, 825, 315, 697, 23150, 438, 264, 6930, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, you can tell who liked your tweet by using a website that can check Twitterers who favorite tweets. Additionally, you can set up your account on Twitter to receive email notifications whenever someone marks one of your tweets as a favorite.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.3548405782381694, "g_worst_claim": 0.2865915894508362, "g_content": 2.3086020151774087, "g_traj_mean": 1.615098237991333}}], "selected_rollout": 0, "random_sel_rollout": 3, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 32.09, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "495", "question": "salary exchange pension contributions", "gt": "Salary exchange pension contributions, also known as salary sacrifice, is a method where an employee agrees to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment paid by their employer. This can allow both employers and employees to save money. The salary that is waived under the salary exchange is equal to the value of the regular pension contribution made. For example, it could be 7.5% or 9% of the pay, depending on the pension scheme. The employer then pays a corresponding amount into the relevant pension scheme.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment paid by their employer. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money. Employers pay lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) as a result of paying their employees a reduced salary. The value of the salary waived under the Salary Exchange is equal to the value of the employee's regular pension contribution to USS, USPAS, or SGSS, excluding additional contributions. For example, for members of USS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 October 2011 is 7.5% of their pay, and for members of USPAS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 August 2011 is 9%.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment paid by their employer. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money. Employers pay lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) as a result of paying their employees a reduced salary. The value of the salary waived under the Salary Exchange is equal to the value of the employee's regular pension contribution to USS, USPAS, or SGSS, excluding additional contributions. For example, for members of USS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 October 2011 is 7.5% of their pay, and for members of USPAS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 August 2011 is 9%.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment paid by their employer. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money. Employers pay lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) as a result of paying their employees a reduced salary. The value of the salary waived under the Salary Exchange is equal to the value of the employee's regular pension contribution to USS, USPAS, or SGSS, excluding additional contributions. For example, for members of USS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 October 2011 is 7.5% of their pay, and for members of USPAS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 August 2011 is 9%.\", \"Based on the given passages, salary exchange pension contributions involve individuals giving up part of their salary in exchange for a non-cash benefit, typically a pension contribution. This arrangement allows employers to reduce their National Insurance Contributions (NICs) by paying reduced salaries to their employees. In the case of the University, members of USS contribute 7.5% of their pay, while USPAS members contribute 9% of their pay. These contributions are then matched by the university into the respective pension schemes.\", \"Based on the given passages, salary exchange allows both employers and employees to save money by reducing their salary in exchange for pension contributions. The employer pays lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) and the employee contributes to their pension scheme. For example, for members of USS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 October 2011 will be 7.5% of the pay; for members of USPAS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 August 2011 is 9%.\", \"Salary exchange pension contributions involve an arrangement where an employee agrees to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment paid by their employer. The employer pays lower NICs as a result of paying their employees a reduced salary. Employers pay a corresponding amount into the relevant pension scheme as a non-cash benefit. For example, for members of USS, the regular contribution is 7.5% of the pay, and for members of USPAS, it is 9%.\"]", "time_sec": 18.386, "generation_time_seconds": 17.6144, "selection_time_seconds": 0.7671, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 16107, 9289, 27681, 19026, 21058, 458, 9364, 38327, 311, 2968, 705, 949, 315, 862, 16107, 476, 12037, 304, 9289, 369, 264, 27681, 8160, 7171, 553, 862, 19136, 13, 1096, 26112, 6147, 2176, 22426, 323, 8256, 311, 3581, 3220, 13, 20782, 388, 2291, 4722, 5055, 21674, 96004, 320, 44947, 82, 8, 438, 264, 1102, 315, 12515, 862, 8256, 264, 11046, 16107, 13, 576, 897, 315, 279, 16107, 81482, 1212, 279, 41758, 18705, 374, 6144, 311, 279, 897, 315, 279, 9364, 594, 5792, 27681, 18527, 311, 38508, 11, 2274, 47, 1911, 11, 476, 29626, 1220, 11, 43778, 5107, 19026, 13, 1752, 3110, 11, 369, 3613, 315, 38508, 11, 279, 5792, 18527, 448, 2456, 504, 220, 16, 6527, 220, 17, 15, 16, 16, 374, 220, 22, 13, 20, 4, 315, 862, 2291, 11, 323, 369, 3613, 315, 2274, 47, 1911, 11, 279, 5792, 18527, 448, 2456, 504, 220, 16, 6156, 220, 17, 15, 16, 16, 374, 220, 24, 14360, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment paid by their employer. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money. Employers pay lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) as a result of paying their employees a reduced salary. The value of the salary waived under the Salary Exchange is equal to the value of the employee's regular pension contribution to USS, USPAS, or SGSS, excluding additional contributions. For example, for members of USS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 October 2011 is 7.5% of their pay, and for members of USPAS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 August 2011 is 9%.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.7226864609952273, "g_worst_claim": 0.4708307981491089, "g_content": 2.494851726375214, "g_traj_mean": 1.7502542734146118}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 16107, 9289, 27681, 19026, 21058, 7775, 7086, 705, 949, 315, 862, 16107, 304, 9289, 369, 264, 2477, 1786, 988, 8760, 11, 11136, 264, 27681, 18527, 13, 1096, 26112, 6147, 22426, 311, 7949, 862, 5055, 21674, 96004, 320, 44947, 82, 8, 553, 12515, 11046, 36432, 311, 862, 8256, 13, 758, 279, 1142, 315, 279, 3822, 11, 3613, 315, 38508, 16792, 220, 22, 13, 20, 4, 315, 862, 2291, 11, 1393, 2274, 47, 1911, 3613, 16792, 220, 24, 4, 315, 862, 2291, 13, 4220, 19026, 525, 1221, 18054, 553, 279, 12103, 1119, 279, 19511, 27681, 30856, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, salary exchange pension contributions involve individuals giving up part of their salary in exchange for a non-cash benefit, typically a pension contribution. This arrangement allows employers to reduce their National Insurance Contributions (NICs) by paying reduced salaries to their employees. In the case of the University, members of USS contribute 7.5% of their pay, while USPAS members contribute 9% of their pay. These contributions are then matched by the university into the respective pension schemes.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.6035888406489107, "g_worst_claim": 0.4602554639180501, "g_content": 2.2394261360168457, "g_traj_mean": 1.5119071006774902}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 