{"id": "497", "question": "salary exchange pension contributions", "gt": "Based on the given passages, it appears that salary exchange is a way for employees to give up part of their salary in exchange for a pension contribution made by their employer. The salary that is waived is equal to the value of the employee's regular pension contribution, minus any additional contributions. The employer then pays the corresponding amount into the relevant pension scheme. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to potentially save money through reduced national insurance contributions.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment paid by their employer. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money. Employers pay lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) as a result of paying their employees a reduced salary. The value of the salary waived under the Salary Exchange is equal to the value of the employee's regular pension contribution to USS, USPAS, or SGSS, excluding additional contributions. For example, for members of USS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 October 2011 is 7.5% of their pay, and for members of USPAS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 August 2011 is 9%.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment paid by their employer. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money. Employers pay lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) as a result of paying their employees a reduced salary. The value of the salary waived under the Salary Exchange is equal to the value of the employee's regular pension contribution to USS, USPAS, or SGSS, excluding additional contributions. For example, for members of USS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 October 2011 is 7.5% of their pay, and for members of USPAS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 August 2011 is 9%.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment paid by their employer. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money. Employers pay lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) as a result of paying their employees a reduced salary. The value of the salary waived under the Salary Exchange is equal to the value of the employee's regular pension contribution to USS, USPAS, or SGSS, excluding additional contributions. For example, for members of USS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 October 2011 is 7.5% of their pay, and for members of USPAS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 August 2011 is 9%.\", \"Salary exchange pension contributions involve giving up part of one's salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment paid by the employer. This arrangement results in employers paying lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) as they pay their employees a reduced salary. Examples of pension schemes include USS, USPAS, and SGSS. For instance, for USS members, a regular contribution of 7.5% of pay is waived, and the university contributes a corresponding amount into the relevant pension scheme.\", \"Based on the given passages, salary exchange is a pension contribution arrangement where an employee agrees to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment paid by their employer. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money. Employers pay lower NICs as a result of paying their employees a reduced salary. The corresponding amount paid by the employer into the relevant pension scheme is equal to the value of the employee's regular pension contribution to USS, USPAS, or SGSS. For example, for members of USS, the regular contribution is 7.5% of the pay, and for members of USPAS, it is 9%.\", \"Salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee giving up part of their salary in exchange for a pension payment paid by their employer. For example, for members of USS, your regular contribution with effect from 1 October 2011 will be 7.5% of your pay; for members of USPAS, your regular contribution with effect from 1 August 2011 is 9%. This exchange allows both employers and employees to make savings. Employers pay lower NICs as a result of paying their employees a reduced salary.\"]", "time_sec": 19.404, "generation_time_seconds": 18.6267, "selection_time_seconds": 0.7716, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 16107, 9289, 27681, 19026, 21058, 458, 9364, 38327, 311, 2968, 705, 949, 315, 862, 16107, 476, 12037, 304, 9289, 369, 264, 27681, 8160, 7171, 553, 862, 19136, 13, 1096, 26112, 6147, 2176, 22426, 323, 8256, 311, 3581, 3220, 13, 20782, 388, 2291, 4722, 5055, 21674, 96004, 320, 44947, 82, 8, 438, 264, 1102, 315, 12515, 862, 8256, 264, 11046, 16107, 13, 576, 897, 315, 279, 16107, 81482, 1212, 279, 41758, 18705, 374, 6144, 311, 279, 897, 315, 279, 9364, 594, 5792, 27681, 18527, 311, 38508, 11, 2274, 47, 1911, 11, 476, 29626, 1220, 11, 43778, 5107, 19026, 13, 1752, 3110, 11, 369, 3613, 315, 38508, 11, 279, 5792, 18527, 448, 2456, 504, 220, 16, 6527, 220, 17, 15, 16, 16, 374, 220, 22, 13, 20, 4, 315, 862, 2291, 11, 323, 369, 3613, 315, 2274, 47, 1911, 11, 279, 5792, 18527, 448, 2456, 504, 220, 16, 6156, 220, 17, 15, 16, 16, 374, 220, 24, 14360, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment paid by their employer. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money. Employers pay lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) as a result of paying their employees a reduced salary. The value of the salary waived under the Salary Exchange is equal to the value of the employee's regular pension contribution to USS, USPAS, or SGSS, excluding additional contributions. For example, for members of USS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 October 2011 is 7.5% of their pay, and for members of USPAS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 August 2011 is 9%.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.7085605566498177, "g_worst_claim": 0.3955509662628174, "g_content": 2.472156681426584, "g_traj_mean": 1.7408539056777954}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [41673, 9289, 27681, 19026, 21058, 7086, 705, 949, 315, 825, 594, 16107, 476, 12037, 304, 9289, 369, 264, 27681, 8160, 7171, 553, 279, 19136, 13, 1096, 26112, 3059, 304, 22426, 12515, 4722, 5055, 21674, 96004, 320, 44947, 82, 8, 438, 807, 2291, 862, 8256, 264, 11046, 16107, 13, 25311, 315, 27681, 30856, 2924, 38508, 11, 2274, 47, 1911, 11, 323, 29626, 1220, 13, 1752, 2867, 11, 369, 38508, 3613, 11, 264, 5792, 18527, 315, 220, 22, 13, 20, 4, 315, 2291, 374, 81482, 11, 323, 279, 12103, 42972, 264, 12159, 3311, 1119, 279, 9760, 27681, 12859, 13, 151645], "pred": "Salary exchange pension contributions involve giving up part of one's salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment paid by the employer. This arrangement results in employers paying lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) as they pay their employees a reduced salary. Examples of pension schemes include USS, USPAS, and SGSS. For instance, for USS members, a regular contribution of 7.5% of pay is waived, and the university contributes a corresponding amount into the relevant pension scheme.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 2.0064559433863978, "g_worst_claim": 1.6391679382324218, "g_content": 2.7264749145507814, "g_traj_mean": 2.032236576080