, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 6672, 1948, 264, 27048, 385, 258, 48959, 323, 264, 37937, 7675, 48959, 374, 429, 264, 27048, 385, 258, 48959, 374, 3931, 504, 279, 1182, 315, 279, 9864, 11, 11689, 504, 279, 14639, 1182, 13348, 315, 279, 9864, 11, 1393, 264, 37937, 7675, 48959, 374, 3931, 504, 4623, 1182, 389, 279, 27048, 385, 258, 11, 8482, 264, 2699, 803, 315, 279, 27582, 385, 258, 476, 1461, 1149, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, the difference between a sirloin steak and a Porterhouse steak is that a sirloin steak is cut from the back of the animal, specifically from the rear back portion of the animal, while a Porterhouse steak is cut from further back on the sirloin, containing a bit more of the tenderloin or fillet.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.3604865680638927, "g_worst_claim": 0.44339585304260254, "g_content": 2.57254480432581, "g_traj_mean": 1.2433011531829834}}], "selected_rollout": 3, "random_sel_rollout": 0, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 42.42, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "516", "question": "history of the word gemini", "gt": "Sure! Here's a brief history of the word Gemini based on the given passages:\n\nThe word \"Gemini\" comes from Latin, meaning \"twins.\" The constellation Gemini was first mentioned in Aristotle's Meteorologica over 300 years BC. In Greek mythology, the twins represented in Gemini are Castor and Pollux, who share the same mother, Leda, but have different fathers. The constellation Gemini is not in the same position as the constellation of Gemini and is a mutable sign that announces change and rules the time of year when Taurus spring has ended.\n\nI hope this helps! Let me know if you need further clarification or if there's anything else I can help you with.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, the word \"Gemini\" is a Latin word for twins and it is one of the constellations that actually look like its name would suggest. The first known reference of the Gemini constellation was in Aristotle’s Meteorologica, over 300 years BC. He mentions that he observed an occulting a star in Gemini and speaks of observing Jupiter in conjunction with it.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the word \"Gemini\" is a Latin word for twins and it is one of the constellations that actually look like its name would suggest. The first known reference of the Gemini constellation was in Aristotle’s Meteorologica, over 300 years BC. He mentions that he observed an occulting a star in Gemini and speaks of observing Jupiter in conjunction with it.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, the word \\\"Gemini\\\" is a Latin word for twins and it is one of the constellations that actually look like its name would suggest. The first known reference of the Gemini constellation was in Aristotle’s Meteorologica, over 300 years BC. He mentions that he observed an occulting a star in Gemini and speaks of observing Jupiter in conjunction with it.\", \"Based on the given passages, the word \\\"Gemini\\\" has a history that dates back over 300 years BC. It was first mentioned in Aristotle’s Meteorologica, where he observed an occulting star in the Gemini constellation and spoke of observing Jupiter in conjunction with it.\", \"Based on the given passages, the history of the word \\\"Gemini\\\" can be traced back to its Latin origin meaning \\\"twins.\\\" The constellation associated with this word was first mentioned in Aristotle’s Meteorologica, over 300 years BC.\", \"Based on the given passages, the word \\\"Gemini\\\" is a Latin word for twins and it is one of the constellations that actually look like its name would suggest. The first known reference of the Gemini constellation was in Aristotle’s Meteorologica, over 300 years BC. He mentions that he observed an occulting a star in Gemini and speaks of observing Jupiter in conjunction with it.\"]", "time_sec": 12.92, "generation_time_seconds": 12.2435, "selection_time_seconds": 0.6709, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 3409, 330, 95040, 6591, 1, 374, 264, 19458, 3409, 369, 43636, 323, 432, 374, 825, 315, 279, 733, 613, 804, 429, 3520, 1401, 1075, 1181, 829, 1035, 4190, 13, 576, 1156, 3881, 5785, 315, 279, 85711, 82386, 572, 304, 77901, 748, 39632, 1609, 3001, 11, 916, 220, 18, 15, 15, 1635, 18040, 13, 1260, 33845, 429, 566, 13166, 458, 73826, 287, 264, 6774, 304, 85711, 323, 21191, 315, 44971, 49689, 304, 31446, 448, 432, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, the word \"Gemini\" is a Latin word for twins and it is one of the constellations that actually look like its name would suggest. The first known reference of the Gemini constellation was in Aristotle’s Meteorologica, over 300 years BC. He mentions that he observed an occulting a star in Gemini and speaks of observing Jupiter in conjunction with it.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 2.054141808134137, "g_worst_claim": 0.4107789198557536, "g_content": 3.2058904311236214, "g_traj_mean": 2.3897926807403564}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 3409, 330, 95040, 6591, 1, 702, 264, 3840, 429, 12713, 1182, 916, 220, 18, 15, 15, 1635, 18040, 13, 1084, 572, 1156, 9733, 304, 77901, 748, 39632, 1609, 3001, 11, 1380, 566, 13166, 458, 73826, 287, 6774, 304, 279, 85711, 82386, 323, 12290, 315, 44971, 49689, 304, 31446, 448, 432, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, the word \"Gemini\" has a history that dates back over 300 years BC. It was first mentioned in Aristotle’s Meteorologica, where he observed an occulting star in the Gemini constellation and spoke of observing Jupiter in conjunction with it.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.7279213597248126, "g_worst_claim": 0.4107789198557536, "g_content": 3.311941464742025, "g_traj_mean": 2.0526981353759766}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 3840, 315, 279, 3409, 330, 95040, 6591, 1, 646, 387, 50300, 1182, 311, 1181, 19458, 6238, 7290, 330, 15560, 1330, 1189, 576, 82386, 5815, 448, 419, 3409, 572, 1156, 9733, 304, 77901, 748, 39632, 1609, 3001, 11, 916, 220, 18, 15, 15, 1635, 18040, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, the history of the word \"Gemini\" can be traced back to its Latin origin meaning \"twins.\" The constellation associated with this word was first mentioned in Aristotle’s Meteorologica, over 300 years BC.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.372296214800829, "g_worst_claim": 0.4107789198557536, "g_content": 2.2816855389138926, "g_traj_mean": 1.501173734664917}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 3409, 330, 95040, 6591, 1, 374, 264, 19458, 3409, 369, 43636, 323, 432, 374, 825, 315, 279, 733, 613, 804, 429, 3520, 1401, 1075, 1181, 829, 1035, 4190, 13, 576, 1156, 3881, 5785, 315, 279, 85711, 82386, 572, 304, 77901, 748, 39632, 1609, 3001, 11, 916, 220, 18, 15, 15, 1635, 18040, 13, 1260, 33845, 429, 566, 13166, 458, 73826, 287, 264, 6774, 304, 85711, 323, 21191, 315, 44971, 49689, 304, 31446, 448, 432, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, the word \"Gemini\" is a Latin word for twins and it is one of the constellations that actually look like its name would suggest. The first known reference of the Gemini constellation was in Aristotle’s Meteorologica, over 300 years BC. He mentions that he observed an occulting a star in Gemini and speaks of observing Jupiter in conjunction with it.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 2.054141808134137, "g_worst_claim": 0.4107789198557536, "g_content": 3.2058904311236214, "g_traj_mean": 2.3897926807403564}}], "selected_rollout": 1, "random_sel_rollout": 3, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 29.31, "GAVIE-Rel": 7.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 5.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "518", "question": "how do raffles work", "gt": "Raffles work by selling tickets to participants, who have the opportunity to win prizes. The specific details and mechanics of a raffle can vary. In a reverse raffle, participants purchase tickets with numbers on them, and the last ticket drawn wins the main prize. In a 50-50 draw, tickets are sold during an event, and 50% of the proceeds go to the winner of a random draw, while the other 50% is allocated for a charitable purpose. In some cases, raffles may also offer additional opportunities to win prizes, such as cash tip board raffles.", "pred": "3. **Cash Tip Board Raffles (from Passage 3)**: These are additional raffles not included in the ticket price. Participants have the chance to win $10,000 through these raffles.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "3. **Cash Tip Board Raffles (from Passage 3)**: These are additional raffles not included in the ticket price. Participants have the chance to win $10,000 through these raffles.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, raffles work as follows:\\n\\n1. **Reverse Raffle**: In a reverse raffle, participants buy tickets with numbers from 1 to approximately 55. Each ticket costs $50, and a table of 20 individuals constitutes one complete ticket worth $1000. There are two $500 prizes: one for the early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pulled.\\n\\n2. **50-50 Draw**: The 50-50 draw is a raffle held during a single day at a particular event. Tickets are sold to patrons throughout the stadium during the event by roaming staff equipped with handheld electronic mobile devices. The gross proceeds of 50-50 tickets sold at the event go into a prize pool. 50% of the prize pool will go to the winner of the random draw which occurs towards the end of the event. The remaining 50% will be set aside for a charitable purpose chosen by the sporting club.\\n\\n3. **Cash Tip Board Raffles**: These are included with the ticket price and offer the chance to win $10,000.\", \"Based on the given passages, raffles work by selling tickets at a specified price, with a randomly selected ticket being drawn to win a prize. The prize can be a monetary amount or a physical item. These raffles can be conducted in various forms such as a reverse raffle, where tickets are sold in a specific range and prizes are distributed based on the order the tickets are pulled, or a 50-50 draw, where 50% of the proceeds go to a random winner and the remaining 50% goes to a charitable cause. Additionally, there are cash tip board raffles where participants have the chance to win significant cash amounts.\", \"Based on the given passages, raffles work by selling tickets to participants. Each ticket has a unique number. The winner is determined by a random draw, and the prize money is divided between the winner and an additional prize. The specific rules and prizes can vary depending on the type of raffle, such as a reverse raffle, 50-50 draw, or cash tip board raffle.\", \"Based on the given passages, raffles work as follows:\\n\\n1. **Reverse Raffle (from Passage 1)**: Participants buy tickets with numbers ranging from 1 to approximately 55. Each ticket costs $50 and a table consists of 20 individuals, making one complete ticket worth $1000. There are two $500 prizes: one for the early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pulled.\\n\\n2. **50-50 Draw (from Passage 2)**: This is a raffle held during a single day at an event. Tickets are sold to patrons at the stadium, and the proceeds go into a prize pool. Fifty percent of the prize pool goes to the winner, and the remaining fifty percent is set aside for charity.\\n\\n3. **Cash Tip Board Raffles (from Passage 3)**: These are additional raffles not included in the ticket price. Participants have the chance to win $10,000 through these raffles.\"]", "time_sec": 23.068, "generation_time_seconds": 22.3038, "selecti