Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "519", "question": "how do raffles work", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is how raffles work:\n\n1. Reverse Raffle: In this type of raffle, the numbers on the entry tickets are drawn in reverse order, starting with the last number and ending with the first number. For example, if there are 55 entries, the last number drawn wins the grand prize, followed by the next-to-last number, and so on until the first number. Each entry ticket costs $50, and a table of 20 individuals constitutes one complete ticket worth $1,000 and two $500 prizes (for the early bird drawing and the first ticket pulled).\n2. 50-50 Draw: This is a raffle held during a single day at a specific event. Tickets are sold to patrons throughout the event by roving staff with handheld electronic devices. The gross proceeds of 50-50 tickets sold at the event go into a prize pool, and 50% of the prize pool is awarded to the winner of a random draw that takes place at the end of the event. The remaining 50% goes to a charitable purpose chosen by the sporting club.\n3. Additional Raffles: In addition to the main raffle, there may be additional raffles for drinks and dinner. These raffles are not included in the original ticket price and require separate entry fees. One of these raffles offers the chance to win $10,000!\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages.", "pred": "3. **Cash Tip Board Raffles**: These raffles are not included in the ticket price. Participants have the opportunity to win $10,000 through these raffles, which are typically held during events and require separate ticket purchases.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "3. **Cash Tip Board Raffles**: These raffles are not included in the ticket price. Participants have the opportunity to win $10,000 through these raffles, which are typically held during events and require separate ticket purchases.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, raffles work as follows:\\n\\n1. **Reverse Raffle**: In a reverse raffle, participants buy tickets with numbers from 1 to approximately 55. Each ticket costs $50, and a table of 20 individuals constitutes one complete ticket worth $1000. There are two $500 prizes: one for the early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pulled.\\n\\n2. **50-50 Draw**: The 50-50 draw is a raffle held during a single day at a particular event. Tickets are sold to patrons throughout the stadium during the event by roaming staff equipped with handheld electronic mobile devices. The gross proceeds of 50-50 tickets sold at the event go into a prize pool. 50% of the prize pool will go to the winner of the random draw which occurs towards the end of the event. The remaining 50% will be set aside for a charitable purpose chosen by the sporting club.\\n\\n3. **Cash Tip Board Raffles**: These are included with the ticket price and offer the chance to win $10,000.\", \"Based on the given passages, raffles work as follows:\\n\\n1. **Reverse Raffle**: In this type of raffle, participants buy tickets with numbers ranging from 1 to approximately 55. Each ticket costs $50. A table consists of 20 individuals, making one complete ticket worth $1000. There are two $500 prizes: one awarded in the early bird drawing and another for the first ticket pulled.\\n\\n2. **50-50 Draw**: This raffle involves selling tickets to patrons throughout a stadium during a specific event. The proceeds from 50-50 tickets go into a prize pool. At the end of the event, a random draw occurs for the prize pool, with 50% of the proceeds going to the winner and the other 50% set aside for a charitable purpose chosen by the sporting club.\\n\\n3. **Cash Tip Board Raffles**: These raffles are not included in the ticket price. Participants have the opportunity to win $10,000 through these raffles, which are typically held during events and require separate ticket purchases.\", \"Based on the given passages, a raffle works as follows:\\n\\n- A reverse raffle is a raffle but backwards. Each entry ticket has a number from 1 to approximately 55. Tickets cost $50 each, and a table of 20 individuals constitutes one complete ticket at $1000. There are two $500 prizes: one for the early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pulled.\\n\\n- A 50-50 draw is a single-day event where tickets are sold throughout the stadium during the event. The gross proceeds of the tickets go into a prize pool. The winner of the random draw receives 50% of the prize pool, while the remaining 50% is set aside for a charitable purpose chosen by the sporting club.\\n\\n- Cash tip board raffles are separate from the ticket price and offer the opportunity to win $10,000. They are not included in the ticket price and are an additional feature of the event.\", \"Based on the given passages, raffles work in different ways depending on the specific type of raffle:\\n\\n1. **Reverse Raffle**: In a reverse raffle, each entry ticket has a number from 1 to approximately 55. The table consists of 20 individuals, making it one complete ticket worth $1000. There are also two $500 prizes, one for the early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pulled.\\n\\n2. **50-50 Draw**: The 50-50 draw is a raffle held during a single day at a particular event. Tickets are sold to patrons throughout the stadium by roaming staff. The gross proceeds from the tickets go into a prize pool. The winner of the random draw, occurring towards the end of the event, receives 50% of the prize pool. The remaining 50% is set aside for a charitable purpose chosen by the sporting club.\\n\\n3. **Cash Tip Board Raffles**: These raffles are not included in the ticket price but offer the chance to win $10,000. Participants have the opportunity to participate in these cash tip board raffles.\"]", "time_sec": 30.04, "generation_time_seconds": 29.1976, "selection_time_seconds": 0.8381, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "samp