, 400, 16, 15, 15, 15, 13, 9043, 400, 20, 15, 15, 37408, 525, 14764, 11, 825, 369, 279, 4124, 11958, 13330, 323, 825, 369, 279, 1156, 11727, 13238, 13, 576, 4734, 1372, 315, 13026, 374, 537, 5189, 714, 374, 2163, 220, 20, 20, 13, 758, 264, 220, 20, 15, 12, 20, 15, 4038, 11, 5644, 2337, 264, 3175, 1899, 518, 264, 3953, 1538, 11, 279, 19952, 33455, 315, 14403, 6088, 728, 1119, 264, 21882, 7314, 13, 220, 20, 15, 4, 315, 279, 21882, 7314, 5780, 311, 279, 13632, 11, 323, 220, 20, 15, 4, 374, 738, 15663, 369, 264, 47701, 7428, 13, 576, 48349, 315, 1246, 1008, 4494, 315, 435, 80535, 2578, 975, 525, 537, 3897, 304, 279, 2661, 46769, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, raffles typically involve selling tickets for a chance to win a prize. The prize pool is created by the money collected from ticket sales. In a reverse raffle, participants buy tickets with numbers from 1 to approximately 55, and a table of 20 individuals constitutes one complete ticket at $1000. Two $500 prizes are drawn, one for the early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pulled. The exact number of participants is not specified but is around 55. In a 50-50 draw, held during a single day at a particular event, the gross proceeds of tickets sold go into a prize pool. 50% of the prize pool goes to the winner, and 50% is set aside for a charitable purpose. The specifics of how other types of raffles might work are not provided in the given passages.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.4437924375556632, "g_worst_claim": 0.18970857168498792, "g_content": 2.1981483010684744, "g_traj_mean": 1.4355934858322144}}], "selected_rollout": 0, "random_sel_rollout": 1, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 22.22, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "521", "question": "how do raffles work", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's how raffles work:\n\nA raffle is a game of chance where participants buy tickets with a unique number, and a prize is awarded to the holder of the winning ticket drawn at random. In a reverse raffle, the process is similar, but the tickets are numbered from 1 to approximately 55, and tables consist of 20 individuals, constituting one complete ticket at $1000, with two $500 prizes available. In a 50-50 draw, tickets are sold during an event, and the gross proceeds go into a prize pool, with 50% going to the winner of the random draw and the remaining 50% going to a charitable cause.\n\nTherefore, raffles typically involve buying tickets with unique numbers, and a random draw determines the winner, with a percentage of the proceeds often going to a charitable cause.", "pred": "3. **Cash Tip Board Raffles**: These raffles are included with the ticket price and offer the chance to win prize money, such as $10,000.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "3. **Cash Tip Board Raffles**: These raffles are included with the ticket price and offer the chance to win prize money, such as $10,000.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, raffles work as follows:\\n\\n1. **Reverse Raffle**: In a reverse raffle, participants buy tickets with numbers from 1 to approximately 55. Each ticket costs $50, and a table of 20 individuals constitutes one complete ticket worth $1000. There are two $500 prizes: one for the early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pulled.\\n\\n2. **50-50 Draw**: The 50-50 draw is a raffle held during a single day at a particular event. Tickets are sold to patrons throughout the stadium during the event by roaming staff equipped with handheld electronic mobile devices. The gross proceeds of 50-50 tickets sold at the event go into a prize pool. 50% of the prize pool will go to the winner of the random draw which occurs towards the end of the event. The remaining 50% will be set aside for a charitable purpose chosen by the sporting club.\\n\\n3. **Cash Tip Board Raffles**: These are included with the ticket price and offer the chance to win $10,000.\", \"Based on the given passages, the reverse raffle and 50-50 draw are two different types of raffles that work as follows:\\n\\nThe reverse raffle involves a table of 20 individuals, each contributing $1000 to create a complete ticket worth $5000, with two $500 prizes for the early bird and first ticket drawn. The entry ticket numbers range from 1 to 55. Each ticket costs $50. This raffle is described as a raffle but backwards.\\n\\nThe 50-50 draw is held during a single event, with tickets sold to the patrons throughout the event by roaming staff. The proceeds from the 50-50 tickets go into a prize pool, with 50% going to the winner of the random draw and the remaining 50% set aside for charitable purposes chosen by the sporting club.\\n\\nBoth raffles operate with a common theme of providing prizes to participants through the sale of tickets.\", \"Based on the given passages, raffles can be understood in several ways:\\n\\n1. **Reverse Raffle**: In a reverse raffle, participants buy tickets with numbers, and the highest number wins. The table consists of 20 individuals, and tickets can cost $50 each. The table itself costs $1000, and two prizes are awarded (one for early bird and one for the first ticket pulled).\\n\\n2. **50-50 Raffle**: This type of raffle is held on a single day and involves selling tickets at an event. The gross proceeds from ticket sales go into a prize pool, with 50% going to a random winner and the other 50% set aside for charity.\\n\\n3. **Cash Tip Board Raffles**: These raffles are included with the ticket price and offer the chance to win prize money, such as $10,000.\", \"Based on the given passages:\\n\\n1. The reverse raffle works by selling tickets with numbers from 1 to approximately 55, where each ticket costs $50. A table of 20 individuals constitutes a complete ticket worth $1000, and includes two $500 prizes (one for the early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pulled).\\n\\n2. The 50-50 draw is held during a single event, with tickets sold to patrons throughout the stadium. The gross proceeds from 50-50 ticket sales go into a prize pool, and 50% goes to the winner, while the remaining 50% is set aside for a charitable purpose chosen by the sporting club.\\n\\n3. The cash tip board raffles are included in the ticket price and offer the chance to win $10,000.\\n\\nIn summary, raffles typically involve selling tickets where a random number is drawn. The winner is determined based on the drawing, and the prize money is often split between the winner and a charitable purpose.\"]", "time_sec": 28.52, "generation_time_seconds": 27.6147, "selection_time_seconds": 0.9013, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 435, 80535, 975, 438, 11017, 1447, 16, 13, 3070, 45695, 431, 44090, 95518, 758, 264, 9931, 435, 44090, 11, 13026, 3695, 14403, 448, 5109, 504, 220, 16, 311, 13187, 220, 20, 20, 13, 8886, 11727, 7049, 400, 20, 15, 11, 323, 264, 1965, 315, 220, 17, 15, 7775, 41575, 825, 4583, 11727, 5802, 400, 16, 15, 15, 15, 13, 2619, 525, 1378, 400, 20, 15, 15, 37408, 25, 825, 369, 279, 4124, 11958, 13330, 323, 825, 369, 279, 1156, 11727, 13238, 382, 17, 13, 3070, 20, 15, 12, 20, 15, 11992, 95518, 576, 220, 20, 15, 12, 20, 15, 4038, 374, 264, 435, 44090, 5644, 2337, 264, 3175, 1899, 518, 264, 3953, 1538, 13, 35615, 525, 6088, 311, 51760, 6814, 279, 23889, 2337, 279, 1538, 553, 68253, 5570, 18650, 448, 64949, 14346, 6371, 7611, 13, 576, 19952, 33455, 315, 220, 20, 15, 12, 20, 15, 14403, 6088, 518, 279, 1538, 728, 1119, 264, 21882, 7314, 13, 220, 20, 15, 4, 315, 279, 21882, 7314, 686, 728, 311, 279, 13632, 315, 279, 4194, 4038, 892, 13666, 6974,