{"id": "548", "question": "what is a transistor how does it work", "gt": "A transistor is a miniature electronic component made of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. It can function as an amplifier or a switch. As an amplifier, it takes a small electric current at one end (input current) and produces a larger electric current (output current) at the other end. As a switch, it uses a small amount of electricity to control a gate on a larger supply of electricity. The transistor works when the electrons and the holes start moving across the two junctions between the n-type and p-type silicon. The current passes from the emitter to the collector through the base. The amount of collector current is approximately β (common-emitter current gain) times the base current.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, a transistor is a miniature electronic component that can function as an amplifier or a switch. To work as an amplifier, it takes in a tiny electric current at one end (input current) and produces a much bigger electric current (output current) at the other end. When working as a switch, the holes in the base act like a barrier, preventing any significant current flow from the emitter to the collector while the transistor is in its off state. The movement of electrons from the emitter to the collector through the base is what creates a flow of electricity through the transistor.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, a transistor is a miniature electronic component that can function as an amplifier or a switch. To work as an amplifier, it takes in a tiny electric current at one end (input current) and produces a much bigger electric current (output current) at the other end. When working as a switch, the holes in the base act like a barrier, preventing any significant current flow from the emitter to the collector while the transistor is in its off state. The movement of electrons from the emitter to the collector through the base is what creates a flow of electricity through the transistor.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, a transistor is a miniature electronic component that can function as an amplifier or a switch. It works by using a small amount of electricity to control a gate on a much larger supply of electricity, similar to turning a valve to control a supply of water. When working as an amplifier, it takes in a tiny electric current at one end (an input current) and produces a much bigger electric current (an output current) at the other. Transistors can also work as switches, where the holes in the base act like a barrier, preventing any significant current flow from the emitter to the collector while the transistor is in its off state. A transistor works when the electrons and the holes start moving across the two junctions between the n-type and p-type silicon.\", \"Based on the given passages, a transistor is a miniature electronic component that can function as an amplifier or a switch. It is composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit.\\n\\nWhen a transistor works as an amplifier, it takes in a tiny electric current at one end (an input current) and produces a much bigger electric current (an output current) at the other end. This is achieved by using a small amount of electricity to control a gate on a much larger supply of electricity, much like turning a valve to control a supply of water.\\n\\nThe base of the transistor acts like a barrier, preventing any significant current flow from the emitter to the collector while the transistor is in its off state. When the transistor works as a switch, the electrons and holes start moving across the two junctions between the n-type and p-type silicon, allowing current to flow. The current passes from the emitter to the collector through the base.\", \"Based on the given passages, a transistor is a miniature electronic component that can either function as an amplifier or a switch. When working as an amplifier, it takes in a small electric current (input current) and produces a much larger electric current (output current). Transistors can also function as switches.\\n\\nThe operation of a transistor is based on the movement of charge carriers (electrons and holes) across junctions between different types of semiconductor material (n-type and p-type silicon). In its off state, the holes in the base act as a barrier, preventing significant current flow from the emitter to the collector. To turn the transistor on, the electrons and holes must start moving across the two junctions.\\n\\nThe transistor operates by controlling the flow of electricity. It does this by using a small amount of electricity to control a gate on a much larger supply of electricity, similar to how a valve controls the flow of water. This control mechanism allows the transistor to amplify or switch electronic signals and electrical power effectively.\", \"Based on the given passages, a transistor is a miniature electronic component that can function as an amplifier or a switch. To work as an amplifier, it takes in a tiny electric current at one end (input current) and produces a much bigger electric current (output current) at the other end. When working as a switch, the holes in the base act like a barrier, preventing any significant current flow from the emitter to the collector while the transistor is in its off state. The movement of electrons from the emitter to the collector through the base is what creates a flow of electricity through the transistor.\"]", "time_sec": 23.25, "generation_time_seconds": 22.4565, "selection_time_seconds": 0.7877, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 264, 97941, 374, 264, 56763, 14346, 3692, 429, 646, 729, 438, 458, 60066, 476, 264, 3398, 13, 1084, 4278, 553, 1667, 264, 2613, 3311, 315, 17728, 311, 2524, 264, 18126, 389, 264, 1753, 8131, 8149, 315, 17728, 11, 4428, 311, 13054, 264, 31430, 311, 2524, 264, 8149, 315, 3015, 13, 3197, 3238, 438, 458, 60066, 11, 432, 4990, 304, 264, 13673, 9072, 1482, 518, 825, 835, 320, 276, 1946, 1482, 8, 323, 18644, 264, 1753, 11243, 9072, 1482, 320, 276, 2550, 1482, 8, 518, 279, 1008, 13, 4058, 380, 1087, 646, 1083, 975, 438, 31167, 11, 1380, 279, 19766, 304, 279, 2331, 1160, 1075, 264, 22103, 11, 26160, 894, 5089, 1482, 6396, 504, 279, 47263, 311, 279, 31953, 1393, 279, 97941, 374, 304, 1181, 1007, 1584, 13, 362, 97941, 4278, 979, 279, 56578, 323, 279, 19766, 1191, 7218, 3941, 279, 1378, 48241, 82, 1948, 279, 308, 10604, 323, 281, 10604, 50592, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, a transistor is a miniature electronic component that can function as an amplifier or a switch. It works by using a small amount of electricity to control a gate on a much larger supply of electricity, similar to turning a valve to control a supply of water. When working as an amplifier, it takes in a tiny electric current at one end (an input current) and produces a much bigger electric current (an output current) at the other. Transistors can also work as switches, where the holes in the base act like a barrier, preventing any significant current flow from the emitter to the collector while the transistor is in its off state. A transistor works when the electrons and the holes start moving across the two junctions between the n-type and p-type silicon.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.6639021898903934, "g_worst_claim": 0.5446375608444214, "g_content": 2.5130352444118924, "g_traj_mean": 1.7396607398986816}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 264, 97941, 374, 264, 56763, 14346, 3692, 429, 646, 729, 438, 458, 60066, 476, 264, 3398, 13, 1084, 374, 23415, 315, 86736, 3684, 448, 518, 3245, 2326, 52979, 369, 3633, 311, 458, 9250, 16224, 382, 4498, 264, 97941, 4278, 438, 458, 60066, 11, 432, 4990, 304, 264, 13673, 9072, 1482, 518, 825, 835, 320, 276, 1946, 1482, 8, 323, 18644, 264, 1753, 11243, 9072, 1482, 320, 276, 2550, 1482, 8, 518, 279, 1008, 835, 13, 1096, 374, 16994, 553, 1667, 264, 2613, 3311, 315, 17728, 311, 2524, 264, 18126, 389, 264, 1753, 8131, 8149, 315, 17728, 11, 1753, 1075, 13054, 264, 31430, 311, 2524, 264, 8149, 315, 3015, 382, 785, 2331, 315, 279, 97941, 14056, 1075, 264, 22103, 11, 26160, 894, 5089, 1482, 6396, 504, 279, 47263, 311, 279, 31953, 1393, 279, 97941, 374, 304, 1181, 1007, 1584, 13, 3197, 279, 97941, 4278, 438, 264, 3398, 11, 279, 56578, 323, 19766, 1191, 7218, 3941, 279, 1378, 48241, 82, 1948, 279, 308, 10604, 323, 281, 10604, 50592, 11, 10693, 1482, 311, 6396, 13, 576, 1482, 16211, 504, 279, 47263, 311, 279, 31953, 1526, 279, 2331, 13, 151645], "pred": "The base of the transistor acts like a barrier, preventing any significant current flow from the emitter to the collector while the transistor is in its off state. When the transistor works as a switch, the electrons and holes start moving across the two junctions between the n-type and p-type silicon, allowing current to flow. The current passes from the emitter to the collector through the base.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.361462835630646, "g_worst_claim": 0.45771446228027346, "g_content": 2.060752699110243, "g_traj_mean": 1.4640566110610962}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 264, 97941, 374, 264, 56763, 14346, 3692, 429, 646, 2987, 729, 438, 458, 60066, 476, 264, 3398, 13, 3197, 3238, 438, 458, 60066, 11, 432, 4990, 304, 264, 2613, 9072, 1482, 320, 1355, 1482, 8, 323, 18644, 264, 1753, 8131, 9072, 1482, 320, 3006, 1482, 568, 4058, 380, 1087, 646, 1083, 729, 438, 31167, 382, 785, 5666, 315, 264, 97941, 374, 3118, 389, 279, 7203, 315, 6757, 34891, 320, 18957, 25610, 323, 19766, 8, 3941, 48241, 82, 1948, 2155, 4494, 315, 86736, 3684, 320, 77, 10604, 323, 281, 10604, 50592, 568, 758, 1181, 1007, 1584, 11, 279, 19766, 304, 279, 2331, 1160, 438, 264, 22103, 11, 26160, 5089, 1482, 6396, 504, 279, 47263, 311, 279, 31953, 13, 2014, 2484, 279, 97941, 389, 11, 279, 56578, 323, 19766, 1969, 1191, 7218, 3941, 279, 1378, 48241, 82, 382, 785, 97941, 26057, 553, 25902, 279, 6396, 315, 17728, 13, 1084, 1558, 419, 553, 1667, 264, 2613, 3311, 315, 17728, 311, 2524, 264, 18126, 389, 264, 1753, 8131, 8149, 315, 17728, 11, 4428, 311, 1246, 264, 31430, 11574, 279, 6396, 315, 3015, 13, 1096, 2524, 16953, 6147, 279, 97941, 311, 96068, 476, 3398, 14346, 17289, 323, 19734, 2355, 13444, 13, 151645], "pred": "The transistor operates by controlling the flow of electricity. It does this by using a small amount of electricity to control a gate on a much larger supply of electricity, similar to how a valve controls the flow of water. This control mechanism allows the transistor to amplify or switch electronic signals and electrical power effectively.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.9890680303236958, "g_worst_claim": 0.14685345689455667, "g_content": 1.5072546890101481, "g_traj_mean": 1.0427100658416748}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 264, 97941, 374, 264, 56763, 14346, 3692, 429, 646, 729, 438, 458, 60066, 476, 264, 3398, 13, 2014, 975, 438, 458, 60066, 11, 432, 4990, 304, 264, 13673, 9072, 1482, 518, 825, 835, 320, 1355, 1482, 8, 323, 18644, 264, 1753, 11243, 9072, 1482, 320, 3006, 1482, 8, 518, 279, 1008, 835, 13, 3197, 3238, 438, 264, 3398, 11, 279, 19766, 304, 279, 2331, 1160, 1075, 264, 22103, 11, 26160, 894, 5089, 1482, 6396, 504, 279, 47263, 311, 279, 31953, 1393, 279, 97941, 374, 304, 1181, 1007, 1584, 13, 576, 7203, 315, 56578, 504, 279, 47263, 311, 279, 31953, 1526, 279, 2331, 374, 1128, 11450, 264, 6396, 315, 17728, 1526, 279, 97941, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, a transistor is a miniature electronic component that can function as an amplifier or a switch. To work as an amplifier, it takes in a tiny electric current at one end (input current) and produces a much bigger electric current (output current) at the other end. When working as a switch, the holes in the base act like a barrier, preventing any significant current flow from the emitter to the collector while the transistor is in its off state. The movement of electrons from the emitter to the collector through the base is what creates a flow of electricity through the transistor.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.206902108005449, "g_worst_claim": 0.4541744788487752, "g_content": 1.8654469710129957, "g_traj_mean": 1.272802710533142}}], "selected_rollout": 0, "random_sel_rollout": 3, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 53.33, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "550", "question": "what is a transistor how does it work", "gt": "Based on the given passages, a transistor is an electronic component made of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. It functions as an amplifier or a switch depending on the flow of electrons through its base. When working as an amplifier, it takes in a small electric current at one end and produces a larger electric current at the other. The flow of electrons is controlled by using a small amount of electricity to turn a valve-like structure in the transistor. In its off state, the holes in the base act as a barrier, preventing any significant current flow from the emitter to the collector. Transistors can have common-emitter configuration where collector current is approximately β times the base current.", "pred": "Transistors are composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. The collector current is approximately β (common-emitter current gain) times the base current, which is typically greater than 100 for small-signal transistors but can be smaller in transistors designed for high-power applications. Unlike the field-effect transistor, the BJT is a low-input-impedance device.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Transistors are composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. The collector current is approximately β (common-emitter current gain) times the base current, which is typically greater than 100 for small-signal transistors but can be smaller in transistors designed for high-power applications. Unlike the field-effect transistor, the BJT is a low-input-impedance device.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, a transistor is a miniature electronic component that can function as an amplifier or a switch. It works by using a small amount of electricity to control a gate on a much larger supply of electricity, similar to turning a valve to control a supply of water. When working as an amplifier, it takes in a tiny electric current at one end (an input current) and produces a much bigger electric current (an output current) at the other. Transistors can also work as switches, where the holes in the base act like a barrier, preventing any significant current flow from the emitter to the collector while the transistor is in its off state. A transistor works when the electrons and the holes start moving across the two junctions between the n-type and p-type silicon.\", \"Based on the given passages, a transistor is a semiconductor device used to amplify and switch electronic signals and electrical power. \\n\\nA transistor works as an amplifier by taking in a tiny electric current at one end (an input current) and producing a much bigger electric current (an output current) at the other end. It can also function as a switch. \\n\\nThe functioning of a transistor is related to the movement of electrons and holes across the n-type and p-type silicon junctions. When the transistor is in its off state, holes in the base act like a barrier, preventing significant current flow from the emitter to the collector. \\n\\nFor amplification, the movement of electrons from the emitter to the collector is controlled by the base, which has a more positive voltage than the emitter. This movement of electrons creates a flow of electricity through the transistor. \\n\\nIn the case of a switch, the transistor is designed to allow or prevent the flow of current through the collector, depending on the state of the base. When the base is off, the holes in the base act as a barrier, while when the base is on, the holes allow current to flow, thus switching the transistor on or off. \\n\\nIt is composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. The collector current is approximately β (common-emitter current gain) times the base current. Collectors is typically greater than 100 for small-signal transistors but can be smaller in transistors designed for high-power applications. Unlike the field-effect transistor, the BJT (Bipolar Junction Transistor) is a low-input-impedance device.\", \"Based on the given passages, a transistor is a miniature electronic component that functions as either an amplifier or a switch. When used as an amplifier, it takes in a small electric current (input current) and outputs a larger electric current (output current). When functioning as a switch, it can control the flow of electricity by using a small amount of electricity to control a larger supply, similar to using a valve to control a supply of water.\\n\\nTransistors are composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. They operate by manipulating the movement of electrons and holes across junctions between n-type and p-type silicon. In its off state, the holes act as a barrier preventing significant current flow between the emitter and collector. The transistor operates by controlling the flow of electrons and holes through its junctions, which allows it to amplify or switch electronic signals and electrical power.\", \"Based on the given passages, a transistor is a semiconductor device used to amplify and switch electronic signals and electrical power. It allows it to function as an amplifier or a switch. \\n\\nWhen it works as an amplifier, it takes in a tiny electric current at one end (an input current) and produces a much bigger electric current (an output current) at the other. Transistors can also work as switches.\\n\\nThe design of a transistor allows it to function as an amplifier or a switch by using a small amount of electricity to control a gate on a much larger supply of electricity, much like turning a valve to control a supply of water. The base pulls these electrons from the emitter because it has a more positive voltage than does the emitter. This movement of electrons creates a flow of electricity through the transistor. The current passes from the emitter to the collector through the base.\\n\\nTransistors are composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. The collector current is approximately β (common-emitter current gain) times the base current, which is typically greater than 100 for small-signal transistors but can be smaller in transistors designed for high-power applications. Unlike the field-effect transistor, the BJT is a low-input-impedance device.\"]", "time_sec": 30.935, "generation_time_seconds": 30.0253, "selection_time_seconds": 0.9045, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 264, 97941, 374, 264, 56763, 14346, 3692, 429, 646, 729, 438, 458, 60066, 476, 264, 3398, 13, 1084, 4278, 553, 1667, 264, 2613, 3311, 315, 17728, 311, 2524, 264, 18126, 389, 264, 1753, 8131, 8149, 315, 17728, 11, 4428, 311, 13054, 264, 31430, 311, 2524, 264, 8149, 315, 3015, 13, 3197, 3238, 438, 458, 60066, 11, 432, 4990, 304, 264, 13673, 9072, 1482, 518, 825, 835, 320, 276, 1946, 1482, 8, 323, 18644, 264, 1753, 11243, 9072, 1482, 320, 276, 2550, 1482, 8, 518, 279, 1008, 13, 4058, 380, 1087, 646, 1083, 975, 438, 31167, 11, 1380, 279, 19766, 304, 279, 2331, 1160, 1075, 264, 22103, 11, 26160, 894, 5089, 1482, 6396, 504, 279, 47263, 311, 279, 31953, 1393, 279, 97941, 374, 304, 1181, 1007, 1584, 13, 362, 97941, 4278, 979, 279, 56578, 323, 279, 19766, 1191, 7218, 3941, 279, 1378, 48241, 82, 1948, 279, 308, 10604, 323, 281, 10604, 50592, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, a transistor is a miniature electronic component that can function as an amplifier or a switch. It works by using a small amount of electricity to control a gate on a much larger supply of electricity, similar to turning a valve to control a supply of water. When working as an amplifier, it takes in a tiny electric current at one end (an input current) and produces a much bigger electric current (an output current) at the other. Transistors can also work as switches, where the holes in the base act like a barrier, preventing any significant current flow from the emitter to the collector while the transistor is in its off state. A transistor works when the electrons and the holes start moving across the two junctions between the n-type and p-type silicon.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.656004326207168, "g_worst_claim": 0.6095074812571207, "g_content": 2.5189230177137585, "g_traj_mean": 1.737998127937317}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 264, 97941, 374, 264, 86736, 3671, 1483, 311, 96068, 323, 3398, 14346, 17289, 323, 19734, 2355, 13, 4710, 32, 97941, 4278, 438, 458, 60066, 553, 4633, 304, 264, 13673, 9072, 1482, 518, 825, 835, 320, 276, 1946, 1482, 8, 323, 17387, 264, 1753, 11243, 9072, 1482, 320, 276, 2550, 1482, 8, 518, 279, 1008, 835, 13, 1084, 646, 1083, 729, 438, 264, 3398, 13, 4710, 785, 30201, 315, 264, 97941, 374, 5435, 311, 279, 7203, 315, 56578, 323, 19766, 3941, 279, 308, 10604, 323, 281, 10604, 50592, 48241, 82, 13, 3197, 279, 97941, 374, 304, 1181, 1007, 1584, 11, 19766, 304, 279, 2331, 1160, 1075, 264, 22103, 11, 26160, 5089, 1482, 6396, 504, 279, 47263, 311, 279, 31953, 13, 4710, 2461, 22395, 2404, 11, 279, 7203, 315, 56578, 504, 279, 47263, 311, 279, 31953, 374, 14071, 553, 279, 2331, 11, 892, 702, 264, 803, 6785, 21720, 1091, 279, 47263, 13, 1096, 7203, 315, 56578, 11450, 264, 6396, 315, 17728, 1526, 279, 97941, 13, 4710, 641, 279, 1142, 315, 264, 3398, 11, 279, 97941, 374, 6188, 311, 2138, 476, 5358, 279, 6396, 315, 1482, 1526, 279, 31953, 11, 11649, 389, 279, 1584, 315, 279, 2331, 13, 3197, 279, 2331, 374, 1007, 11, 279, 19766, 304, 279, 2331, 1160, 438, 264, 22103, 11, 1393, 979, 279, 2331, 374, 389, 11, 279, 19766, 2138, 1482, 311, 6396, 11, 8450, 27765, 279, 97941, 389, 476, 1007, 13, 4710, 2132, 374, 23415, 315, 86736, 3684, 448, 518, 3245, 2326, 52979, 369, 3633, 311, 458, 9250, 16224, 13, 576, 31953, 1482, 374, 13187, 33218, 320, 5464, 36512, 3248, 1482, 8722, 8, 3039, 279, 2331, 1482, 13, 20513, 1087, 374, 11136, 7046, 1091, 220, 16, 15, 15, 369, 2613, 1331, 25719, 1356, 380, 1087, 714, 646, 387, 9155, 304, 1356, 380, 1087, 6188, 369, 1550, 26526, 8357, 13, 26048, 279, 2070, 23067, 97941, 11, 279, 425, 81181, 320, 33, 573, 7417, 67956, 4058, 5323, 8, 374, 264, 3347, 13933, 12, 6664, 291, 681, 3671, 13, 151645], "pred": "It is composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. The collector current is approximately β (common-emitter current gain) times the base current. Collectors is typically greater than 100 for small-signal transistors but can be smaller in transistors designed for high-power applications. Unlike the field-effect transistor, the BJT (Bipolar Junction Transistor) is a low-input-impedance device.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.9989496424802403, "g_worst_claim": -0.06969118118286133, "g_content": 1.6141028095492356, "g_traj_mean": 1.0403003692626953}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 264, 97941, 374, 264, 56763, 14346, 3692, 429, 5746, 438, 2987, 458, 60066, 476, 264, 3398, 13, 3197, 1483, 438, 458, 60066, 11, 432, 4990, 304, 264, 2613, 9072, 1482, 320, 1355, 1482, 8, 323, 16275, 264, 8131, 9072, 1482, 320, 3006, 1482, 568, 3197, 30201, 438, 264, 3398, 11, 432, 646, 2524, 279, 6396, 315, 17728, 553, 1667, 264, 2613, 3311, 315, 17728, 311, 2524, 264, 8131, 8149, 11, 4428, 311, 1667, 264, 31430, 311, 2524, 264, 8149, 315, 3015, 382, 3167, 380, 1087, 525, 23415, 315, 86736, 3684, 448, 518, 3245, 2326, 52979, 369, 3633, 311, 458, 9250, 16224, 13, 2379, 14476, 553, 62514, 279, 7203, 315, 56578, 323, 19766, 3941, 48241, 82, 1948, 308, 10604, 323, 281, 10604, 50592, 13, 758, 1181, 1007, 1584, 11, 279, 19766, 1160, 438, 264, 22103, 26160, 5089, 1482, 6396, 1948, 279, 47263, 323, 31953, 13, 576, 97941, 26057, 553, 25902, 279, 6396, 315, 56578, 323, 19766, 1526, 1181, 48241, 82, 11, 892, 6147, 432, 311, 96068, 476, 3398, 14346, 17289, 323, 19734, 2355, 13, 151645], "pred": "Transistors are composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. They operate by manipulating the movement of electrons and holes across junctions between n-type and p-type silicon. In its off state, the holes act as a barrier preventing significant current flow between the emitter and collector. The transistor operates by controlling the flow of electrons and holes through its junctions, which allows it to amplify or switch electronic signals and electrical power.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.9088133525113378, "g_worst_claim": 0.1738616686600905, "g_content": 1.5363764269598599, "g_traj_mean": 1.0303103923797607}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 264, 97941, 374, 264, 86736, 3671, 1483, 311, 96068, 323, 3398, 14346, 17289, 323, 19734, 2355, 13, 1084, 6147, 432, 311, 729, 438, 458, 60066, 476, 264, 3398, 13, 4710, 4498, 432, 4278, 438, 458, 60066, 11, 432, 4990, 304, 264, 13673, 9072, 1482, 518, 825, 835, 320, 276, 1946, 1482, 8, 323, 18644, 264, 1753, 11243, 9072, 1482, 320, 276, 2550, 1482, 8, 518, 279, 1008, 13, 4058, 380, 1087, 646, 1083, 975, 438, 31167, 382, 785, 2884, 315, 264, 97941, 6147, 432, 311, 729, 438, 458, 60066, 476, 264, 3398, 553, 1667, 264, 2613, 3311, 315, 17728, 311, 2524, 264, 18126, 389, 264, 1753, 8131, 8149, 315, 17728, 11, 1753, 1075, 13054, 264, 31430, 311, 2524, 264, 8149, 315, 3015, 13, 576, 2331, 33045, 1493, 56578, 504, 279, 47263, 1576, 432, 702, 264, 803, 6785, 21720, 1091, 1558, 279, 47263, 13, 1096, 7203, 315, 56578, 11450, 264, 6396, 315, 17728, 1526, 279, 97941, 13, 576, 1482, 16211, 504, 279, 47263, 311, 279, 31953, 1526, 279, 2331, 382, 3167, 380, 1087, 525, 23415, 315, 86736, 3684, 448, 518, 3245, 2326, 52979, 369, 3633, 311, 458, 9250, 16224, 13, 576, 31953, 1482, 374, 13187, 33218, 320, 5464, 36512, 3248, 1482, 8722, 8, 3039, 279, 2331, 1482, 11, 892, 374, 11136, 7046, 1091, 220, 16, 15, 15, 369, 2613, 1331, 25719, 1356, 380, 1087, 714, 646, 387, 9155, 304, 1356, 380, 1087, 6188, 369, 1550, 26526, 8357, 13, 26048, 279, 2070, 23067, 97941, 11, 279, 425, 81181, 374, 264, 3347, 13933, 12, 6664, 291, 681, 3671, 13, 151645], "pred": "Transistors are composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. The collector current is approximately β (common-emitter current gain) times the base current, which is typically greater than 100 for small-signal transistors but can be smaller in transistors designed for high-power applications. Unlike the field-effect transistor, the BJT is a low-input-impedance device.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.4963071135068504, "g_worst_claim": 0.5791231604183421, "g_content": 2.0880760982118804, "g_traj_mean": 1.569572925567627}}], "selected_rollout": 0, "random_sel_rollout": 3, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 35.09, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "551", "question": "what is a transistor how does it work", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is how a transistor works:\n\n1. A transistor functions as both an amplifier and a switch.\n2. The transistor's design allows it to control a larger supply of electricity with a small amount of electricity, much like turning a valve to control a supply of water.\n3. The base of the transistor pulls electrons from the emitter, creating a flow of electricity through the transistor.\n4. The transistor can take in a tiny electric current at one end (the input current) and produce a much bigger electric current at the other end (the output current) when it works as an amplifier.\n5. The holes in the base of the transistor act as a barrier, preventing any significant current flow from the emitter to the collector when the transistor is in its off state.\n6. When the electrons and holes move across the two junctions between the n-type and p-type silicon, the transistor begins to work.\n7. The collector current is approximately beta (common-emitter current gain) times the base current, and it is typically greater than 100 for small-signal transistors but can be smaller in transistors designed for high-power applications.\n\nUnable to answer based on the given passages.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, a transistor is a miniature electronic component that can function as either an amplifier or a switch. It operates by controlling a gate with a small amount of electricity, similar to how a valve controls the flow of water. When used as an amplifier, it takes in a small electric current at one end (input current) and produces a much larger electric current at the other end (output current). When functioning as a switch, it allows current to flow between the emitter and collector by manipulating the movement of electrons and holes across the junctions between n-type and p-type silicon. Transistors are composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit, and the collector current is approximately β (common-emitter current gain) times the base current.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, a transistor is a miniature electronic component that can function as either an amplifier or a switch. It operates by controlling a gate with a small amount of electricity, similar to how a valve controls the flow of water. When used as an amplifier, it takes in a small electric current at one end (input current) and produces a much larger electric current at the other end (output current). When functioning as a switch, it allows current to flow between the emitter and collector by manipulating the movement of electrons and holes across the junctions between n-type and p-type silicon. Transistors are composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit, and the collector current is approximately β (common-emitter current gain) times the base current.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, a transistor is a miniature electronic component that can function as an amplifier or a switch. It works by using a small amount of electricity to control a gate on a much larger supply of electricity, similar to turning a valve to control a supply of water. When working as an amplifier, it takes in a tiny electric current at one end (an input current) and produces a much bigger electric current (an output current) at the other. Transistors can also work as switches, where the holes in the base act like a barrier, preventing any significant current flow from the emitter to the collector while the transistor is in its off state. A transistor works when the electrons and the holes start moving across the two junctions between the n-type and p-type silicon.\", \"Based on the given passages, a transistor is a semiconductor device used to amplify and switch electronic signals and electrical power. It is composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. The three terminals are the emitter, the base, and the collector.\\n\\nIn simple terms, a transistor works by using a small amount of electricity to control a gate on a much larger supply of electricity, much like turning a valve to control a supply of water. The base pulls electrons from the emitter because it has a more positive voltage than the emitter, creating a flow of electricity through the transistor. The current passes from the emitter to the collector through the base.\\n\\nWhen the transistor works as an amplifier, it takes in a tiny electric current at one end (an input current) and produces a much bigger electric current (an output current) at the other. When it works as a switch, the holes in the base act like a barrier, preventing any significant current flow from the emitter to the collector while the transistor is in its off state. A transistor works when the electrons and the holes start moving across the two junctions between the n-type and p-type silicon.\", \"Based on the given passages, a transistor is a semiconductor device used to amplify and switch electronic signals and electrical power. It is composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. \\n\\nA transistor can function as either an amplifier or a switch. When acting as an amplifier, it takes in a tiny electric current at one end (input current) and produces a much bigger electric current at the other end (output current). When functioning as a switch, it allows current to flow from the emitter to the collector under certain conditions.\\n\\nThe operation of a transistor involves the movement of electrons and holes across the two junctions between the n-type and p-type silicon, which is controlled by the input current. This movement of charge carriers creates the desired electrical output, either as an amplified signal or as a switch.\", \"Based on the given passages, a transistor is a miniature electronic component that can function as either an amplifier or a switch. It operates by controlling a gate with a small amount of electricity, similar to how a valve controls the flow of water. When used as an amplifier, it takes in a small electric current at one end (input current) and produces a much larger electric current at the other end (output current). When functioning as a switch, it allows current to flow between the emitter and collector by manipulating the movement of electrons and holes across the junctions between n-type and p-type silicon. Transistors are composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit, and the collector current is approximately β (common-emitter current gain) times the base current.\"]", "time_sec": 24.587, "generation_time_seconds": 23.6953, "selection_time_seconds": 0.887, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 264, 97941, 374, 264, 56763, 14346, 3692, 429, 646, 729, 438, 458, 60066, 476, 264, 3398, 13, 1084, 4278, 553, 1667, 264, 2613, 3311, 315, 17728, 311, 2524, 264, 18126, 389, 264, 1753, 8131, 8149, 315, 17728, 11, 4428, 311, 13054, 264, 31430, 311, 2524, 264, 8149, 315, 3015, 13, 3197, 3238, 438, 458, 60066, 11, 432, 4990, 304, 264, 13673, 9072, 1482, 518, 825, 835, 320, 276, 1946, 1482, 8, 323, 18644, 264, 1753, 11243, 9072, 1482, 320, 276, 2550, 1482, 8, 518, 279, 1008, 13, 4058, 380, 1087, 646, 1083, 975, 438, 31167, 11, 1380, 279, 19766, 304, 279, 2331, 1160, 1075, 264, 22103, 11, 26160, 894, 5089, 1482, 6396, 504, 279, 47263, 311, 279, 31953, 1393, 279, 97941, 374, 304, 1181, 1007, 1584, 13, 362, 97941, 4278, 979, 279, 56578, 323, 279, 19766, 1191, 7218, 3941, 279, 1378, 48241, 82, 1948, 279, 308, 10604, 323, 281, 10604, 50592, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, a transistor is a miniature electronic component that can function as an amplifier or a switch. It works by using a small amount of electricity to control a gate on a much larger supply of electricity, similar to turning a valve to control a supply of water. When working as an amplifier, it takes in a tiny electric current at one end (an input current) and produces a much bigger electric current (an output current) at the other. Transistors can also work as switches, where the holes in the base act like a barrier, preventing any significant current flow from the emitter to the collector while the transistor is in its off state. A transistor works when the electrons and the holes start moving across the two junctions between the n-type and p-type silicon.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.6488363734363514, "g_worst_claim": 0.5224390824635824, "g_content": 2.495285881890191, "g_traj_mean": 1.7355679273605347}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 264, 97941, 374, 264, 86736, 3671, 1483, 311, 96068, 323, 3398, 14346, 17289, 323, 19734, 2355, 13, 1084, 374, 23415, 315, 86736, 3684, 448, 518, 3245, 2326, 52979, 369, 3633, 311, 458, 9250, 16224, 13, 576, 2326, 52979, 525, 279, 47263, 11, 279, 2331, 11, 323, 279, 31953, 382, 641, 4285, 3793, 11, 264, 97941, 4278, 553, 1667, 264, 2613, 3311, 315, 17728, 311, 2524, 264, 18126, 389, 264, 1753, 8131, 8149, 315, 17728, 11, 1753, 1075, 13054, 264, 31430, 311, 2524, 264, 8149, 315, 3015, 13, 576, 2331, 33045, 56578, 504, 279, 47263, 1576, 432, 702, 264, 803, 6785, 21720, 1091, 279, 47263, 11, 6825, 264, 6396, 315, 17728, 1526, 279, 97941, 13, 576, 1482, 16211, 504, 279, 47263, 311, 279, 31953, 1526, 279, 2331, 382, 4498, 279, 97941, 4278, 438, 458, 60066, 11, 432, 4990, 304, 264, 13673, 9072, 1482, 518, 825, 835, 320, 276, 1946, 1482, 8, 323, 18644, 264, 1753, 11243, 9072, 1482, 320, 276, 2550, 1482, 8, 518, 279, 1008, 13, 3197, 432, 4278, 438, 264, 3398, 11, 279, 19766, 304, 279, 2331, 1160, 1075, 264, 22103, 11, 26160, 894, 5089, 1482, 6396, 504, 279, 47263, 311, 279, 31953, 1393, 279, 97941, 374, 304, 1181, 1007, 1584, 13, 362, 97941, 4278, 979, 279, 56578, 323, 279, 19766, 1191, 7218, 3941, 279, 1378, 48241, 82, 1948, 279, 308, 10604, 323, 281, 10604, 50592, 13, 151645], "pred": "When the transistor works as an amplifier, it takes in a tiny electric current at one end (an input current) and produces a much bigger electric current (an output current) at the other. When it works as a switch, the holes in the base act like a barrier, preventing any significant current flow from the emitter to the collector while the transistor is in its off state. A transistor works when the electrons and the holes start moving across the two junctions between the n-type and p-type silicon.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.1790868885042411, "g_worst_claim": -0.30583715438842773, "g_content": 1.8847852234446674, "g_traj_mean": 1.3950706720352173}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 264, 97941, 374, 264, 86736, 3671, 1483, 311, 96068, 323, 3398, 14346, 17289, 323, 19734, 2355, 13, 1084, 374, 23415, 315, 86736, 3684, 448, 518, 3245, 2326, 52979, 369, 3633, 311, 458, 9250, 16224, 13, 4710, 32, 97941, 646, 729, 438, 2987, 458, 60066, 476, 264, 3398, 13, 3197, 15358, 438, 458, 60066, 11, 432, 4990, 304, 264, 13673, 9072, 1482, 518, 825, 835, 320, 1355, 1482, 8, 323, 18644, 264, 1753, 11243, 9072, 1482, 518, 279, 1008, 835, 320, 3006, 1482, 568, 3197, 30201, 438, 264, 3398, 11, 432, 6147, 1482, 311, 6396, 504, 279, 47263, 311, 279, 31953, 1212, 3654, 4682, 382, 785, 5666, 315, 264, 97941, 17601, 279, 7203, 315, 56578, 323, 19766, 3941, 279, 1378, 48241, 82, 1948, 279, 308, 10604, 323, 281, 10604, 50592, 11, 892, 374, 14071, 553, 279, 1946, 1482, 13, 1096, 7203, 315, 6757, 34891, 11450, 279, 12685, 19734, 2550, 11, 2987, 438, 458, 82498, 8286, 476, 438, 264, 3398, 13, 151645], "pred": "The operation of a transistor involves the movement of electrons and holes across the two junctions between the n-type and p-type silicon, which is controlled by the input current. This movement of charge carriers creates the desired electrical output, either as an amplified signal or as a switch.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.5531221897689145, "g_worst_claim": 0.02098706364631653, "g_content": 0.9973517417907715, "g_traj_mean": 0.7202603816986084}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 264, 97941, 374, 264, 56763, 14346, 3692, 429, 646, 729, 438, 2987, 458, 60066, 476, 264, 3398, 13, 1084, 26057, 553, 25902, 264, 18126, 448, 264, 2613, 3311, 315, 17728, 11, 4428, 311, 1246, 264, 31430, 11574, 279, 6396, 315, 3015, 13, 3197, 1483, 438, 458, 60066, 11, 432, 4990, 304, 264, 2613, 9072, 1482, 518, 825, 835, 320, 1355, 1482, 8, 323, 18644, 264, 1753, 8131, 9072, 1482, 518, 279, 1008, 835, 320, 3006, 1482, 568, 3197, 30201, 438, 264, 3398, 11, 432, 6147, 1482, 311, 6396, 1948, 279, 47263, 323, 31953, 553, 62514, 279, 7203, 315, 56578, 323, 19766, 3941, 279, 48241, 82, 1948, 308, 10604, 323, 281, 10604, 50592, 13, 4058, 380, 1087, 525, 23415, 315, 86736, 3684, 448, 518, 3245, 2326, 52979, 369, 3633, 311, 458, 9250, 16224, 11, 323, 279, 31953, 1482, 374, 13187, 33218, 320, 5464, 36512, 3248, 1482, 8722, 8, 3039, 279, 2331, 1482, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, a transistor is a miniature electronic component that can function as either an amplifier or a switch. It operates by controlling a gate with a small amount of electricity, similar to how a valve controls the flow of water. When used as an amplifier, it takes in a small electric current at one end (input current) and produces a much larger electric current at the other end (output current). When functioning as a switch, it allows current to flow between the emitter and collector by manipulating the movement of electrons and holes across the junctions between n-type and p-type silicon. Transistors are composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit, and the collector current is approximately β (common-emitter current gain) times the base current.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.1515625393061995, "g_worst_claim": 0.12415738900502522, "g_content": 1.98980030260588, "g_traj_mean": 1.3185361623764038}}], "selected_rollout": 0, "random_sel_rollout": 3, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 51.67, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "554", "question": "why does the company Oracle have 2 CEOs", "gt": "Oracle has two CEOs because the former CEO, Larry Ellison, stepped down and chose to have two of his co-presidents, Safra Catz and Mark Hurd, share the role of CEO.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, Oracle has two CEOs because Larry Ellison, the founder of the company, unexpectedly announced his step down from his CEO post. Ellison named Safra Catz and Mark Hurd, the co-presidents of Oracle, to replace him as CEOs. This decision was made to ensure a smooth transition and to maintain the company's leadership structure.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, Oracle has two CEOs because Larry Ellison, the founder of the company, unexpectedly announced his step down from his CEO post. Ellison named Safra Catz and Mark Hurd, the co-presidents of Oracle, to replace him as CEOs. This decision was made to ensure a smooth transition and to maintain the company's leadership structure.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, Oracle has two CEOs because Larry Ellison, the founder of the company, unexpectedly announced his step down from his CEO post. Ellison named Safra Catz and Mark Hurd, the co-presidents of Oracle, to replace him as CEOs. This decision was made to ensure a smooth transition and to maintain the company's leadership structure.\", \"Based on the given passages, Oracle has two CEOs because Larry Ellison, the founder and former CEO, stepped down from his position, and he appointed both Mark Hurd and Safra Catz to take over as co-CEOs. This decision aligns with the trend of major companies having multiple CEOs, as evidenced by examples like Chipotle, Whole Foods, Deutsche Bank, Samsung, and SAP.\", \"Based on the given passages, Oracle has two CEOs because Larry Ellison, the founder of the company, announced that he is stepping down from his CEO post. To replace him, he named Mark Hurd and Safra Catz as the new CEOs, who will share the role of CEO. This decision was made in an unexpected manner and is not unique to Oracle, as other major companies like Chipotle, Whole Foods, Deutsche Bank, Samsung, and SAP also have two CEOs.\", \"Based on the given passages, Oracle has two CEOs because Larry Ellison has stepped down as CEO and has been replaced in the position by former HP CEO Mark Hurd and Safra Catz. Ellison's new titles are Executive Chairman and Chief Technology Officer. This change is unusual as Oracle isn't the first major company to have more than one CEO at the same time, as Chipotle, Whole Foods, and Deutsche Bank also have two CEOs.\"]", "time_sec": 14.872, "generation_time_seconds": 14.124, "selection_time_seconds": 0.7423, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 21422, 702, 1378, 74217, 1576, 29290, 81647, 11, 279, 18996, 315, 279, 2813, 11, 50609, 7228, 806, 3019, 1495, 504, 806, 12156, 1736, 13, 81647, 6941, 12582, 956, 17358, 89, 323, 4389, 472, 16081, 11, 279, 1062, 79776, 6880, 315, 21422, 11, 311, 8290, 1435, 438, 74217, 13, 1096, 5480, 572, 1865, 311, 5978, 264, 10876, 9142, 323, 311, 10306, 279, 2813, 594, 11438, 5944, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, Oracle has two CEOs because Larry Ellison, the founder of the company, unexpectedly announced his step down from his CEO post. Ellison named Safra Catz and Mark Hurd, the co-presidents of Oracle, to replace him as CEOs. This decision was made to ensure a smooth transition and to maintain the company's leadership structure.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.0381825620348835, "g_worst_claim": 0.06955933570861816, "g_content": 1.561586155610926, "g_traj_mean": 0.9658756852149963}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 21422, 702, 1378, 74217, 1576, 29290, 81647, 11, 279, 18996, 323, 4741, 12156, 11, 24326, 1495,