 achieve equality among individuals across the world. According to the first passage, this concept rejects the ideas of equality of resources and equality of opportunity as workable principles of global justice. The second passage highlights the striking economic inequality existing in the world, suggesting that global egalitarianism seeks to address this disparity. The third passage clarifies that instrumental egalitarianism values equality as a means to an end, while non-instrumental egalitarianism values equality for its own sake.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.9164967174768012, "g_worst_claim": 0.2845900058746338, "g_content": 1.7770978693376507, "g_traj_mean": 1.0627937316894531}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 3644, 76171, 20030, 2142, 374, 264, 1910, 55603, 42556, 315, 3644, 12161, 429, 21538, 311, 11075, 21777, 369, 678, 1251, 15484, 315, 862, 3728, 13, 1084, 28533, 429, 13866, 21777, 315, 4963, 6329, 21777, 315, 6638, 10868, 264, 975, 480, 17508, 315, 3644, 12161, 11, 438, 13866, 646, 387, 16878, 41599, 745, 1948, 26735, 13, 576, 7286, 315, 3644, 76171, 20030, 2142, 374, 40876, 304, 279, 4522, 429, 21777, 1265, 387, 32547, 369, 1181, 1828, 24483, 323, 537, 438, 264, 3363, 311, 11075, 1008, 8845, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, global egalitarianism is a free-standing conception of global justice that aims to achieve equality for all people regardless of their location. It argues that neither equality of resources nor equality of opportunity represents a workable principle of global justice, as neither can be measured neutrally between cultures. The concept of global egalitarianism is rooted in the idea that equality should be valued for its own sake and not as a means to achieve other goals.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.34620056199002, "g_worst_claim": 0.32990099986394245, "g_content": 2.2831940761832303, "g_traj_mean": 1.2802371978759766}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": 