3, 5942, 525, 34219, 553, 8225, 11055, 23783, 624, 18, 13, 35324, 9435, 14886, 525, 2989, 304, 2176, 5942, 382, 35, 58326, 510, 16, 13, 576, 22596, 1849, 374, 1865, 315, 48284, 7761, 1393, 279, 3972, 77638, 739, 374, 1865, 315, 81275, 624, 17, 13, 47025, 374, 10660, 553, 279, 24394, 30956, 65457, 304, 279, 22596, 1849, 323, 553, 25266, 320, 71, 493, 3154, 8, 304, 279, 3972, 77638, 739, 624, 18, 13, 576, 22596, 1849, 374, 19259, 448, 82316, 7761, 11, 1393, 3972, 77638, 739, 35753, 525, 43179, 323, 537, 21893, 8433, 624, 19, 13, 576, 22596, 1849, 5711, 33213, 311, 29282, 17289, 11, 19853, 279, 3972, 77638, 739, 5711, 279, 4225, 37120, 1849, 311, 29282, 43215, 13, 151645], "pred": "Differences:\n1. The nervous system is made of neuron cells while the Endocrine System is made of glands.\n2. Transmission is performed by the electrochemical pulses in the nervous system and by chemicals (hormones) in the Endocrine System.\n3. The nervous system is continuous with interconnected cells, while Endocrine System organs are discrete and not physically connected.\n4. The nervous system uses neurons to transmit signals, whereas the Endocrine System uses the circulatory system to transmit hormones.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.4989567676969437, "g_worst_claim": 0.2097217321395874, "g_content": 2.011180623372396, "g_traj_mean": 1.3183410167694092}}], "selected_rollout": 3, "random_sel_rollout": 0, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 61.97, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "635", "question": "similarity and difference between nervous system and endocrine system", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here are the key similarities and differences between the nervous system and the endocrine system:\n\nSimilarities:\n\n* Both systems are involved in maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems.\n* Both systems use chemical messengers (neurotransmitters and hormones) to transmit signals.\n* Both systems have negative feedback mechanisms to regulate their function.\n\nDifferences:\n\n* The nervous system is made up of neuron cells, while the endocrine system is composed of glands.\n* Transmission of signals occurs through electrical impulses in the nervous system, while hormones are responsible for signal transmission in the endocrine system.\n* The nervous system has a continuous structure, with cells connected to each other, while the endocrine system consists of discrete organs not physically connected to each other.\n* The nervous system uses neurons to transmit signals, while the endocrine system relies on the circulatory system to transmit signals.\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages.", "pred": "Differences:\n1. The nervous system is made up of neuron cells, while the endocrine system consists of glands.\n2. Transmission in the nervous system occurs through electrochemical pulses, whereas hormones in the endocrine system transmit signals.\n3. The nervous system is continuous, with cells interconnected, in contrast to the endocrine system's discrete organs.\n4. The nervous system uses neurons for signal transmission, while the endocrine system relies on the circulatory system for signal transmission.\n5. The nervous system is continuous and interconnected, whereas the organs of the endocrine system are not physically connected.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Differences:\n1. The nervous system is made up of neuron cells, while the endocrine system consists of glands.\n2. Transmission in the nervous system occurs through electrochemical pulses, whereas hormones in the endocrine system transmit signals.\n3. The nervous system is continuous, with cells interconnected, in contrast to the endocrine system's discrete organs.\n4. The nervous system uses neurons for signal transmission, while the endocrine system relies on the circulatory system for signal transmission.\n5. The nervous system is continuous and interconnected, whereas the organs of the endocrine system are not physically connected.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, the similarities between the nervous system and the endocrine system include:\\n\\n1. Both systems are devoted to maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems.\\n2. Both systems are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms.\\n3. Chemical messengers are important in both systems.\\n\\nThe differences between the nervous system and the endocrine system include:\\n\\n1. The nervous system is made of neuron cells, while the endocrine system is made of glands.\\n2. Transmission in the nervous system is performed by electrochemical pulses, whereas in the endocrine system, it is done by chemicals known as hormones.\\n3. The nervous system is continuous, with cells interconnected, whereas the organs of the endocrine system are discrete and not physically connected.\\n4. The nervous system utilizes neurons to transmit signals, while the endocrine system uses the circulatory system to transmit signals.\", \"Based on the given passages, the nervous system and the endocrine system have several similarities and differences:\\n\\nSimilarities:\\n1. Both systems are involved in maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems.\\n2. Both systems are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms.\\n3. Chemical messengers are essential in both systems.\\n\\nDifferences:\\n1. The nervous system is made up of neuron cells, while the endocrine system consists of glands.\\n2. Transmission in the nervous system occurs through electrochemical pulses, whereas hormones in the endocrine system transmit signals.\\n3. The nervous system is continuous, with cells interconnected, in contrast to the endocrine system's discrete organs.\\n4. The nervous system uses neurons for signal transmission, while the endocrine system relies on the circulatory system for signal transmission.\\n5. The nervous system is continuous and interconnected, whereas the organs of the endocrine system are not physically connected.\", \"Based on the given passages, here are the similarities and differences between the nervous system and the endocrine system:\\n\\nSimilarities:\\n1. Both systems are devoted to maintaining homeostasis.\\n2. Both systems are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms.\\n3. Chemical messengers are important in both systems.\\n\\nDifferences:\\n1. The nervous system is composed of neuron cells, while the endocrine system is made of glands.\\n2. Transmission in the nervous system is performed by electrochemical pulses, whereas in the endocrine system, it is done by chemical hormones.\\n3. The nervous system is continuous, with cells interconnected, whereas the organs of the endocrine system are not physically connected, being discrete in nature.\\n4. The nervous system transmits signals using neurons, while the endocrine system utilizes the circulatory system for signal transmission.\", \"Based on the given passages, the differences between the nervous system and the endocrine system are as follows: The nervous system is made of neuron cells while the Endocrine System is made of glands. Transmission is performed by the electrochemical pulses in the nervous system, whereas hormones are responsible for signal transmission in the Endocrine System. The nervous system is continuous, with cells interconnected, whereas the organs of the Endocrine System are discrete and not physically connected. The nervous system utilizes neurons to transmit signals, while the Endocrine System uses the circulatory system for transmission. The similarities between the nervous system and the endocrine system include their role in maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems, as well as their regulation by negative feedback mechanisms. Both systems rely on chemical messengers for signaling, although their methods of transmission and release differ.\"]", "time_sec": 28.937, "generation_time_seconds": 28.1777, "selection_time_seconds": 0.7491, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 42774, 1948, 279, 22596, 1849, 323, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 2924, 1447, 16, 13, 11733, 5942, 525, 28229, 311, 20337, 2114, 535, 10718, 553, 65415, 323, 57399, 279, 7488, 315, 1008, 7761, 11, 38781, 11, 35753, 11, 323, 5942, 624, 17, 13, 11733, 5942, 525, 34219, 553, 8225, 11055, 23783, 624, 18, 13, 35324, 9435, 14886, 525, 2989, 304, 2176, 5942, 382, 785, 11799, 1948, 279, 22596, 1849, 323, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 2924, 1447, 16, 13, 576, 22596, 1849, 374, 1865, 315, 48284, 7761, 11, 1393, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 374, 1865, 315, 81275, 624, 17, 13, 47025, 304, 279, 22596, 1849, 374, 10660, 553, 24394, 30956, 65457, 11, 19853, 304, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 11, 432, 374, 2814, 553, 25266, 3881, 438, 43215, 624, 18, 13, 576, 22596, 1849, 374, 19259, 11, 448, 7761, 82316, 11, 19853, 279, 35753, 315, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 525, 43179, 323, 537, 21893, 8433, 624, 19, 13, 576, 22596, 1849, 59780, 33213, 311, 29282, 17289, 11, 1393, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 5711, 279, 4225, 37120, 1849, 311, 29282, 17289, 13, 151645], "pred": "1. The nervous system is made of neuron cells, while the endocrine system is made of glands.\n2. Transmission in the nervous system is performed by electrochemical pulses, whereas in the endocrine system, it is done by chemicals known as hormones.\n3. The nervous system is continuous, with cells interconnected, whereas the organs of the endocrine system are discrete and not physically connected.\n4. The nervous system utilizes neurons to transmit signals, while the endocrine system uses the circulatory system to transmit signals.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.231249453940288, "g_worst_claim": 0.04852795600891113, "g_content": 1.6601497350114116, "g_traj_mean": 1.0894755125045776}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 22596, 1849, 323, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 614, 3807, 42774, 323, 11799, 1447, 34402, 1361, 510, 16, 13, 11733, 5942, 525, 6398, 304, 20337, 2114, 535, 10718, 553, 65415, 323, 57399, 279, 7488, 315, 1008, 7761, 11, 38781, 11, 35753, 11, 323, 5942, 624, 17, 13, 11733, 5942, 525, 34219, 553, 8225, 11055, 23783, 624, 18, 13, 35324, 9435, 14886, 525, 7565, 304, 2176, 5942, 382, 35, 58326, 510, 16, 13, 576, 22596, 1849, 374, 1865, 705, 315, 48284, 7761, 11, 1393, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 17167, 315, 81275, 624, 17, 13, 47025, 304, 279, 22596, 1849, 13666, 1526, 24394, 30956, 65457, 11, 19853, 43215, 304, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 29282, 17289, 624, 18, 13, 576, 22596, 1849, 374, 19259, 11, 448, 7761, 82316, 11, 304, 12872, 311, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 594, 43179, 35753, 624, 19, 13, 576, 22596, 1849, 5711, 33213, 369, 8286, 18368, 11, 1393, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 33644, 389, 279, 4225, 37120, 1849, 369, 8286, 18368, 624, 20, 13, 576, 22596, 1849, 374, 19259, 323, 82316, 11, 19853, 279, 35753, 315, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 525, 537, 21893, 8433, 13, 151645], "pred": "Differences:\n1. The nervous system is made up of neuron cells, while the endocrine system consists of glands.\n2. Transmission in the nervous system occurs through electrochemical pulses, whereas hormones in the endocrine system transmit signals.\n3. The nervous system is continuous, with cells interconnected, in contrast to the endocrine system's discrete organs.\n4. The nervous system uses neurons for signal transmission, while the endocrine system relies on the circulatory system for signal transmission.\n5. The nervous system is continuous and interconnected, whereas the organs of the endocrine system are not physically connected.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.179351379520027, "g_worst_claim": 0.10852006822824478, "g_content": 1.487921601982527, "g_traj_mean": 1.0242246389389038}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 1588, 525, 279, 42774, 323, 11799, 1948, 279, 22596, 1849, 323, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 1447, 34402, 1361, 510, 16, 13, 11733, 5942, 525, 28229, 311, 20337, 2114, 535, 10718, 624, 17, 13, 11733, 5942, 525, 34219, 553, 8225, 11055, 23783, 624, 18, 13, 35324, 9435, 14886, 525, 2989, 304, 2176, 5942, 382, 35, 58326, 510, 16, 13, 576, 22596, 1849, 374, 23415, 315, 48284, 7761, 11, 1393, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 374, 1865, 315, 81275, 624, 17, 13, 47025, 304, 279, 22596, 1849, 374, 10660, 553, 24394, 30956, 65457, 11, 19853, 304, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 11, 432, 374, 2814, 553, 11483, 43215, 624, 18, 13, 576, 22596, 1849, 374, 19259, 11, 448, 7761, 82316, 11, 19853, 279, 35753, 315, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 525, 537, 21893, 8433, 11, 1660, 43179, 304, 6993, 624, 19, 13, 576, 22596, 1849, 1356, 44703, 17289, 1667, 33213, 11, 1393, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 59780, 279, 4225, 37120, 1849, 369, 8286, 18368, 13, 151645], "pred": "Differences:\n1. The nervous system is composed of neuron cells, while the endocrine system is made of glands.\n2. Transmission in the nervous system is performed by electrochemical pulses, whereas in the endocrine system, it is done by chemical hormones.\n3. The nervous system is continuous, with cells interconnected, whereas the organs of the endocrine system are not physically connected, being discrete in nature.\n4. The nervous system transmits signals using neurons, while the endocrine system utilizes the circulatory system for signal transmission.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.3006542265078598, "g_worst_claim": 0.09735437801906041, "g_content": 1.614571410340148, "g_traj_mean": 1.0240124464035034}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 11799, 1948, 279, 22596, 1849, 323, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 525, 438, 11017, 25, 576, 22596, 1849, 374, 1865, 315, 48284, 7761, 1393, 279, 3972, 77638, 739, 374, 1865, 315, 81275, 13, 47025, 374, 10660, 553, 279, 24394, 30956, 65457, 304, 279, 22596, 1849, 11, 19853, 43215, 525, 8480, 369, 8286, 18368, 304, 279, 3972, 77638, 739, 13, 576, 22596, 1849, 374, 19259, 11, 448, 7761, 82316, 11, 19853, 279, 35753, 315, 279, 3972, 77638, 739, 525, 43179, 323, 537, 21893, 8433, 13, 576, 22596, 1849, 59780, 33213, 311, 29282, 17289, 11, 1393, 279, 3972, 77638, 739, 5711, 279, 4225, 37120, 1849, 369, 18368, 13, 576, 42774, 1948, 279, 22596, 1849, 323, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 2924, 862, 3476, 304, 20337, 2114, 535, 10718, 553, 65415, 323, 57399, 279, 7488, 315, 1008, 7761, 11, 38781, 11, 35753, 11, 323, 5942, 11, 438, 1632, 438, 862, 19256, 553, 8225, 11055, 23783, 13, 11733, 5942, 17188, 389, 11483, 9435, 14886, 369, 41980, 11, 7892, 862, 5413, 315, 18368, 323, 4879, 1745, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, the differences between the nervous system and the endocrine system are as follows: The nervous system is made of neuron cells while the Endocrine System is made of glands. Transmission is performed by the electrochemical pulses in the nervous system, whereas hormones are responsible for signal transmission in the Endocrine System. The nervous system is continuous, with cells interconnected, whereas the organs of the Endocrine System are discrete and not physically connected. The nervous system utilizes neurons to transmit signals, while the Endocrine System uses the circulatory system for transmission. The similarities between the nervous system and the endocrine system include their role in maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems, as well as their regulation by negative feedback mechanisms. Both systems rely on chemical messengers for signaling, although their methods of transmission and release differ.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.474766569631644, "g_worst_claim": 0.17958988746007284, "g_content": 1.9838829927666242, "g_traj_mean": 1.337803602218628}}], "selected_rollout": 3, "random_sel_rollout": 1, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 57.32, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.8, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "636", "question": "similarity and difference between nervous system and endocrine system", "gt": "Sure! Based on the given passages, here are the key differences and similarities between the nervous system and the endocrine system:\n\nDifferences:\n\n* The nervous system is made of neuron cells, while the endocrine system is made of glands.\n* Transmission in the nervous system occurs through electrochemical pulses, while in the endocrine system, it is done through chemicals called hormones.\n* The nervous system is continuous and interconnected, while the endocrine system consists of discrete organs.\n* The nervous system uses neurons to transmit signals, while the endocrine system uses the circulatory system.\n\nSimilarities:\n\n* Both systems are devoted to maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems.\n* Both systems are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms.\n* Chemical messengers are important in both systems, although their method of transmission and release differs.\n\nTherefore, based on the given passages, the nervous system and endocrine system have different structures and methods of transmission, but they share similar functions and regulatory mechanisms.", "pred": "The similarity between the nervous system and the endocrine system is that both systems coordinate and regulate the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems to maintain homeostasis. Additionally, both systems are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms and rely on chemical messengers. The nervous system uses neurons to transmit signals, whereas the Endocrine System uses the circulatory system to transmit signals.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "The similarity between the nervous system and the endocrine system is that both systems coordinate and regulate the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems to maintain homeostasis. Additionally, both systems are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms and rely on chemical messengers. The nervous system uses neurons to transmit signals, whereas the Endocrine System uses the circulatory system to transmit signals.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, the similarities between the nervous system and the endocrine system include:\\n\\n1. Both systems are devoted to maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems.\\n2. Both systems are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms.\\n3. Chemical messengers are important in both systems.\\n\\nThe differences between the nervous system and the endocrine system include:\\n\\n1. The nervous system is made of neuron cells, while the endocrine system is made of glands.\\n2. Transmission in the nervous system is performed by electrochemical pulses, whereas in the endocrine system, it is done by chemicals known as hormones.\\n3. The nervous system is continuous, with cells interconnected, whereas the organs of the endocrine system are discrete and not physically connected.\\n4. The nervous system utilizes neurons to transmit signals, while the endocrine system uses the circulatory system to transmit signals.\", \"Based on the given passages, the difference between the nervous system and the endocrine system lies in the type of cells they are made of. The nervous system is made up of neuron cells, while the Endocrine System is made of glands. The nervous system transmits signals through electrochemical pulses, whereas the Endocrine System uses chemicals called hormones for signal transmission. \\n\\nThe similarity between the nervous system and the endocrine system is that both systems coordinate and regulate the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems to maintain homeostasis. Additionally, both systems are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms and rely on chemical messengers. The nervous system uses neurons to transmit signals, whereas the Endocrine System uses the circulatory system to transmit signals.\", \"Based on the given passages, the similarities between the nervous system and the endocrine system are:\\n\\n1. Both systems are involved in maintaining homeostasis.\\n2. Both systems are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms.\\n\\nThe differences between the nervous system and the endocrine system are:\\n\\n1. The nervous system is made of neuron cells, while the endocrine system is made of glands.\\n2. Transmission in the nervous system is performed by electrochemical pulses, whereas hormones are responsible for signal transmission in the endocrine system.\\n3. The nervous system is continuous and interconnected, whereas the organs of the endocrine system are not physically connected and are discrete in nature.\\n4. The nervous system uses neurons to transmit signals, while the endocrine system uses the circulatory system to transmit signals.\", \"Based on the given passages, the similarities between the nervous system and the endocrine system are that both systems are devoted to maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems, and both systems are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms. Chemical messengers are important in both systems, although their method of transmission and release differs in the two systems.\\n\\nThe differences between the nervous system and the endocrine system are that the nervous system is made of neuron cells while the endocrine system is made of glands. Transmission in the nervous system is performed by electrochemical pulses, while in the endocrine system, it is done by chemicals known as hormones. The nervous system is continuous, with cells interconnected, whereas the organs of the endocrine system are not physically connected and are discrete in nature. The nervous system utilizes neurons to transmit signals, while the endocrine system uses the circulatory system to transmit signals.\"]", "time_sec": 27.729, "generation_time_seconds": 26.7571, "selection_time_seconds": 0.9671, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 42774, 1948, 279, 22596, 1849, 323, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 2924, 1447, 16, 13, 11733, 5942, 525, 28229, 311, 20337, 2114, 535, 10718, 553, 65415, 323, 57399, 279, 7488, 315, 1008, 7761, 11, 38781, 11, 35753, 11, 323, 5942, 624, 17, 13, 11733, 5942, 525, 34219, 553, 8225, 11055, 23783, 624, 18, 13, 35324, 9435, 14886, 525, 2989, 304, 2176, 5942, 382, 785, 11799, 1948, 279, 22596, 1849, 323, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 2924, 1447, 16, 13, 576, 22596, 1849, 374, 1865, 315, 48284, 7761, 11, 1393, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 374, 1865, 315, 81275, 624, 17, 13, 47025, 304, 279, 22596, 1849, 374, 10660, 553, 24394, 30956, 65457, 11, 19853, 304, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 11, 432, 374, 2814, 553, 25266, 3881, 438, 43215, 624, 18, 13, 576, 22596, 1849, 374, 19259, 11, 448, 7761, 82316, 11, 19853, 279, 35753, 315, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 525, 43179, 323, 537, 21893, 8433, 624, 19, 13, 576, 22596, 1849, 59780, 33213, 311, 29282, 17289, 11, 1393, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 5711, 279, 4225, 37120, 1849, 311, 29282, 17289, 13, 151645], "pred": "1. The nervous system is made of neuron cells, while the endocrine system is made of glands.\n2. Transmission in the nervous system is performed by electrochemical pulses, whereas in the endocrine system, it is done by chemicals known as hormones.\n3. The nervous system is continuous, with cells interconnected, whereas the organs of the endocrine system are discrete and not physically connected.\n4. The nervous system utilizes neurons to transmit signals, while the endocrine system uses the circulatory system to transmit signals.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.257293700844317, "g_worst_claim": 0.05458577970663706, "g_content": 1.7060155975684692, "g_traj_mean": 1.1096971035