 things such as oxygen throughout the body, while white blood cells are part of the immune system and help the body combat disease, viruses, etc.\n3. Red blood cells are found in the circulatory system, while white blood cells are found in the circulatory system and can be carried by blood from one part to another part of the body.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.8264294220663313, "g_worst_claim": 0.03462763043010936, "g_content": 1.3707231182918371, "g_traj_mean": 0.8924252986907959}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 42774, 1948, 2518, 323, 4158, 6543, 7761, 2924, 510, 12, 11733, 2518, 6543, 7761, 320, 49, 4897, 82, 8, 323, 4158, 6543, 7761, 320, 54, 4897, 82, 8, 525, 14122, 5424, 315, 6543, 624, 12, 11733, 431, 4897, 82, 323, 467, 4897, 82, 525, 14122, 504, 59117, 573, 63532, 19101, 7761, 304, 2518, 17240, 82648, 624, 12, 11733, 4494, 315, 7761, 614, 2779, 78248, 323, 9503, 98605, 10530, 624, 785, 11799, 1948, 2518, 323, 4158, 6543, 7761, 2924, 510, 12, 3731, 6543, 7761, 525, 949, 315, 279, 4225, 37120, 1849, 323, 6777, 23552, 11, 1393, 4158, 6543, 7761, 525, 949, 315, 279, 22077, 1849, 323, 1492, 12610, 8457, 11, 40968, 11, 4992, 624, 12, 3731, 6543, 7761, 525, 8480, 369, 23988, 23552, 311, 279, 2487, 594, 38781, 11, 19853, 4158, 6543, 7761, 6016, 279, 2487, 2348, 77184, 624, 12, 576, 2518, 6543, 7761, 323, 4158, 6543, 7761, 646, 387, 11691, 553, 6543, 504, 825, 949, 311, 2441, 949, 315, 279, 2487, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, the similarities between red and white blood cells include:\n- Both red blood cells (RBCs) and white blood cells (WBCs) are formed elements of blood.\n- Both RBCs and WBCs are formed from pluripotent stem cells in red bone marrow.\n- Both types of cells have cell membranes and cytoplasm.\nThe differences between red and white blood cells include:\n- Red blood cells are part of the circulatory system and carry oxygen, while white blood cells are part of the immune system and help combat disease, viruses, etc.\n- Red blood cells are responsible for delivering oxygen to the body's tissues, whereas white blood cells protect the body against pathogens.\n- The red blood cells and white blood cells can be carried by blood from one part to another part of the body.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.7459981215808643, "g_worst_claim": 0.20739585823482937, "g_content": 1.3003378681752873, "g_traj_mean": 0.8845714926719666}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 42774, 1948, 2518, 323, 4158, 6543, 7761, 2924, 1447, 12, 11733, 2518, 6543, 7761, 320, 49, 4897, 82, 8, 323, 4158, 6543, 7761, 320, 54, 4897, 82, 8, 525, 14122, 5424, 315, 6543, 624, 12, 11733, 431, 4897, 82, 323, 467, 4897, 82, 646, 387, 11691, 553, 6543, 504, 825, 949, 315, 279, 2487, 311, 2441, 624, 12, 11733, 431, 4897, 82, 323, 467, 4897, 82, 525, 1730, 304, 279, 4225, 37120, 1849, 624, 12, 11733, 431, 4897, 82, 323, 467, 4897, 82, 614, 2779, 78248, 323, 9503, 98605, 10530, 624, 12, 11733, 431, 4897, 82, 323, 467, 4897, 82, 525, 14122, 504, 59117, 573, 63532, 19101, 7761, 304, 279, 2518, 17240, 82648, 382, 11209, 11, 279, 21085, 1558, 537, 3410, 3151, 11799, 1948, 2518, 323, 4158, 6543, 7761, 13, 151645], "pred": "However, the passage does not provide specific differences between red and white blood cells.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.6067066960074384, "g_worst_claim": -0.0703124850988388, "g_content": 1.3763795332475142, "g_traj_mean": 0.6802555918693542}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 2518, 6543, 7761, 320, 49, 4897, 82, 8, 323, 4158, 6543, 7761, 320, 54, 4897, 82, 8, 4332, 3807, 42774, 510, 12, 11733, 525, 14122, 5424, 315, 6543, 624, 12, 11733, 525, 14122, 504, 59117, 573, 63532, 19101, 7761, 304, 279, 2518, 17240, 82648, 382, 34797, 11799, 2924, 510, 12, 431, 4897, 82, 525, 8480, 369, 23552, 9691, 504, 279, 44174, 311, 279, 19847, 304, 279, 2487, 323, 6777, 2513, 1741, 438, 23552, 6814, 279, 2487, 624, 12, 467, 4897, 82, 525, 949, 315, 279, 22077, 1849, 323, 1492, 279, 2487, 12610, 8457, 11, 40968, 11, 4992, 624, 12, 431, 4897, 82, 525, 949, 315, 279, 4225, 37120, 1849, 11, 1393, 467, 4897, 82, 525, 949, 315, 279, 22077, 1849, 624, 12, 431, 4897, 82, 525, 1730, 304, 279, 4225, 37120, 1849, 11, 1393, 467, 4897, 82, 525, 1730, 304, 279, 4225, 37120, 1849, 624, 12, 431, 4897, 82, 525, 14122, 504, 59117, 573, 63532, 19101, 7761, 304, 279, 2518, 17240, 82648, 11, 1393, 467, 4897, 82, 525, 14122, 504, 59117, 573, 63532, 19101, 7761, 304, 279, 2518, 17240, 82648, 13, 151645], "pred": "Their differences include:\n- RBCs are responsible for oxygen delivery from the lungs to the tissue in the body and carry things such as oxygen throughout the body.\n- WBCs are part of the immune system and help the body combat disease, viruses, etc.\n- RBCs are part of the circulatory system, while WBCs are part of the immune system.\n- RBCs are found in the circulatory system, while WBCs are found in the circulatory system.\n- RBCs are formed from pluripotent stem cells in the red bone marrow, while WBCs are formed from pluripotent stem cells in the red bone marrow.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.7799801343716646, "g_worst_claim": 0.04508944352467855, "g_content": 1.5793878555297851, "g_traj_mean": 0.8275487422943115}}], "selected_rollout": 3, "random_sel_rollout": 0, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 44.44, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "644", "question": "how to plant a potato that has sprouted", "gt": "To plant a potato that has sprouted, first cut large potatoes into chunks with two or three eyes each, being careful not to break the long sprouts. Place these chunks on a tray and let them dry out in a cool, dark place overnight. Small, sprouted potatoes can be planted whole. Next, prepare your planting area by turning the soil over with a hoe or tiller to a depth of 12 inches, removing any large rocks or debris. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep. Cut your potatoes into cubes of about 1 to 2 inches, making sure to leave a sprout on each cube. Place these cubes in the trench, sprouted side facing up, spaced about 6 to 8 inches apart. Cover the potatoes with compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings. Water the potatoes thoroughly and regularly when the soil is dry to the touch. Finally, add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, keeping it at least 4 inches away from the sprouting potato vines. This mulch will help keep the soil cool and maintain a consistent moisture level.", "pred": "1. Cut the sprouted potato into chunks with two or three eyes each using a sharp knife.\n2. Place the potato chunks on a tray or cookie sheet.\n3. Set the potato chunks in a cool, dark location overnight so the cut ends can dry out.\n4. Small potatoes can be planted whole.\n5. Water the potatoes thoroughly.\n6. Continue watering regularly when the soil is dry to the touch.\n7. Add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants.\n8. Pull the mulch back at least 4 inches from the sprouting potato vines.\n9. The mulch helps keep the soil cool and maintains a consistent moisture level.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "1. Cut the sprouted potato into chunks with two or three eyes each using a sharp knife.\n2. Place the potato chunks on a tray or cookie sheet.\n3. Set the potato chunks in a cool, dark location overnight so the cut ends can dry out.\n4. Small potatoes can be planted whole.\n5. Water the potatoes thoroughly.\n6. Continue watering regularly when the soil is dry to the touch.\n7. Add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants.\n8. Pull the mulch back at least 4 inches from the sprouting potato vines.\n9. The mulch helps keep the soil cool and maintains a consistent moisture level.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, to plant a sprouted potato, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. Prepare the planting area by turning the soil over with a hoe or tiller to a depth of 12 inches and breaking it up finely. Remove any large rocks and other debris.\\n2. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep.\\n3. Cut the sprouted potato into cubes, about 1 to 2 inches on each side, leaving a sprout on each cube. Nonsprouted sections should have two or three eyes on each.\\n4. Place the cubes into the bottom of the trench, separating each cube from the other by about 6 to 8 inches. Ensure the sprouted or eye side of the cubes is facing up.\\n5. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings.\", \"Based on the given passages, to plant a sprouted potato, follow these steps:\\n1. Prepare the garden space by turning the soil over to a depth of 12 inches, breaking it up finely, and removing large rocks and debris.\\n2. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep.\\n3. Cut the sprouted potato into cubes, ensuring each cube has a sprout, with sections having two or three eyes on each.\\n4. Place the cubes in the trench, separating each by about 6 to 8 inches, with the sprouted or eye side facing up.\\n5. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost.\\n6. Replace the dirt on top of the plantings.\", \"Based on the given passages, to plant a sprouted potato:\\n\\n1. Cut the sprouted potato into chunks with two or three eyes each using a sharp knife.\\n2. Place the potato chunks on a tray or cookie sheet.\\n3. Set the potato chunks in a cool, dark location overnight so the cut ends can dry out.\\n4. Small potatoes can be planted whole.\\n5. Water the potatoes thoroughly.\\n6. Continue watering regularly when the soil is dry to the touch.\\n7. Add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants.\\n8. Pull the mulch back at least 4 inches from the sprouting potato vines.\\n9. The mulch helps keep the soil cool and maintains a consistent moisture level.\", \"Based on the given passages, to plant a sprouted potato, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. Prepare the planting area by turning the soil over with a hoe or tiller to a depth of 12 inches and breaking the dirt up finely. Remove any large rocks and other debris while hoeing.\\n\\n2. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep. If planting more than one row, space them apart by about 24 to 36 inches.\\n\\n3. Cut the sprouted potato into cubes, about 1 to 2 inches on each side, ensuring each cube has a sprout on it.\\n\\n4. Place the cubes into the bottom of the trench, keeping a spacing of about 6 to 8 inches between each cube.\\n\\n5. Place the sprouted or eye side of the cubes facing up in the trench.\\n\\n6. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings.\"]", "time_sec": 28.054, "generation_time_seconds": 26.8859, "selection_time_seconds": 1.1599, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 311, 6008, 264, 8151, 18533, 38734, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 31166, 279, 47014, 3082, 553, 13054, 279, 17171, 916, 448, 264, 45876, 476, 11956, 261, 311, 264, 7990, 315, 220, 16, 17, 14924, 323, 14719, 432, 705, 60702, 13, 10783, 894, 3460, 23035, 323, 1008, 33998, 624, 17, 13, 37969, 264, 53252, 911, 220, 19, 14924, 6884, 323, 220, 21, 311, 220, 23, 14924, 5538, 624, 18, 13, 19649, 279, 8151, 18533, 38734, 1119, 54104, 11, 911, 220, 16, 311, 220, 17, 14924, 389, 1817, 3108, 11, 9380, 264,