21, 8290, 279, 25284, 389, 1909, 315, 279, 6008, 819, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, to plant a sprouted potato, follow these steps: First, prepare the planting area by turning the soil over with a hoe or tiller to a depth of 12 inches, breaking the dirt up finely and removing any large rocks and debris. Next, dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep. If planting more than one row, space them apart by 24 to 36 inches. Cut the sprouted potato into cubes, about 1 to 2 inches on each side, leaving a sprout on each cube. Place the cubes into the bottom of the trench, separating each cube from the other by about 6 to 8 inches, with the sprouted or eye side facing up. Finally, cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.1889359046792622, "g_worst_claim": 0.20015418529510498, "g_content": 1.6226511373148336, "g_traj_mean": 1.264440894126892}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 311, 6008, 264, 8151, 18533, 38734, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 3070, 50590, 279, 17171, 95518, 11999, 279, 17171, 916, 304, 697, 12909, 47014, 3082, 448, 264, 45876, 476, 11956, 261, 311, 264, 7990, 315, 220, 16, 17, 14924, 323, 1438, 279, 25284, 705, 60702, 13, 10783, 894, 3460, 23035, 323, 1008, 33998, 1393, 45876, 287, 13, 37969, 264, 53252, 911, 220, 19, 14924, 6884, 323, 220, 21, 311, 220, 23, 14924, 5538, 382, 17, 13, 3070, 50590, 279, 38734, 95518, 19649, 279, 38734, 1119, 54104, 11, 911, 220, 16, 311, 220, 17, 14924, 389, 1817, 3108, 11, 9380, 264, 8151, 411, 389, 1817, 23739, 13, 451, 2382, 649, 18533, 14158, 1265, 614, 1378, 476, 2326, 6414, 389, 1817, 382, 18, 13, 3070, 54647, 279, 38734, 95518, 10771, 279, 54104, 1119, 279, 5622, 315, 279, 53252, 11, 49445, 1817, 23739, 504, 279, 1008, 553, 911, 220, 21, 311, 220, 23, 14924, 13, 10771, 279, 8151, 18533, 476, 7912, 3108, 315, 279, 54104, 12880, 705, 382, 19, 13, 3070, 30896, 323, 6248, 47014, 95518, 17757, 279, 34167, 448, 264, 6193, 315, 56723, 323, 1221, 8290, 279, 25284, 389, 1909, 315, 279, 6008, 819, 13, 151645], "pred": "4. **Cover and finish planting**: Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.3579615972018344, "g_worst_claim": -0.019910237441460293, "g_content": 2.0545934632767078, "g_traj_mean": 1.6152375936508179}}], "selected_rollout": 3, "random_sel_rollout": 2, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 46.06, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "646", "question": "how to plant a potato that has sprouted", "gt": "To plant a potato that has sprouted, follow these steps:\n\n1. Cut large, sprouted potatoes into chunks with two or three eyes each using a sharp knife.\n2. Set the potato chunks on a tray or cookie sheet and allow them to dry out for a cool, dark location overnight.\n3. Once the cut ends are dry, thoroughly wash the potatoes.\n4. Plant the potatoes in the ground, either whole or cut into cubes with a sprout on each cube.\n5. Space the potatoes about 6 to 8 inches apart and cover them with a layer of compost.\n6. Replace the dirt on top of the plantings.\n7. Water the potatoes regularly when the soil is dry to the touch.\n8. Add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, pulling it back at least 4 inches from the sprouting potato vines.", "pred": "1. Turn the soil over in your planned planting area with a hoe or tiller to a depth of 12 inches and break the dirt up finely. Remove any large rocks and other debris while hoeing. \n2. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep.\n3. Cut the sprouted potato into cubes, about 1 to 2 inches on each side, leaving a sprout on each cube.\n4. Nonsprouted sections should have two or three eyes on each.\n5. Place the cubes into the bottom of the trench, separating each cube from the other by about 6 to 8 inches. Ensure the sprouted or eye side of the cubes is facing up.\n6. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "1. Turn the soil over in your planned planting area with a hoe or tiller to a depth of 12 inches and break the dirt up finely. Remove any large rocks and other debris while hoeing. \n2. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep.\n3. Cut the sprouted potato into cubes, about 1 to 2 inches on each side, leaving a sprout on each cube.\n4. Nonsprouted sections should have two or three eyes on each.\n5. Place the cubes into the bottom of the trench, separating each cube from the other by about 6 to 8 inches. Ensure the sprouted or eye side of the cubes is facing up.\n6. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, to plant a sprouted potato, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. Prepare the planting area by turning the soil over with a hoe or tiller to a depth of 12 inches and breaking it up finely. Remove any large rocks and other debris.\\n2. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep.\\n3. Cut the sprouted potato into cubes, about 1 to 2 inches on each side, leaving a sprout on each cube. Nonsprouted sections should have two or three eyes on each.\\n4. Place the cubes into the bottom of the trench, separating each cube from the other by about 6 to 8 inches. Ensure the sprouted or eye side of the cubes is facing up.\\n5. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings.\", \"Based on the given passages:\\n\\n1. Cut large, sprouted potatoes into chunks with two or three eyes each, using a sharp knife, being careful to not break the long sprouts.\\n2. Place the potato chunks on a tray or cookie sheet, and set them in a cool, dark location overnight so the cut ends can dry out.\\n3. Small potatoes can be planted whole.\\n4. Water the potatoes thoroughly.\\n5. Continue watering regularly when the soil is dry to the touch.\\n6. Add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, pulling it back at least 4 inches from the sprouting potato vines.\\n7. Mulch keeps the soil cool while maintaining a consistent moisture level.\\n8. Turn the soil over in your planned planting area with a hoe or tiller to a depth of 12 inches and break the dirt up finely.\\n9. Remove any large rocks and other debris while hoeing.\\n10. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep.\\n11. Cut your potatoes into cubes, about 1 to 2 inches on each side, leaving a sprout on each cube. Nonsprouted sections should have two or three eyes on each.\\n12. Place the cubes into the bottom of the trench, separating each cube from the other by about 6 to 8 inches. Place the sprouted or eye side of the cubes facing up.\\n13. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings.\", \"Based on the given passages, to plant a sprouted potato, follow these steps:\\n1. Cut large, sprouted potatoes into chunks with two or three eyes each using a sharp knife. Avoid breaking the long sprouts.\\n2. Place the potato chunks on a tray or cookie sheet and set them in a cool, dark location overnight so the cut ends can dry out.\\n3. Small potatoes can be planted whole.\\n4. Water the potatoes thoroughly.\\n5. Continue watering regularly when the soil is dry to the touch.\\n6. Add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, pulling it back at least 4 inches from the sprouting potato vines.\\n7. Mulch keeps the soil cool while maintaining a consistent moisture level.\", \"Based on the given passages, to plant a sprouted potato, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. Turn the soil over in your planned planting area with a hoe or tiller to a depth of 12 inches and break the dirt up finely. Remove any large rocks and other debris while hoeing. \\n2. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep.\\n3. Cut the sprouted potato into cubes, about 1 to 2 inches on each side, leaving a sprout on each cube.\\n4. Nonsprouted sections should have two or three eyes on each.\\n5. Place the cubes into the bottom of the trench, separating each cube from the other by about 6 to 8 inches. Ensure the sprouted or eye side of the cubes is facing up.\\n6. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings.\"]", "time_sec": 33.941, "generation_time_seconds": 32.6214, "selection_time_seconds": 1.3127, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 311, 6008, 264, 8151, 18533, 38734, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 31166, 279, 47014, 3082, 553, 13054, 279, 17171, 916, 448, 264, 45876, 476, 11956, 261, 311, 264, 7990, 315, 220, 16, 17, 14924, 323, 14719, 432, 705, 60702, 13, 10783, 894, 3460, 23035, 323, 1008, 33998, 624, 17, 13, 37969, 264, 53252, 911, 220, 19, 14924, 6884, 323, 220, 21, 311, 220, 23, 14924, 5538, 624, 18, 13, 19649, 279, 8151, 18533, 38734, 1119, 54104, 11, 911, 220, 16, 311, 220, 17, 14924, 389, 1817, 3108, 11, 9380, 264, 8151, 411, 389, 1817, 23739, 13, 451, 2382, 649, 18533, 14158, 1265, 614, 1378, 476, 2326, 6414, 389, 1817, 624, 19, 13, 10771, 279, 54104, 1119, 279, 5622, 315, 279, 53252, 11, 49445, 1817, 23739, 504, 279, 1008, 553, 911, 220, 21, 311, 220, 23, 14924, 13, 29279, 279, 8151, 18533, 476, 7912, 3108, 315, 279, 54104, 374, 12880, 705, 624, 20, 13, 17757, 279, 34167, 448, 264, 6193, 315, 56723, 323, 1221, 8290, 279, 25284, 389, 1909, 315, 279, 6008, 819, 13, 151645], "pred": "1. Prepare the planting area by turning the soil over with a hoe or tiller to a depth of 12 inches and breaking it up finely. Remove any large rocks and other debris.\n2. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep.\n3. Cut the sprouted potato into cubes, about 1 to 2 inches on each side, leaving a sprout on each cube. Nonsprouted sections should have two or three eyes on each.\n4. Place the cubes into the bottom of the trench, separating each cube from the other by about 6 to 8 inches. Ensure the sprouted or eye side of the cubes is facing up.\n5. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.251560755847575, "g_worst_claim": 0.005056172609329224, "g_content": 1.7499118160891842, "g_traj_mean": 1.3909220695495605}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 1447, 16, 13, 19649,