es can be planted whole.\n4. Water the potatoes thoroughly.\n5. Continue watering regularly when the soil is dry to the touch.\n6. Add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, pulling it back at least 4 inches from the sprouting potato vines.\n7. Mulch keeps the soil cool while maintaining a consistent moisture level.\n8. Turn the soil over in your planned planting area with a hoe or tiller to a depth of 12 inches and break the dirt up finely.\n9. Remove any large rocks and other debris while hoeing.\n10. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep.\n11. Cut your potatoes into cubes, about 1 to 2 inches on each side, leaving a sprout on each cube. Nonsprouted sections should have two or three eyes on each.\n12. Place the cubes into the bottom of the trench, separating each cube from the other by about 6 to 8 inches. Place the sprouted or eye side of the cubes facing up.\n13. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 2.099182154155869, "g_worst_claim": 0.2971452236175537, "g_content": 2.2503352753103596, "g_traj_mean": 1.75150465965271}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 311, 6008, 264, 8151, 18533, 38734, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 510, 16, 13, 19649, 3460, 11, 8151, 18533, 34167, 1119, 26757, 448, 1378, 476, 2326, 6414, 1817, 1667, 264, 17232, 21430, 13, 34006, 14719, 279, 1293, 8151, 11672, 624, 17, 13, 10771, 279, 38734, 26757, 389, 264, 34688, 476, 12544, 10834, 323, 738, 1105, 304, 264, 7010, 11, 6319, 3728, 24398, 773, 279, 3931, 10335, 646, 9058, 700, 624, 18, 13, 14994, 34167, 646, 387, 38341, 4361, 624, 19, 13, 9959, 279, 34167, 26364, 624, 20, 13, 15003, 72741, 15502, 979, 279, 17171, 374, 9058, 311, 279, 5796, 624, 21, 13, 2691, 264, 220, 19, 24241, 6193, 315, 15602, 331, 2163, 279, 10779, 11, 22266, 432, 1182, 518, 3245, 220, 19, 14924, 504, 279, 8151, 10909, 38734, 85429, 624, 22, 13, 29351, 331, 13598, 279, 17171, 7010, 1393, 20337, 264, 12966, 31157, 2188, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, to plant a sprouted potato, follow these steps:\n1. Cut large, sprouted potatoes into chunks with two or three eyes each using a sharp knife. Avoid breaking the long sprouts.\n2. Place the potato chunks on a tray or cookie sheet and set them in a cool, dark location overnight so the cut ends can dry out.\n3. Small potatoes can be planted whole.\n4. Water the potatoes thoroughly.\n5. Continue watering regularly when the soil is dry to the touch.\n6. Add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, pulling it back at least 4 inches from the sprouting potato vines.\n7. Mulch keeps the soil cool while maintaining a consistent moisture level.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.994212463289624, "g_worst_claim": 0.005056172609329224, "g_content": 2.088954544067383, "g_traj_mean": 1.6129995584487915}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 311, 6008, 264, 8151, 18533, 38734, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 11999, 279, 17171, 916, 304, 697, 12909, 47014, 3082, 448, 264, 45876, 476, 11956, 261, 311, 264, 7990, 315, 220, 16, 17, 14924, 323, 1438, 279, 25284, 705, 60702, 13, 10783, 894, 3460, 23035, 323, 1008, 33998, 1393, 45876, 287, 13, 715, 17, 13, 37969, 264, 53252, 911, 220, 19, 14924, 6884, 323, 220, 21, 311, 220, 23, 14924, 5538, 624, 18, 13, 19649, 279, 8151, 18533, 38734, 1119, 54104, 11, 911, 220, 16, 311, 220, 17, 14924, 389, 1817, 3108, 11, 9380, 264, 8151, 411, 389, 1817, 23739, 624, 19, 13, 451, 2382, 649, 18533, 14158, 1265, 614, 1378, 476, 2326, 6414, 389, 1817, 624, 20, 13, 10771, 279, 54104, 1119, 279, 5622, 315, 279, 53252, 11, 49445, 1817, 23739, 504, 279, 1008, 553, 911, 220, 21, 311, 220, 23, 14924, 13, 29279, 279, 8151, 18533, 476, 7912, 3108, 315, 279, 54104, 374, 12880, 705, 624, 21, 13, 17757, 279, 34167, 448, 264, 6193, 315, 56723, 323, 1221, 8290, 279, 25284, 389, 1909, 315, 279, 6008, 819, 13, 151645], "pred": "1. Turn the soil over in your planned planting area with a hoe or tiller to a depth of 12 inches and break the dirt up finely. Remove any large rocks and other debris while hoeing. \n2. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep.\n3. Cut the sprouted potato into cubes, about 1 to 2 inches on each side, leaving a sprout on each cube.\n4. Nonsprouted sections should have two or three eyes on each.\n5. Place the cubes into the bottom of the trench, separating each cube from the other by about 6 to 8 inches. Ensure the sprouted or eye side of the cubes is facing up.\n6. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.348524311414557, "g_worst_claim": 0.005056172609329224, "g_content": 1.9852262496948243, "g_traj_mean": 1.5209306478500366}}], "selected_rollout": 1, "random_sel_rollout": 3, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 40.44, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "647", "question": "how to plant a potato that has sprouted", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here are the steps to plant a potato that has sprouted:\n\n1. Cut the sprouted potato into chunks with two or three eyes each, making sure to not break the long sprouts.\n2. Allow the cut ends to dry out overnight in a cool, dark location.\n3. Plant the potato chunks or whole potatoes in well-draining soil, spacing them 24 to 36 inches apart.\n4. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep, and place the potato pieces in the bottom of the trench with the sprouted side facing up.\n5. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings.\n\nNote that the passages do not provide specific information on how to care for the potatoes after planting, such as watering and fertilization.", "pred": "1. Prepare the planting area by turning the soil over to a depth of 12 inches, breaking it up finely with a hoe or tiller, and removing any large rocks and debris.\n2. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep.\n3. Cut the sprouted potato into cubes, ensuring each cube has a sprout or eye side.\n4. Place the cubes in the trench, spacing them about 6 to 8 inches apart and positioning the sprouted or eye side facing up.\n5. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost, then replace the dirt on top of the plantings.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "1. Prepare the planting area by turning the soil over to a depth of 12 inches, breaking it up finely with a hoe or tiller, and removing any large rocks and debris.\n2. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep.\n3. Cut the sprouted potato into cubes, ensuring each cube has a sprout or eye side.\n4. Place the cubes in the trench, spacing them about 6 to 8 inches apart and positioning the sprouted or eye side facing up.\n5. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost, then replace the dirt on top of the plantings.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, to plant a sprouted potato, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. Prepare the planting area by turning the soil over with a hoe or tiller to a depth of 12 inches and breaking it up finely. Remove any large rocks and other debris.\\n2. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep.\\n3. Cut the sprouted potato into cubes, about 1 to 2 inches on each side, leaving a sprout on each cube. Nonsprouted sections should have two or three eyes on each.\\n4. Place the cubes into the bottom of the trench, separating each cube from the other by about 6 to 8 inches. Ensure the sprouted or eye side of the cubes is facing up.\\n5. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings.\", \"Based on the given passages:\\n1. Cut large, sprouted potatoes into chunks with two or three eyes each, using a sharp knife, being careful to not break the long sprouts.\\n2. Place the potato chunks on a tray or cookie sheet, and set them in a cool, dark location overnight so the cut ends can dry out.\\n3. Small potatoes can be planted whole. Water the potatoes thoroughly.\\n4. Continue watering regularly when the soil is dry to the touch.\\n5. Add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, pulling it back at least 4 inches from the sprouting potato vines.\\n6. Mulch keeps the soil cool while maintaining a consistent moisture level.\\nTo plant a sprouted potato, cut it into chunks with two or three eyes each, place the chunks on a tray or cookie sheet, set them in a cool, dark location overnight so the cut ends can dry out, and then plant them in the ground following the steps provided in the instructions.\", \"Based on the given passages, to plant a sprouted potato:\\n\\n1. Cut the sprouted potato into chunks with 2-3 eyes each. Ensure no long sprouts are broken.\\n\\n2. Place the potato chunks on a tray or cookie sheet in a cool, dark location overnight to dry out the cut ends.\\n\\n3. Water the potatoes thoroughly.\\n\\n4. Continue watering regularly when the soil is dry to the touch.\\n\\n5. Add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, keeping it at least 4 inches away from the sprouting potato vines.\\n\\n6. Turn the soil over in your planned planting area to a depth of 12 inches, breaking up the dirt finely and removing large rocks and debris.\\n\\n7. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6-8 inches deep.\\n\\n8. If planting multiple rows, space them apart by 24-36 inches.\\n\\n9. Cut the potatoes into cubes, about 1-2 inches on each side, leaving a sprout on each.\\n\\n10. Place the cubes into the trench, separating each by about 6-8 inches, with the sprouted or eye side facing up.\\n\\n11. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings.\", \"Based on the given passages, to plant a potato that has sprouted, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. Prepare the planting area by turning the soil over to a depth of 12 inches, breaking it up finely with a hoe or tiller, and removing any large rocks and debris.\\n2. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep.\\n3. Cut the sprouted potato into cubes, ensuring each cube has a sprout or eye side.\\n4. Place the cubes in the trench, spacing them about 6 to 8 inches apart and positioning the sprouted or eye side facing up.\\n5. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost, then replace the dirt on top of the plantings.\"]", "time_sec": 32.244, "generation_time_seconds": 30.9598, "selection_time_seconds": 1.2719, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 311, 6008, 264, 8151, 18533, 38734, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 31166, 279, 47014, 3082, 553, 13054, 279, 17171, 916, 448, 264, 45876, 476, 11956, 261, 311, 264, 7990, 315, 220, 16, 17, 14924, 323, 14719, 432, 705, 60702, 13, 10783, 894, 3460, 23035, 323, 1008, 33998, 624, 17, 13, 37969, 264, 53252, 911, 220, 19, 14924, 6884, 323, 220, 21, 311, 220, 23, 14924, 5538, 624, 18, 13, 19649, 279, 8151, 18533, 38734, 1119, 54104, 11, 911, 220, 16, 311, 220, 17, 14924, 389, 1817, 3108, 11, 9380, 264, 8151, 411, 389, 1817, 23739, 13, 451, 2382, 649, 18533, 14158, 1265, 614, 1378, 476, 2326, 6414, 389, 1817, 624, 19, 13, 10771, 279, 54104, 1119, 279, 5622, 315, 279, 53252, 11, 49445, 1817, 23739, 504, 279, 1008, 553, 911, 220, 21, 311, 220, 23, 14924, 13, 29279, 279, 8151, 18533, 476, 7912, 3108, 315, 279, 54104, 374, 12880, 705, 624, 20, 13, 17757, 279, 34167, 448, 264, 6193, 315, 56723, 323, 1221, 8290, 279, 25284, 389, 1909, 315, 279, 6008, 819, 13, 151645], "pred": "1. Prepare the planting area by turning the soil over with a hoe or tiller to a depth of 12 inches and breaking it up finely. Remove any large rocks and other debris.\n2. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep.\n3. Cut the sprouted potato into cubes, about 1 to 2 inches on each side, leaving a sprout on each cube. Nonsprouted sections should have two or three eyes on each.\n4. Place the cubes into the bottom of the trench, separating each cube from the other by about 6 to 8 inches. Ensure the sprouted or eye side of the cubes is facing up.\n5. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.2697047921334992, "g_worst_claim": -0.014578130096197128, "g_content": 1.746929020076603, "g_traj_mean": 1.4020438194274902}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 510, 16, 13, 19649, 3460, 11, 8151, 18533, 34167, 1119, 26757, 448, 1378, 476, 2326, 6414, 1817, 11, 1667, 264, 17232, 21430, 11, 1660, 16585, 311, 537, 1438, 279, 1293, 8151, 11672, 624, 17, 13, 10771, 279, 38734, 26757, 389, 264, 34688, 476, 12544, 10834, 11, 323, 738, 1105, 304, 264, 7010, 11, 6319, 3728, 24398, 773, 279, 3931, 10335, 646, 9058, 700, 624, 18, 13, 14994, 34167, 646, 387, 38341, 4361, 13, 9959, 279, 34167, 26364, 624, 19, 13, 15003, 72741, 15502, 979, 279, 17171, 374, 9058, 311, 279, 5796, 624, 20, 13, 2691, 264, 220, 19, 24241, 6193, 315, 15602, 331, 2163, 279, 10779, 11, 22266, 432, 1182, 518, 3245, 220, 19, 14924, 504, 279, 8151, 10909, 38734, 85429, 624, 21, 13, 29351, 331, 13598, 279, 17171, 7010, 1393, 20337, 264, 12966, 31157, 2188, 624, 1249, 6008, 264, 8151, 18533, 38734, 11, 3931, 432, 1119, 26757, 448, 1378, 476, 2326, 6414, 1817, 11, 1992, 279, 26757, 389, 264, 34688, 476, 12544, 10834, 11, 738, 1105, 304, 264, 7010, 11, 6319, 3728, 24398, 773, 279, 3931, 10335, 646, 9058, 700, 11, 323, 1221, 6008, 1105, 304, 279, 4910, 2701, 279, 7354, 3897, 304, 279, 11221, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages:\n1. Cut large, sprouted potatoes into chunks with two or three eyes each, using a sharp knife, being careful to not break the long sprouts.\n2. Place the potato chunks on a tray or cookie sheet, and set them in a cool, dark location overnight so the cut ends can dry out.\n3. Small potatoes can be planted whole. Water the potatoes thoroughly.\n4. Continue watering regularly when the soil is dry to the touch.\n5. Add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, pulling it back at least 4 inches from the sprouting potato vines.\n6. Mulch keeps the soil cool while maintaining a consistent moisture level.\nTo plant a sprouted potato, cut it into chunks with two or three eyes each, place the chunks on a tray or cookie sheet, set them in a cool, dark location overnight so the cut ends can dry out, and then plant them in the ground following the steps provided in the instructions.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.7141916017370864, "g_worst_claim": -0.017601167613809757, "g_content": 1.7328037979579207, "g_traj_mean": 1.3917601108551025}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 311, 6008, 264, 8151, 18533, 38734, 1447, 16, 13, 19649, 279, 8151, 18533, 38734, 1119, 26757, 448, 220, 17, 12, 18, 6414, 1817, 13, 29279, 902, 1293, 8151, 11672, 525, 10865, 382, 17, 13, 10771, 279, 38734, 26757, 389, 264, 34688, 476, 12544, 10834, 304, 264, 7010, 11, 6319, 3728, 24398, 311, 9058, 700, 279, 3931, 10335, 382, 18, 13, 9959, 279, 34167, 26364, 382, 19, 13, 15003, 72741, 15502, 979, 279, 17171, 374, 9058, 311, 279, 5796, 382, 20, 13, 2691, 264, 220, 19, 24241, 6193, 315, 15602, 331, 2163, 279, 10779, 11, 10282, 432, 518, 3245, 220, 19, 14924, 3123, 504, 279, 8151, 10909, 38734, 85429, 382, 21, 13, 11999, 279, 17171, 916, 304, 697, 12909, 47014, 3082, 311, 264, 7990, 315, 220, 16, 17, 14924, 11, 14719, 705, 279, 25284, 60702, 323, 17592, 3460, 23035, 323, 33998, 382, 22, 13, 37969, 264, 53252, 911, 220, 19, 14924, 6884, 323, 220, 21, 12, 23, 14924, 5538, 382, 23, 13, 1416, 47014, 5248, 6978, 11, 3550, 1105, 10747, 553, 220, 17, 19, 12, 18, 21, 14924, 382, 24, 13, 19649, 279, 34167, 1119, 54104, 11, 911, 220, 16, 12, 17, 14924, 389, 1817, 3108, 11, 9380, 264, 8151, 411, 389, 1817, 382, 16, 15, 13, 10771, 279, 54104, 1119, 279, 53252, 11, 49445, 1817, 553, 911, 220, 21, 12, 23, 14924, 11, 448, 279, 8151, 18533, 476, 7912, 3108, 12880, 705, 382, 16, 16, 13, 17757, 279, 34167, 448, 264, 6193, 315, 56723, 323, 1221, 8290, 279, 25284, 389, 1909, 315, 279, 6008, 819, 13, 151645], "pred": "11. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.7245202692642183, "g_worst_claim": 0.32134777307510376, "g_content": 1.94827639672064, "g_traj_mean": 1.4856250286102295}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 311, 6008, 264, 38734, 429, 702, 8151, 18533, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 31166, 279, 47014, 3082, 553, 13054, 279, 17171, 916, 311, 264, 7990, 315, 220, 16, 17, 14924, 11, 14719, 432, 705, 60702, 448, 264, 45876, 476, 11956, 261, 11, 323, 17592, 894, 3460, 23035, 323, 33998, 624, 17, 13, 37969, 264, 53252, 911, 220, 19, 14924, 6884, 323, 220, 21, 311, 220, 23, 14924, 5538, 624, 18, 13, 19649, 279, 8151, 18533, 38734, 1119, 54104, 11, 22573, 1817, 23739, 702, 264, 8151, 411, 476, 7912, 3108, 624, 19, 13, 10771, 279, 54104, 304, 279, 53252, 11, 25943, 1105, 911, 220, 21, 311, 220, 23, 14924, 10747, 323, 38725, 279, 8151, 18533, 476, 7912, 3108, 12880, 705, 624, 20, 13, 17757, 279, 34167, 448, 264, 6193, 315, 56723, 11, 1221, 8290, 279, 25284, 389, 1909, 315, 279, 6008, 819, 13, 151645], "pred": "1. Prepare the planting area by turning the soil over to a depth of 12 inches, breaking it up finely with a hoe or tiller, and removing any large rocks and debris.\n2. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep.\n3. Cut the sprouted potato into cubes, ensuring each cube has a sprout or eye side.\n4. Place the cubes in the trench, spacing them about 6 to 8 inches apart and positioning the sprouted or eye side facing up.\n5. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost, then replace the dirt on top of the plantings.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.9776334826802557, "g_worst_claim": 0.0050125519434611005, "g_content": 1.3423781675450943, "g_traj_mean": 0.9836918711662292}}], "selected_rollout": 2, "random_sel_rollout": 3, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 50.31, "GAVIE-Rel": 7.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "648", "question": "color of urin meaning", "gt": "The color of urine can indicate various health conditions. Normal urine is typically pale yellow, due to the pigment urochrome. If kidney diseases are present, the filtration and re-absorption stages of urine production may be malfunctioning, altering this pale color. Red urine is typically a sign of blood in the urine, which can indicate serious conditions like infections or cancer. Deep purple urine is an identifying characteristic of porphyria, a rare, inherited disorder of red blood cells. Foods and medications can also affect urine color, with beets, berries, and fava beans being among the most likely to cause changes.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, the color of urine can vary due to several factors. The normal color of urine is pale yellow, which is due to the pigment urochrome. However, this color can be altered in cases of kidney diseases where the filtration and selective re-absorption stages of urine production are malfunctioning. Additionally, certain foods and medications, such as beets, berries, and fava beans, can change the color of urine. Deep purple urine is an identifying characteristic of porphyria, a rare, inherited disorder of red blood cells. Lastly, red urine can be a sign of blood in the urine, which can indicate serious conditions like infections or cancer and warrants a visit to a doctor or urologist.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the color of urine can vary due to several factors. The normal color of urine is pale yellow, which is due to the pigment urochrome. However, this color can be altered in cases of kidney diseases where the filtration and selective re-absorption stages of urine production are malfunctioning. Additionally, certain foods and medications, such as beets, berries, and fava beans, can change the color of urine. Deep purple urine is an identifying characteristic of porphyria, a rare, inherited disorder of red blood cells. Lastly, red urine can be a sign of blood in the urine, which can indicate serious conditions like infections or cancer and warrants a visit to a doctor or urologist.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, the color of urine can vary due to several factors. The normal color of urine is pale yellow, which is due to the pigment urochrome. However, this color can be altered in cases of kidney diseases where the filtration and selective re-absorption stages of urine production are malfunctioning. Additionally, certain foods and medications, such as beets, berries, and fava beans, can change the color of urine. Deep purple urine is an identifying characteristic of porphyria, a rare, inherited disorder of red blood cells. Lastly, red urine can be a sign of blood in the urine, which can indicate serious conditions like infections or cancer and warrants a visit to a doctor or urologist.\", \"Based on the given passages, the color of urine can indicate various conditions. Pale yellow urine is considered normal, caused by the pigment urochrome. However, changes in urine color can be indicative of different issues:\\n\\n1. **Red urine** (seen as a sign of blood in the urine): This can be due to serious conditions like infections or cancer and suggests a need for medical attention.\\n2. **Deep purple urine**: This is characteristic of porphyria, a rare inherited disorder.\\n3. **Other colors**: Changes in urine color can also be caused by pigments and compounds from certain foods and medications. For example, consuming foods like beets, berries, and fava beans can affect urine color. Additionally, altered filtration and selective re-absorption stages in kidney diseases can also change urine color.\", \"Based on the given passages, the normal color of urine is pale yellow due to the pigment urochrome. This pale color of urine can sometimes be altered in cases of kidney diseases where the filtration and selective re-absorption stages of urine production are malfunctioning. If the color changes to red, it may indicate blood in the urine, which can be a sign of a serious condition like an infection or cancer, requiring a visit to a doctor or urologist. If the color turns deep purple, it is an identifying characteristic of porphyria, a rare, inherited disorder of red blood cells.\", \"Based on the given passages, the color of urine can indicate various conditions:\\n\\n1. Red urine indicates the presence of blood in the urine, which can be a sign of serious health issues such as infections or cancer, and should prompt a visit to a doctor or urologist.\\n2. Deep purple urine is indicative of porphyria, a rare inherited disorder of red blood cells.\\n3. The normal color of urine is pale yellow due to the pigment urochrome, but this color can be altered in cases of kidney diseases where the filtration and selective re-absorption stages of urine production are malfunctioning.\\n4. Foods like beets, berries, and fava beans can also cause changes in urine color.\\n5. Pigments and other compounds in certain foods and medications can affect urine color.\"]", "time_sec": 27.353, "generation_time_seconds": 26.5308, "selection_time_seconds": 0.8157, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 1894, 315, 39235, 646, 13289, 4152, 311, 3807, 9363, 13, 576, 4622, 1894, 315, 39235, 374, 27539, 13753, 11, 892, 374, 4152, 311, 279, 76578, 575, 299, 31902, 13, 4354, 11, 419, 1894, 646, 387, 28752, 304, 5048, 315, 37942, 18808, 1380, 279, 74938, 323, 42910, 312, 96410, 65081, 17628, 315, 39235, 5670, 525, 71187, 287, 13, 22406, 11, 3654, 15298, 323, 29910, 11, 1741, 438, 387, 1415, 11, 62394, 11, 323, 282, 2907, 26896, 11, 646, 2297, 279, 1894, 315, 39235, 13, 18183, 24932, 39235, 374, 458, 24588, 28583, 315, 4154, 12700, 4204, 11, 264, 8848, 11, 26990, 19267, 315, 2518, 6543, 7761, 13, 70709, 11, 2518, 39235, 646, 387, 264, 1841, 315, 6543, 304, 279, 39235, 11, 892, 646, 13216, 6001, 4682, 1075, 28920, 476, 9387, 323, 54588, 264, 3947, 311, 264, 10668, 476, 575, 299, 839, 380, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, the color of urine can vary due to several factors. The normal color of urine is pale yellow, which is due to the pigment urochrome. However, this color can be altered in cases of kidney diseases where the filtration and selective re-absorption stages of urine production are malfunctioning. Additionally, certain foods and medications, such as beets, berries, and fava beans, can change the color of urine. Deep purple urine is an identifying characteristic of porphyria, a rare, inherited disorder of red blood cells. Lastly, red urine can be a sign of blood in the urine, which can indicate serious conditions like infections or cancer and warrants a visit to a doctor or urologist.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.2308511865237919, "g_worst_claim": 0.064803204366139, "g_content": 2.2994126712574676, "g_traj_mean": 1.5062230825424194}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 1894, 315, 39235, 646, 13216, 5257, 4682, 13, 12349, 13753, 39235, 374, 6509, 4622, 11, 8881, 553, 279, 76578, 575, 299, 31902, 13, 4354, 11, 4344, 304, 39235, 1894, 646, 387, 62905, 315, 2155, 4714, 1447, 16, 13, 3070, 6033, 39235, 334, 320, 28312, 438, 264, 1841, 315, 6543, 304, 279, 39235, 1648, 1096, 646, 387, 4152, 311, 6001, 4682, 1075, 28920, 476, 9387, 323, 13230, 264, 1184, 369, 6457, 6529, 624, 17, 13, 3070, 33464, 24932, 39235, 95518, 1096, 374, 28583, 315, 4154, 12700, 4204, 11, 264, 8848, 26990, 19267, 624, 18, 13, 3070, 11409, 7987, 95518, 28140, 304, 39235, 1894, 646, 1083, 387, 8881, 553, 23694, 1368, 323, 31146, 504, 3654, 15298, 323, 29910, 13, 1752, 3110, 11, 34108, 15298, 1075, 387, 1415, 11, 62394, 11, 323, 282, 2907, 26896, 646, 7802, 39235, 1894, 13, 22406, 11, 28752, 74938, 323, 42910, 312, 96410, 65081, 17628, 304, 37942, 18808, 646, 1083, 2297, 39235, 1894, 13, 151645], "pred": "1. **Red urine** (seen as a sign of blood in the urine): This can be due to serious conditions like infections or cancer and suggests a need for medical attention.\n2. **Deep purple urine**: This is characteristic of porphyria, a rare inherited disorder.\n3. **Other colors**: Changes in urine color can also be caused by pigments and compounds from certain foods and medications. For example, consuming foods like beets, berries, and fava beans can affect urine color. Additionally, altered filtration and selective re-absorption stages in kidney diseases can also change urine color.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.1542365505279273, "g_worst_claim": 0.386479655901591, "g_content": 1.921976229039634, "g_traj_mean": 1.2364568710327148}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 4622, 1894, 315, 39235, 374, 27539, 13753, 4152, 311, 279, 76578, 575, 299, 31902, 13, 1096, 27539, 1894, 315, 39235, 646, 7025, 387, 28752, 304, 5048, 315, 37942, 18808, 1380, 279, 74938, 323, 42910, 312, 96410, 65081, 17628, 315, 39235, 5670, 525, 71187, 287, 13, 1416, 279, 1894, 4344, 311, 2518, 11, 432, 1231, 13216, 6543, 304, 279, 39235, 11, 892, 646, 387, 264, 1841, 315, 264, 6001, 2971, 1075, 458, 18873, 476, 9387, 11, 22703, 264, 3947, 311, 264, 10668, 476, 575, 299, 839, 380, 13, 1416, 279, 1894, 10577, 5538, 24932, 11, 432, 374, 458, 24588, 28583, 315, 4154, 12700, 4204, 11, 264, 8848, 11, 26990, 19267, 315, 2518, 6543, 7761, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, the normal color of urine is pale yellow due to the pigment urochrome. This pale color of urine can sometimes be altered in cases of kidney diseases where the filtration and selective re-absorption stages of urine production are malfunctioning. If the color changes to red, it may indicate blood in the urine, which can be a sign of a serious condition like an infection or cancer, requiring a visit to a doctor or urologist. If the color turns deep purple, it is an identifying characteristic of porphyria, a rare, inherited disorder of red blood cells.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.3827217373961493, "g_worst_claim": 0.38683025042215985, "g_content": 2.520261219569615, "g_traj_mean": 1.6551533937454224}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 1894, 315, 39235, 646, 13216, 5257, 4682, 1447, 16, 13, 3731, 39235, 14807, 279, 9362, 315, 6543, 304, 279, 39235, 11, 892, 646, 387, 264, 1841, 315, 6001, 2820, 4714, 1741, 438, 28920, 476, 9387, 11, 323, 1265, 9934, 264, 3947, 311, 264, 10668, 476, 575, 299, 839, 380, 624, 17, 13, 18183, 24932, 39235, 374, 62905, 315, 4154, 12700, 4204, 11, 264, 8848, 26990, 19267, 315, 2518, 6543, 7761, 624, 18, 13, 576, 4622, 1894, 315, 39235, 374, 27539, 13753, 4152, 311, 279, 76578, 575, 299, 31902, 11, 714, 419, 1894, 646, 387, 28752, 304, 5048, 315, 37942, 18808, 1380, 279, 74938, 323, 42910, 312, 96410, 65081, 17628, 315, 39235, 5670, 525, 71187, 287, 624, 19, 13, 39060, 1075, 387, 1415, 11, 62394, 11, 323, 282, 2907, 26896, 646, 1083, 5240, 4344, 304, 39235, 1894, 624, 20, 13, 48554, 1368, 323, 1008, 31146, 304, 3654, 15298, 323, 29910, 646, 7802, 39235, 1894, 13, 151645], "pred": "1. Red urine indicates the presence of blood in the urine, which can be a sign of serious health issues such as infections or cancer, and should prompt a visit to a doctor or urologist.\n2. Deep purple urine is indicative of porphyria, a rare inherited disorder of red blood cells.\n3. The normal color of urine is pale yellow due to the pigment urochrome, but this color can be altered in cases of kidney diseases where the filtration and selective re-absorption stages of urine production are malfunctioning.\n4. Foods like beets, berries, and fava beans can also cause changes in urine color.\n5. Pigments and other compounds in certain foods and medications can affect urine color.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.3187874567490172, "g_worst_claim": 0.386479655901591, "g_content": 1.9446080847631526, "g_traj_mean": 1.3797856569290161}}], "selected_rollout": 2, "random_sel_rollout": 0, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 71.62, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.8, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "649", "question": "color of urin meaning", "gt": "The color of urine can indicate different health conditions. Red urine may indicate the presence of blood and could be a sign of an infection or cancer. Deep purple urine can be a characteristic of porphyria, a rare disorder. The normal color of urine is pale yellow due to the pigment urochrome.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, the color of urine can vary due to several factors. The normal color of urine is pale yellow, which is due to the pigment urochrome. However, this color can be altered in cases of kidney diseases where the filtration and selective re-absorption stages of urine production are malfunctioning. Additionally, certain foods and medications, such as beets, berries, and fava beans, can change the color of urine. Deep purple urine is an identifying characteristic of porphyria, a rare, inherited disorder of red blood cells. Lastly, red urine can be a sign of blood in the urine, which can indicate serious conditions like infections or cancer and warrants a visit to a doctor or urologist.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the color of urine can vary due to several factors. The normal color of urine is pale yellow, which is due to the pigment urochrome. However, this color can be altered in cases of kidney diseases where the filtration and selective re-absorption stages of urine production are malfunctioning. Additionally, certain foods and medications, such as beets, berries, and fava beans, can change the color of urine. Deep purple urine is an identifying characteristic of porphyria, a rare, inherited disorder of red blood cells. Lastly, red urine can be a sign of blood in the urine, which can indicate serious conditions like infections or cancer and warrants a visit to a doctor or urologist.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, the color of urine can vary due to several factors. The normal color of urine is pale yellow, which is due to the pigment urochrome. However, this color can be altered in cases of kidney diseases where the filtration and selective re-absorption stages of urine production are malfunctioning. Additionally, certain foods and medications, such as beets, berries, and fava beans, can change the color of urine. Deep purple urine is an identifying characteristic of porphyria, a rare, inherited disorder of red blood cells. Lastly, red urine can be a sign of blood in the urine, which can indicate serious conditions like infections or cancer and warrants a visit to a doctor or urologist.\", \"Based on the given passages, the normal color of urine is pale yellow due to the pigment urochrome. However, the color of urine can be altered by various factors such as kidney diseases, certain foods and medications, and rare inherited disorders like porphyria. For example, eating foods like beets, berries, and fava beans can change urine color to deep purple. Additionally, a sign of blood in the urine is red-colored urine, which can be a serious indicator of conditions like infections or cancer.\", \"Based on the given passages, the color of urine can vary based on several factors:\\n\\n1. **Normal Color**: The normal color of urine is typically pale yellow, due to the pigment urochrome.\\n\\n2. **Red Urine**: Red urine, or hematuria, is often a sign of blood in the urine and can be caused by an infection or cancer, requiring medical attention.\\n\\n3. **Deep Purple Urine**: Deep purple urine is associated with porphyria, a rare, inherited disorder of red blood cells.\\n\\n4. **Food and Medications**: Certain foods and medications, such as beets, berries, and fava beans, can change urine color.\\n\\n5. **Kidney Disease**: In cases of kidney diseases, the normal pale yellow color of urine may be altered due to issues in the filtration and selective re-absorption stages of urine production.\", \"Based on the given passages, the color of urine can vary depending on factors like health conditions and dietary intake. Normal urine color is typically pale yellow due to the pigment urochrome. However, changes in urine color can indicate different issues. For example, red urine may signal a blood-related condition such as an infection or cancer, and warrants medical attention. Purple urine is a sign of porphyria, a rare genetic disorder of red blood cells. On the other hand, colors like red, deep purple, and pale yellow are also influenced by the consumption of certain foods and medications.\"]", "time_sec": 23.647, "generation_time_seconds": 22.6927, "selection_time_seconds": 0.9468, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 1894, 315, 39235, 646, 13289, 4152, 311, 3807, 9363, 13, 576, 4622, 1894, 315, 39235, 374, 27539, 13753, 11, 892, 374, 4152, 311, 279, 76578, 575, 299, 31902, 13, 4354, 11, 419, 1894, 646, 387, 28752, 304, 5048, 315, 37942, 18808, 1380, 279, 74938, 323, 42910, 312, 96410, 65081, 17628, 315, 39235, 5670, 525, 71187, 287, 13, 22406, 11, 3654, 15298, 323, 29910, 11, 1741, 438, 387, 1415, 11, 62394, 11, 323, 282, 2907, 26896, 11, 646, 2297, 279, 1894, 315, 39235, 13, 18183, 24932, 39235, 374, 458, 24588, 28583, 315, 4154, 12700, 4204, 11, 264, 8848, 11, 26990, 19267, 315, 2518, 6543, 7761, 13, 70709, 11, 2518, 39235, 646, 387, 264, 1841, 315, 6543, 304, 279, 39235, 11, 892, 646, 13216, 6001, 4682, 1075, 28920, 476, 9387, 323, 54588, 264, 3947, 311, 264, 10668, 476, 575, 299, 839, 380, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, the color of urine can vary due to several factors. The normal color of urine is pale yellow, which is due to the pigment urochrome. However, this color can be altered in cases of kidney diseases where the filtration and selective re-absorption stages of urine production are malfunctioning. Additionally, certain foods and medications, such as beets, berries, and fava beans, can change the color of urine. Deep purple urine is an identifying characteristic of porphyria, a rare, inherited disorder of red blood cells. Lastly, red urine can be a sign of blood in the urine, which can indicate serious conditions like infections or cancer and warrants a visit to a doctor or urologist.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.2578420820181446, "g_worst_claim": 0.06681297080857414, "g_content": 2.3281074972713696, "g_traj_mean": 1.529186487197876}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 4622, 1894, 315, 39235, 374, 27539, 13753, 4152, 311, 279, 76578, 575, 299, 31902, 13, 4354, 11, 279, 1894, 315, 39235, 646, 387, 28752, 553, 5257, 9363, 1741, 438, 37942, 18808, 11, 3654, 15298, 323, 29910, 11, 323, 8848, 26990, 23741, 1075, 4154, 12700, 4204, 13, 1752, 3110, 11, 12182, 15298, 1075, 387, 1415, 11, 62394, 11, 323, 282, 2907, 26896, 646, 2297, 39235, 1894, 311, 5538, 24932, 13, 22406, 11, 264, 1841, 315, 6543, 304, 279, 39235, 374, 2518, 57722, 39235, 11, 892, 646, 387, 264, 6001, 20438, 315, 4682, 1075, 28920, 476, 9387, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, the normal color of urine is pale yellow due to the pigment urochrome. However, the color of urine can be altered by various factors such as kidney diseases, certain foods and medications, and rare inherited disorders like porphyria. For example, eating foods like beets, berries, and fava beans can change urine color to deep purple. Additionally, a sign of blood in the urine is red-colored urine, which can be a serious indicator of conditions like infections or cancer.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.2235232318960385, "g_worst_claim": 0.48506561915079754, "g_content": 2.2139191260704627, "g_traj_mean": 1.3119324445724487}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 1894, 315, 39235, 646, 13289, 3118, 389, 3807, 9363, 1447, 16, 13, 3070, 12206, 3478, 95518, 576, 4