{"id": "665", "question": "what happens when you breath in and out", "gt": "When you breathe in, the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. The diaphragm also contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, allowing air to be sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out, the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward, and the intercostal muscles also relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. This forces air rich in carbon dioxide out of the lungs and windpipe, and then out of the nose or mouth.", "pred": "When you breathe out, your diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity; the intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "When you breathe out, your diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity; the intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, when you breathe in (inspiration), the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. The diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. Pressure inside the chest is lowered, and air is sucked into the lungs.\\n\\nWhen you breathe out (expiration), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity. The intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.\", \"Based on the given passages, when you breathe in (inhalation), the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest, and the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. This results in a reduction in pressure inside the chest, causing air to be sucked into the lungs.\\n\\nWhen you breathe out (exhalation), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. This causes air rich in carbon dioxide to be forced out of your lungs, windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.\", \"When you breathe in, the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage; the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest; and the pressure inside the chest is lowered, which causes air to be sucked into the lungs.\\n\\nWhen you breathe out, your diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity; the intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.\", \"Based on the given passages, when you breathe in (inspiration), the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. The diaphragm also contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, and air is sucked into the lungs.\\n\\nWhen you breathe out (expiration), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity. The intercostal muscles relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.\"]", "time_sec": 22.864, "generation_time_seconds": 22.0259, "selection_time_seconds": 0.8297, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715,