3, 151645], "pred": "When you breathe in, the intercostal muscles contract, causing the ribcage to expand and the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest, lowering the pressure inside the chest, and causing air to be sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out, the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles also relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth. Breathing out requires no effort from your body unless you have a lung disease or are doing physical activity.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.1977721776716193, "g_worst_claim": 0.13058980873652867, "g_content": 1.8092711130777994, "g_traj_mean": 1.2526882886886597}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 1588, 594, 1128, 8573, 979, 498, 36297, 304, 323, 700, 1447, 50229, 43561, 758, 320, 641, 11866, 367, 982, 16, 13, 576, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 5116, 11, 23175, 279, 20131, 66, 424, 624, 17, 13, 576, 1853, 94796, 76, 17080, 11, 22266, 91270, 311, 5263, 279, 8123, 315, 279, 15138, 624, 18, 13, 38990, 4766, 279, 15138, 374, 38820, 11, 323, 3720, 374, 52212, 1119, 279, 44174, 382, 50229, 43561, 4371, 320, 840, 11866, 367, 982, 16, 13, 576, 1853, 94796, 76, 11967, 288, 323, 10797, 39772, 1119, 279, 15138, 55329, 624, 17, 13, 576, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 1948, 279, 55149, 1083, 11967, 311, 7949, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 624, 18, 13, 1634, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 5221, 9155, 11, 3720, 9080, 304, 12499, 39489, 374, 9575, 700, 315, 279, 44174, 323, 9956, 13768, 11, 323, 1221, 700, 315, 279, 19142, 476, 10780, 13, 151645], "pred": "Breathing Out (Exhalation):\n1. The diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity.\n2. The intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity.\n3. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of the lungs and windpipe, and then out of the nose or mouth.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.151110101893202, "g_worst_claim": -0.0006593117889549051, "g_content": 1.621430193791624, "g_traj_mean": 1.1473746299743652}}], "selected_rollout": 2, "random_sel_rollout": 1, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 43.1, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "669", "question": "what happens when you breath in and out", "gt": "Sure! Here's my answer based on the given passages:\n\nWhen you breathe in, the diaphragm contracts and pulls downwards, increasing the volume of the chest cavity. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, allowing air to enter the lungs. The intercostal muscles between the ribs also contract, expanding the ribcage and further increasing the space in the chest cavity. Air enters the lungs through the windpipe (trachea) and fills the expanded lung tissue.\n\nWhen you breathe out, the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, reducing the space in the chest cavity. The intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax, allowing the chest cavity to decrease in size. As the space in the chest cavity decreases, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of the lungs and windpipe, and then out of the nose or mouth.\n\nTherefore, when you breathe in and out, air enters the lungs during inhalation and leaves the lungs during exhalation.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, when you breathe in (inspiration), the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. The intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, and air is sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out (expiration), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles also relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs, windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, when you breathe in (inspiration), the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. The intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, and air is sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out (expiration), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles also relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs, windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, when you breathe in (inspiration), the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. The diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. Pressure inside the chest is lowered, and air is sucked into the lungs.\\n\\nWhen you breathe out (expiration), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity. The intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.\", \"Based on the given passages, when you breathe in (inspiration), the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. The intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, and air is sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out (expiration), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles also relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs, windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.\", \"Based on the given passages, when you breathe out (exhalation), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, reducing the volume of the chest. The intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax, decreasing the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth. This process requires no effort unless you have a lung disease or are doing physical activity. When you breathe in (inhale), the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. The diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. This causes pressure inside the chest to lower, allowing air to be sucked into the lungs.\", \"Based on the given passages, when you breathe in (inhale):\\n1. The intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage.\\n2. The diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest.\\n3. Pressure inside the chest is lowered and air is sucked into the lungs.\\n\\nWhen you breathe out (exhale):\\n1. The diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity.\\n2. The intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity.\\n3. The air, rich in carbon dioxide, is forced out of your lungs, windpipe, and out of your nose or mouth.\\n4. Exhaling requires no effort unless you have a lung disease or are doing physical activity.\"]", "time_sec": 23.462, "generation_time_seconds": 22.5935, "selection_time_seconds": 0.8587, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 979, 498, 36297, 304, 320, 1330, 28479, 701, 279, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 5116, 11, 23175, 279, 20131, 66, 424, 13, 576, 1853, 94796, 76, 17080, 11, 22266, 91270, 311, 5263, 279, 8123, 315, 279, 15138, 13, 38990, 4766, 279, 15138, 374, 38820, 11, 323, 3720, 374, 52212, 1119, 279, 44174, 382, 4498, 498, 36297, 700, 320, 63643, 701, 279, 1853, 94796, 76, 11967, 288, 323, 10797, 39772, 1119, 279, 15138, 55329, 13, 576, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 1948, 279, 55149, 1083, 11967, 311, 7949, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 13, 1634, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 5221, 9155, 11, 3720, 9080, 304, 12499, 39489, 374, 9575, 700, 315, 697, 44174, 323, 9956, 13768, 11, 323, 1221, 700, 315, 697, 19142, 476, 10780, 13, 151645], "pred": "When you breathe out (expiration), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity. The intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.192734705541927, "g_worst_claim": -0.004437801561185292, "g_content": 1.7597760800962094, "g_traj_mean": 1.2824193239212036}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 979, 498, 36297, 304, 320, 1330, 28479, 701, 279, 1853, 94796, 76, 17080, 11, 22266, 91270, 311, 5263, 279, 8123, 315, 279, 15138, 13, 576, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 5116, 11, 23175, 279, 20131, 66, 424, 13, 1096, 72015, 279, 7262, 4766, 279, 15138, 11, 323, 3720, 374, 52212, 1119, 279, 44174, 13, 3197, 498, 36297, 700, 320, 63643, 701, 279, 1853, 94796, 76, 11967, 288, 323, 10797, 39772, 1119, 279, 15138, 55329, 11, 323, 279, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 1083, 11967, 311, 7949, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 13, 1634, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 5221, 9155, 11, 3720, 9080, 304, 12499, 39489, 374, 9575, 700, 315, 697, 44174, 11, 9956, 13768, 11, 323, 1221, 700, 315, 697, 19142, 476, 10780, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, when you breathe in (inspiration), the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. The intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, and air is sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out (expiration), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles also relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs, windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.93989982