7078971862793}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 979, 498, 36297, 304, 320, 20868, 1574, 982, 16, 13, 576, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 5116, 11, 23175, 279, 20131, 66, 424, 624, 17, 13, 576, 1853, 94796, 76, 17080, 11, 22266, 91270, 311, 5263, 279, 8123, 315, 279, 15138, 624, 18, 13, 38990, 4766, 279, 15138, 374, 38820, 323, 3720, 374, 52212, 1119, 279, 44174, 382, 4498, 498, 36297, 700, 320, 327, 71, 1574, 982, 16, 13, 576, 1853, 94796, 76, 11967, 288, 323, 10797, 39772, 1119, 279, 15138, 55329, 624, 17, 13, 576, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 1948, 279, 55149, 1083, 11967, 311, 7949, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 624, 18, 13, 576, 3720, 11, 9080, 304, 12499, 39489, 11, 374, 9575, 700, 315, 697, 44174, 11, 9956, 13768, 11, 323, 700, 315, 697, 19142, 476, 10780, 624, 19, 13, 1374, 71, 6132, 7460, 902, 5041, 7241, 498, 614, 264, 20622, 8457, 476, 525, 3730, 6961, 5702, 13, 151645], "pred": "When you breathe out (exhale):\n1. The diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity.\n2. The intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity.\n3. The air, rich in carbon dioxide, is forced out of your lungs, windpipe, and out of your nose or mouth.\n4. Exhaling requires no effort unless you have a lung disease or are doing physical activity.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.1974549008290905, "g_worst_claim": 0.0011243359185755253, "g_content": 1.5880284309387207, "g_traj_mean": 1.21406090259552}}], "selected_rollout": 0, "random_sel_rollout": 1, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 65.06, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "670", "question": "inventory costing weighted average", "gt": "The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each item of inventory when it is sold. This is calculated by taking the average cost of all inventories currently available. This cost fluctuates as inventory prices change. The weighted average cost per unit is determined by dividing the cost of goods available for sale by the number of units available for sale. This cost is then assigned to both ending inventory and the cost of goods sold. This method is often used when it's impossible to assign specific costs to individual units or when the accounting system isn't sophisticated enough to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers. The recorded amount of inventory on hand using this method represents a value between the oldest and newest units purchased into stock.", "pred": "The weighted average method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "The weighted average method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, the weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and assigns it to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.\", \"Based on the given passages, the weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method calculates the average cost of all inventories currently available when a unit is sold. The weighted average cost is determined by dividing the cost of goods available for sale by the number of units available for sale, which yields the weighted-average cost per unit. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or where the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.\", \"Based on the given passages, the weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available when a unit of inventory is sold. The weighted average cost will change as inventory prices fluctuate. This method is used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers. The net result of using weighted average costing is that the recorded amount of inventory on hand represents a value somewhere between the oldest and newest units purchased into stock.\", \"Based on the given passages:\\n\\nThe inventory costing method known as the weighted average method assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method uses the average cost of all inventories currently available to determine the price of the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change. \\n\\nTo determine the weighted average cost per unit, the cost of goods available for sale is divided by the number of units available for sale. This calculation includes the sum of beginning inventory and net purchases. Using this weighted-average figure, the cost is assigned to both ending inventory and the cost of goods sold.\\n\\nThe weighted average method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.\"]", "time_sec": 27.977, "generation_time_seconds": 26.7928, "selection_time_seconds": 1.1785, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 36824, 5461, 1714, 315, 15444, 53724, 49912, 458, 5461, 2783, 311, 1817, 6573, 315, 15444, 979, 432, 374, 6088, 13, 1096, 1714, 4990, 279, 5461, 2783, 315, 678, 17023, 2433, 5023, 2500, 323, 49912, 432, 311, 279, 1509, 6088, 13, 8704, 15444, 7576, 38288, 6292, 11, 279, 36824, 5461, 2783, 686, 1083, 2297, 13, 1096, 1714, 374, 16626, 1483, 304, 14740, 1380, 15444, 3589, 525, 773, 946, 5311, 832, 429, 432, 374, 11997, 311, 9793, 264, 3151, 2783, 311, 458, 3842, 4982, 11, 476, 979, 279, 23168, 1849, 374, 537, 38944, 26779, 311, 3754, 47007, 476, 444, 25997, 15444, 13617, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, the weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and assigns it to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.6560383405433006, "g_worst_claim": 0.330463985602061, "g_content": 2.3293765032732927, "g_traj_mean": 1.613392949104309}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 36824, 5461, 1714, 315, 15444, 53724, 49912, 458, 5461, 2783, 311, 1817, 6573, 315, 15444, 979, 432, 374, 6088, 13, 1096, 1714, 47417, 279, 5461, 2783, 315, 678, 17023, 2433, 5023, 2500, 979, 264, 4982, 374, 6088, 13, 576, 36824, 5461, 2783, 374, 10838, 553, 49702, 279, 2783, 315, 11561, 2500, 369, 6278, 553, 279, 1372, 315, 8153, 2500, 369, 6278, 11, 892, 35408, 279, 36824, 77426, 2783, 817, 4982, 13, 1096, 1714, 374, 16626, 1483, 304, 14740, 1380, 15444, 3589, 525, 773, 946, 5311, 832, 429, 432, 374, 11997, 311, 9793, 264, 3151, 2783, 311, 458, 3842, 4982, 11, 476, 1380, 279, 23168, 1849, 374, 537, 38944, 26779, 311, 3754, 47007, 476, 444, 25997, 15444, 13617, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, the weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method calculates the average cost of all inventories currently available when a unit is sold. The weighted average cost is determined by dividing the cost of goods available for sale by the number of units available for sale, which yields the weighted-average cost per unit. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or where the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.2402105533924754, "g_worst_claim": 0.330463985602061, "g_content": 1.6839840601360987, "g_traj_mean": 1.2460873126983643}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 36824, 5461, 1714, 315, 15444, 53724, 49912, 458, 5461, 2783, 311, 1817, 6573, 315, 15444, 979, 432, 374, 6088, 13, 1096, 1714, 4990, 279, 5461, 2783, 315, 678, 17023, 2433, 5023, 2500, 979, 264, 4982, 315, 15444, 374, 6088, 13, 576, 36824, 5461, 2783, 686, 2297, 438, 15444, 7576, 38288, 6292, 13, 1096, 1714, 374, 1483, 304, 14740, 1380, 15444, 3589, 525, 773, 946, 5311, 832, 429, 432, 374, 11997, 311, 9793, 264, 3151, 2783, 311, 458, 3842, 4982, 11, 476, 979, 279, 23168, 1849, 374, 537, 38944, 26779, 311, 3754, 47007, 476, 444, 25997, 15444, 13617, 13, 576, 4179, 1102, 315, 1667, 36824, 5461, 53724, 374, 429, 279, 12433, 3311, 315, 15444, 389, 1424, 10868, 264, 897, 14696, 1948, 279, 23513, 323, 23601, 8153, 14733, 1119, 5591, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, the weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available when a unit of inventory is sold. The weighted average cost will change as inventory prices fluctuate. This method is used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers. The net result of using weighted average costing is that the recorded amount of inventory on hand represents a value somewhere between the oldest and newest units purchased into stock.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.6268990771448366, "g_worst_claim": 0.330463985602061, "g_content": 2.397444152832031, "g_traj_mean": 1.7728617191314697}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 1447, 785, 15444, 53724, 1714, 3881, 438, 279, 36824, 5461, 1714, 49912, 458, 5461, 2783, 311, 1817, 6573, 315, 15444, 979, 432, 374, 6088, 13, 1096, 1714, 5711, 279, 5461, 2783, 315, 678, 17023, 2433, 5023, 2500, 311, 8253, 279, 3349, 315, 279, 1509, 6088, 13, 8704, 15444, 7576, 38288, 6292, 11, 279, 36824, 5461, 2783, 686, 1083, 2297, 13, 4710, 1249, 8253, 279, 36824, 5461, 2783, 817, 4982, 11, 279, 2783, 315, 11561, 2500, 369, 6278, 374, 17779, 553, 279, 1372, 315, 8153, 2500, 369, 6278, 13, 1096, 21937, 5646, 279, 2629, 315, 7167, 15444, 323, 4179, 23491, 13, 12091, 419, 36824, 77426, 7071, 11, 279, 2783, 374, 12607, 311, 2176, 13391, 15444, 323, 279, 2783, 315, 11561, 6088, 382, 785, 36824, 5461, 1714, 374, 16626, 1483, 304, 14740, 1380, 15444, 3589, 525, 773, 946, 5311, 832, 429, 432, 374, 11997, 311, 9793, 264, 3151, 2783, 311, 458, 3842, 4982, 11, 476, 979, 279, 23168, 1849, 374, 537, 38944, 26779, 311, 3754, 47007, 476, 444, 25997, 15444, 13617, 13, 151645], "pred": "The weighted average method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.3406220147137267, "g_worst_claim": 0.1332308186425103, "g_content": 1.6411062387319713, "g_traj_mean": 1.2291417121887207}}], "selected_rollout": 2, "random_sel_rollout": 3, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 35.71, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "672", "question": "inventory costing weighted average", "gt": "Based on the given passages, it can be understood that the Weighted Average Costing (WAC) method is used to determine the average cost of inventory when a unit is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and uses it to calculate the weighted average cost per unit. WAC is commonly used when inventory items are intermingled and it's difficult to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sophisticated enough to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, the weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method involves taking the average cost of all inventories currently available when a unit is sold. The average price assigned to the item sold reflects the fluctuating inventory prices, which consequently changes the weighted average cost. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers. The net result of using the weighted average costing method is that the recorded amount of inventory on hand represents a value somewhere between the oldest and newest units purchased into stock.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method involves taking the average cost of all inventories currently available when a unit is sold. The average price assigned to the item sold reflects the fluctuating inventory prices, which consequently changes the weighted average cost. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers. The net result of using the weighted average costing method is that the recorded amount of inventory on hand represents a value somewhere between the oldest and newest units purchased into stock.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, the weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and assigns it to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.\", \"Based on the given passages, the weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. The calculation involves dividing the cost of goods available for sale by the number of units available for sale, which yields the weighted-average cost per unit. This average price is then assigned to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.\", \"Based on the given passages, the weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. When a unit of inventory is sold, the weighted average method uses the average cost of all inventories currently available to assign a cost to the item sold. This average price fluctuates as inventory prices change. The weighted average method is frequently used in situations where inventory items are intermingled and it is difficult to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sophisticated enough to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers. This method results in the recorded amount of inventory on hand representing a value somewhere between the oldest and newest units purchased into stock.\", \"Based on the given passages, the weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method involves taking the average cost of all inventories currently available when a unit is sold. The average price assigned to the item sold reflects the fluctuating inventory prices, which consequently changes the weighted average cost. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers. The net result of using the weighted average costing method is that the recorded amount of inventory on hand represents a value somewhere between the oldest and newest units purchased into stock.\"]", "time_sec": 24.186, "generation_time_seconds": 22.9645, "selection_time_seconds": 1.2142, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 36824, 5461, 1714, 315, 15444, 53724, 49912, 458, 5461, 2783, 311, 1817, 6573, 315, 15444, 979, 432, 374, 6088, 13, 1096, 1714, 4990, 279, 5461, 2783, 315, 678, 17023, 2433, 5023, 2500, 323, 49912, 432, 311, 279, 1509, 6088, 13, 8704, 15444, 7576, 38288, 6292, 11, 279, 36824, 5461, 2783, 686, 1083, 2297, 13, 1096, 1714, 374, 16626, 1483, 304, 14740, 1380, 15444, 3589, 525, 773, 946, 5311,