{"id": "673", "question": "inventory costing weighted average", "gt": "Sure! Here's my answer to the question:\n\nThe weighted average method of costing inventory assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. When a unit of inventory is sold, the weighted average method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and assigns that average price to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change.\n\nTo calculate the weighted average cost per unit, divide the cost of goods available for sale by the number of units available for sale. The weighted-average figure can then be used to assign costs to both ending inventory and cost of goods sold. The recorded amount of inventory on hand using the weighted average costing method represents a value between the oldest and newest units purchased into stock.\n\nWeighted average costing is commonly used in situations where inventory items are intermingled and it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sophisticated enough to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, the weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and assigns it to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and assigns it to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, the weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and assigns it to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.\", \"Based on the given passages, the weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This average cost is calculated by dividing the cost of goods available for sale by the number of units available for sale, which yields the weighted-average cost per unit. The cost of goods available for sale is the sum of beginning inventory and net purchases. The weighted average cost per unit is then used to assign a cost to both ending inventory and the cost of goods sold. This method is commonly used when inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.\", \"Based on the given passages, the weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method divides the cost of goods available for sale by the number of units available for sale to determine the weighted-average cost per unit. This cost is then used to assign a cost to both the ending inventory and the cost of goods sold. The weighted average costing method is commonly used when inventory items are intermingled, making it impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sophisticated enough to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers. This method results in inventory on hand being valued somewhere between the oldest and newest units purchased into stock.\", \"Based on the given passages, the weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method involves dividing the cost of goods available for sale by the number of units available for sale to determine the weighted-average cost per unit. The weighted average cost per unit is then used to assign a cost to both the ending inventory and the cost of goods sold. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers. Inventory prices fluctuating means the weighted average cost will also change.\"]", "time_sec": 23.443, "generation_time_seconds": 22.3728, "selection_time_seconds": 1.0621, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 36824, 5461, 1714, 315, 15444, 53724, 49912, 458, 5461, 2783, 311, 1817, 6573, 315, 15444, 979, 432, 374, 6088, 13, 1096, 1714, 4990, 279, 5461, 2783, 315, 678, 17023, 2433, 5023, 2500, 323, 49912, 432, 311, 279, 1509, 6088, 13, 8704, 15444, 7576, 38288, 6292, 11, 279, 36824, 5461, 2783, 686, 1083, 2297, 13, 1096, 1714, 374, 16626, 1483, 304, 14740, 1380, 15444, 3589