{"id": "675", "question": "inventory costing weighted average", "gt": "Sure, I can help you with that! Based on the given passages, here's a brief answer to your question:\n\nThe weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. The cost of goods available for sale is divided by the number of units available for sale to calculate the weighted-average cost per unit. This figure is then used to assign a cost to both ending inventory and the cost of goods sold. The weighted average method is commonly used when inventory items are intermingled and cannot be assigned a specific cost, or when the accounting system is not sophisticated enough to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, the weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and assigns it to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and assigns it to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, the weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and assigns it to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.\", \"Based on the given passages, inventory costing using the weighted average method assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This average cost is calculated by dividing the cost of goods available for sale by the number of units available for sale, yielding a weighted-average cost per unit. The cost of goods available for sale is the sum of beginning inventory and net purchases. This method is commonly used when inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, and the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers. The net result is that the recorded amount of inventory on hand represents a value somewhere between the oldest and newest units purchased into stock.\", \"Based on the given passages, the inventory costing method known as the weighted average method involves assigning an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method calculates the weighted-average cost per unit by dividing the cost of goods available for sale by the number of units available for sale. The cost of goods available for sale includes the beginning inventory plus net purchases. \\n\\nThe weighted-average cost per unit is then used to assign a cost to both the ending inventory and the cost of goods sold. This method results in a recorded inventory value that is somewhere between the oldest and newest units purchased. It is particularly useful when inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sophisticated enough to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.\", \"Based on the given passages, inventory costing using the weighted average method involves assigning an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available, which can change as inventory prices fluctuate. The weighted average method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are intermingled and it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sophisticated enough to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers. This costing method ensures that the recorded amount of inventory on hand represents a value somewhere between the oldest and newest units purchased.\"]", "time_sec": 24.276, "generation_time_seconds": 23.2438, "selection_time_seconds": 1.0226, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 36824, 5461, 1714, 315, 15444, 53724, 49912, 458, 5461, 2783, 311, 1817, 6573, 315, 15444, 979, 432, 374, 6088, 13, 1096, 1714, 4990, 279, 5461, 2783, 315, 678, 17023, 2433, 5023, 2500, 323, 49912, 432, 311, 279, 1509, 6088, 13, 8704, 15444, 7576, 38288, 6292, 11, 279, 36824, 5461, 2783, 686, 1083, 2297, 13, 1096, 1714, 374, 16626, 1483, 304, 14740, 1380, 15444, 3589, 525, 773, 946, 5311, 832, 429, 432, 374, 11997, 311, 9793, 264, 3151, 2783, 311, 458, 3842, 4982, 11, 476, 979, 279, 23168, 1849, 374, 537, 38944, 26779, 311, 3754, 47007, 476, 444, 25997, 15444, 13617, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, the weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and assigns it to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.902091884145111, "g_worst_claim": 0.4032274881998698, "g_content": 2.53257977520978, "g_traj_mean": 1.7194676399230957}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 15444, 53724, 1667, 279, 36824, 5461, 1714, 49912, 458, 5461, 2783, 311, 1817, 6573, 315, 15444, 979, 432, 374, 6088, 13, 1096, 5461, 2783, 374, 16588, 553, 49702, 279, 2783, 315, 11561, 2500, 369, 6278, 553, 279, 1372, 315, 8153, 2500, 369, 6278, 11, 77404, 264, 36824, 77426, 2783, 817, 4982, 13, 576, 2783, 315, 11561, 2500, 369, 6278, 374, 279, 2629, 315, 7167, 15444, 323, 4179, 23491, 13, 1096, 1714, 374, 16626, 1483, 979, 15444, 3589, 525, 773, 946, 5311, 832, 429, 432, 374, 11997, 311, 9793, 264, 3151, 2783, 311, 458, 3842, 4982, 11, 323, 279, 23168, 1849, 374, 537, 38944, 26779, 311, 3754, 47007, 476, 444, 25997, 15444, 13617, 13, 576, 4179, 1102, 374, 429, 279, 12433, 3311, 315, 15444, 389, 1424, 10868, 264, 897, 14696, 1948, 279, 23513, 323, 23601, 8153, 14733, 1119, 5591, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, inventory costing using the weighted average method assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This average cost is calculated by dividing the cost of goods available for sale by the number of units available for sale, yielding a weighted-average cost per unit. The cost of goods available for sale is the sum of beginning inventory and net purchases. This method is commonly used when inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, and the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers. The net result is that the recorded amount of inventory on hand represents a value somewhere between the oldest and newest units purchased into stock.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.4128763260575141, "g_worst_claim": 0.40520862738291424, "g_content": 2.0638118543122945, "g_traj_mean": 1.5130218267440796}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 15444, 53724, 1714, 3881, 438, 279, 36824, 5461, 1714, 17601, 60753, 458, 5461, 2783, 311, 1817, 6573, 315, 15444, 979, 432, 374, 6088, 13, 1096, 1714, 47417, 279, 36824, 77426, 2783, 817, 4982, 553, 49702, 279, 2783, 315, 11561, 2500, 369, 6278, 553, 279, 1372, 315, 8153, 2500, 369, 6278, 13, 576, 2783, 315, 11561, 2500, 369, 6278, 5646, 279, 7167, 15444, 5519, 4179, 23491, 13, 4710, 785, 36824, 77426, 2783, 817, 4982, 374, 1221, 1483, 311, 9793, 264, 2783, 311, 2176, 279, 13391, 15444, 323, 279, 2783, 315, 11561, 6088, 13, 1096, 1714, 3059, 304, 264, 12433, 15444, 897, 429, 374, 14696, 1948, 279, 23513, 323, 23601, 8153, 14733, 13, 1084, 374, 7945, 5390, 979, 15444, 3589, 525, 773, 946, 5311, 832, 429, 432, 374, 11997, 311, 9793, 264, 3151, 2783, 311, 458, 3842, 4982, 11, 476, 979, 279, 23168, 1849, 374, 537, 26779, 3322, 311, 3754, 47007, 476, 444, 25997, 15444, 13617, 13, 151645], "pred": "The weighted-average cost per unit is then used to assign a cost to both the ending inventory and the cost of goods sold. This method results in a recorded inventory value that is somewhere between the oldest and newest units purchased. It is particularly useful when inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sophisticated enough to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.9743168216298448, "g_worst_claim": 0.39035011927286783, "g_content": 1.356931413922991, "g_traj_mean": 0.9965724945068359}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 15444, 53724, 1667, 279, 36824, 5461, 1714, 17601, 60753, 458, 5461, 2783, 311, 1817, 6573, 315, 15444, 979, 432, 374, 6088, 13, 1096, 1714, 4990, 279, 5461, 2783, 315, 678, 17023, 2433, 5023, 2500, 11, 892, 646, 2297, 438, 15444, 7576, 38288, 6292, 13, 576, 36824, 5461, 1714, 374, 16626, 1483, 304, 14740, 1380, 15444, 3589, 525, 946, 5311, 832, 323, 432, 374, 11997, 311, 9793, 264, 3151, 2783, 311, 458, 3842, 4982, 11, 476, 979, 279, 23168, 1849, 374, 537, 26779, 3322, 311, 3754, 47007, 476, 444, 25997, 15444, 13617, 13, 1096, 53724, 1714, 25351, 429, 279, 12433, 3311, 315, 15444, 389, 1424, 10868, 264, 897, 14696, 1948, 279, 23513, 323, 23601, 8153, 14733, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, inventory costing using the weighted average method involves assigning an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available, which can change as inventory prices fluctuate. The weighted average method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are intermingled and it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sophisticated enough to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers. This costing method ensures that the recorded amount of inventory on hand represents a value somewhere between the oldest and newest units purchased.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.5478531906059334, "g_worst_claim": 0.4032274881998698, "g_content": 2.3665381798377405, "g_traj_mean": 1.5693612098693848}}], "selected_rollout": 0, "random_sel_rollout": 0, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 63.24, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "677", "question": "benefits and side effects of drinking clove tea", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the benefits of drinking clove tea include soothing the senses, relieving stress, providing relief from abdominal pain and aiding digestion. The side effects of drinking clove tea are not mentioned in the given passages.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, drinking clove tea provides various benefits including stress relief, pain relief for muscle, joint, and ligament injuries, and aiding in digestion. In terms of side effects, it should be applied to unbroken skin and a cold tea compress should be used if there is a lot of swelling noted with the injury. Warm tea works better for arthritic pain.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, drinking clove tea provides various benefits including stress relief, pain relief for muscle, joint, and ligament injuries, and aiding in digestion. In terms of side effects, it should be applied to unbroken skin and a cold tea compress should be used if there is a lot of swelling noted with the injury. Warm tea works better for arthritic pain.", "cot": "[\"Based on the given passages, the benefits of drinking clove tea include:\\n\\n1. Soothing the senses and relieving stress in the body.\\n2. Providing relief from stress when mixed with basil, mint, and cardamom in water and taken with honey.\\n3. Having skin benefits, although specific details are not provided.\\n4. Relieving pain from muscle, joint, or ligament injuries due to its analgesic effects.\\n5. Being applied to unbroken skin for pain relief.\\n6. Working better with arthritic pain when applied as a warm tea compress.\\n7. Soothing abdominal pain and aiding in digestion for those suffering from gas pain or other stomach upset.\\n8. Stimulating blood flow, saliva production, and gastric secretions to aid in digestion.\\n\\nThere are no side effects mentioned in the provided passages.\", \"Based on the given passages, drinking clove tea provides various benefits including stress relief, pain relief for muscle, joint, and ligament injuries, and aiding in digestion. In terms of side effects, it should be applied to unbroken skin and a cold tea compress should be used if there is a lot of swelling noted with the injury. Warm tea works better for arthritic pain.\", \"Based on the given passages, the benefits of drinking clove tea include:\\n\\n1. Soothing the senses and relieving stress.\\n2. Providing relief from stress when mixed with honey and other herbs like basil, mint, and cardamom.\\n3. Offering skin benefits.\\n4. Relieving pain from muscle, joint, or ligament injuries due to its analgesic effects.\\n5. Being effective for oral infusion to soothe abdominal pain and aid in digestion by stimulating blood flow, saliva production, and gastric secretions.\\n6. Working well for those suffering from gas pain or other stomach upset.\\n\\nThere is no mention of side effects in the provided passages, so no side effects can be determined from the given information.\", \"Based on the given passages, the benefits of drinking clove tea include soothing the senses, relieving stress, promoting digestion, and providing relief from abdominal pain, gas pain, and other stomach upsets. Clove tea also contains analgesic qualities that can help with pain relief from muscle, joint, and ligament injuries, as well as arthritis pain. It is recommended to apply clove tea compresses to unbroken skin for pain relief. The passages do not mention any side effects of drinking clove tea.\"]", "time_sec": 22.454, "generation_time_seconds": 21.3332, "selection_time_seconds": 1.1133, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 7567, 315, 16163, 5292, 586, 15243, 2924, 1447, 16, 13, 2055, 78, 1596, 279, 37107, 323, 58544, 4405, 8464, 304, 279, 2487, 624, 17, 13, 80100, 15957, 504, 8464, 979, 9519, 448, 67086, 11, 28337, 11, 323, 3701, 309, 316, 304, 3015, 323, 4429, 448, 25744, 624, 18, 13, 20035, 6787, 7567, 11, 7892, 3151, 3565, 525, 537, 3897, 624, 19, 13, 6512, 83346, 6646, 504, 15747, 11, 10284, 11, 476, 28313, 2838, 14970, 4152, 311, 1181, 3181, 4188, 292, 6239, 624, 20, 13, 20690, 9251, 311, 650, 48909, 6787, 369, 6646, 15957, 624, 21, 13, 22150, 2664, 448, 796, 339, 49208, 6646, 979, 9251, 438, 264, 8205, 15243, 24611, 624, 22, 13, 2055, 78, 1596, 55956, 6646, 323, 85287, 304, 73402, 369, 1846, 15691, 504, 6819, 6646, 476, 1008, 22350, 22459, 624, 23, 13, 70772, 15486, 6543, 6396, 11, 84557, 5670, 11, 323, 88285, 6234, 908, 311, 12296, 304, 73402, 382, 3862, 525, 902, 3108, 6239, 9733, 304, 279, 3897, 46769, 13, 151645], "pred": "There are no side effects mentioned in the provided passages.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 2.0846874495641017, "g_worst_claim": 0.26539351723410864, "g_content": 2.4605419017650463, "g_traj_mean": 1.7262943983078003}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 16163, 5292, 586, 15243, 5707, 5257, 7567, 2670, 8464, 15957, 11, 6646, 15957, 369, 15747, 11, 10284, 11, 323, 28313, 2838, 14970, 11, 323, 85287, 304, 73402, 13, 758, 3793, 315, 3108, 6239, 11, 432, 1265, 387, 9251, 311, 650, 48909, 6787, 323, 264, 9255, 15243, 24611, 1265, 387, 1483, 421, 1052, 374, 264, 2696, 315, 54207, 10342, 448, 279, 10895, 13, 45763, 15243, 4278, 2664, 369, 796, 339, 49208, 6646, 13, 151645], "pred": "Based on the given passages, drinking clove tea provides various benefits including stress relief, pain relief for muscle, joint, and ligament injuries, and aiding in digestion. In terms of side effects, it should be applied to unbroken skin and a cold tea compress should be used if there is a lot of swelling noted with the injury. Warm tea works better for arthritic pain.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.3041750028697185, "g_worst_claim": -0.16560